// Longer, DK-style material for History articles: a lead, extra sections, key facts and key dates.
window.DEEP=window.DEEP||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP,{
egypt:{l:"Ancient Egypt was one of the world’s first great civilizations. For about 3,000 years, kings called pharaohs ruled a long, narrow strip of fertile land along the Nile, building colossal pyramids and temples, developing one of the earliest writing systems and leaving behind treasures that still fascinate the world.",
s:[["The gift of the Nile","Egypt is mostly desert, but every summer the Nile flooded and spread rich black silt over its banks. Farmers grew wheat, barley and flax in this soil, and the Egyptians called their land Kemet, the black land. The river was also the country’s highway, carrying boats loaded with grain, stone and people."],
["Kingdoms and dynasties","Historians divide Egyptian history into the Old, Middle and New Kingdoms, separated by periods of disunity. The pyramids belong to the Old Kingdom. During the New Kingdom, from about 1550 BCE, warrior pharaohs such as Thutmose III and Ramesses II built an empire that reached into Syria and Nubia."],
["Pharaohs and power","The pharaoh was thought to be a living god who kept order, called maat, in the universe. A chief minister, the vizier, ran the government, while scribes kept records of taxes and harvests. Hatshepsut was one of the few women to rule as pharaoh, and she sent a famous trading expedition to the land of Punt."],
["Building the pyramids","The pyramids were royal tombs. They were built not by slaves, as once believed, but by paid workers and farmers serving in rotation, who lived in nearby villages. Blocks were quarried with copper tools and hauled on sledges, probably up ramps. The Great Pyramid contains about 2.3 million blocks."]],
k:[["Location","Nile Valley, northeast Africa"],["Lasted","c. 3100 BCE to 30 BCE"],["Writing","Hieroglyphs, hieratic, demotic"],["Famous rulers","Khufu, Hatshepsut, Ramesses II, Cleopatra VII"],["Great Pyramid","About 146 m tall when built"]],
d:[["c. 3100 BCE","Upper and Lower Egypt are unified"],["c. 2560 BCE","Great Pyramid of Giza completed"],["c. 1550 BCE","New Kingdom begins"],["1323 BCE","Tutankhamun dies young"],["30 BCE","Cleopatra dies; Rome takes Egypt"],["1822","Champollion deciphers hieroglyphs"]]},
rome:{l:"The Roman Empire was one of the largest and longest-lasting states in history. From a small city on the Tiber River, Rome grew to rule about 60 million people around the Mediterranean, spreading its language, laws, roads and buildings across Europe, North Africa and the Middle East.",
s:[["From village to city","According to legend, Rome was founded in 753 BCE by the twins Romulus and Remus. At first it was ruled by kings, then by a republic in which two elected consuls led the state each year. Rome steadily conquered its neighbors in Italy and then defeated Carthage in the Punic Wars, gaining control of the western Mediterranean."],
["The army","Rome’s power rested on its legions, units of about 5,000 heavily armed foot soldiers. Legionaries trained hard, marched up to 30 km a day and built forts, bridges and roads wherever they went. Soldiers from conquered lands served as auxiliaries and could earn Roman citizenship."],
["Life in Rome","At its height the city of Rome had perhaps a million people. Rich families lived in fine houses, while most people crowded into apartment blocks. Public baths, markets and the forum were the centers of daily life, and crowds of 50,000 watched gladiators fight in the Colosseum."],
["Decline and division","In the 200s CE the empire suffered civil wars, invasions and economic trouble. Emperor Diocletian divided it to make it easier to rule, and Constantine founded a new capital, Constantinople, in 330. Constantine also supported Christianity, which became the official religion by the end of the 300s."]],
k:[["Capital","Rome; later also Constantinople"],["Peak size","About 5 million km² (117 CE)"],["Language","Latin in the west, Greek in the east"],["First emperor","Augustus, from 27 BCE"],["Greatest extent","Under Trajan, 117 CE"]],
d:[["753 BCE","Legendary founding of Rome"],["509 BCE","The Republic is founded"],["146 BCE","Rome destroys Carthage"],["27 BCE","Augustus becomes emperor"],["117 CE","Empire reaches its largest size"],["476 CE","Last western emperor is deposed"]]},
indrev:{l:"The Industrial Revolution was the change from making goods by hand at home to producing them with machines in factories. Starting in Britain in the second half of the 1700s, it transformed how people worked and lived, and created the modern industrial world.",
s:[["Why Britain?","Britain had large deposits of coal and iron ore, a growing population, rich merchants with money to invest and colonies that supplied raw cotton and bought finished goods. Its canals, rivers and coastline made transport easy, and a culture of tinkering produced a stream of inventors."],
["Steam power","Early factories used water wheels, so they had to be built by rivers. James Watt’s improved steam engine, patented in 1769, used fuel far more efficiently and could drive machines anywhere. Steam later powered locomotives and ships, shrinking travel times across land and sea."],
["Life in the factory towns","Workers moved from villages to fast-growing towns, where they lived in crowded, often dirty housing. Shifts of 12 to 14 hours were common, and children worked in mills and mines. Laws such as Britain’s Factory Act of 1833 began to limit child labor."],
["New ideas and movements","The new industrial society produced new ways of thinking. Adam Smith described free markets in The Wealth of Nations in 1776, and later writers such as Karl Marx criticized factory owners. Workers formed trade unions to fight for better pay and conditions."]],
k:[["Began","Britain, c. 1760"],["Key power source","Coal and steam"],["Leading industry","Cotton textiles"],["Key inventors","Hargreaves, Arkwright, Watt, Stephenson"],["Second phase","c. 1870 to 1914"]],
d:[["1764","Spinning jenny invented"],["1769","Watt patents his steam engine improvement"],["1779","Iron Bridge built in Shropshire"],["1825","First public steam railway opens"],["1833","British Factory Act limits child labor"],["1851","Great Exhibition in London"]]},
ww2:{l:"World War II was the deadliest conflict in human history. Fought between 1939 and 1945, it pitted the Allies, led by Britain, the Soviet Union, the United States and China, against the Axis powers of Germany, Italy and Japan, and it reshaped the political map of the world.",
s:[["Causes","The harsh peace after World War I, the Great Depression and the rise of dictators set the stage. Adolf Hitler, who came to power in Germany in 1933, rearmed the country and annexed Austria and part of Czechoslovakia. Japan invaded China, and Italy conquered Ethiopia. Britain and France declared war when Germany invaded Poland."],
["War in Europe","Germany used fast tank attacks, called blitzkrieg, to conquer much of Europe by 1940. In 1941 it invaded the Soviet Union, but the Soviets stopped it at Stalingrad in 1943. Allied troops landed in Normandy on D-Day, 6 June 1944, and Berlin fell in May 1945."],
["Occupation and resistance","In occupied countries, many people resisted. The French Resistance sabotaged railways and helped Allied airmen escape, Tito’s partisans fought the Germans in Yugoslavia, and Poland’s Home Army rose up in Warsaw in 1944. In Asia, guerrillas fought the Japanese in China, Malaya and the Philippines. Others helped quietly, hiding Jews and other hunted people."],
["After the war","The war left Europe and Asia in ruins. War crimes trials were held at Nuremberg and Tokyo. The United States and the Soviet Union emerged as superpowers, Germany was divided and many colonies in Asia and Africa moved toward independence."]],
k:[["Dates","1 September 1939 to 2 September 1945"],["Allies","UK, USSR, USA, China and others"],["Axis","Germany, Italy, Japan"],["Deaths","About 70 to 85 million"],["Ended with","Japan’s formal surrender in Tokyo Bay"]],
d:[["1939","Germany invades Poland"],["1940","Fall of France; Battle of Britain"],["1941","Germany invades USSR; Pearl Harbor attacked"],["1942","Battle of Midway; fall of Bataan"],["1944","D-Day landings; Battle of Leyte Gulf"],["1945","Germany and Japan surrender"]]},
renais:{l:"The Renaissance, from a French word meaning rebirth, was a period of extraordinary creativity in European art, learning and science. Beginning in the Italian city-states in the 1300s and lasting until about 1600, it marked the passage from the Middle Ages to the modern world.",
s:[["Humanism","At the heart of the Renaissance was humanism, a movement that studied the writings of ancient Greece and Rome and celebrated human ability. Scholars such as Petrarch hunted for lost classical texts, and Greek scholars fleeing the fall of Constantinople in 1453 brought more ancient works to Italy."],
["New ways of painting","Renaissance artists studied anatomy and used linear perspective, worked out by Filippo Brunelleschi in Florence, to give paintings depth. Oil paint allowed rich color and fine detail. Artists moved from flat religious images toward lifelike people in real-looking spaces."],
["Architecture","Architects revived Roman columns, arches and domes. Brunelleschi’s huge dome for Florence Cathedral, finished in 1436, was an engineering wonder. In Rome, Donato Bramante and later Michelangelo designed the new St. Peter’s Basilica."],
["The Renaissance man","The ideal of the time was a person skilled in many fields. Leonardo da Vinci was painter, engineer, anatomist and inventor all at once. The courtier Baldassare Castiglione described the perfect gentleman as one who was learned, athletic, musical and graceful."]],
k:[["Period","c. 1300 to c. 1600"],["Began in","Florence and other Italian city-states"],["Key idea","Humanism"],["Famous artists","Leonardo, Michelangelo, Raphael, Botticelli"],["Key patrons","The Medici family and the popes"]],
d:[["1436","Florence Cathedral dome completed"],["c. 1450","Gutenberg develops printing"],["c. 1485","Botticelli paints The Birth of Venus"],["1503","Leonardo begins the Mona Lisa"],["1512","Sistine Chapel ceiling finished"],["1543","Copernicus publishes his Sun-centered theory"]]},
maya:{l:"The Maya were a civilization of Mesoamerica who built great stone cities in the rainforests of what are now southern Mexico, Guatemala, Belize and Honduras. They were brilliant astronomers, mathematicians and artists, and they created the most complete writing system of the ancient Americas.",
s:[["City-states","Maya cities were never united into one empire. Each was ruled by a holy lord who claimed descent from the gods, and cities such as Tikal and Calakmul fought long wars for power. Their centers had towering stepped pyramids, palaces, plazas and ball courts."],
["Writing and books","Maya writing used several hundred signs, some standing for whole words and others for syllables. Scribes carved inscriptions on stone monuments called stelae and painted books on bark paper. Spanish priests burned most of these books, and only four are known to survive."],
["Farming and food","Maize was the center of Maya life, and their sacred book, the Popol Vuh, says the gods made people from maize dough. Farmers also grew beans, squash and chili peppers, and raised fields and terraces fed large populations. Cacao was drunk as a frothy chocolate drink by the elite."],
["The ball game","Across Mesoamerica people played a ball game with a heavy rubber ball, which players struck with their hips. It was both sport and religious ritual linked to myths about the underworld. The great ball court at Chichén Itzá is about 168 m long."]],
k:[["Region","Southern Mexico and northern Central America"],["Classic period","c. 250 to 900 CE"],["Great cities","Tikal, Palenque, Copán, Chichén Itzá"],["Number system","Base 20, with a symbol for zero"],["Maya today","Several million speakers of Mayan languages"]],
d:[["c. 2000 BCE","First Maya farming villages"],["c. 250 CE","Classic period begins"],["c. 900 CE","Many southern cities are abandoned"],["c. 1000","Chichén Itzá at its height"],["1697","Last independent Maya kingdom, Nojpetén, falls"],["2012","Long Count completes a great cycle"]]},
silk:{l:"The Silk Road was not one road but a vast web of land and sea routes that linked China, Central Asia, India, the Middle East and Europe for more than 1,500 years. Along it flowed silk, spices, horses and porcelain, as well as religions, inventions, languages and diseases.",
s:[["Opening the routes","In 138 BCE the Han emperor Wu sent an envoy, Zhang Qian, westward to seek allies against nomadic raiders. Zhang Qian returned with news of rich kingdoms and fine horses in Central Asia. Soon Chinese silk was reaching Rome, where it became a costly luxury."],
["Relay trade","Few merchants traveled the whole route. Goods passed from trader to trader, each carrying them a stage farther and adding to the price. Camel caravans crossed deserts such as the Taklamakan, stopping at oasis towns and roadside inns called caravanserais."],
["Sea routes","Ships also carried goods between China, Southeast Asia, India, Arabia and East Africa, using the monsoon winds that change direction with the seasons. Ports such as Guangzhou, Malacca and Calicut thrived. Chinese porcelain from this trade has been found from the coasts of East Africa to the islands of Southeast Asia."],
["Decline","The overland routes flourished under Mongol rule in the 1200s and 1300s, but the Black Death and the breakup of the Mongol Empire disrupted them. When European ships began sailing directly to Asia around Africa in the 1500s, much of the trade shifted to the sea."]],
k:[["Main period","c. 130 BCE to c. 1450 CE"],["Length","Over 6,000 km overland"],["Linked","China with the Mediterranean"],["Key goods","Silk, spices, porcelain, horses, glass"],["Name coined","1877, by Ferdinand von Richthofen"]],
d:[["138 BCE","Zhang Qian sets out westward"],["c. 100 CE","Silk reaches Rome regularly"],["c. 629","Monk Xuanzang travels to India"],["1271","Marco Polo begins his journey east"],["1347","Black Death reaches Europe along trade routes"],["2014","Silk Road routes listed as World Heritage"]]},
space:{l:"The Space Race was a contest between the United States and the Soviet Union to achieve firsts in spaceflight. Running from the late 1950s to the early 1970s, it was driven by Cold War rivalry, and it produced some of the greatest technological achievements in history, ending with humans walking on the Moon.",
s:[["Rockets from war","Both sides built on German wartime rockets. The V-2 missile’s designer, Wernher von Braun, went to work for the United States, while the Soviet program was led by the brilliant but secret chief designer Sergei Korolev. Rockets that could carry nuclear weapons could also launch satellites."],
["Soviet firsts","The Soviets led the early race. After Sputnik and the first human in orbit, Yuri Gagarin, they launched the first woman, Valentina Tereshkova, in 1963 and performed the first spacewalk in 1965. Their Luna probes were the first to reach and photograph the far side of the Moon."],
["The Apollo program","In 1961 President John F. Kennedy set the goal of landing a man on the Moon before the decade was out. The Apollo program employed some 400,000 people. The giant Saturn V rocket, 110 m tall, carried astronauts toward the Moon, and six Apollo missions landed between 1969 and 1972."],
["Spinoffs","The race pushed forward computing, materials and communications. Satellites for weather forecasting, navigation and television grew out of it. Twelve astronauts walked on the Moon and brought back 382 kg of rock and soil that scientists still study."]],
k:[["Rivals","United States and Soviet Union"],["Began","1957, with Sputnik 1"],["First human in space","Yuri Gagarin, 1961"],["First Moon landing","Apollo 11, 20 July 1969"],["People on the Moon","12, all American"]],
d:[["1957","Sputnik 1 and Laika in Sputnik 2"],["1961","Gagarin orbits Earth"],["1963","Tereshkova is first woman in space"],["1965","Leonov makes the first spacewalk"],["1969","Apollo 11 lands on the Moon"],["1975","Apollo-Soyuz docking"]]},
greece:{l:"Ancient Greece was a civilization of city-states around the Aegean Sea that flourished from about 800 BCE. Its thinkers, writers, artists and citizens created ideas about democracy, philosophy, science, drama and sport that remain at the foundation of Western culture.",
s:[["Land and sea","Greece is a land of rugged mountains, narrow valleys and many islands, so communities grew up separately and became independent city-states, each called a polis. The sea linked them, and Greeks founded colonies from southern Italy and France to the coasts of the Black Sea."],
["Athens and Sparta","Athens was a center of trade, art and democracy, where male citizens voted in an assembly. Sparta was a military state where boys trained as soldiers from age seven. The two cities fought together against Persia but then against each other in the Peloponnesian War, which Sparta won in 404 BCE."],
["Wars with Persia","The Persian Empire invaded Greece twice. The Athenians defeated the first invasion at Marathon in 490 BCE. Ten years later a small Spartan-led force held the pass at Thermopylae, and the Greek fleet won a great victory at Salamis."],
["Gods and temples","Greeks worshipped a family of gods led by Zeus, who were believed to live on Mount Olympus. Temples such as the Parthenon in Athens, completed in 432 BCE for the goddess Athena, used columns and proportions that architects still copy. People consulted oracles, especially at Delphi."]],
k:[["Classical period","c. 480 to 323 BCE"],["Major cities","Athens, Sparta, Corinth, Thebes"],["Language","Ancient Greek"],["First Olympics","776 BCE"],["Famous thinkers","Socrates, Plato, Aristotle, Pythagoras"]],
d:[["776 BCE","First recorded Olympic Games"],["508 BCE","Democracy introduced in Athens"],["490 BCE","Battle of Marathon"],["432 BCE","Parthenon completed"],["323 BCE","Alexander the Great dies"],["146 BCE","Rome conquers Greece"]]},
mongol:{l:"The Mongol Empire was the largest land empire the world has ever seen. Built in the 1200s by nomadic horsemen from the grasslands of Mongolia under Genghis Khan and his successors, it stretched from the Pacific Ocean to Eastern Europe and linked East and West as never before.",
s:[["People of the steppe","The Mongols were herders who moved with their horses, sheep and goats across the grasslands, living in round felt tents called gers. Children learned to ride almost as soon as they could walk, and every man was a skilled mounted archer, able to shoot accurately while galloping."],
["Genghis Khan","Born around 1162 with the name Temüjin, he survived a harsh childhood and rose to unite the warring tribes. He was proclaimed Genghis Khan, meaning universal ruler, in 1206. He promoted officers for skill rather than birth and organized his army into units of 10, 100, 1,000 and 10,000."],
["Conquest","Mongol armies moved fast, used feigned retreats to draw enemies into traps and adopted siege engines from the Chinese and Persians. Cities that surrendered were often spared, but those that resisted could be destroyed. Baghdad fell in 1258, ending the Abbasid caliphate."],
["Limits of the empire","The Mongols failed to conquer Japan, where storms wrecked their invasion fleets in 1274 and 1281, and were defeated by the Mamluks of Egypt at Ain Jalut in 1260. Over time the khanates adopted local religions and cultures, and the empire broke apart."]],
k:[["Founder","Genghis Khan (c. 1162 to 1227)"],["Founded","1206"],["Peak area","About 24 million km²"],["Capital","Karakorum; later Khanbaliq (Beijing)"],["Successor states","Golden Horde, Ilkhanate, Chagatai, Yuan"]],
d:[["1206","Temüjin becomes Genghis Khan"],["1227","Genghis Khan dies"],["1241","Mongols defeat armies in Poland and Hungary"],["1258","Fall of Baghdad"],["1271","Kublai Khan founds the Yuan dynasty"],["1368","Yuan dynasty falls in China"]]},
frrev:{l:"The French Revolution was a period of radical upheaval in France between 1789 and 1799. The people overthrew an absolute monarchy, abolished the privileges of the nobles and clergy, and proclaimed ideals of liberty, equality and fraternity that spread across Europe and the world.",
s:[["A kingdom in crisis","By the 1780s France was nearly bankrupt after costly wars, including its support for the American Revolution. Bad harvests made bread expensive, and the poor went hungry. To raise money, King Louis XVI called the Estates-General, an assembly that had not met since 1614."],
["The National Assembly","Representatives of the Third Estate declared themselves a National Assembly and swore in the Tennis Court Oath not to separate until France had a constitution. When Paris crowds stormed the Bastille prison, the king lost control. In August the Assembly abolished feudal privileges."],
["The Terror","As war broke out with Austria and Prussia, radicals called the Jacobins took control. The Committee of Public Safety, led by Maximilien Robespierre, ordered the execution of suspected enemies, including Queen Marie Antoinette. Around 17,000 people were officially executed before Robespierre himself was guillotined in 1794."],
["Lasting changes","The revolution introduced the metric system, ended feudal dues, gave rights to religious minorities and spread the idea that power comes from the people. France briefly abolished slavery in its colonies in 1794, and the slave revolt in Saint-Domingue led to the independence of Haiti in 1804."]],
k:[["Dates","1789 to 1799"],["King","Louis XVI (executed 1793)"],["Motto","Liberty, equality, fraternity"],["Key leaders","Robespierre, Danton, Lafayette"],["Ended with","Napoleon’s coup of 1799"]],
d:[["May 1789","Estates-General meets"],["July 1789","Storming of the Bastille"],["1791","First French constitution"],["1792","France becomes a republic"],["1793–94","The Reign of Terror"],["1799","Napoleon seizes power"]]},
wwi:{l:"World War I, called the Great War at the time, was a global conflict fought from 1914 to 1918. Centered on Europe, it drew in empires from around the world, killed millions in brutal trench warfare and brought down four great empires, setting the stage for the troubles of the twentieth century.",
s:[["Causes","Historians point to four long-term causes: militarism, alliances, imperial rivalry and nationalism. European powers had built huge armies and navies and formed rival alliances. When the Austro-Hungarian heir was murdered by a Bosnian Serb, Austria-Hungary declared war on Serbia, and the alliances pulled the great powers in one after another."],
["The Western Front","Germany’s plan to defeat France quickly was stopped at the Marne in 1914. Both sides then dug lines of trenches from the English Channel to Switzerland. Attacks across no man’s land, such as at Verdun and the Somme in 1916, cost hundreds of thousands of lives for a few kilometers of ground."],
["A world war","Fighting spread to Eastern Europe, Italy, the Middle East and Africa, and at sea. Troops came from India, Australia, New Zealand, Canada and Africa. German submarines attacked shipping, and their sinking of American ships helped bring the United States into the war."],
["End of empires","Revolution took Russia out of the war in 1917, and the Russian Empire was replaced by Soviet rule. By 1918 the German, Austro-Hungarian and Ottoman empires had also collapsed. The League of Nations was created in 1920 to prevent future wars."]],
k:[["Dates","28 July 1914 to 11 November 1918"],["Allies","France, Britain, Russia, Italy, USA and others"],["Central Powers","Germany, Austria-Hungary, Ottoman Empire, Bulgaria"],["Deaths","About 20 million"],["Peace treaty","Treaty of Versailles, 1919"]],
d:[["June 1914","Franz Ferdinand assassinated"],["Sept 1914","First Battle of the Marne"],["1915","Gallipoli campaign"],["1916","Battles of Verdun and the Somme"],["1917","USA enters; Russian Revolution"],["1918","Armistice ends the fighting"]]},
phrev:{l:"The Philippine Revolution was the armed struggle of Filipinos against more than three centuries of Spanish colonial rule. Beginning in 1896, it produced the first constitutional republic in Asia and created a sense of Filipino nationhood that endured through the war with the United States that followed.",
s:[["The Propaganda Movement","Before the revolution, educated Filipinos known as ilustrados, including José Rizal, Marcelo H. del Pilar and Graciano López Jaena, campaigned peacefully in Spain for reforms. Through the newspaper La Solidaridad they asked for representation in the Spanish parliament, equal rights and an end to abuses by friars. Spain ignored their demands."],
["The Katipunan","The Katipunan was a secret society whose members swore oaths and signed with their own blood. It grew quickly among workers and farmers in Manila and nearby provinces. When the Spanish discovered it in August 1896, Bonifacio and his followers tore up their cedulas, or tax certificates, and began the revolt."],
["Fighting in Cavite","Revolutionaries won early victories in Cavite under Emilio Aguinaldo. Rivalry between factions led to the Tejeros Convention of 1897, where Aguinaldo was elected president, and to Bonifacio’s execution. Later that year the Pact of Biak-na-Bato sent Aguinaldo into exile in Hong Kong."],
["The Republic","Aguinaldo returned in May 1898 during the Spanish–American War. After the declaration of independence, a congress met at Barasoain Church in Malolos and wrote a constitution. The First Philippine Republic was inaugurated in January 1899, but the United States refused to recognize it."]],
k:[["Dates","1896 to 1898"],["Against","Spanish colonial rule"],["Secret society","Katipunan, founded 7 July 1892"],["Leaders","Bonifacio, Aguinaldo, Mabini"],["Result","Declaration of independence, 12 June 1898"]],
d:[["1872","Execution of the priests Gomburza"],["1892","Katipunan founded"],["1896","Revolt begins; Rizal executed"],["1897","Tejeros Convention; Biak-na-Bato"],["1898","Independence declared at Kawit"],["1899","Malolos Republic inaugurated"]]},
coldwar:{l:"The Cold War was a long struggle for power and influence between the United States and its allies and the Soviet Union and its allies. It lasted from the end of World War II until 1991. The two superpowers never fought each other directly, but their rivalry shaped politics, wars and science around the globe.",
s:[["Two systems","The United States favored democracy and capitalism, while the Soviet Union had a one-party communist government that controlled the economy. In 1946 Winston Churchill said an iron curtain had descended across Europe, dividing the free West from the Soviet-controlled East."],
["Alliances","Western nations formed NATO, the North Atlantic Treaty Organization, in 1949. The Soviet Union and its Eastern European allies answered with the Warsaw Pact in 1955. In Asia, the United States signed defense treaties with Japan, South Korea, Australia, New Zealand and others, and set up the Southeast Asia Treaty Organization in 1954."],
["The Berlin Wall","Germany was split into a Western-backed West Germany and a Soviet-backed East Germany. Berlin, inside East Germany, was also divided. To stop people escaping to the West, East Germany built a wall through the city in 1961. It became the great symbol of the divided world."],
["Thaw and collapse","After the scare of the Cuban Missile Crisis, the superpowers signed arms-control treaties. In the 1980s Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev introduced reforms called glasnost (openness) and perestroika (restructuring). In 1989 peaceful revolutions swept Eastern Europe, and the Soviet Union broke into 15 countries."]],
k:[["Period","c. 1947 to 1991"],["Superpowers","USA and USSR"],["Western alliance","NATO (1949)"],["Eastern alliance","Warsaw Pact (1955)"],["Closest to nuclear war","Cuban Missile Crisis, October 1962"]],
d:[["1947","Truman Doctrine announced"],["1948–49","Berlin Blockade and Airlift"],["1950–53","Korean War"],["1961","Berlin Wall built"],["1962","Cuban Missile Crisis"],["1991","Soviet Union dissolves"]]},
aztec:{l:"The Aztecs, who called themselves the Mexica, built a powerful empire in central Mexico in the 1400s and early 1500s. From their island capital, Tenochtitlan, they ruled millions of people, building great temples, busy markets and remarkable floating farms before their empire fell to Spanish conquerors.",
s:[["The eagle on the cactus","According to legend, the Mexica wandered for many years until they saw an eagle perched on a cactus, a sign from their god Huitzilopochtli. There, on a swampy island, they founded their city. The image appears on the flag of Mexico today."],
["A city on a lake","Tenochtitlan was linked to the shore by long causeways and supplied with fresh water by an aqueduct. Canals ran through it like streets. Its population may have reached 200,000 or more. The Spanish soldiers who first saw it compared it to a city from a fairy tale."],
["Markets and tribute","The great market at Tlatelolco drew tens of thousands of people a day, trading food, cloth, feathers, pottery and gold. Conquered peoples paid tribute to the empire in goods such as maize, cotton cloaks, cacao and jaguar skins, recorded in picture books called codices."],
["The conquest","Hernán Cortés landed in 1519 with a few hundred Spanish soldiers. He gained thousands of allies among peoples who resented Aztec rule, especially the Tlaxcalans. A smallpox epidemic, brought by the Europeans, killed many of the city’s defenders, and Tenochtitlan fell after a long siege."]],
k:[["Capital","Tenochtitlan (now Mexico City)"],["Founded","c. 1325"],["Language","Nahuatl"],["Last emperors","Moctezuma II, Cuitláhuac, Cuauhtémoc"],["Fell","13 August 1521"]],
d:[["c. 1325","Tenochtitlan founded"],["1428","Triple Alliance formed"],["1487","Great Temple rededicated"],["1502","Moctezuma II becomes ruler"],["1519","Cortés arrives in Mexico"],["1521","Tenochtitlan falls"]]},
ottoman:{l:"The Ottoman Empire was one of the most powerful and long-lasting states in history. Founded in Anatolia around 1299, it ruled for more than 600 years over a vast territory spanning three continents, bringing together many peoples, languages and faiths under the rule of the sultans.",
s:[["From frontier state to empire","The Ottomans began as one of many small Turkish states on the border of the Byzantine Empire. They expanded into the Balkans in the 1300s. Mehmed II’s capture of Constantinople, using enormous cannons to batter its walls, made the city, later called Istanbul, the Ottoman capital."],
["The sultan’s court","The sultan ruled from the Topkapı Palace in Istanbul, assisted by a grand vizier and a council called the divan. The palace was a city in itself, with thousands of servants, guards and officials. Ottoman law combined Islamic law with the sultan’s own decrees, called kanun."],
["Art and architecture","The great architect Mimar Sinan designed more than 300 buildings, including the Süleymaniye Mosque in Istanbul and the Selimiye Mosque in Edirne. Ottoman artists excelled in calligraphy, tile work, miniature painting and carpets. Tulips, later prized in Europe, were a favorite Ottoman motif."],
["Decline and reform","After a failed siege of Vienna in 1683, the empire slowly lost territory. In the 1800s reformers known as the Tanzimat modernized the army, laws and schools. Mustafa Kemal Atatürk led the national movement that founded the Republic of Turkey after World War I."]],
k:[["Founded","c. 1299 by Osman I"],["Capital","Bursa, Edirne, then Istanbul"],["Peak","16th–17th centuries"],["Famous sultans","Mehmed II, Suleiman the Magnificent"],["Ended","1922 (sultanate abolished)"]],
d:[["c. 1299","Osman I founds the state"],["1453","Constantinople captured"],["1520–66","Reign of Suleiman"],["1529","First siege of Vienna"],["1683","Second siege of Vienna fails"],["1923","Republic of Turkey founded"]]},
china:{l:"Imperial China was ruled by a long succession of dynasties for more than 2,000 years, from the first emperor in 221 BCE until 1912. It was the world’s largest and most advanced civilization for much of that time, and its inventions, philosophy and art influenced all of East Asia.",
s:[["The first emperor","Qin Shi Huang conquered the rival Warring States and made China one country. He standardized writing, coins, weights and the width of cart axles, and began linking earlier walls into a great wall. His tomb near Xi’an is guarded by an army of more than 7,000 life-size terracotta soldiers."],
["The Mandate of Heaven","Chinese rulers were called Sons of Heaven. They believed heaven granted the right to rule to a just emperor, and that floods, famine and rebellion showed a dynasty had lost that mandate. This idea explained why dynasties rose and fell."],
["Scholars and officials","Confucian ideas about duty, respect and good government guided the state. Officials were chosen through difficult exams on the Confucian classics, which in principle anyone could take. Successful candidates formed a class of scholar-officials who ran the vast empire."],
["The last dynasty","The Qing dynasty, founded by the Manchus in 1644, ruled China at its largest. In the 1800s it was weakened by rebellions and by defeat in the Opium Wars with Britain. Revolutionaries led by Sun Yat-sen overthrew it, and the six-year-old emperor Puyi abdicated in 1912."]],
k:[["Began","221 BCE, Qin dynasty"],["Ended","1912, Qing dynasty"],["Major dynasties","Han, Tang, Song, Yuan, Ming, Qing"],["Famous inventions","Paper, printing, gunpowder, compass"],["Imperial palace","Forbidden City, Beijing"]],
d:[["221 BCE","Qin unifies China"],["206 BCE","Han dynasty begins"],["618","Tang dynasty begins"],["1271","Yuan (Mongol) dynasty"],["1420","Forbidden City completed"],["1912","Last emperor abdicates"]]},
explor:{l:"The Age of Exploration was a period, from the 1400s to the 1600s, when European sailors made long ocean voyages to Africa, Asia and the Americas. Seeking spices, gold and new trade routes, they connected the world’s continents for the first time, with consequences both wondrous and devastating.",
s:[["Why explore?","Spices such as pepper, cloves and nutmeg were enormously valuable in Europe but came through Venetian and Middle Eastern merchants at high prices. Rulers wanted direct sea routes to Asia. Explorers also sought gold, glory and the spread of Christianity."],
["Ships and tools","Small, nimble ships called caravels could sail against the wind using triangular lateen sails. Sailors navigated with the magnetic compass, the astrolabe and the quadrant, which measured the height of the Sun or stars to find latitude. Longitude could not be measured accurately at sea until the 1700s."],
["Elcano completes the circle","After Magellan’s death, Juan Sebastián Elcano took command of the last ship, the Victoria. It crossed the Indian Ocean, rounded Africa and reached Spain in September 1522 with just 18 of the roughly 270 men who had set out. The sailor Antonio Pigafetta’s journal recorded the voyage, including a strange discovery: by sailing west around the world, they had lost a day."],
["Empires overseas","Spain built an empire in the Americas and, from 1565, in the Philippines. Portugal set up trading posts from Africa to Brazil, India and Macau. In 1494 the Treaty of Tordesillas divided newly found lands between Spain and Portugal. The Dutch, English and French soon followed."]],
k:[["Period","15th to 17th centuries"],["Leading nations","Portugal and Spain"],["Key explorers","Dias, da Gama, Columbus, Magellan"],["Ship type","Caravel, carrack"],["Main prize","The spice trade"]],
d:[["1488","Dias rounds the Cape of Good Hope"],["1492","Columbus reaches the Caribbean"],["1498","Da Gama reaches India"],["1521","Magellan reaches the Philippines"],["1522","First circumnavigation completed"],["1565","Spanish galleons begin regular voyages across the Pacific between Asia and Mexico"]]},
usrev:{l:"The American Revolution was the struggle by thirteen British colonies in North America to break free from Britain. Between 1765 and 1783 it grew from protests over taxes into a war for independence, creating the United States of America and spreading ideas of liberty and self-government across the world.",
s:[["No taxation without representation","After the costly Seven Years’ War, Britain taxed the colonies to help pay its debts. Colonists, who had no members in Parliament, protested. In 1773 protesters dumped British tea into Boston Harbor in the Boston Tea Party, and Britain answered with harsh laws that closed the port."],
["The Declaration","Thomas Jefferson drafted the Declaration of Independence, which stated that all men are created equal, with rights to life, liberty and the pursuit of happiness. Its ideals were not applied to enslaved people or women at the time, but they inspired later struggles for equal rights."],
["The war","The Continental Army was often short of food, pay and supplies, and it suffered through the winter at Valley Forge. The American victory at Saratoga in 1777 persuaded France to join the war. A combined American and French force trapped the British at Yorktown in 1781, effectively ending the fighting."],
["A new nation","The first government, under the Articles of Confederation, was weak. Delegates met in Philadelphia in 1787 to write the Constitution, which divided power among Congress, the president and the courts. George Washington became the first president in 1789."]],
k:[["Dates","1765 to 1783"],["Colonies","Thirteen, along the Atlantic coast"],["Commander","George Washington"],["Ally","France, from 1778"],["Result","Independence of the United States"]],
d:[["1765","Stamp Act passed"],["1773","Boston Tea Party"],["1775","Fighting at Lexington and Concord"],["1776","Declaration of Independence"],["1781","British surrender at Yorktown"],["1783","Treaty of Paris"]]},
viking:{l:"The Vikings were seafaring people from Scandinavia, in what are now Denmark, Norway and Sweden, who raided, traded, explored and settled across Europe and beyond between about 800 and 1066. They are remembered as fierce warriors, but they were also farmers, craftspeople, poets and skilled navigators.",
s:[["Who were the Vikings?","Most Norse people were farmers who raised cattle, sheep and crops on small homesteads. The word viking originally meant going on an overseas expedition. Society was divided into nobles called jarls, free farmers called karls and enslaved people called thralls."],
["Longships","Viking longships were long, narrow and light, with a shallow keel. They could be sailed across the open sea or rowed up rivers and pulled onto beaches. Ships found buried in burial mounds, such as the Oseberg and Gokstad ships in Norway, show their beautiful carving and clever design."],
["Gods and sagas","The Norse worshipped gods such as Odin, the wise chief god, Thor, the thunder god, and Freyja, goddess of love. Warriors hoped to reach Valhalla after death. Their stories were later written down in Iceland as sagas and in the poems of the Edda. Our days Tuesday to Friday are named after Norse gods."],
["Runes and writing","Vikings wrote with runes, an alphabet of straight-lined letters suited to carving in wood and stone. Thousands of runestones still stand in Scandinavia, many raised in memory of family members who died on journeys abroad."]],
k:[["Homeland","Denmark, Norway, Sweden"],["Viking Age","c. 793 to 1066"],["Reached","North America, Russia, Constantinople"],["Writing","Runes"],["Famous figures","Erik the Red, Leif Erikson, Cnut"]],
d:[["793","Raid on Lindisfarne"],["c. 874","Settlement of Iceland begins"],["930","Althing assembly founded"],["c. 985","Erik the Red settles Greenland"],["c. 1000","Leif Erikson reaches North America"],["1066","Battle of Stamford Bridge"]]},
reform:{l:"The Reformation was a religious movement in 16th-century Europe that split the Western Christian Church. Reformers challenged the teachings and power of the Pope, creating Protestant churches. The upheaval transformed religion, politics and culture, and its effects are still felt today.",
s:[["A church under criticism","By 1500 many Christians were troubled by corruption in the Church, such as the buying of church offices and the sale of indulgences, certificates said to reduce punishment for sins. Earlier critics, such as John Wycliffe in England and Jan Hus in Bohemia, had called for change."],
["Luther’s stand","Martin Luther, a German monk and professor, taught that people are saved by faith alone, not by good works or payments, and that the Bible is the highest authority. Summoned before Emperor Charles V at the Diet of Worms in 1521, he refused to take back his writings and was declared an outlaw."],
["The Bible for everyone","Luther translated the Bible into German so ordinary people could read it, and William Tyndale translated it into English. The Reformation encouraged literacy, since believers were expected to read scripture for themselves. Congregations also began singing hymns in their own languages."],
["The Catholic response","At the Council of Trent, which met between 1545 and 1563, the Catholic Church reformed abuses and restated its teachings. New orders such as the Jesuits, founded by Ignatius of Loyola, opened schools and sent missionaries worldwide, to India, Japan, China and the Americas."]],
k:[["Began","1517, in Wittenberg, Germany"],["Key reformers","Luther, Calvin, Zwingli"],["Result","Protestant churches"],["Catholic response","Council of Trent"],["Wars ended","Peace of Westphalia, 1648"]],
d:[["1517","Luther’s Ninety-five Theses"],["1521","Diet of Worms"],["1534","Act of Supremacy in England"],["1536","Calvin publishes his Institutes"],["1545","Council of Trent opens"],["1648","Peace of Westphalia"]]},
mesop:{l:"Mesopotamia, meaning the land between the rivers, is often called the cradle of civilization. In the plains of the Tigris and Euphrates, in present-day Iraq, people built the first cities, invented writing and the wheel, and created the first known laws, epics and empires.",
s:[["Farming the plains","The land was hot and dry, but the rivers brought water and fertile silt. Farmers dug canals to irrigate fields of barley and wheat and grew date palms. Surplus food let people specialize as priests, potters, metalworkers and merchants, and villages grew into cities."],
["Temples and ziggurats","Each Sumerian city was believed to belong to a god. At its center stood a temple raised on a huge stepped platform of mud bricks called a ziggurat. The Great Ziggurat of Ur, built around 2100 BCE, has been partly restored and can still be visited."],
["Empires","Sargon of Akkad united the cities around 2334 BCE in what is often called the first empire. Later came the Babylonians under Hammurabi and the warlike Assyrians, whose capital Nineveh held a great library. Nebuchadnezzar II rebuilt Babylon with the blue-glazed Ishtar Gate."],
["Inventions","Mesopotamians are credited with some of the earliest wheels, used first for pottery and then for carts, along with the plow and sailboats. They also made bronze by mixing copper and tin, and they divided the day into hours and the circle into degrees."]],
k:[["Location","Between the Tigris and Euphrates, modern Iraq"],["First cities","c. 3500 BCE"],["Peoples","Sumerians, Akkadians, Babylonians, Assyrians"],["Writing","Cuneiform"],["Famous text","Epic of Gilgamesh"]],
d:[["c. 3500 BCE","Cities such as Uruk grow"],["c. 3300 BCE","Cuneiform writing appears"],["c. 2334 BCE","Sargon founds the Akkadian Empire"],["c. 1754 BCE","Code of Hammurabi"],["c. 575 BCE","Ishtar Gate built in Babylon"],["539 BCE","Persians conquer Babylon"]]}
});

