// Longer, DK-style material for Technology articles: a lead, extra sections, key facts and key dates.
window.DEEP=window.DEEP||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP,{
internet:{l:"The internet is a worldwide system of connected computer networks that lets billions of people and devices exchange information almost instantly. It carries the World Wide Web, email, video calls, streaming and online shopping, and it has transformed how people work, learn, play and communicate.",
s:[["From ARPANET to the internet","ARPANET was funded by the US Defense Department to link university computers. In 1974 Vint Cerf and Bob Kahn designed TCP/IP, a common language that let different networks connect. On 1 January 1983 ARPANET switched to TCP/IP, which is often counted as the birth of the modern internet."],
["The physical internet","The internet is not a cloud but a web of real cables, routers and buildings full of servers called data centers. More than 500 undersea fiber-optic cables carry about 99% of data between continents."],
["Browsers and search","Early web pages were simple text and links. Graphical browsers such as Mosaic in 1993 made the web easy to use, and search engines helped people find pages among billions. Google, founded in 1998, became the most popular."],
["Staying safe online","The internet also brings risks, including scams, viruses, stolen passwords and false information. Good habits include using strong, different passwords, turning on two-step sign-in, checking sources before sharing and thinking carefully about what personal information to post."]],
k:[["Began","1969 (ARPANET); 1983 (TCP/IP)"],["Key protocol","TCP/IP"],["Web inventor","Tim Berners-Lee"],["Users","About 5.5 billion (mid-2020s)"],["Long-distance carriers","Undersea fiber-optic cables"]],
d:[["1969","First ARPANET message"],["1971","First email between computers"],["1983","ARPANET adopts TCP/IP"],["1991","First website goes live"],["1993","The World Wide Web is released free for everyone to use"],["2007","Smartphones bring the web to pockets"]]},
elec:{l:"Electricity is a form of energy produced by the movement of tiny charged particles called electrons. It lights our homes, runs our machines and powers our computers, and it occurs naturally too, in lightning and in the nerve signals of our own bodies.",
s:[["Charges and current","Atoms contain positive protons and negative electrons. Opposite charges attract and like charges repel. When electrons are pushed along a wire, they form an electric current, measured in amperes. Voltage, measured in volts, is the push that drives them, like water pressure in a pipe."],
["Conductors and insulators","Metals such as copper and aluminum let electrons flow easily, so they are used for wires. Rubber, plastic and glass resist the flow and are used as insulators to keep electricity where it belongs. Water with dissolved salts conducts electricity, which is why electrical devices and water are a dangerous mix."],
["Discovering electricity","The ancient Greeks noticed that rubbed amber attracts feathers, and the word electricity comes from elektron, Greek for amber. In 1752 Benjamin Franklin is said to have shown that lightning is electrical. Michael Faraday’s discovery of induction in 1831 made generators possible."],
["Safety","Electricity can cause burns, fires and deadly shocks. Fuses and circuit breakers cut the power when too much current flows, and grounding wires carry stray current safely away. Never touch fallen power lines, and keep plugs and sockets dry."]],
k:[["Unit of current","Ampere (A)"],["Unit of voltage","Volt (V)"],["Unit of power","Watt (W)"],["Key law","Ohm’s law, V = I × R"],["Mains supply","Mostly 220–240 V at 50 Hz; 120 V at 60 Hz in North America"]],
d:[["1752","Franklin’s kite experiment (traditional date)"],["1800","Volta builds the first battery"],["1831","Faraday discovers electromagnetic induction"],["1879","Edison’s long-lasting light bulb"],["1882","First central power stations"],["1891","Frankfurt’s electrical exhibition shows long-distance AC power transmission"]]},
comp:{l:"A computer is a machine that follows instructions, called programs, to store and process information. From room-sized machines in the 1940s to smartphones today, computers have become one of the most important inventions in history, running everything from banks and hospitals to games and space probes.",
s:[["Hardware","The physical parts of a computer are its hardware. The central processing unit, or CPU, carries out instructions. Memory, called RAM, holds data the computer is working on, while drives store files long-term. Input devices such as keyboards and touchscreens, and output devices such as screens and speakers, connect it to people."],
["Software","Programs are the computer’s software. The operating system, such as Windows, macOS, Android or Linux, manages the hardware and runs other programs, called apps. Programmers write software in languages such as Python, Java and C, which are translated into the binary code the processor understands."],
["Pioneers","Ada Lovelace wrote what is often called the first computer program for Babbage’s engine in 1843. Alan Turing described a universal computing machine in 1936 and helped break German codes in World War II. Grace Hopper developed early programming languages that let people write instructions in words."],
["Supercomputers and the cloud","The most powerful supercomputers can perform more than a billion billion calculations per second and are used for weather forecasting and scientific research. Today much computing happens in the cloud, on huge data centers reached over the internet."]],
k:[["Early electronic computer","ENIAC, 1945"],["Basic language","Binary (0 and 1)"],["Brain of the computer","CPU, the processor"],["First programmer","Ada Lovelace (1843)"],["Key theorist","Alan Turing"]],
d:[["1837","Babbage designs the Analytical Engine"],["1936","Turing describes a universal machine"],["1945","ENIAC is completed"],["1971","First microprocessor, Intel 4004"],["1981","IBM PC launched"],["2007","The iPhone popularizes smartphones"]]},
print:{l:"The printing press is a machine that presses inked type onto paper to make many identical copies of a text. Johannes Gutenberg’s press with movable metal type, developed in the 1400s, made books cheap and plentiful, and it is often counted among the most important inventions in history.",
s:[["Before printing","Before printing, books in Europe were copied by hand, often by monks in monasteries. A single Bible could take a scribe more than a year. Books were rare and expensive, and few people could read."],
["How Gutenberg’s press worked","Gutenberg cast individual letters from a metal alloy of lead, tin and antimony using a hand mold. Typesetters arranged the letters into lines and pages, inked them with an oil-based ink and pressed paper onto them with a screw press adapted from wine presses. The type could then be taken apart and reused."],
["Printing reaches the world","European missionaries and colonists carried the printing press around the world. The first press in the Americas was set up in Mexico City in 1539, and presses followed in Goa, India, in 1556 and in Manila in the 1590s. In the Ottoman Empire, Ibrahim Müteferrika opened the first Arabic-script press run by a Muslim in Istanbul in 1727."],
["Modern printing","Steam-powered presses in the 1800s printed thousands of pages an hour, making cheap newspapers possible. Offset printing, developed in the early 1900s, is still used for books and magazines, while digital and 3D printing now let people print at home."]],
k:[["Inventor","Johannes Gutenberg"],["Place","Mainz, Germany"],["Date","c. 1450"],["Famous book","Gutenberg Bible, c. 1455"],["Copies of Gutenberg Bible surviving","About 49"]],
d:[["868","Diamond Sutra printed in China"],["c. 1040","Bi Sheng invents movable type"],["c. 1455","Gutenberg Bible completed"],["1476","Caxton sets up the first press in England"],["1539","The first printing press in the Americas is set up in Mexico City"],["1814","Steam press prints The Times of London"]]},
flight:{l:"Flight is the ability to travel through the air. For thousands of years people dreamed of flying like birds. Balloons first lifted people in the 1700s, and the airplane, invented in 1903, went on to shrink the world, carrying billions of passengers each year to every corner of the globe.",
s:[["Four forces","Four forces act on an airplane. Lift, produced by the wings, pushes it up, while weight pulls it down. Thrust from the engines drives it forward, while drag, the resistance of the air, holds it back. To climb, lift must beat weight, and to speed up, thrust must beat drag."],
["Controlling a plane","Pilots steer using movable surfaces. Ailerons on the wings roll the plane left or right, the elevator on the tail tips the nose up or down, and the rudder turns the nose from side to side. The Wright brothers’ great breakthrough was finding a way to control a plane in all three directions."],
["Aviation milestones","Louis Blériot flew across the English Channel in 1909. In 1927 Charles Lindbergh made the first solo nonstop flight across the Atlantic, and Amelia Earhart repeated the feat in 1932. Chuck Yeager broke the sound barrier in 1947 in the Bell X-1 rocket plane."],
["Airlines","The first airlines carried mail and a few passengers after World War I. KLM of the Netherlands, founded in 1919, is the oldest airline still flying under its original name, and Avianca of Colombia and Qantas of Australia followed soon after. Today more than 4 billion passenger journeys are made by air each year, and the busiest airports, such as Atlanta, Dubai and Tokyo, handle tens of millions of travelers."]],
k:[["First powered flight","17 December 1903"],["Inventors","Orville and Wilbur Wright"],["First balloon flight","1783, Paris"],["Fastest airliner","Concorde, about Mach 2"],["Largest airliner","Airbus A380"]],
d:[["1783","Montgolfier balloon carries people"],["1903","Wright brothers’ first flight"],["1909","Blériot crosses the English Channel"],["1927","Lindbergh flies solo across the Atlantic"],["1947","Sound barrier broken"],["1969","Boeing 747 first flies"]]},
renew:{l:"Renewable energy comes from natural sources that are constantly replenished, such as sunlight, wind, flowing water and the Earth’s internal heat. Unlike coal, oil and gas, these sources will not run out and produce little or no greenhouse gas, making them central to efforts to slow climate change.",
s:[["Solar power","Solar panels are made of cells, usually silicon, that release electrons when sunlight strikes them. Panels can be fitted to roofs or spread across large solar farms. Solar thermal systems instead use the Sun’s heat to warm water or, with mirrors, to boil water and drive turbines."],
["Bioenergy and tides","Bioenergy comes from burning or fermenting plant material, such as wood, crop waste or sugar cane. Brazil runs many cars on ethanol from sugar cane. Tidal and wave power, still small, harness the movement of the sea."],
["Leaders in clean energy","China installs more solar panels and wind turbines than any other country. Iceland and Norway get almost all their electricity from renewables, mainly geothermal and hydropower, and Denmark gets about half from wind. Kenya, Indonesia, the Philippines and New Zealand are major geothermal producers, and Brazil and Paraguay rely heavily on hydropower."],
["Storing energy","Because the Sun sets and the wind drops, renewable energy must be stored or balanced. Large batteries store power for hours, and pumped hydro plants use spare electricity to pump water uphill, releasing it through turbines later. Green hydrogen may store energy for longer periods."]],
k:[["Main sources","Solar, wind, hydro, geothermal, bioenergy"],["Largest renewable","Hydropower"],["Fastest-growing","Solar power"],["Largest hydro dam","Three Gorges, China"],["Leading installer","China"]],
d:[["1882","First hydroelectric plant, Wisconsin"],["1904","First geothermal power, Larderello, Italy"],["1954","Bell Labs makes a practical solar cell"],["1913","The world’s first commercial geothermal power plant opens at Larderello, Italy"],["2008","Solar power begins its rapid fall in cost"],["2015","Paris Agreement on climate change"]]},
gps:{l:"The Global Positioning System, or GPS, is a network of satellites that lets a receiver anywhere on Earth work out exactly where it is and what time it is. Built for the US military, it is now free for everyone and is used by billions of phones, cars, ships, planes and farm machines.",
s:[["The satellites","GPS uses at least 24 working satellites, arranged so that at least four are always above the horizon from any point on Earth. They circle about 20,200 km up, each orbiting twice a day. Ground stations around the world monitor them and correct their clocks and orbits."],
["Finding your place","Each satellite broadcasts its position and the exact time. A receiver measures how long the signals took to arrive and works out its distance from each satellite. Using several distances, a method called trilateration, it pinpoints its location in three dimensions and corrects its own clock."],
["Relativity in your pocket","Because the satellites move fast, their clocks run slightly slow, as Einstein’s special relativity predicts. Because gravity is weaker up there, they also run fast, as general relativity predicts. Together the effects add up to about 38 microseconds a day, which would cause errors of about 10 km a day if not corrected."],
["Everyday uses","GPS guides drivers and delivery riders, helps ships and planes navigate, and allows farmers to steer tractors precisely. Scientists use it to measure how much the ground moves before earthquakes and volcanic eruptions."]],
k:[["Operator","US Space Force"],["Satellites","At least 24 (about 31 in use)"],["Orbit height","About 20,200 km"],["Signals needed","At least 4"],["Similar systems","GLONASS, Galileo, BeiDou"]],
d:[["1973","GPS project approved"],["1978","First satellite launched"],["1983","Promise of civilian access"],["1995","Full operational capability"],["2000","Civilian accuracy greatly improved"],["2020","BeiDou global system completed"]]},
ai:{l:"Artificial intelligence, or AI, is the science of making machines that can do things that normally need human intelligence, such as recognizing speech and images, translating languages, playing games and answering questions. Once a dream of science fiction, it now runs in phones, cars and online services every day.",
s:[["Early ideas","In 1950 Alan Turing asked whether machines could think and proposed a test, now called the Turing test, in which a machine tries to pass as human in a typed conversation. Early AI programs in the 1950s and 1960s solved logic puzzles and played checkers."],
["AI winters","Early researchers were very optimistic, but computers were too slow and data too scarce. Funding dried up twice, in periods called AI winters, in the 1970s and late 1980s. Progress resumed when computers grew faster and the internet supplied huge amounts of data."],
["How neural networks learn","Neural networks are loosely inspired by the brain. They are made of layers of simple units connected by adjustable numbers called weights. During training the network makes guesses, measures its errors and adjusts the weights slightly, repeating this millions of times until it performs well."],
["Using AI wisely","AI can make mistakes, reflect unfair patterns in its training data and be misused to create fake images or misleading text. Many countries are writing rules for AI. Experts advise checking important answers from AI against reliable sources."]],
k:[["Term coined","1956, Dartmouth workshop"],["Key approach today","Machine learning"],["Famous test","Turing test (1950)"],["Chess milestone","Deep Blue beats Kasparov, 1997"],["Go milestone","AlphaGo beats Lee Sedol, 2016"]],
d:[["1950","Turing proposes his test"],["1956","Dartmouth workshop names the field"],["1997","Deep Blue wins at chess"],["2012","Deep learning breakthrough in image recognition"],["2016","AlphaGo defeats a Go champion"],["2022","Chatbots based on language models go mainstream"]]},
battery:{l:"A battery is a portable store of electrical energy. Inside, chemical reactions push electrons through a circuit, powering everything from watches and phones to cars and entire power grids. As the world shifts to clean energy, better batteries have become one of technology’s most important goals.",
s:[["How a battery works","Every battery cell has three main parts: a negative electrode called the anode, a positive electrode called the cathode and an electrolyte between them. When the battery is connected, a chemical reaction releases electrons at the anode, which flow through the device to the cathode, while charged atoms called ions move through the electrolyte."],
["Rechargeable batteries","In a rechargeable battery the chemical reaction can be reversed by pushing electricity back in. Gaston Planté invented the rechargeable lead-acid battery in 1859. Nickel-cadmium and nickel-metal hydride batteries followed, before lithium-ion took over."],
["Battery minerals","Lithium-ion batteries need materials such as lithium, cobalt, nickel, manganese and graphite. Much of the world’s lithium comes from Australia and Chile, cobalt from the Democratic Republic of the Congo and nickel from Indonesia. Mining these minerals raises environmental and human rights concerns, which is why recycling old batteries is becoming important."],
["Future batteries","Researchers are working on solid-state batteries, which replace the liquid electrolyte with a solid and could be safer and hold more energy, and on sodium-ion batteries, which use abundant sodium instead of lithium."]],
k:[["First battery","Voltaic pile, 1800"],["Inventor","Alessandro Volta"],["Rechargeable pioneer","Gaston Planté, 1859"],["Most common today","Lithium-ion"],["Unit of charge stored","Ampere-hour (Ah)"]],
d:[["1800","Volta builds the voltaic pile"],["1859","Lead-acid battery invented"],["1886","Dry cell invented by Carl Gassner"],["1991","Sony sells the first lithium-ion battery"],["2017","Giant grid battery opens in South Australia"],["2019","Nobel Prize for lithium-ion inventors"]]},
nuclear:{l:"Nuclear power is electricity produced from the energy stored in the nuclei of atoms. A small amount of uranium fuel can generate enormous amounts of heat without burning anything, which makes nuclear power a large low-carbon energy source, though one that raises concerns about safety, waste and cost.",
s:[["Inside a reactor","In a reactor, uranium fuel is formed into pellets and packed into long metal rods. When a uranium nucleus splits, it releases heat and neutrons that split more nuclei in a chain reaction. Control rods absorb neutrons to keep the reaction steady, and water carries away the heat to make steam."],
["Radiation and waste","Used fuel stays highly radioactive for thousands of years. It is first cooled in deep pools, then stored in strong steel and concrete casks. Finland is building Onkalo, the world’s first permanent deep underground repository, in ancient bedrock."],
["Plants that never ran","Some nuclear plants were built but never used. Austria’s Zwentendorf plant was finished in 1978, but a referendum that year rejected nuclear power, and it never produced electricity. The Bataan plant in the Philippines, completed in 1984, was never switched on after concerns about safety, nearby faults and a volcano, and corruption. Such plants show how politics can shape energy."],
["Small reactors","Engineers are designing small modular reactors that could be built in factories and shipped to sites. Supporters say they could be cheaper and safer, while critics question their cost and how to manage their waste."]],
k:[["Energy source","Nuclear fission"],["Main fuel","Uranium-235"],["Share of world electricity","About 9%"],["Largest producer","United States"],["Most reliant country","France, about 65–70%"]],
d:[["1938","Nuclear fission discovered"],["1942","Chicago Pile-1 starts up"],["1954","Obninsk plant supplies the grid"],["1979","Three Mile Island accident"],["1986","Chernobyl disaster"],["2011","Fukushima accident"]]},
rail:{l:"A railway is a track of steel rails along which trains carry passengers and goods. Railways transformed the world in the 1800s, making travel faster and cheaper than ever before, and today high-speed trains, subways and freight lines remain among the most efficient ways to move people and cargo.",
s:[["Rails before steam","Wooden wagonways, with horses pulling carts along rails, were used in mines from the 1500s. Smooth rails greatly reduced friction, so a horse could pull far heavier loads. Iron rails replaced wood in the 1700s."],
["Stephenson’s Rocket","In 1829 the Liverpool and Manchester Railway held a contest, the Rainhill Trials, to choose its locomotives. George and Robert Stephenson’s Rocket won, reaching about 48 km/h. Its design, with many fire tubes in the boiler, became the model for steam locomotives for over a century."],
["Railways in Asia","Railways reached Asia under colonial rule and through modernizing governments. India’s first passenger train ran in 1853, Japan’s first railway linked Tokyo and Yokohama in 1872, and lines followed in Java, Burma, the Philippines and China. Today China has the world’s largest high-speed network, and India Railways is one of the world’s largest employers."],
["Maglev","Magnetic levitation trains float above the track on powerful magnets, so there is no friction from wheels. The Shanghai Maglev, opened in 2004, reaches 431 km/h in regular service, and Japan’s experimental maglev has reached 603 km/h."]],
k:[["First public steam railway","Stockton and Darlington, 1825"],["Standard gauge","1,435 mm"],["Longest line","Trans-Siberian, about 9,289 km"],["Fastest train record","603 km/h (Japanese maglev, 2015)"],["First underground railway","London, 1863"]],
d:[["1804","Trevithick’s steam locomotive"],["1829","Rocket wins the Rainhill Trials"],["1863","London opens the first underground railway"],["1869","First US transcontinental railroad"],["1872","Japan’s first railway opens between Tokyo and Yokohama"],["1964","Shinkansen opens in Japan"]]},
phone:{l:"The telephone is a device that turns the sound of a voice into electrical signals, sends them over a distance and turns them back into sound. Since its invention in 1876 it has grown from a wooden box on the wall into the smartphone, a pocket computer that most people on Earth carry every day.",
s:[["How it works","In early phones, a thin metal disk called a diaphragm vibrated when someone spoke, changing an electric current to match the sound waves. At the other end, the current moved another diaphragm in the earpiece, recreating the voice. Modern phones turn sound into digital numbers before sending it."],
["Who invented it?","Bell filed his patent on 14 February 1876, the same day Elisha Gray filed a notice for a similar invention. An Italian immigrant, Antonio Meucci, had also worked on voice transmission years earlier. Bell won the patent battles, but historians still debate the credit."],
["Phones around the world","For most of the 20th century, landlines were scarce outside rich countries, and people waited years for a connection. Mobile phones changed that. From the late 1990s, many countries in Africa and Asia skipped landlines altogether. In Kenya, the M-Pesa service, launched in 2007, let people send money by text message, and mobile money spread across the continent."],
["The smartphone","A smartphone combines a phone, computer, camera, music player and map. Touchscreens, app stores and fast mobile data turned it into the most widely used computer in history. Many people now use phones mainly for messaging and apps rather than calls."]],
k:[["Patent","US Patent 174,465, 7 March 1876"],["Inventor","Alexander Graham Bell"],["First mobile call","3 April 1973"],["First text message","1992"],["Mobile subscriptions","More than 8 billion worldwide"]],
d:[["1876","Bell patents the telephone"],["1878","First telephone exchange, New Haven"],["1915","First coast-to-coast call in the US"],["1928","PLDT founded"],["1973","First handheld mobile call"],["2007","iPhone launched"]]},
sat:{l:"A satellite is an object that orbits a larger one. Besides natural satellites such as the Moon, thousands of human-made satellites now circle Earth. They relay phone calls and television, guide navigation, watch the weather, study the planet and peer deep into space.",
s:[["Staying in orbit","A satellite is constantly falling toward Earth, but it moves sideways so fast that the ground curves away beneath it, so it never lands. The higher the orbit, the slower a satellite needs to move. A satellite in low orbit circles Earth in about 90 minutes."],
["Kinds of orbit","Geostationary satellites stay over one spot, ideal for television and weather images. Polar satellites pass over the poles, scanning the whole Earth as it turns beneath them. Navigation satellites such as GPS use medium orbits, and the newest internet networks use thousands of small satellites in low orbit."],
["Watching the weather","Weather satellites track hurricanes, typhoons and cyclones from space, allowing forecasters to warn communities days in advance. Satellites also measure sea temperatures, ice cover, forests and crops, helping scientists study climate change."],
["Space agencies","More than 70 countries now have government space agencies, though only about a dozen can launch satellites on their own rockets. The largest include NASA in the United States, founded in 1958, Russia’s Roscosmos, the European Space Agency, the China National Space Administration, India’s ISRO and Japan’s JAXA. Others buy launches from these countries or from private companies."]],
k:[["First satellite","Sputnik 1, 4 October 1957"],["Geostationary height","About 35,786 km"],["Low-orbit period","About 90 minutes"],["Active satellites","More than 10,000 (mid-2020s)"],["First CubeSats","Launched in 2003"]],
d:[["1945","Arthur C. Clarke proposes communications satellites"],["1957","Sputnik 1 launched"],["1960","First weather satellite, TIROS-1"],["1962","Telstar relays live TV across the Atlantic"],["2016","Diwata-1 deployed"],["2019","SpaceX begins launching Starlink internet satellites in large batches"]]},
laser:{l:"A laser is a device that produces an intense, narrow beam of light of a single color. Since the first laser shone in 1960, these remarkable beams have found their way into supermarket scanners, internet cables, surgery, factories, concerts and scientific experiments that probe the universe.",
s:[["How a laser works","Inside a laser, energy from electricity or a flash of light excites atoms in a material called the gain medium. When an excited atom is struck by a photon, it releases an identical photon, a process Einstein predicted in 1917 called stimulated emission. Mirrors at each end bounce the light back and forth, building up a powerful beam."],
["Kinds of laser","Lasers can use gases such as helium-neon or carbon dioxide, crystals such as ruby, liquids with dyes, or tiny semiconductor chips. Semiconductor laser diodes are the most common, found in pointers, printers and fiber-optic links."],
["Light in fibers","Optical fibers are hair-thin strands of glass. Laser pulses travel along them by bouncing off the inside walls, a process called total internal reflection. A single fiber can carry trillions of bits of information per second across oceans."],
["Laser safety","Even a small laser can damage the eye if shone directly into it, because the lens focuses the beam onto the retina. Lasers are labeled by class according to their power. Shining lasers at aircraft is dangerous and illegal in many countries."]],
k:[["Acronym","Light Amplification by Stimulated Emission of Radiation"],["First laser","Ruby laser, 1960"],["Inventor","Theodore Maiman"],["Theory","Albert Einstein, 1917"],["Common type","Semiconductor laser diode"]],
d:[["1917","Einstein describes stimulated emission"],["1954","Maser, a microwave forerunner, built"],["1960","First working laser"],["1974","First supermarket barcode scan"],["1982","Compact discs read by lasers"],["1999","LASIK approved in the US"]]},
chip:{l:"Semiconductors are materials that can act either as conductors or insulators of electricity. Made into tiny switches called transistors and packed by the billion onto microchips, they are the building blocks of every computer, phone, car and appliance in the digital age.",
s:[["The transistor","A transistor controls the flow of electricity, switching it on or off or amplifying it. John Bardeen, Walter Brattain and William Shockley invented it at Bell Labs in 1947 and won the Nobel Prize in 1956. Transistors replaced bulky vacuum tubes, making electronics smaller, cheaper and more reliable."],
["The integrated circuit","In 1958 Jack Kilby at Texas Instruments built the first integrated circuit, putting several components on one piece of material. Robert Noyce soon made a silicon version. In 1971 Intel’s 4004 put a whole computer processor on a single chip, the first microprocessor."],
["Where chips are made","Making chips is a global chain. Companies in the United States design many of them, the Netherlands makes the advanced machines that print them, Taiwan’s TSMC manufactures most of the world’s most advanced chips, and South Korea leads in memory chips. Factories in Malaysia, Vietnam and the Philippines assemble, test and package chips for companies around the world."],
["Clean rooms","Chips are made in clean rooms where the air is thousands of times cleaner than a hospital operating room, because a single speck of dust can ruin a circuit. Workers wear full-body suits, often called bunny suits. Making a chip can take hundreds of steps over several months."]],
k:[["Main material","Silicon"],["Transistor invented","1947, Bell Labs"],["First integrated circuit","1958, Jack Kilby"],["First microprocessor","Intel 4004, 1971"],["Leading maker of advanced chips","TSMC, Taiwan"]],
d:[["1947","Transistor invented"],["1958","First integrated circuit"],["1965","Moore’s law proposed"],["1971","Intel 4004 microprocessor"],["1987","TSMC is founded in Taiwan"],["2020s","Chips with features of a few nanometers"]]},
radio:{l:"Radio is the use of invisible electromagnetic waves to carry sound, pictures and data through the air without wires. It began as a way to send Morse code to ships and grew into broadcasting, which brought news, music and drama into homes, and into the wireless technology all around us today.",
s:[["Radio waves","Radio waves are electromagnetic waves like light, but much longer, from millimeters to kilometers. They travel at the speed of light. A transmitter makes electrons surge back and forth in an antenna, sending out waves, and a receiver’s antenna picks them up and turns them back into signals."],
["Saving lives at sea","Wireless telegraphy was first used mainly by ships. When the Titanic sank in 1912, its radio operators sent distress calls that brought the ship Carpathia, which rescued more than 700 people. Afterward, laws required ships to keep radio watch around the clock."],
["Radio and history","Radio let leaders speak directly to whole nations. Franklin D. Roosevelt’s fireside chats calmed Americans during the Great Depression, and in 1940 Charles de Gaulle called on the French to resist from a BBC studio in London. In 1986, Radio Veritas helped rally crowds during the People Power Revolution in the Philippines, and in many places radio remains vital during disasters."],
["Television and beyond","Television sends pictures as well as sound using radio waves. Radio technology also underlies mobile phones, Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, radar, garage door openers and the contactless cards people tap to pay."]],
k:[["Waves predicted","James Clerk Maxwell, 1860s"],["Waves demonstrated","Heinrich Hertz, 1887"],["Wireless pioneer","Guglielmo Marconi"],["Unit of frequency","Hertz (Hz)"],["Famous broadcast","The War of the Worlds, 1938"]],
d:[["1887","Hertz produces radio waves"],["1895","Marconi sends wireless signals"],["1901","First transatlantic radio signal"],["1912","Titanic’s distress call"],["1920","Commercial broadcasting begins"],["1986","Radio Veritas during People Power"]]},
steam:{l:"A steam engine uses the expanding power of steam, made by boiling water, to do mechanical work. It was the engine of the Industrial Revolution, pumping mines, driving factories, pulling trains and pushing ships, and its descendant, the steam turbine, still generates most of the world’s electricity.",
s:[["How it works","Fire heats water in a boiler to make steam, which takes up about 1,700 times more space than the water. In a piston engine, steam pushes a piston along a cylinder, and a rod and crank turn this back-and-forth motion into turning motion. In a turbine, jets of steam spin blades on a shaft."],
["Watt’s big idea","Newcomen’s engine wasted fuel because its cylinder was heated and cooled with every stroke. While repairing a model in 1765, James Watt realized that a separate condenser could cool the steam while the cylinder stayed hot. He and his partner Matthew Boulton sold engines that used about a quarter of the coal."],
["Steamships","Robert Fulton’s Clermont began regular steamboat service on the Hudson River in 1807. In 1838 the Great Western began regular steam crossings of the Atlantic. Steam freed ships from depending on the wind, and huge ocean liners carried millions of emigrants."],
["Steam around the world","Steam power spread far beyond Britain. India’s first passenger train ran from Bombay to Thane in 1853, and Japan opened its first railway in 1872. Steamships linked colonies to Europe after the Suez Canal opened, and steam-powered mills crushed sugarcane from the Caribbean to Java. Some old steam locomotives still run on heritage railways today."]],
k:[["First practical engine","Newcomen, 1712"],["Key improvement","Watt’s separate condenser, 1765"],["Watt’s patent","1769"],["Steam turbine","Charles Parsons, 1884"],["Steam vs. water","About 1,700 times the volume"]],
d:[["c. 50 CE","Hero of Alexandria’s spinning steam toy"],["1698","Savery’s steam pump"],["1712","Newcomen engine"],["1769","Watt’s patent"],["1807","Fulton’s steamboat service"],["1884","Parsons’ steam turbine"]]},
scope:{l:"A telescope is an instrument that collects light or other radiation from distant objects and makes them appear closer and brighter. Telescopes transformed astronomy, revealing moons, planets, galaxies and the dawn of the universe, and they are now placed on mountaintops and in space.",
s:[["Refractors","Refracting telescopes use lenses. A large lens at the front, the objective, gathers light and brings it to a focus, and a small eyepiece lens magnifies the image. Big refractors are hard to build because large lenses sag and blur colors, so the largest, at Yerkes Observatory, has a lens of 102 cm."],
["Giant mirrors","Reflecting telescopes use curved mirrors, which can be made much larger than lenses and supported from behind. The largest optical telescopes today have segmented mirrors more than 10 m across, and the Extremely Large Telescope being built in Chile will have a mirror 39 m wide."],
["Beyond visible light","Different telescopes see different kinds of light. Radio telescopes detect cold gas and pulsars, infrared telescopes see through dust and spot the earliest galaxies, and X-ray telescopes, which must be in space, study black holes and exploding stars."],
["Stargazing","Many planetariums and astronomy clubs hold public viewings of eclipses, meteor showers and planets. A small telescope or even binoculars can show the Moon’s craters, Jupiter’s moons and Saturn’s rings. Dark-sky parks and reserves, from Namibia to New Zealand, protect places where the night sky is free of light pollution."]],
k:[["Invented","1608, Netherlands"],["First reflector","Isaac Newton, 1668"],["Largest refractor","Yerkes, 102 cm lens"],["Famous space telescopes","Hubble, James Webb"],["Largest optical (under construction)","Extremely Large Telescope, 39 m"]],
d:[["1608","Telescope patent applied for"],["1610","Galileo publishes his discoveries"],["1668","Newton’s reflector"],["1937","Grote Reber builds an early radio telescope"],["1990","Hubble launched"],["2021","James Webb launched"]]},
robot:{l:"A robot is a machine that can sense its surroundings, process information and carry out tasks, either on its own or under remote control. Robots weld cars, perform surgery, vacuum floors, explore other planets and dive to the ocean floor, doing work that is dull, dirty or dangerous for people.",
s:[["Senses, brain and muscles","Robots use sensors such as cameras, microphones, touch pads and laser scanners to gather information. A computer acts as their brain, running programs that decide what to do. Motors, called actuators, move their arms, wheels or legs. Some robots learn from experience using artificial intelligence."],
["Ancient automata","People have built self-moving machines for thousands of years. Hero of Alexandria designed mechanical theaters, and the engineer al-Jazari described programmable automata in 1206. In the 1700s, clockwork figures could write, draw and play music."],
["Laws of robotics","The writer Isaac Asimov imagined three laws of robotics in a 1942 story, saying a robot must not harm a human, must obey orders and must protect itself. The laws are fiction, but they still inspire discussions about how to make robots safe."],
["Robots in schools","Robotics competitions let students design, build and program robots. Teams from dozens of countries compete in events such as the FIRST Robotics Competition and the World Robot Olympiad, and robotics is increasingly taught in schools."]],
k:[["Word coined","1920, Karel Čapek’s play R.U.R."],["First industrial robot","Unimate, 1961"],["Famous Mars rovers","Sojourner, Curiosity, Perseverance"],["Laws of robotics","Isaac Asimov, 1942"],["Most robot-dense country","South Korea"]],
d:[["1206","Al-Jazari’s book of machines"],["1920","R.U.R. introduces the word robot"],["1961","Unimate at General Motors"],["1997","Sojourner rover on Mars"],["2002","Roomba vacuum robot released"],["2021","Perseverance lands with the Ingenuity helicopter"]]},
crypto:{l:"Cryptography is the art and science of keeping messages secret by turning them into code that only the right person can read. Once used mainly by generals and spies, it now protects nearly everything we do online, from passwords and bank payments to private messages.",
s:[["Codes and ciphers","A code replaces whole words with other words or symbols, while a cipher scrambles individual letters. The original message is called plaintext and the scrambled version ciphertext. A key, such as the number of places to shift each letter, tells the receiver how to unscramble it."],
["Breaking codes","In the 800s the Arab scholar al-Kindi showed that simple ciphers can be cracked by counting how often each letter appears, since some letters, such as e in English, are far more common than others. This method, frequency analysis, defeated most ciphers for centuries."],
["Enigma and Bletchley Park","In World War II Germany used Enigma, a machine with rotating wheels that changed the cipher with every letter. Polish mathematicians first broke early versions, and British codebreakers at Bletchley Park, including Alan Turing, built machines called bombes to read German messages. Historians believe this shortened the war."],
["Keeping secrets today","When you see a padlock in a web browser, your connection is encrypted. Messaging apps use end-to-end encryption, so only the sender and receiver can read messages. Researchers are now designing new methods that could resist attack by future quantum computers."]],
k:[["Word origin","Greek kryptos (hidden) and graphein (to write)"],["Oldest famous cipher","Caesar cipher"],["Code-breaking method","Frequency analysis (al-Kindi)"],["WWII machine","Enigma"],["Public-key pioneers","Diffie, Hellman; Rivest, Shamir, Adleman"]],
d:[["c. 50 BCE","Caesar’s shift cipher"],["c. 850","Al-Kindi describes frequency analysis"],["1932","Polish codebreakers crack Enigma"],["1940","Turing’s bombe begins work"],["1976","Public-key cryptography described"],["1977","RSA algorithm published"]]},
camera:{l:"A camera is a device that captures images by focusing light onto a light-sensitive surface. From the first fuzzy photograph in the 1820s to the high-resolution cameras in every smartphone, cameras have changed how people record history, share memories and see the world.",
s:[["The camera obscura","Camera obscura is Latin for dark room. Light passing through a small hole into a darkened room projects an upside-down image of the scene outside onto the opposite wall. The Arab scientist Ibn al-Haytham described it around 1020, and later artists used portable versions to help them draw."],
["Early photographs","Niépce’s first photograph needed an exposure of many hours. In 1839 Louis Daguerre announced the daguerreotype, a sharp image on a silvered copper plate, and William Henry Fox Talbot invented a negative process that allowed many prints to be made from one picture."],
["Color and movies","The first color photograph was made in 1861 using a method proposed by James Clerk Maxwell. Kodachrome color film arrived in 1935. Cameras that took rapid sequences of pictures led to motion pictures in the 1890s."],
["Photography and truth","Photographs have recorded wars, disasters and great moments, shaping how people understand events. Today, photos can easily be edited or generated by computers, so checking where an image came from has become an important skill."]],
k:[["Earliest surviving photo","c. 1826, by Nicéphore Niépce"],["Daguerreotype","Announced 1839"],["First Kodak camera","1888"],["First digital camera","1975, Steven Sasson"],["Key parts","Lens, aperture, shutter, sensor"]],
d:[["c. 1020","Ibn al-Haytham describes the camera obscura"],["c. 1826","First surviving photograph"],["1839","Daguerreotype announced"],["1888","Kodak camera launched"],["1935","Kodachrome color film"],["2000","First camera phones sold in Japan"]]},
ev:{l:"An electric vehicle, or EV, is a car, bus, motorcycle or other vehicle powered by electric motors and batteries instead of a gasoline or diesel engine. EVs produce no exhaust fumes on the road and are becoming more common as batteries improve and countries work to cut pollution.",
s:[["How an EV works","A large battery pack, usually under the floor, stores electricity. A controller sends power to one or more electric motors, which turn the wheels. Electric motors give strong pulling power from a standstill, so EVs accelerate quickly and quietly, and they have far fewer moving parts than engine cars."],
["Kinds of electric vehicle","Battery electric vehicles run only on electricity. Hybrids combine a small engine with a battery and motor, and plug-in hybrids can be charged from the grid. Fuel cell vehicles make electricity on board from hydrogen."],
["Electric buses, minibuses and tricycles","In many cities, electric versions of popular shared vehicles are replacing diesel ones. China’s cities run tens of thousands of electric buses, India’s electric rickshaws carry millions of passengers a day, and cities such as Nairobi and Manila are testing electric minibuses, jeepneys and tricycles to cut air pollution."],
["Clean or not?","An EV is only as clean as the electricity that charges it. Even on grids powered partly by coal, EVs usually produce lower emissions over their lifetime than gasoline cars, and the benefit grows as grids add more renewable energy. Making batteries, however, requires mining and energy."]],
k:[["Power source","Rechargeable battery"],["Main motor type","Electric motor"],["Key advantage","No tailpipe emissions"],["Largest market","China"],["Most electric new cars","Norway"]],
d:[["1830s","First crude electric carriages"],["1899","Electric car La Jamais Contente passes 100 km/h"],["1912","Electric starter helps gasoline cars win"],["1997","Toyota Prius hybrid launched"],["2008","Tesla Roadster uses lithium-ion cells"],["2022","Electric cars pass 10 percent of new car sales worldwide"]]},
wind:{l:"Wind power uses the energy of moving air to generate electricity. People have used windmills to grind grain and pump water for more than a thousand years. Today giant wind turbines, on land and at sea, are one of the fastest-growing sources of clean electricity in the world.",
s:[["Windmills of old","Persian windmills with sails on a vertical axis were grinding grain by the 800s. In Europe, windmills appeared in the 1100s, and the Dutch used thousands of them to pump water from low-lying land. In 1887 James Blyth in Scotland built one of the first windmills to make electricity."],
["Inside a turbine","Wind turns the blades, which spin a shaft connected to a generator inside the housing at the top, called the nacelle. Sensors turn the turbine to face the wind and adjust the angle of the blades. Turbines stop in very strong winds to avoid damage, and in storm-prone regions they are designed to withstand hurricanes and typhoons."],
["Where the wind blows","Turbines work best where winds are strong and steady, such as coasts, hilltops and open plains. Engineers measure wind for a year or more before building. Doubling the wind speed gives about eight times as much power, so a good site matters enormously."],
["Wind and wildlife","Wind farms take up land and can harm birds and bats, but careful siting, radar systems and pausing turbines during migration reduce the risk. Many people also debate how wind farms change the look of landscapes."]],
k:[["Leading country","China"],["Largest turbines","Over 15 megawatts"],["Blade length","Over 100 m (largest)"],["First offshore wind farm","Vindeby, Denmark, 1991"],["Power rule","Double the wind, about 8 times the power"]],
d:[["c. 900","Persian windmills grind grain"],["1100s","Windmills spread in Europe"],["1887","James Blyth’s electricity windmill"],["1991","First offshore wind farm, Vindeby, Denmark"],["2005","Bangui wind farm begins operating"],["2020s","Offshore turbines pass 15 megawatts"]]}
});

