// Longer, DK-style material for Philippines articles (part 3): a lead, extra sections, key facts and key dates.
window.DEEP=window.DEEP||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP,{
lapulapu:{l:"Lapulapu was the chieftain of Mactan who led his warriors to victory over Ferdinand Magellan’s Spanish force in 1521. His stand at the Battle of Mactan made him the first recorded native of the islands to resist European colonization, and he is honored today as the first Filipino hero.",
s:[["Rival chiefs","When Magellan arrived in Cebu, Rajah Humabon accepted Spanish friendship and Christianity. Lapulapu, who ruled part of nearby Mactan and was a rival of Humabon, refused to pay tribute or submit to the Spanish king. Magellan decided to make an example of him."],
["The battle","Magellan landed at dawn with about 50 armed men, leaving his ships too far out because of the coral reef. Lapulapu’s force, perhaps 1,500 warriors, fought with spears, bamboo lances and kampilan swords. The Spaniards’ armor protected their bodies, so the warriors aimed at their legs. Magellan was wounded and killed."],
["What we know","Almost everything known about the battle comes from Antonio Pigafetta, an Italian chronicler on the voyage, who survived it. He did not record Lapulapu’s age, appearance or fate. Some versions of his account spell the chief’s name Cilapulapu."],
["A national hero","Lapulapu is honored in place names, statues and on the badge of the Philippine National Police. In 2021, the 500th anniversary of the battle, the government commemorated the Victory at Mactan. Each 27 April, Lapulapu Day is celebrated with a reenactment of the battle in Mactan."]],
k:[["Role","Chieftain (datu) of Mactan"],["Famous for","Victory at the Battle of Mactan"],["Battle date","27 April 1521"],["Opponent","Ferdinand Magellan"],["Main source","Antonio Pigafetta’s chronicle"]],
d:[["16 March 1521","Magellan reaches the Philippines"],["14 April 1521","Humabon baptized in Cebu"],["27 April 1521","Battle of Mactan"],["1961","Opon renamed Lapu-Lapu City"],["2017","27 April declared Lapulapu Day"],["2021","500th anniversary of the battle"]]},
bonifacio:{l:"Andrés Bonifacio was a self-taught revolutionary from Tondo, Manila, who founded and led the Katipunan, the secret society that launched the Philippine Revolution against Spain in 1896. A man of the working class, he is honored as the Father of the Philippine Revolution and one of the country’s greatest heroes.",
s:[["Self-taught","Orphaned at 14, Bonifacio left school to support his younger brothers and sisters, making canes and paper fans and later working for foreign trading firms. He educated himself by reading books in Spanish and Tagalog, including Rizal’s novels, Victor Hugo’s Les Misérables and histories of the French Revolution."],
["The Supremo","As Supremo of the Katipunan, Bonifacio built a network of thousands of members organized in councils. With Emilio Jacinto, the brains of the Katipunan, he wrote its rules and published the newspaper Kalayaan. He also wrote the patriotic poem Pag-ibig sa Tinubuang Lupa, Love of the Motherland."],
["The Cry of Pugad Lawin","In late August 1896, when the Spanish discovered the Katipunan, Bonifacio gathered members in the hills north of Manila. They tore up their cedulas, tax certificates that marked them as Spanish subjects, and shouted for freedom. The event is known as the Cry of Pugad Lawin or the Cry of Balintawak."],
["Tragic end","At the Tejeros Convention in March 1897, Aguinaldo’s faction won the leadership, and Bonifacio rejected the result. He was arrested, tried for treason by a revolutionary court and executed with his brother Procopio in the mountains of Maragondon, Cavite, on 10 May 1897. His wife, Gregoria de Jesús, became a revered heroine."]],
k:[["Born","30 November 1863, Tondo, Manila"],["Died","10 May 1897, Maragondon, Cavite"],["Title","Supremo of the Katipunan"],["Wife","Gregoria de Jesús"],["Holiday","Bonifacio Day, 30 November"]],
d:[["1863","Born in Tondo"],["1892","Founds the Katipunan"],["1896","Cry of Pugad Lawin"],["1897","Tejeros Convention"],["1897","Executed in Maragondon"],["1933","Bonifacio Monument unveiled"]]},
commonwealth:{l:"The Commonwealth of the Philippines was the self-governing government of the islands from 1935 to 1946, a transition period promised by the United States before full independence. Under Presidents Manuel L. Quezon and Sergio Osmeña, Filipinos ran their own internal affairs and prepared for nationhood, even through war and exile.",
s:[["The road to self-rule","Filipino leaders sent several independence missions to Washington in the 1920s and 1930s. The Hare–Hawes–Cutting Act of 1933 was rejected by the Philippine legislature, but the Tydings–McDuffie Act of 1934 was accepted. A constitutional convention then wrote the 1935 Constitution."],
["Quezon’s programs","President Quezon promoted social justice, land reform and a national defense plan led by General Douglas MacArthur as military adviser. Quezon City, planned as a new capital, was founded in 1939. Quezon also offered refuge in the Philippines to Jewish refugees fleeing Nazi Germany."],
["Government in exile","When Japan invaded, Quezon and Vice President Osmeña left Corregidor and set up a government in exile in Washington, D.C. Quezon, ill with tuberculosis, died in New York in August 1944. Osmeña became president and returned with MacArthur to Leyte in October 1944."],
["The 1946 election","In April 1946 Manuel Roxas defeated Osmeña in the last Commonwealth election. On 4 July 1946 the Commonwealth ended and Roxas became the first president of the independent Republic of the Philippines."]],
k:[["Period","15 November 1935 to 4 July 1946"],["Presidents","Manuel L. Quezon, Sergio Osmeña"],["Legal basis","Tydings–McDuffie Act, 1934"],["Constitution","1935 Constitution"],["Capital","Manila"]],
d:[["1934","Tydings–McDuffie Act"],["1935","Commonwealth inaugurated"],["1937","Women win the vote; national language chosen"],["1939","Quezon City founded"],["1942","Government goes into exile"],["1946","Independence; Commonwealth ends"]]},
sulu:{l:"The Sultanate of Sulu was a Muslim state centered on the Sulu Archipelago in the far southwest of the Philippines. For centuries its sultans ruled a seafaring trading kingdom that stretched to northern Borneo, resisting Spanish conquest and leaving a lasting legacy in the culture of the southern Philippines.",
s:[["The first sultan","According to tradition, an Arab scholar named Sharif ul-Hashim of Johor married a local princess and became the first sultan, Paduka Mahasari Maulana al-Sultan Sharif ul-Hashim. Earlier, a missionary called Karim ul-Makhdum is said to have built the first mosque in the Philippines, at Simunul in Tawi-Tawi, around 1380."],
["Trade and power","Sulu traded sea cucumbers, pearls, bird’s nests and wax with China for silk, porcelain and tea. In the 1700s and 1800s the sultanate grew rich and powerful, and its sea raiders captured people from coastal towns across the Visayas and Luzon to sell as slaves, which caused great suffering."],
["North Borneo","In 1878 the Sultan of Sulu leased or ceded, depending on how the agreement is translated, the territory of North Borneo to European businessmen. The area later became the Malaysian state of Sabah. The Philippines has kept a claim to Sabah, which Malaysia does not accept."],
["Culture","The Tausug, meaning people of the current, are known for their seafaring, fine textiles, weapons such as the barong blade and colorful boats called vinta. Sulu’s traditional law and the sultan’s title survive, though the sultanate lost its political power in the early 1900s."]],
k:[["Founded","c. 1405 (traditional)"],["Capital","Jolo"],["Religion","Islam"],["Main people","Tausug"],["Lost power","Kiram–Bates and Carpenter agreements, 1899–1915"]],
d:[["c. 1380","Mosque built at Simunul (traditional)"],["c. 1405","Sultanate founded"],["1638","Spanish forces capture Jolo"],["1878","North Borneo agreement"],["1899","Kiram–Bates Treaty with the US"],["1915","Sultan gives up temporal power"]]},
phsport:{l:"Sports are a passion in the Philippines, where basketball courts stand in nearly every barangay and the whole country stops to watch a big boxing match. Filipinos have produced world champions in boxing, billiards and weightlifting, and the country now celebrates Olympic gold medals of its own.",
s:[["Basketball nation","American teachers introduced basketball in the early 1900s, and it quickly became a national obsession. The national team finished fifth at the 1936 Berlin Olympics and won bronze at the 1954 world championship, its best results. The country co-hosted the 2023 FIBA World Cup, with games at the Philippine Arena, one of the largest indoor arenas in the world."],
["Pacman","Manny Pacquiao rose from poverty in General Santos City to become one of the greatest boxers of all time, winning world titles in eight weight divisions. His fights drew so many viewers that streets were said to empty and crime to drop. He later served as a senator."],
["Olympic glory","The Philippines first competed at the Olympics in 1924. Swimmer Teófilo Yldefonso won its first medal, a bronze, in 1928. Hidilyn Diaz’s weightlifting gold in Tokyo ended a 97-year wait, and gymnast Carlos Yulo won two golds at the 2024 Paris Olympics."],
["Volleyball, chess and more","Women’s volleyball draws huge crowds to college and professional games. Eugene Torre became Asia’s first chess grandmaster in 1974. Filipinos also excel in sepak takraw, esports, bowling, where Paeng Nepomuceno won many world titles, and martial arts such as arnis."]],
k:[["Most popular sport","Basketball"],["Oldest pro league in Asia","PBA, founded 1975"],["First Olympic gold","Hidilyn Diaz, 2021"],["Double Olympic champion","Carlos Yulo, 2024"],["National sport","Arnis (since 2009)"]],
d:[["1913","Manila hosts the first Far Eastern Games"],["1928","First Olympic medal"],["1954","Bronze at the basketball world championship"],["1975","PBA founded"],["2021","First Olympic gold"],["2024","Yulo wins two golds in Paris"]]},
phdance:{l:"Philippine music and dance reflect the many influences that have shaped the islands, from ancient Austronesian rituals and Islamic traditions of the south to Spanish fiestas and American pop. Folk dances, sung poetry and modern hits all express a deep Filipino love of music and celebration.",
s:[["Dances of many peoples","Folk dances are often grouped by origin. Cordillera dances, such as the banga, in which women balance clay pots on their heads, come from mountain peoples. Muslim dances, such as the singkil, are performed by the Maranao. Spanish-influenced dances include the cariñosa and the jota, and rural dances celebrate farming and fishing."],
["Singkil","The singkil retells part of the Darangen epic, in which a princess escapes through a forest during an earthquake. Dancers step gracefully in and out of crisscrossing bamboo poles while waving fans, and a princess wears heavy bracelets that jingle with each step. Kulintang music accompanies the dance."],
["Bayanihan and the world stage","The Bayanihan Philippine National Folk Dance Company, founded in 1957, toured the world and helped preserve folk dances that were disappearing. Francisca Reyes Aquino, a pioneer researcher, traveled through rural areas to record dances and is honored as a National Artist."],
["From kundiman to P-pop","The kundiman rose to prominence in the late 1800s and early 1900s, with composers such as Nicanor Abelardo and Francisco Santiago. In later decades, Filipino singers and bands from Pilita Corrales to the Eraserheads shaped popular music, and P-pop groups such as SB19 now draw fans across Asia."]],
k:[["Famous dances","Tinikling, singkil, cariñosa, pandanggo sa ilaw"],["Southern gong music","Kulintang"],["Spanish-influenced ensemble","Rondalla"],["Traditional love song","Kundiman"],["National folk dance company","Bayanihan (1957)"]],
d:[["1600s","Spanish dances blend with local traditions"],["1920s","Kundiman golden age"],["1920s","Francisca Reyes Aquino begins documenting folk dances"],["1957","Bayanihan dance company founded"],["1973","Francisca Reyes Aquino named National Artist"],["2018","SB19 debuts, helping launch P-pop"]]},
jeepney:{l:"The jeepney is the most popular form of public transport in the Philippines and a beloved national symbol. Born from leftover American military jeeps after World War II, these long, brightly painted vehicles carry millions of passengers every day along set routes in cities and towns across the country.",
s:[["From jeep to jeepney","When American forces left after the war, thousands of Willys and Ford jeeps were sold or given away. Filipinos lengthened them, added benches facing each other along the sides and a roof, and painted them brightly. The name is a mix of jeep and jitney, an old word for a cheap shared taxi."],
["Sarao and the builders","Leonardo Sarao, a former calesa driver, founded Sarao Motors in Las Piñas in 1953 and became the most famous jeepney maker. Francisco Motors and others also built them. Workshops crafted the stainless steel bodies by hand, often decorating them with horses, chrome ornaments and the owner’s family name."],
["Riding a jeepney","Passengers flag down a jeepney anywhere along its route and climb in the back. Fares are passed forward hand to hand to the driver, with people calling bayad po, here’s my payment. To get off, passengers say para po or tap a coin on the metal roof. The route is painted on the side."],
["The future","Old jeepneys run on diesel and contribute to air pollution. The modernization program requires operators to form cooperatives and switch to vehicles with cleaner engines or electric motors, safety features and side doors. Many drivers have protested the high cost, and the program has been debated for years."]],
k:[["Origin","Converted US military jeeps, after 1945"],["Capacity","About 16 to 20 passengers"],["Famous maker","Sarao Motors (1953)"],["Name","Jeep plus jitney"],["Modernization program","Launched 2017"]],
d:[["1945","Surplus jeeps left after the war"],["1953","Sarao Motors founded"],["1964","Jeepney shown at the New York World’s Fair"],["1970s","Jeepneys dominate Manila transport"],["2017","Modernization program launched"],["2020s","Electric and modern jeepneys introduced"]]},
pulag:{l:"Mount Pulag is the highest mountain on Luzon and the third highest in the Philippines. Rising above the Cordillera Central, it is famous for its sea of clouds at dawn, its cold grasslands and mossy forests, and its importance to the Indigenous peoples who live on its slopes.",
s:[["Playground of the gods","Mount Pulag’s summit grasslands are covered in dwarf bamboo that sways like a green sea. The mountain is sometimes called the playground of the gods. On clear nights hikers see the Milky Way, and at dawn the sun rises over clouds that fill the valleys below."],
["Cold at the top","The summit is one of the coldest places in the Philippines. Temperatures can fall close to freezing, and frost sometimes forms on the grass in the cool months of January and February. Hikers need warm clothing, raincoats and good boots."],
["Sacred ground","For the Ibaloi and Kankanaey, Pulag is a resting place of the spirits of the ancestors. Burial caves on its slopes once held mummified bodies. Hikers must attend an orientation at the ranger station before climbing and are asked to respect the mountain and leave nothing behind."],
["Protecting Pulag","Mount Pulag National Park was set up in 1987. Its mossy forests, dripping with lichens and ferns, store water for rivers that feed farmland and hydroelectric dams below. Rare animals include the Luzon pygmy fruit bat and the Luzon cloud rat. Climber numbers are limited to protect the trails."]],
k:[["Height","2,922 m"],["Rank","Highest on Luzon, third in the Philippines"],["Provinces","Benguet, Ifugao, Nueva Vizcaya"],["National park","Since 1987"],["Famous for","Sea of clouds"]],
d:[["1987","Mount Pulag National Park established"],["1992","Joins the national protected areas system"],["2000s","Hiking to the summit becomes popular"],["2020","Trails closed during the pandemic"],["2022","Trails reopen with visitor limits"]]}
});

