// Longer, DK-style material for Arts and Literature articles (part 1): a lead, extra sections, key facts and key dates.
window.DEEP=window.DEEP||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP,{
shakes:{l:"William Shakespeare is widely regarded as the greatest writer in the English language. An actor and playwright in Elizabethan and Jacobean London, he wrote comedies, tragedies, histories and poems that explore love, power, jealousy and ambition, and his works are performed more than those of any other playwright.",
s:[["Early life","Shakespeare was the son of John Shakespeare, a glove maker and town official, and Mary Arden. He probably attended the local grammar school, where pupils studied Latin authors. At 18 he married Anne Hathaway, and they had three children, Susanna and the twins Hamnet and Judith. Hamnet died aged 11."],
["Kinds of plays","His plays are usually grouped into comedies such as Twelfth Night, tragedies such as King Lear and Othello, and histories about English kings such as Henry V and Richard III. Late in his career he wrote romances such as The Tempest, which mix magic, sorrow and reconciliation."],
["Theater in his time","Elizabethan theaters were open-air, and poorer audience members, called groundlings, paid a penny to stand in the yard. Plays were performed in daylight with little scenery. Women were not allowed on the public stage, so boys played female roles. Shakespeare’s company became the King’s Men under James I."],
["The First Folio","Shakespeare did not publish most of his plays himself. In 1623, seven years after his death, two fellow actors, John Heminges and Henry Condell, collected 36 of them in a book now called the First Folio. Without it, about half his plays, including Macbeth, might have been lost."]],
k:[["Born","April 1564, Stratford-upon-Avon"],["Died","23 April 1616"],["Plays","About 37–39"],["Sonnets","154"],["Theater","The Globe, London"]],
d:[["1564","Born in Stratford-upon-Avon"],["1582","Marries Anne Hathaway"],["c. 1592","Working as a playwright in London"],["1599","Globe Theatre opens"],["1616","Dies in Stratford"],["1623","First Folio published"]]},
homer:{l:"Homer is the legendary ancient Greek poet credited with the Iliad and the Odyssey, the oldest surviving works of European literature. These two great epic poems, about the Trojan War and its aftermath, have been read, recited and retold for nearly 3,000 years.",
s:[["Who was Homer?","Almost nothing is known about Homer. Ancient Greeks said he was a blind singer from Ionia, on the coast of what is now Turkey, and several cities claimed to be his birthplace. Some scholars think the poems were shaped by many singers over generations, while others believe one great poet gave them their final form."],
["The Iliad","The Iliad covers a few weeks in the tenth year of the Trojan War. The Greek hero Achilles quarrels with his commander Agamemnon and refuses to fight. Only when his friend Patroclus is killed by the Trojan prince Hector does Achilles return to battle, kill Hector and, finally, show mercy to Hector’s grieving father."],
["The Odyssey","The Odyssey follows the clever hero Odysseus on his journey home to Ithaca. He outwits the one-eyed Cyclops, escapes the witch Circe, resists the deadly song of the Sirens and passes between the monsters Scylla and Charybdis. At home, his faithful wife Penelope holds off suitors until he returns in disguise."],
["Was Troy real?","In the 1870s Heinrich Schliemann excavated a mound at Hisarlik in Turkey and declared it to be Troy. Archaeologists have found the remains of a city destroyed several times, including around 1200 BCE. Whether a war like Homer’s took place is still debated."]],
k:[["Works","Iliad and Odyssey"],["Composed","c. 8th century BCE"],["Language","Ancient Greek (Homeric dialect)"],["Meter","Dactylic hexameter"],["Length","Iliad about 15,700 lines; Odyssey about 12,100"]],
d:[["c. 1200 BCE","Troy VII destroyed (traditional time of the war)"],["c. 750 BCE","The epics take shape"],["c. 520 BCE","Official texts said to be fixed in Athens"],["c. 19 BCE","Virgil’s Aeneid continues the Trojan story"],["1488","First printed edition in Florence"],["1870s","Schliemann excavates Hisarlik"]]},
novel:{l:"A novel is a long work of fiction written in prose, telling a story about characters and events through many chapters. Since its rise in the 1600s and 1700s, the novel has become the world’s most popular literary form, able to explore every kind of human experience.",
s:[["Earlier long stories","Long prose stories existed long before the modern novel. The Tale of Genji, written by the Japanese court lady Murasaki Shikibu around 1010, is sometimes called the world’s first novel. Ancient Greek and Roman writers also wrote prose romances, and China produced great novels such as Journey to the West in the 1500s."],
["The rise of the English novel","In the 1700s writers such as Daniel Defoe, author of Robinson Crusoe in 1719, Samuel Richardson and Henry Fielding developed the novel in England. A growing middle class with time to read, and lending libraries, created eager audiences."],
["Kinds of novels","Novels come in many genres, including historical novels, mysteries, science fiction, fantasy, romance and adventure. Mary Shelley’s Frankenstein of 1818 is often called the first science fiction novel, and Edgar Allan Poe and Arthur Conan Doyle shaped detective fiction."],
["The novel today","Novels are now written in almost every language, and authors such as Gabriel García Márquez, Toni Morrison, Chinua Achebe and Haruki Murakami are read worldwide. E-books and audiobooks have changed how people read, but printed novels remain widely loved."]],
k:[["Definition","Long work of prose fiction"],["Early milestone","The Tale of Genji, c. 1010"],["First modern novel","Don Quixote (1605, 1615)"],["Nobel Prize in Literature","First awarded in 1901"],["Word origin","Italian novella, a new story"]],
d:[["c. 1010","The Tale of Genji"],["1605","Don Quixote, part one"],["1719","Robinson Crusoe"],["1813","Pride and Prejudice"],["1818","Frankenstein"],["1887","Noli Me Tángere"]]},
impress:{l:"Impressionism was a revolutionary art movement that began in France in the 1860s and 1870s. Its painters rejected the strict rules of the official art world and set out to capture fleeting moments of light, color and modern life, changing the course of Western art.",
s:[["Rejecting the Salon","The official exhibition in Paris, called the Salon, preferred smooth, highly finished paintings of history and myth. Many young artists were rejected. In 1874 they held their own exhibition in the former studio of the photographer Nadar, and they went on to stage eight independent shows by 1886."],
["Painting light","Impressionists placed dabs of pure color side by side rather than mixing them, so the eye blended them from a distance. They noticed that shadows contain color, often blue or purple, rather than being simply gray or black. Monet painted the same haystacks, cathedral and water lilies many times to show changing light."],
["Modern life","Instead of gods and battles, Impressionists painted everyday scenes: railway stations, cafés, dance halls, boating parties and city streets. Degas painted ballet dancers in rehearsal, and Renoir painted crowds enjoying a Sunday afternoon in Montmartre."],
["Women Impressionists","Berthe Morisot and Mary Cassatt were important members of the group. Because women were limited in where they could go, they often painted domestic scenes, mothers and children and gardens, with great sensitivity and bold technique."]],
k:[["Began","France, 1860s–1870s"],["First exhibition","Paris, 1874"],["Named after","Monet’s Impression, Sunrise"],["Leading artists","Monet, Renoir, Degas, Pissarro, Morisot"],["Key idea","Capturing light and the moment"]],
d:[["1863","Salon des Refusés shows rejected works"],["1872","Monet paints Impression, Sunrise"],["1874","First Impressionist exhibition"],["1886","Eighth and last group show"],["1890s","Monet’s series paintings"],["1926","Monet dies at Giverny"]]},
musicw:{l:"Western classical music is a tradition of composed music that developed in Europe over more than a thousand years, from medieval church chants to symphonies, operas and concertos. Its great composers, such as Bach, Mozart and Beethoven, are still performed and loved around the world.",
s:[["Medieval and Renaissance","Early church music was plainchant, a single melody sung in Latin, later called Gregorian chant. Over time, composers added more voices, creating polyphony, in which several melodies are sung together. Renaissance composers such as Palestrina wrote rich choral works for the church."],
["The Baroque","Baroque music, from about 1600 to 1750, was ornate and dramatic. Composers invented opera and wrote concertos, in which a soloist contrasts with an orchestra. Bach’s complex fugues, Handel’s Messiah and Vivaldi’s The Four Seasons are famous Baroque works."],
["Classical and Romantic","Classical-period composers favored balance and clear forms such as the symphony and sonata. Romantic composers of the 1800s, such as Chopin, Tchaikovsky and Wagner, expressed powerful emotions, told stories in music and drew on folk songs from their homelands."],
["Classical music around the world","Western classical music has spread to every continent. Japan, South Korea and China now train many of the world’s leading performers, and orchestras play from Tokyo to São Paulo. Composers outside Europe blended its forms with their own traditions, such as Heitor Villa-Lobos of Brazil, whose Bachianas Brasileiras mixed Bach with Brazilian folk music."]],
k:[["Main periods","Medieval, Renaissance, Baroque, Classical, Romantic, Modern"],["Famous composers","Bach, Mozart, Beethoven, Chopin, Tchaikovsky"],["Common forms","Symphony, concerto, sonata, opera"],["Orchestra size","Up to about 100 players"],["Oldest orchestra","Staatskapelle Dresden, founded 1548"]],
d:[["c. 1000","Guido of Arezzo improves notation"],["c. 1700","Cristofori builds the piano"],["1742","Handel’s Messiah first performed"],["1824","Beethoven’s Ninth Symphony premieres"],["1824","Las Piñas Bamboo Organ completed"],["1913","Stravinsky’s The Rite of Spring premieres"]]},
cinema:{l:"Cinema, the art of moving pictures, was born in the 1890s when inventors found ways to record and project a rapid series of photographs. Within a few decades films had become one of the world’s most popular entertainments and a powerful new art form.",
s:[["Persistence of vision","A film is a series of still pictures shown quickly, usually 24 per second. The brain blends them into smooth motion. Before cinema, toys such as the zoetrope and Eadweard Muybridge’s photographs of a galloping horse in 1878 showed that motion could be broken into frames."],
["Silent stars","Silent films used title cards for dialogue, and actors expressed feelings through gesture and face. Charlie Chaplin’s Little Tramp and Buster Keaton’s daring stunts made audiences laugh around the world. Directors such as D. W. Griffith and Sergei Eisenstein developed editing techniques still used today."],
["Bollywood and Nollywood","Hollywood is not the world’s biggest film factory. India makes more feature films than any other country, about 1,800 a year in more than 20 languages, with Hindi-language Bollywood the best known. Nigeria’s Nollywood, which grew from cheap video films in the 1990s, is second by number of films. China, Japan, South Korea and France also have major industries."],
["New technologies","Widescreen, 3D, computer-generated imagery and digital cameras have transformed filmmaking. Today streaming services deliver films directly to homes, while film festivals such as Cannes and Venice celebrate cinema as art."]],
k:[["First public paid screening","28 December 1895, Paris"],["Pioneers","Lumière brothers, Thomas Edison, Georges Méliès"],["Standard frame rate","24 frames per second"],["Largest film producer","India"],["First sound feature","The Jazz Singer (1927)"]],
d:[["1878","Muybridge photographs a galloping horse"],["1895","Lumière screening in Paris"],["1896","The Lumière brothers’ films are shown from Bombay to Buenos Aires"],["1919","Dalagang Bukid released"],["1927","The Jazz Singer"],["1939","The Wizard of Oz and Gone with the Wind showcase color"]]},
davinci:{l:"Leonardo da Vinci was an Italian Renaissance artist, scientist and inventor, often described as the most curious mind in history. His paintings, including the Mona Lisa and The Last Supper, are among the most famous ever made, and his notebooks reveal ideas centuries ahead of his time.",
s:[["Apprentice in Florence","At about 14, Leonardo became an apprentice in the Florence workshop of the artist Andrea del Verrocchio, where he learned painting, sculpture and metalwork. According to legend, an angel he painted in Verrocchio’s Baptism of Christ was so beautiful that his master gave up painting."],
["The Mona Lisa","The Mona Lisa is a portrait of Lisa Gherardini, wife of a Florentine merchant. Leonardo used sfumato, soft blending of tones without hard outlines, which gives her face its mysterious smile. He kept working on it for years. It was stolen from the Louvre in 1911 and recovered two years later, which made it even more famous."],
["Artist as scientist","Leonardo dissected more than 30 human bodies to draw muscles, bones and organs accurately, and his anatomical drawings were the best of his age. He studied water, birds, plants, rocks and light, believing that an artist must understand nature to paint it."],
["Unfinished works","Leonardo often left projects unfinished. A giant bronze horse for the Duke of Milan was never cast. The Last Supper, painted with an experimental technique on a dry wall, began flaking within his lifetime and has been restored many times."]],
k:[["Born","15 April 1452, Anchiano, near Vinci"],["Died","2 May 1519, Amboise, France"],["Famous paintings","Mona Lisa, The Last Supper"],["Famous drawing","Vitruvian Man"],["Notebook pages surviving","About 7,000"]],
d:[["1452","Born near Vinci"],["c. 1466","Apprenticed to Verrocchio"],["1482","Moves to Milan"],["1498","Completes The Last Supper"],["1503","Begins the Mona Lisa"],["1516","Moves to France"]]},
gothic:{l:"Gothic architecture is a style of building that flourished in Europe from the 1100s to the 1500s. Its soaring cathedrals, with pointed arches, ribbed vaults, flying buttresses and glowing stained-glass windows, were designed to lift the eyes and spirits of worshippers toward heaven.",
s:[["Abbot Suger’s vision","Abbot Suger rebuilt the choir of Saint-Denis in the 1140s, filling it with light from large colored windows. He believed light was a reflection of God. His new combination of pointed arches and ribbed vaults inspired builders across northern France."],
["How it stands up","Earlier Romanesque churches had thick walls and small windows. Pointed arches direct the weight of the roof downward more efficiently, and ribbed vaults concentrate it onto columns. Flying buttresses, stone arms outside the walls, push back against the outward thrust, so the walls can be thin and full of glass."],
["Building a cathedral","Cathedrals took decades or even centuries to build. Master masons led teams of stonecutters, carpenters and glassmakers, working without modern machines. Cologne Cathedral was begun in 1248 and not finished until 1880. Carvings of saints, kings and gargoyles decorated the outside."],
["Gothic Revival","In the 1800s architects revived the Gothic style for churches, universities, railway stations and government buildings, such as the Houses of Parliament in London and the Hungarian Parliament in Budapest."]],
k:[["Period","c. 1140 to c. 1500"],["Began","Saint-Denis, near Paris"],["Key features","Pointed arch, ribbed vault, flying buttress"],["Famous cathedrals","Notre-Dame, Chartres, Cologne, Reims"],["Tallest church","Ulm Minster, Germany, 161.5 m"]],
d:[["1144","Saint-Denis choir consecrated"],["1163","Notre-Dame de Paris begun"],["1194","Chartres rebuilding begins"],["1248","Cologne Cathedral begun"],["1880","Cologne Cathedral completed"],["2024","Notre-Dame reopens after the fire"]]},
jazz:{l:"Jazz is a style of music that began in African American communities in New Orleans at the start of the 1900s. Built on improvisation, swinging rhythms and the blues, it became America’s great musical gift to the world and has influenced almost every kind of popular music since.",
s:[["New Orleans roots","New Orleans was a meeting place of African, Caribbean, French, Spanish and American cultures. Brass bands played at parades and funerals, and in Congo Square enslaved and free Black people had gathered to drum and dance. Cornetists such as Buddy Bolden and King Oliver created the early jazz sound."],
["The Jazz Age","In the 1920s jazz spread to Chicago and New York and became the soundtrack of a lively decade called the Jazz Age. Records and radio carried it everywhere. In Harlem, nightclubs such as the Cotton Club featured Duke Ellington’s orchestra."],
["The swing era","In the 1930s and early 1940s, big bands led by Count Basie, Benny Goodman and Glenn Miller played swing music for dancing. Singers such as Billie Holiday and Ella Fitzgerald became stars. Goodman’s band was one of the first well-known groups to include Black and white musicians together."],
["Jazz around the world","Jazz spread worldwide from the 1920s. In Paris, the guitarist Django Reinhardt created gypsy jazz, and Black American musicians found a warm welcome. Cuban musicians created Afro-Cuban jazz, Brazil gave the world bossa nova, and South African stars such as Hugh Masekela and Abdullah Ibrahim blended jazz with township music. Japan has one of the world’s largest jazz audiences."]],
k:[["Birthplace","New Orleans, USA"],["Began","Early 1900s"],["Key elements","Improvisation, swing, blue notes"],["Famous artists","Louis Armstrong, Duke Ellington, Ella Fitzgerald, Miles Davis"],["Styles","Dixieland, swing, bebop, cool, fusion"]],
d:[["1917","First jazz recording, by the Original Dixieland Jass Band"],["1925","Armstrong’s Hot Five recordings begin"],["1938","Benny Goodman plays Carnegie Hall"],["1945","Bebop emerges with Parker and Gillespie"],["1959","Miles Davis records Kind of Blue"],["2011","UNESCO declares International Jazz Day"]]},
austen:{l:"Jane Austen was an English novelist whose witty, sharply observed stories of love, marriage and money among the country gentry have delighted readers for more than 200 years. Her six completed novels are classics of English literature and remain hugely popular on page and screen.",
s:[["Family life","Jane was the seventh of eight children of George Austen, a clergyman in the village of Steventon in Hampshire. Her family loved reading and staged plays at home. She was especially close to her sister Cassandra. Neither sister married, and Jane spent her last years in a cottage at Chawton."],
["Her six novels","Austen completed Sense and Sensibility, Pride and Prejudice, Mansfield Park, Emma, Northanger Abbey and Persuasion. The last two were published after her death in 1817. Her heroines, such as the lively Elizabeth Bennet, must find happiness in a world where women had few choices beyond marriage."],
["Style","Austen wrote with irony, saying one thing while meaning another, and she let readers hear her characters’ thoughts mixed with the narrator’s voice, a technique called free indirect speech. Her novels are full of sharp dialogue and gentle mockery of snobbery, greed and foolishness."],
["Lasting fame","Austen earned only modest sums from her books. Interest grew after her nephew published a memoir of her in 1869. Today her face appears on the Bank of England’s ten-pound note, and fans called Janeites hold balls and festivals in her honor."]],
k:[["Born","16 December 1775, Steventon, Hampshire"],["Died","18 July 1817, Winchester"],["Novels","6 completed"],["Best known","Pride and Prejudice (1813)"],["Buried","Winchester Cathedral"]],
d:[["1775","Born in Steventon"],["1811","Sense and Sensibility published"],["1813","Pride and Prejudice published"],["1815","Emma published"],["1817","Dies in Winchester"],["2017","Appears on the £10 note"]]},
theatre:{l:"Ancient Greek theatre was born in Athens in the 500s BCE and gave the world tragedy, comedy and many of the basic ideas of drama. Performed at religious festivals in vast open-air theaters, its plays still move and amuse audiences today.",
s:[["The first actor","Tradition says a performer named Thespis was the first to step out from the chorus and speak as a character, around 534 BCE. Actors are still called thespians in his honor. Aeschylus added a second actor, and Sophocles a third, allowing more complex stories."],
["Festivals and contests","At the City Dionysia festival in Athens, playwrights competed for prizes. Each tragic poet presented three tragedies and a comic satyr play. Wealthy citizens paid for the productions as a public duty, and the whole city attended."],
["Tragedy and comedy","Tragedies, such as Sophocles’ Oedipus the King and Euripides’ Medea, showed great figures brought down by fate or their own flaws. Aristotle said tragedy creates catharsis, a release of pity and fear. Comedies, such as Aristophanes’ The Birds, made fun of politicians, philosophers and the gods."],
["The theater building","A Greek theater had a round dancing floor called the orchestra, a stage building called the skene and curved stone seats set into a hillside, the theatron. Even whispers on the stage at Epidaurus can be heard in the back rows. Words such as theater, orchestra and scene come from these Greek terms."]],
k:[["Origin","Athens, 6th century BCE"],["Festival","City Dionysia, honoring Dionysus"],["Tragedians","Aeschylus, Sophocles, Euripides"],["Comic poet","Aristophanes"],["Famous theater","Epidaurus, about 14,000 seats"]],
d:[["c. 534 BCE","Thespis performs at the Dionysia"],["458 BCE","Aeschylus’ Oresteia"],["c. 429 BCE","Sophocles’ Oedipus the King"],["431 BCE","Euripides’ Medea"],["c. 340 BCE","Theater of Epidaurus built"],["c. 335 BCE","Aristotle writes the Poetics"]]},
phlit:{l:"Philippine literature includes the oral epics, poems, novels, stories and plays of the Filipino people, written in more than a dozen languages over many centuries. It reflects the country’s Indigenous roots, its colonial experience and its search for a national identity.",
s:[["Epics and riddles","Before writing was common, communities passed on epics, chants, proverbs called salawikain and riddles called bugtong. Epics such as the Hudhud of the Ifugao and the Darangen of the Maranao could take days to sing. Many tell of heroes with magical powers who protect their people."],
["Spanish period","Most early printed works were religious. Poets later wrote metrical romances called awit and korido, and the pasyon, the story of Christ’s suffering, was sung during Lent. Balagtas, the master of Tagalog poetry, wrote Florante at Laura, which criticized tyranny through a story set in Albania."],
["American period","English became the language of schools, and Filipino writers soon wrote fiction and poetry in English. Paz Márquez Benítez’s Dead Stars of 1925 is often called the first modern Filipino short story in English. At the same time, Tagalog novels and poems flourished in magazines such as Liwayway."],
["Modern voices","After independence, writers such as Nick Joaquin, F. Sionil José, Amado V. Hernandez and Edith Tiempo explored history, poverty and identity. Today Filipino writers in the Philippines and abroad, such as Gina Apostol and Miguel Syjuco, win international prizes, and many young writers publish online."]],
k:[["Languages","Filipino, English, Spanish, regional languages"],["Famous epic","Biag ni Lam-ang"],["Master poet","Francisco Balagtas"],["Famous novels","Noli Me Tángere, El Filibusterismo"],["National honor","Order of National Artists"]],
d:[["1593","Doctrina Christiana printed"],["1838","Florante at Laura published"],["1887","Noli Me Tángere published"],["1922","Liwayway magazine founded"],["1925","Dead Stars published"],["1972","Order of National Artists created"]]}
});

