/* data/deep-health.js */
// Longer, DK-style material for Health and the Body articles (part 1): a lead, extra sections, key facts and key dates.
window.DEEP=window.DEEP||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP,{
heart:{l:"The heart is a hollow, muscular organ in the chest that pumps blood around the body without stopping, from before birth until the end of life. Beating about 100,000 times a day, it sends oxygen and nutrients to every cell through a network of blood vessels that would stretch around the world more than twice.",
s:[["Four chambers","The heart has two upper chambers, the atria, which receive blood, and two lower chambers, the ventricles, which pump it out. The left ventricle has the thickest walls because it pushes blood all around the body. A wall called the septum keeps oxygen-rich and oxygen-poor blood apart."],
["Valves","Four valves act like one-way doors, letting blood flow forward but not back. The familiar lub-dub sound of a heartbeat is made by the valves snapping shut. Doctors listen to these sounds with a stethoscope, invented by René Laennec in 1816."],
["Blood vessels","Arteries have thick, elastic walls to handle high pressure. They branch into tiny capillaries, so thin that red blood cells pass through in single file, where oxygen and nutrients pass into the tissues. Veins carry blood back to the heart, with valves that stop it flowing backward. The heart’s own muscle is fed by the coronary arteries."],
["Heart attacks","A heart attack happens when a coronary artery becomes blocked, often by a fatty build-up called plaque, starving part of the heart muscle of oxygen. Warning signs include chest pain, shortness of breath and pain spreading to the arm or jaw. Quick treatment saves lives, and CPR can keep blood flowing until help arrives."]],
k:[["Size","About the size of a fist"],["Weight","About 250–350 g"],["Chambers","4"],["Resting rate","60–100 beats a minute"],["Blood pumped","About 5 liters a minute at rest"]],
d:[["c. 1550 BCE","Egyptian Ebers Papyrus describes the heart"],["1628","Harvey describes circulation"],["1816","Stethoscope invented"],["1903","Einthoven develops the electrocardiogram"],["1958","First implanted pacemaker"],["1967","First human heart transplant"]]},
lungs:{l:"The lungs are two spongy organs in the chest that let us breathe. With every breath they take in oxygen, which the body needs to release energy from food, and get rid of carbon dioxide, a waste gas. An adult breathes in and out around 20,000 times a day.",
s:[["The airways","Air enters through the nose or mouth, where it is warmed, moistened and filtered by tiny hairs and sticky mucus. It flows down the trachea, or windpipe, which splits into two bronchi, one for each lung. These branch again and again into smaller tubes called bronchioles, like an upside-down tree."],
["Alveoli","At the ends of the bronchioles are about 480 million alveoli, tiny balloon-like sacs wrapped in capillaries. Their walls are only one cell thick, so oxygen passes easily into the blood. Spread out flat, the inner surface of the lungs would cover roughly half a tennis court."],
["Breathing control","Breathing is controlled automatically by the brainstem, which senses the amount of carbon dioxide in the blood. When you exercise, carbon dioxide rises and you breathe faster and deeper. You can also hold or speed your breath on purpose, as singers and swimmers do."],
["Lung diseases","Asthma narrows the airways and makes breathing difficult, and inhalers relax the muscles around them. Pneumonia is an infection that fills alveoli with fluid. Tuberculosis, caused by bacteria, remains a serious problem in many countries, but it can be cured with a full course of medicine."]],
k:[["Number","2 (the left is smaller, making room for the heart)"],["Alveoli","About 480 million"],["Breaths per day","About 20,000"],["Main breathing muscle","Diaphragm"],["Gas taken in","Oxygen"]],
d:[["1660s","Robert Boyle and Robert Hooke show air is needed for life"],["1774","Joseph Priestley isolates oxygen"],["1882","Koch identifies the tuberculosis germ"],["1928","Iron lung invented"],["1964","US report links smoking to lung cancer"],["1990s","Inhaled steroids become standard for asthma"]]},
digest:{l:"Digestion is the process by which the body breaks food down into substances small enough to be absorbed into the blood and used for energy, growth and repair. It takes place along the digestive tract, a long tube running from the mouth to the anus, with help from organs such as the liver and pancreas.",
s:[["Mouth and esophagus","Teeth cut and grind food, and saliva moistens it and starts breaking down starch. The tongue shapes food into a soft ball and pushes it to the back of the throat. Muscles in the esophagus squeeze it down to the stomach in waves, a movement called peristalsis, which works even if you are upside down."],
["The stomach","The stomach is a stretchy, muscular bag that churns food with gastric juice containing strong hydrochloric acid and the enzyme pepsin. A layer of mucus stops it from digesting itself. After a few hours food becomes a thick liquid called chyme and passes into the small intestine."],
["Liver and pancreas","The liver, the body’s largest internal organ, makes bile, which breaks fat into tiny droplets. Bile is stored in the gallbladder. The pancreas sends digestive enzymes into the small intestine and also releases insulin into the blood. The liver also stores nutrients and removes toxins."],
["The small intestine’s lining","The inner wall of the small intestine is covered with millions of tiny finger-like villi, which greatly increase its surface area. Nutrients pass through the villi into blood vessels, which carry them to the liver. Whatever cannot be digested, such as fiber, moves on to the large intestine."]],
k:[["Length of digestive tract","About 9 m"],["Small intestine","About 6 m"],["Large intestine","About 1.5 m"],["Largest internal organ","Liver"],["Time to digest a meal","About 24 to 72 hours in total"]],
d:[["1822","A wounded patient lets William Beaumont study the stomach"],["1833","Beaumont publishes his digestion experiments"],["1836","Pepsin discovered by Theodor Schwann"],["1902","Bayliss and Starling discover secretin, the first hormone"],["1982","Bacteria found to cause stomach ulcers"],["2010s","Gut microbiome research expands"]]},
bones:{l:"Bones and muscles work together to hold the body up, protect its organs and let it move. The skeleton is a strong, living framework of bones, and muscles pull on the bones to produce every movement, from blinking and smiling to running and jumping.",
s:[["Living bone","Bones are not dry and dead but living tissue with blood vessels and nerves. A hard outer layer surrounds lighter spongy bone inside. In the middle of many large bones is bone marrow, which makes millions of new blood cells every second."],
["Joints","Joints are places where bones meet. Hinge joints, such as the knee and elbow, bend in one direction. Ball-and-socket joints, such as the hip and shoulder, move in many directions. Smooth cartilage and a slippery fluid let joints move without grinding, and tough bands called ligaments hold them together."],
["Kinds of muscle","There are three types of muscle. Skeletal muscles, attached to bones, move when you decide to move them. Smooth muscle lines organs such as the stomach and blood vessels and works automatically. Cardiac muscle, found only in the heart, never tires."],
["Working in pairs","Muscles can only pull, not push, so they work in pairs. When the biceps in the upper arm contracts, it bends the elbow, and the triceps relaxes. To straighten the arm, the triceps contracts and the biceps relaxes. The largest muscle is the gluteus maximus in the buttocks."]],
k:[["Adult bones","206"],["Muscles","More than 600"],["Longest bone","Femur (thigh bone)"],["Smallest bone","Stapes, in the ear"],["Strongest muscle by force","Masseter (jaw), by some measures"]],
d:[["1543","Vesalius publishes his anatomy book"],["1895","Röntgen discovers X-rays"],["1896","X-rays used to examine broken bones"],["1960s","Modern hip replacement developed by John Charnley"],["1970s","CT scanning introduced"],["1980s","MRI scanning becomes available"]]},
sleep:{l:"Sleep is a natural state of rest in which the body and mind recover. Humans spend about a third of their lives asleep. Far from being a time when nothing happens, sleep is a busy period in which the brain sorts memories, the body repairs itself and important hormones are released.",
s:[["Deep sleep","During deep sleep, brain waves become slow and large, breathing and heart rate slow down and the body is hardest to wake. Growth hormone is released, helping children grow and the body repair muscles and tissues. Deep sleep is greatest early in the night."],
["Dreams","Most vivid dreams happen during REM sleep, when the eyes dart about under closed lids. The brain is very active, but the muscles of the arms and legs are temporarily paralyzed, which stops people acting out their dreams. Scientists still debate exactly why we dream."],
["Sleep through life","Newborn babies sleep 14 to 17 hours a day, school children need 9 to 12 hours, and teenagers 8 to 10. Teenagers’ body clocks shift later, so they naturally feel sleepy later at night. Older adults often sleep more lightly and wake more often."],
["Sleeping well","Good sleep habits include going to bed at the same time each night, keeping the bedroom dark, quiet and cool, avoiding caffeine late in the day and putting away phones and screens before bed, since their light can delay sleepiness. Insomnia and sleep apnea are common sleep disorders that doctors can treat."]],
k:[["Adult need","7 to 9 hours"],["Teen need","8 to 10 hours"],["Cycle length","About 90 minutes"],["Sleep hormone","Melatonin"],["Dream stage","REM sleep"]],
d:[["1929","Hans Berger records brain waves (EEG)"],["1953","REM sleep discovered"],["1960s","Sleep stages classified"],["1965","Obstructive sleep apnea described"],["1998","Hypocretin (orexin) linked to sleep"],["2017","Nobel Prize for body-clock genes"]]},
nutri:{l:"Nutrition is the science of how food nourishes the body. The foods we eat supply energy and the building materials for growth and repair, and a balanced diet helps protect against disease, while poor nutrition, whether too little food or too much of the wrong kinds, harms health.",
s:[["Energy from food","Food energy is measured in calories or kilojoules. Carbohydrates, found in rice, bread and fruit, are the body’s main fuel. Fats store energy and help absorb some vitamins. Proteins, from fish, meat, eggs, beans and tofu, build and repair muscles, skin and organs."],
["Minerals","The body needs minerals in small amounts. Calcium builds bones and teeth, iron carries oxygen in the blood, and iodine is needed by the thyroid gland. Many countries add iodine to salt and fortify flour, rice or cooking oil with iron and vitamins to prevent deficiencies."],
["Food plate guides","Many countries publish simple food guides. The United States uses MyPlate, introduced in 2011, Britain the Eatwell Guide and Japan a spinning top. Most show that a healthy meal should be about half vegetables and fruit, with a smaller share of grains such as rice, bread or noodles and a portion of fish, meat, eggs or beans, along with water to drink."],
["Malnutrition","Malnutrition includes undernutrition, when people do not get enough food or nutrients, and overnutrition, which can lead to obesity. Many countries now face both at once. Stunting, when children are too short for their age because of poor nutrition, affects their health and learning, and school feeding programs aim to reduce it."]],
k:[["Macronutrients","Carbohydrates, proteins, fats"],["Micronutrients","Vitamins and minerals"],["Body’s main fuel","Carbohydrates"],["Food guides","MyPlate (US), Eatwell Guide (UK) and many others"],["World Food Day","16 October"]],
d:[["1747","Lind’s scurvy experiment"],["1897","Eijkman links beriberi to polished rice"],["1912","Casimir Funk coins the word vitamine"],["1945","The UN Food and Agriculture Organization is founded"],["1974","The World Food Conference meets in Rome"],["2016","Chile requires black warning labels on unhealthy packaged foods"]]},
eye:{l:"The eye is the organ of sight. Working like a living camera, it gathers light and focuses it into an image, which the brain then interprets. Vision is the sense humans rely on most, and the eye is one of the most complex organs in the body.",
s:[["Parts of the eye","The white of the eye, the sclera, protects it. At the front, the clear cornea bends incoming light. The colored iris controls the size of the pupil, the black hole in its center, making it smaller in bright light and larger in dim light. Behind the pupil, the lens changes shape to focus on near or far objects."],
["Upside-down images","The image formed on the retina is upside down and reversed, just like in a camera. The brain flips it so we see the world the right way up. The brain also combines slightly different views from the two eyes to judge depth and distance."],
["The blind spot","Where the optic nerve leaves the eye there are no rods or cones, creating a blind spot in each eye. We do not notice it because the brain fills in the gap using information from the other eye and the surrounding scene."],
["Common problems","In nearsightedness, or myopia, distant objects look blurry, and in farsightedness, near ones do. Both are corrected with glasses, contact lenses or laser surgery. Myopia is becoming more common worldwide, possibly because children spend less time outdoors. Cataracts, a clouding of the lens, are a leading cause of blindness but can be fixed by surgery."]],
k:[["Rods","About 120 million per eye"],["Cones","About 6 million per eye"],["Eyeball size","About 2.4 cm across"],["Color cones","Three kinds: red, green, blue"],["Blinks per minute","About 15 to 20"]],
d:[["c. 1020","Ibn al-Haytham explains vision"],["c. 1286","Eyeglasses invented in Italy"],["1604","Kepler explains how the retina forms images"],["1784","Benjamin Franklin invents bifocals"],["1949","First artificial lens implant"],["1990s","Laser eye surgery spreads"]]},
blood:{l:"Blood is the red fluid that flows through the heart, arteries and veins, carrying oxygen, nutrients, hormones and heat to every part of the body and taking away wastes. It also fights infection and seals wounds, making it essential to life.",
s:[["Plasma","More than half of blood is plasma, a straw-colored liquid that is mostly water. It carries dissolved sugars, salts, proteins, hormones and waste products such as carbon dioxide and urea. Plasma proteins include antibodies that fight infection and clotting factors that help seal wounds."],
["Red cells","Red blood cells are tiny disks, dented in the middle, that are packed with hemoglobin, an iron-rich protein that grabs oxygen in the lungs and releases it in the tissues. There are about 5 million in a single drop of blood. Without enough iron, people develop anemia and feel tired."],
["Clotting","When a blood vessel is cut, platelets rush to the wound and stick together. They trigger a chain of reactions that turns a plasma protein into threads of fibrin, which trap blood cells to form a clot. People with hemophilia lack certain clotting factors and bleed for longer."],
["Matching blood","Blood types include A, B, AB and O, each either Rh positive or negative. A transfusion must match, or the immune system attacks the donated cells. Type O negative can usually be given to anyone in emergencies. Red Cross and Red Crescent societies and hospitals around the world run regular blood drives."]],
k:[["Volume in adults","About 5 liters"],["Main parts","Plasma, red cells, white cells, platelets"],["Oxygen carrier","Hemoglobin"],["Red cell lifespan","About 120 days"],["Universal donor","O negative"]],
d:[["1628","Harvey describes circulation"],["1658","Jan Swammerdam observes red blood cells"],["1901","ABO blood groups discovered"],["1914","Sodium citrate allows blood storage"],["1937","First blood bank opens in Chicago"],["1940","Rh factor discovered"]]},
skin:{l:"The skin is the body’s largest organ, covering about 2 square meters in an adult. It forms a tough, flexible, waterproof barrier that protects the body from germs, injury and the Sun, helps control temperature and is packed with nerves that sense the world around us.",
s:[["Hair and nails","Hair and nails are made of keratin, the same tough protein found in the outer skin. Each hair grows from a follicle in the dermis, and a tiny muscle can make it stand up, causing goosebumps. Fingernails grow about 3 mm a month, faster than toenails."],
["Keeping cool","When the body gets hot, sweat glands release sweat onto the skin, and as it evaporates it carries heat away. Blood vessels near the surface widen to release heat, which is why people flush when hot. In the cold, they narrow to keep warmth in."],
["Skin color","Skin color comes mainly from melanin, a pigment made by cells called melanocytes. Everyone has about the same number of melanocytes, but they make different amounts of melanin. Sunlight increases melanin, producing a tan. Skin color evolved in relation to the strength of sunlight where people’s ancestors lived."],
["Sun protection","Too much ultraviolet light causes sunburn, ages the skin and can lead to skin cancer. Doctors advise using sunscreen, wearing hats and protective clothing and avoiding the strongest midday sun, even in cloudy weather. In the tropics, the Sun’s ultraviolet rays are strong year-round."]],
k:[["Area in adults","About 2 m²"],["Weight","About 4 kg (more with fat layer)"],["Main layers","Epidermis, dermis, subcutaneous fat"],["Color pigment","Melanin"],["Thickest skin","Soles of the feet"]],
d:[["c. 1550 BCE","Egyptian papyrus describes skin treatments"],["1800s","Dermatology becomes a medical specialty"],["1928","First commercial sunscreen sold in the US"],["1962","Franz Greiter introduces the sun protection factor"],["1980s","Link between sunburn and skin cancer widely publicized"],["2000s","Lab-grown skin used for burn victims"]]},
vacc:{l:"Vaccines are medicines that train the immune system to recognize and fight particular germs before they can cause serious illness. Together with clean water and sanitation, vaccination is among the greatest achievements of public health, saving millions of lives every year.",
s:[["How vaccines work","A vaccine contains a harmless piece or weakened form of a germ, or instructions for making one of its proteins. The immune system responds as if it had met the real germ, making antibodies and memory cells. If the real germ arrives later, the body recognizes it and fights it off quickly."],
["Jenner’s discovery","Edward Jenner noticed that milkmaids who caught mild cowpox did not get smallpox. In 1796 he infected a boy, James Phipps, with cowpox and later showed he was protected from smallpox. The word vaccine comes from vacca, Latin for cow."],
["Measles, still a danger","Measles is one of the most contagious diseases known: one infected person can spread it to 12 to 18 others. A vaccine has existed since 1963, and two doses give lasting protection, yet measles still killed more than 100,000 people in 2023, mostly young children in countries where vaccination is low. Outbreaks return wherever fewer than about 95 percent of children are vaccinated."],
["New kinds of vaccine","mRNA vaccines, first widely used against COVID-19 in 2020, give cells instructions to make a harmless virus protein. They can be designed quickly. Katalin Karikó and Drew Weissman won the 2023 Nobel Prize for the research that made them possible. Vaccines are also being developed against cancer and malaria."]],
k:[["First vaccine","Smallpox, Edward Jenner, 1796"],["Word origin","Latin vacca, cow"],["Disease eradicated","Smallpox (1980)"],["Global health body","World Health Organization (1948)"],["Key cells","Antibodies and memory cells"]],
d:[["1796","Jenner’s smallpox vaccine"],["1803","The Balmis Expedition sets out to carry smallpox vaccine around the world"],["1885","Pasteur’s rabies vaccine"],["1955","Salk polio vaccine approved"],["1980","Smallpox declared eradicated"],["2020","First mRNA vaccines authorized"]]},
exercise:{l:"Exercise is physical activity that keeps the body fit and healthy. Whether walking, playing sports, dancing or lifting weights, regular movement strengthens the heart, lungs, muscles and bones, improves mood and helps prevent many diseases.",
s:[["What happens when you exercise","During exercise muscles need more oxygen and energy. The heart beats faster and breathing deepens to deliver it. Blood vessels in working muscles widen. You get warm and sweat to cool down. Over weeks of training, the heart grows stronger and pumps more blood with each beat."],
["Children and teens","Health experts recommend that children and teenagers be active for at least an hour a day, including activities that make them breathe hard and ones that strengthen muscles and bones, such as running, jumping, climbing and sports. Active play also builds coordination and friendships."],
["Warming up and cooling down","A warm-up, such as light jogging and gentle movements, raises the heart rate and loosens muscles before exercise, lowering the risk of injury. Cooling down with slow walking and stretching helps the body return to rest. Drinking water is important, especially in hot weather."],
["Sitting too much","Long hours sitting at desks and screens are linked to health problems even in people who exercise. Experts suggest standing up and moving for a few minutes every half hour or so, walking or cycling instead of riding where possible and taking the stairs."]],
k:[["Adult guideline","150 minutes moderate activity a week"],["Children’s guideline","60 minutes a day"],["Types","Aerobic, strength, flexibility, balance"],["Feel-good chemicals","Endorphins"],["Resting heart rate (fit adults)","Often below 60"]],
d:[["776 BCE","First recorded Olympic Games"],["1811","Friedrich Jahn opens an outdoor gym in Berlin"],["1953","Study links active jobs to less heart disease"],["1968","Aerobics popularized by Kenneth Cooper"],["2010","WHO issues global activity guidelines"],["2020","WHO updates guidelines"]]}
});

;
/* data/deep-health2.js */
// Longer, DK-style material for Health and the Body articles (part 2): a lead, extra sections, key facts and key dates.
window.DEEP=window.DEEP||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP,{
ear:{l:"The ears are the organs of hearing and balance. They collect sound waves from the air, turn them into nerve signals the brain can understand and, deep inside the skull, sense the position and movement of the head so that we can stand, walk and run without falling over.",
s:[["How sound travels","Sound is a vibration that travels through the air as waves. The outer ear funnels these waves down the ear canal to the eardrum, a thin membrane that vibrates. The three tiny middle-ear bones, the hammer, anvil and stirrup, amplify the vibrations and pass them to the inner ear."],
["Inside the cochlea","The cochlea is a snail-shaped tube filled with fluid and lined with about 15,000 hair cells. Vibrations make waves in the fluid that bend the hairs, which send signals along the auditory nerve. Hair cells near the entrance respond to high-pitched sounds and those deep inside to low ones."],
["Pitch and loudness","Pitch depends on frequency, measured in hertz. Young people can hear from about 20 to 20,000 hertz, but the upper limit falls with age. Loudness is measured in decibels. Sounds above about 85 decibels, such as heavy traffic, can harm hearing over time, and sounds above 120 decibels can cause pain."],
["Hearing loss and deafness","Hearing loss can be caused by aging, loud noise, infections or genes. Hearing aids make sounds louder, and cochlear implants, first used in the 1970s and 1980s, send electrical signals directly to the auditory nerve. Many deaf people communicate using sign languages, such as American, British or Japanese Sign Language."]],
k:[["Main parts","Outer, middle and inner ear"],["Smallest bone","Stapes (stirrup), about 3 mm"],["Hearing range","About 20 to 20,000 Hz"],["Hair cells","About 15,000 per cochlea"],["Balance organs","Semicircular canals and vestibule"]],
d:[["1543","Vesalius describes two of the middle-ear bones"],["1561","Fallopius describes the cochlea"],["1851","Corti describes the hearing organ"],["1898","First electric hearing aid"],["1961","Békésy wins the Nobel Prize for hearing research"],["1984","Cochlear implants approved for adults in the US"]]},
teeth:{l:"Teeth are hard structures in the mouth used for biting and chewing food, and they also help us speak clearly and shape our smile. Humans grow two sets of teeth in a lifetime, and with good care, the second, permanent set can last for life.",
s:[["Kinds of teeth","Different teeth do different jobs. Sharp incisors at the front cut and bite. Pointed canines tear food. Premolars and molars at the back, with broad, bumpy surfaces, crush and grind it. The last molars, called wisdom teeth, often appear between ages 17 and 25, and some people never get them."],
["Inside a tooth","Under the hard enamel is dentin, a bony layer that makes up most of the tooth. At the center is the pulp, which holds nerves and blood vessels. The root anchors the tooth in the jawbone and is covered by cementum. Enamel cannot repair itself once it is badly damaged."],
["Gum disease","If plaque is not removed, it hardens into tartar and irritates the gums, causing gingivitis, with red, bleeding gums. Without treatment it can become periodontitis, which damages the bone holding teeth in place and is a leading cause of tooth loss in adults."],
["Dentistry through history","People in ancient times drilled teeth with bows, and the Maya decorated teeth with jade inlays. In the 1700s Pierre Fauchard, called the father of modern dentistry, described fillings and braces. Fluoride toothpaste, introduced widely in the 1950s, greatly reduced tooth decay."]],
k:[["Baby teeth","20"],["Adult teeth","32 (including wisdom teeth)"],["Hardest substance","Enamel"],["Main layers","Enamel, dentin, pulp"],["Brushing","Twice a day with fluoride toothpaste"]],
d:[["c. 7000 BCE","Evidence of tooth drilling in Pakistan"],["1728","Fauchard’s book on dentistry"],["1780","Modern toothbrush mass-produced in England"],["1840","First dental college opens in Baltimore"],["1945","Community water fluoridation begins"],["1955","Fluoride toothpaste marketed widely"]]},
water:{l:"Water is essential to life. Every cell, tissue and organ in the body needs it to work properly. A person can survive for weeks without food but only a few days without water, which is why drinking enough clean water every day is one of the simplest ways to stay healthy.",
s:[["What water does","Water makes up most of the blood, which carries oxygen and nutrients. It cushions the brain and spinal cord, lubricates joints and helps form saliva for digestion. When the body is hot, it releases water as sweat, which cools the skin as it evaporates."],
["Where water goes","An adult loses roughly 2 to 3 liters of water a day through urine, sweat, breath and the gut, and more in hot weather or during exercise. People in hot climates can lose a lot of water through sweating, so it is important to drink regularly."],
["Signs of dehydration","Early signs include thirst, dark yellow urine, a dry mouth, tiredness and headaches. Severe dehydration can cause dizziness, confusion and a fast heartbeat, and it is dangerous, especially for babies and older people. Diarrhea is a major cause of dehydration in children, treated with oral rehydration solution."],
["Clean drinking water","Unsafe water can carry germs that cause diarrhea, cholera and typhoid. Boiling water, filtering it or adding chlorine kills most germs. Around the world about 2 billion people still lack safely managed drinking water at home. The United Nations recognized access to clean water as a human right in 2010."]],
k:[["Body water","About 60% of adult body weight"],["Daily loss","About 2–3 liters"],["Survival without water","About 3 days"],["Water in the brain","About 75%"],["Rehydration treatment","Oral rehydration solution"]],
d:[["1854","John Snow links cholera to a water pump"],["1908","Chlorination of public water begins in the US"],["1960s","Oral rehydration therapy developed"],["1978","The Lancet calls oral rehydration a great medical advance"],["2010","UN declares water a human right"],["2015","Clean water becomes a Sustainable Development Goal"]]},
flu:{l:"Colds and influenza, or flu, are common infections of the nose, throat and lungs caused by viruses. Colds are usually mild, but flu can be serious and even deadly, especially for the very young, the elderly and people with other illnesses, and new flu strains can spread around the world.",
s:[["Cold or flu?","A cold usually starts slowly, with a sore throat, sneezing and a runny nose, and lasts about a week. Flu often strikes suddenly with high fever, chills, aching muscles, headache and great tiredness. Flu is more likely to lead to complications such as pneumonia."],
["How they spread","Cold and flu viruses spread in tiny droplets when infected people cough, sneeze or talk, and by touching surfaces and then the eyes, nose or mouth. In temperate countries flu peaks in winter. In the tropics, flu cases often rise during the rainy season."],
["Treatment","Most people recover with rest, fluids and medicines that ease symptoms such as fever. Antiviral drugs can shorten flu if taken early, but antibiotics do not help because they only kill bacteria. People should see a doctor if they have trouble breathing or symptoms that keep getting worse."],
["Pandemics","When a new flu strain appears that people have no immunity to, it can spread worldwide as a pandemic. Besides 1918, flu pandemics occurred in 1957, 1968 and 2009. Scientists watch birds and pigs closely, because new flu strains often come from animals."]],
k:[["Cold viruses","More than 200 types, mainly rhinoviruses"],["Flu virus types","A, B, C and D"],["Deadliest pandemic","1918 flu, about 50 million deaths"],["Prevention","Yearly flu vaccine, handwashing"],["Antibiotics","Do not work against viruses"]],
d:[["1918","Great influenza pandemic"],["1933","Human flu virus first isolated"],["1945","First flu vaccine approved for civilians"],["1957","Asian flu pandemic"],["1968","Hong Kong flu pandemic"],["2009","H1N1 swine flu pandemic"]]},
kidney:{l:"The kidneys are two bean-shaped organs that clean the blood, removing wastes and extra water to make urine. Working day and night, they filter all of the body’s blood many times each day and keep its water, salts and acids in careful balance.",
s:[["Where they are","The kidneys sit on either side of the spine, just below the ribs, toward the back. Each is about 11 cm long. Urine flows from each kidney down a tube called a ureter to the bladder, which stores it until it is passed out through the urethra."],
["How a nephron works","Each nephron has a tiny ball of capillaries called a glomerulus, which filters water, salts, sugar and waste from the blood. As the fluid travels along a long, looped tube, the body takes back almost all the water and useful substances. What is left, mostly water and urea, becomes urine."],
["More than filters","The kidneys also make hormones. One, erythropoietin, tells the bone marrow to make red blood cells. Another helps control blood pressure. The kidneys also turn vitamin D into its active form, which the body needs for strong bones."],
["Kidney disease worldwide","Chronic kidney disease affects about one in ten adults worldwide and is rising, driven largely by diabetes and high blood pressure. Many people do not know they have it until their kidneys are badly damaged. Millions depend on regular dialysis, and in many poorer countries most people who need it cannot get it."]],
k:[["Number","2"],["Size","About 11 cm long each"],["Nephrons","About 1 million per kidney"],["Blood filtered","About 180 liters a day"],["Urine produced","About 1–2 liters a day"]],
d:[["1842","William Bowman describes the nephron capsule"],["1943","Willem Kolff builds the first artificial kidney"],["1954","First successful kidney transplant"],["1960","Long-term dialysis becomes possible"],["1983","The drug ciclosporin is approved, making transplants far more successful"],["2006","World Kidney Day first held"]]},
hormone:{l:"Hormones are chemical messengers made by glands and released into the bloodstream. Traveling throughout the body, they control growth, energy use, mood, sleep, hunger and how the body responds to stress, acting as a slow but powerful partner to the nervous system.",
s:[["The endocrine system","The glands that make hormones form the endocrine system. The pituitary gland, the size of a pea at the base of the brain, is called the master gland because it controls many others. The thyroid in the neck sets the body’s energy use, the adrenal glands above the kidneys release stress hormones, and the pancreas controls blood sugar."],
["Lock and key","Each hormone fits receptors on particular target cells, like a key in a lock. When a hormone attaches, it triggers changes inside the cell. Feedback loops keep hormone levels balanced: when there is enough of a hormone, the gland that makes it slows down."],
["Fight or flight","When you are frightened, the adrenal glands release adrenaline. Within seconds the heart races, breathing speeds up, the pupils widen and stored sugar is released for energy, preparing the body to fight or run. The stress hormone cortisol helps the body cope with longer-lasting stress."],
["Diabetes","In diabetes, the body cannot control blood sugar properly. In type 1 the pancreas makes little or no insulin, and patients need insulin injections. In type 2, the more common form, the body does not respond well to insulin. Healthy eating, exercise and medicines help manage it. More than 500 million adults worldwide have diabetes."]],
k:[["System","Endocrine system"],["Master gland","Pituitary"],["Blood sugar hormone","Insulin (from the pancreas)"],["Stress hormones","Adrenaline, cortisol"],["Word origin","Greek hormon, to set in motion"]],
d:[["1902","Secretin discovered"],["1905","Ernest Starling coins the word hormone"],["1921","Insulin discovered"],["1922","First patient treated with insulin"],["1950","Nobel Prize for cortisone research"],["1982","First genetically engineered human insulin"]]},
hygiene:{l:"Hygiene is the set of everyday habits, such as washing hands, bathing, cleaning teeth and keeping food and water clean, that help prevent the spread of germs and disease. Simple handwashing with soap is one of the most effective ways to protect health ever discovered.",
s:[["Ignored warnings","Semmelweis noticed that women in a ward run by doctors, who came straight from performing autopsies, died of fever far more often than those in a ward run by midwives. Although handwashing cut deaths dramatically, many doctors rejected his ideas, and he died in 1865 without recognition."],
["Lister and clean surgery","Inspired by Pasteur, the British surgeon Joseph Lister began using carbolic acid to clean wounds, instruments and hands in 1867. Deaths after surgery fell sharply. Antiseptic, and later sterile, methods transformed hospitals."],
["How to wash hands","Wet your hands with clean water and apply soap. Rub palms, backs, between the fingers, the thumbs, fingertips and wrists for at least 20 seconds, about as long as singing Happy Birthday twice. Rinse and dry with a clean towel. Soap works by breaking apart the fatty outer layer of many germs."],
["Food hygiene","Keeping food safe prevents food poisoning. The main rules are to keep hands and kitchen clean, separate raw meat from cooked food, cook food thoroughly and store it at safe temperatures. Restaurants, canteens and street food stalls are inspected by health officials in many countries."]],
k:[["Pioneer","Ignaz Semmelweis, 1847"],["Antiseptic surgery","Joseph Lister, 1867"],["Handwashing time","At least 20 seconds"],["Global Handwashing Day","15 October (since 2008)"],["Word origin","Hygieia, Greek goddess of health"]],
d:[["1847","Semmelweis introduces handwashing"],["1854","John Snow traces cholera"],["1858","London’s Great Stink spurs sewer building"],["1867","Lister’s antiseptic surgery"],["2008","First Global Handwashing Day"],["2020","Handwashing campaigns during COVID-19"]]},
malaria:{l:"Malaria is a serious disease caused by a tiny parasite spread by mosquitoes. It causes fever, chills and sickness and can kill if not treated quickly. Although it has been wiped out in many countries, it remains one of the world’s deadliest diseases, especially for young children in Africa.",
s:[["The parasite’s life cycle","When an infected female Anopheles mosquito bites, Plasmodium parasites enter the blood and travel to the liver, where they multiply. They then burst out and invade red blood cells, multiplying again and destroying the cells. When another mosquito bites, it picks up parasites and carries them to the next person."],
["Symptoms","Malaria usually begins one to four weeks after a bite with fever, shaking chills, sweating, headache and body aches. The fevers may come in cycles as parasites burst from red blood cells. Severe malaria can cause anemia, breathing problems, organ failure and coma."],
["Countries that beat malaria","More than 40 countries and territories have been certified malaria-free by the World Health Organization. They include Sri Lanka in 2016, China and El Salvador in 2021, and Egypt and Cabo Verde in 2024. Bed nets treated with insecticide, indoor spraying and quick testing and treatment were the main tools, along with strong health systems."],
["The fight continues","The name malaria comes from the Italian mala aria, meaning bad air, from the old belief that swamp air caused it. Countries such as China, El Salvador and Sri Lanka have been certified malaria-free. Drug resistance and insecticide resistance remain challenges."]],
k:[["Cause","Plasmodium parasites"],["Spread by","Female Anopheles mosquitoes"],["Deaths per year","About 600,000"],["Main treatment","Artemisinin-based combination therapy"],["Word origin","Italian mala aria, bad air"]],
d:[["1820","Quinine isolated from cinchona bark"],["1880","Laveran sees the parasite in blood"],["1897","Ross shows mosquitoes spread malaria"],["1972","Tu Youyou’s team isolates artemisinin"],["2021","WHO recommends the first malaria vaccine"],["2021","China certified malaria-free"]]},
allergy:{l:"An allergy is a reaction of the immune system to a substance that is normally harmless, such as pollen, dust, pet hair or certain foods. Allergies are very common, ranging from a runny nose and itchy eyes to rare but life-threatening reactions.",
s:[["What triggers allergies","Substances that cause allergies are called allergens. Common ones include pollen from grasses and trees, dust mites, mold, pet dander, insect stings, some medicines and foods such as peanuts, shellfish, eggs, milk and wheat."],
["How the reaction happens","The first time a person meets an allergen, their immune system may make antibodies called IgE against it. Next time, the allergen links to these antibodies on mast cells, which burst open and release histamine and other chemicals. These cause swelling, itching, sneezing and mucus."],
["Kinds of allergic illness","Hay fever, or allergic rhinitis, causes sneezing and a runny nose. Asthma can be triggered by allergies and narrows the airways. Eczema causes itchy, inflamed skin, and hives are raised, itchy bumps. Food allergies can affect the skin, gut and breathing."],
["Testing and treatment","Doctors can test for allergies with skin-prick tests or blood tests. Avoiding the allergen is the main treatment. Antihistamines and nasal sprays ease symptoms, and allergy shots, or immunotherapy, gradually train the immune system to tolerate some allergens. People with severe allergies may carry an epinephrine auto-injector."]],
k:[["Cause","Immune system overreaction"],["Key chemical","Histamine"],["Antibody involved","IgE"],["Emergency treatment","Epinephrine (adrenaline)"],["Common forms","Hay fever, asthma, eczema, food allergy"]],
d:[["1819","John Bostock describes hay fever"],["1906","Clemens von Pirquet coins the word allergy"],["1911","First allergy shots tested"],["1937","First antihistamine developed"],["1967","IgE antibody discovered"],["1987","Epinephrine auto-injector approved for public use"]]},
spine:{l:"The spine, or backbone, is a column of bones running down the middle of the back. It supports the head and body, lets us bend and twist and protects the spinal cord, the main pathway of nerves between the brain and the rest of the body.",
s:[["Regions of the spine","The spine has five regions. Seven cervical vertebrae form the neck, 12 thoracic vertebrae hold the ribs, and five large lumbar vertebrae in the lower back bear the most weight. Below them, five fused vertebrae form the sacrum, and a few tiny ones form the tailbone, or coccyx."],
["Discs and joints","Between the vertebrae are discs with a tough outer ring and a soft, jelly-like center. They act as shock absorbers and allow the spine to bend. Small joints at the back of each vertebra guide movement. Together, the many small movements let the spine curve and twist."],
["The spinal cord","The spinal cord is about 45 cm long and as thick as a finger. It runs through a channel formed by holes in the vertebrae. Pairs of spinal nerves branch out between the vertebrae to every part of the body. Injury to the spinal cord can cause paralysis below the point of damage."],
["Good posture","Good posture means keeping the spine in its natural curves. When sitting, keep your feet flat, your back supported and screens at eye level. When lifting, bend your knees and keep the load close to your body. Heavy school bags should be carried on both shoulders."]],
k:[["Vertebrae","33 (some fused)"],["Regions","Cervical, thoracic, lumbar, sacrum, coccyx"],["Neck vertebrae","7 (same as a giraffe)"],["Spinal cord length","About 45 cm"],["Natural curves","4"]],
d:[["c. 1600 BCE","Egyptian papyrus describes spinal injuries"],["1543","Vesalius illustrates the spine"],["1895","X-rays allow doctors to see the spine"],["1934","Herniated disc linked to sciatica"],["1960s","Spinal surgery techniques advance"],["2010s","Electrical stimulation helps some paralyzed patients walk"]]}
});

