// Longer, DK-style material for Religion and Philosophy articles: a lead, extra sections, key facts and key dates.
window.DEEP=window.DEEP||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP,{
socr:{l:"Socrates, Plato and Aristotle were three philosophers of ancient Athens, linked as teacher and student across three generations. Between them they asked fundamental questions about knowledge, justice, the good life and the nature of reality, and their ideas became the foundation of Western philosophy and science.",
s:[["The gadfly of Athens","Socrates compared himself to a gadfly, a stinging fly that keeps a lazy horse awake, because he challenged people to examine their beliefs. He claimed that his only wisdom was knowing that he knew nothing. At his trial he refused to stop philosophizing and accepted death by drinking hemlock."],
["Plato’s Forms","Plato taught that the world we see is a shadow of a higher world of perfect, unchanging Forms, such as perfect beauty, justice and goodness. In his allegory of the cave, prisoners mistake shadows on a wall for reality until one escapes into the sunlight and sees the truth."],
["The ideal state","In the Republic, Plato described a just city ruled by philosopher-kings, wise rulers trained for years in mathematics and philosophy. He believed rulers should seek the good of the city rather than wealth or power. Critics have argued his ideal state would be too controlling."],
["Aristotle the scientist","Aristotle valued observation. He classified hundreds of animals, studied the stages of a chick developing in its egg and wrote on physics, poetry, politics and ethics. He founded his own school, the Lyceum, and his system of logic was used for about 2,000 years."]],
k:[["Socrates","c. 470–399 BCE"],["Plato","c. 428–348 BCE"],["Aristotle","384–322 BCE"],["Schools","Plato’s Academy; Aristotle’s Lyceum"],["Famous works","Republic (Plato); Ethics, Politics (Aristotle)"]],
d:[["c. 470 BCE","Socrates is born"],["399 BCE","Trial and death of Socrates"],["c. 387 BCE","Plato founds the Academy"],["343 BCE","Aristotle tutors Alexander"],["335 BCE","Aristotle founds the Lyceum"],["529 CE","Emperor Justinian closes the Athens schools"]]},
buddh:{l:"Buddhism is a religion and philosophy based on the teachings of Siddhartha Gautama, known as the Buddha. It teaches that suffering can be overcome through wisdom, ethical living and meditation, and with about 500 million followers it is one of the world’s major religions, especially in East and Southeast Asia.",
s:[["The prince who left home","According to tradition, Siddhartha was a prince who grew up in luxury. Seeing an old man, a sick man, a dead body and a wandering holy man showed him that life involves suffering. He left his palace to seek answers and, after years of searching, reached enlightenment while meditating under a bodhi tree at Bodh Gaya."],
["The Eightfold Path","The path to ending suffering has eight parts: right understanding, intention, speech, action, livelihood, effort, mindfulness and concentration. It is often called the Middle Way, avoiding both indulgence and extreme self-denial. The goal is nirvana, freedom from the cycle of craving and rebirth."],
["Karma and rebirth","Buddhists believe that intentional actions, called karma, shape future experiences, and that beings are reborn in a cycle called samsara. Generosity, kindness and avoiding harm to living things are central values. Many Buddhists are vegetarian."],
["Temples and festivals","Buddhist temples often hold statues of the Buddha, stupas containing relics and prayer halls. Vesak, celebrated in April or May, marks the Buddha’s birth, enlightenment and death. Great Buddhist monuments include Borobudur in Indonesia and the temples of Bagan in Myanmar."]],
k:[["Founder","Siddhartha Gautama, the Buddha"],["Began","Northern India, c. 5th century BCE"],["Followers","About 500 million"],["Main traditions","Theravada, Mahayana, Vajrayana"],["Goal","Nirvana"]],
d:[["c. 5th century BCE","The Buddha teaches in India"],["c. 250 BCE","Emperor Ashoka spreads Buddhism"],["1st century CE","Buddhism reaches China"],["c. 800","Borobudur built in Java"],["1950s","Buddhist ideas spread widely in the West"],["1999","UN recognizes Vesak"]]},
confu:{l:"Confucius was a Chinese teacher and philosopher whose ideas about morality, family, education and government have shaped Chinese and East Asian culture for over two thousand years. His teachings, known as Confucianism, focus on how people should behave toward one another to create a harmonious society.",
s:[["A troubled age","Confucius, whose Chinese name was Kong Qiu, lived during a time when the power of the Zhou kings had faded and rival states fought constantly. He served for a time as an official and traveled from state to state hoping a ruler would put his ideas into practice. Most of his life he spent teaching."],
["Five relationships","Confucius taught that society is built on five relationships: ruler and subject, father and son, husband and wife, older and younger brother, and friend and friend. Each person has duties, and if everyone fulfills them with sincerity, families and the state will be in harmony."],
["Filial piety and ritual","Filial piety, called xiao, means respect and care for one’s parents and ancestors. Li, or ritual propriety, covers good manners and ceremonies that express respect. Confucius taught a version of the golden rule: do not do to others what you would not want done to yourself."],
["Influence across Asia","Confucian ideas spread to Korea, Japan and Vietnam, shaping their governments, schools and family life. Mencius and later thinkers developed his ideas, and Neo-Confucianism, led by Zhu Xi in the 1100s, combined them with ideas from Buddhism and Taoism. Confucius’s birthday is celebrated as Teachers’ Day in Taiwan."]],
k:[["Born","551 BCE, state of Lu (now Shandong, China)"],["Died","479 BCE"],["Chinese name","Kong Qiu (Kongzi, Master Kong)"],["Key text","The Analects"],["Key ideas","Ren (humaneness), li (ritual), xiao (filial piety)"]],
d:[["551 BCE","Confucius is born"],["479 BCE","Confucius dies"],["c. 300 BCE","Mencius develops his ideas"],["136 BCE","Han emperor makes Confucianism the state teaching"],["1190","Zhu Xi’s commentaries on the Four Books"],["1905","Imperial examinations abolished"]]},
relig:{l:"Religions are systems of belief and practice through which people seek meaning, express faith in the sacred and form communities. Across history and around the world, thousands of religious traditions have shaped art, law, morality, festivals and daily life, and most people today identify with a religion.",
s:[["Families of faiths","Religions are often grouped by origin. The Abrahamic faiths, Judaism, Christianity and Islam, trace their roots to the prophet Abraham. The Dharmic faiths of India include Hinduism, Buddhism, Jainism and Sikhism. East Asian traditions include Confucianism, Taoism and Shinto."],
["Indigenous religions","Many peoples follow Indigenous or folk religions tied to their land, ancestors and nature spirits, from the Shinto shrines of Japan to traditional African religions and the beliefs of Native American and Aboriginal Australian peoples. Many of these traditions have survived alongside the world religions."],
["Sacred places","Every faith has places of pilgrimage. Christians visit Jerusalem, Rome and shrines such as Lourdes and Guadalupe. Muslims travel to Mecca, Hindus bathe in the Ganges at Varanasi, Buddhists visit Bodh Gaya and Sikhs worship at the Golden Temple in Amritsar."],
["Religious freedom","Many countries protect the right to practice any religion or none, and many separate religion from government. Interfaith dialogue brings together believers of different faiths to build understanding and peace."]],
k:[["Largest religions","Christianity, Islam, Hinduism, Buddhism"],["Christians","About 2.4 billion"],["Muslims","About 2 billion"],["Unaffiliated","About 1 in 6 people"],["Fastest-growing major religion","Islam"]],
d:[["c. 1500 BCE","Early Vedic texts in India"],["c. 500 BCE","Buddha and Confucius teach"],["c. 30 CE","Jesus’s ministry"],["622","The Hijra marks the start of the Islamic era"],["c. 1500","Guru Nanak founds Sikhism"],["1948","Universal Declaration of Human Rights includes religious freedom"]]},
hindu:{l:"Hinduism is one of the world’s oldest living religions, with roots in the Indian subcontinent going back more than 3,000 years. It is a rich and diverse family of traditions, beliefs and practices, followed by over a billion people and known to many followers as Sanatana Dharma, the eternal way.",
s:[["Brahman and the gods","Many Hindus believe in Brahman, an ultimate reality present in everything. Brahman is worshipped through many gods and goddesses, often seen as different forms of the divine. Three important ones are Brahma the creator, Vishnu the preserver and Shiva the destroyer and transformer. Ganesha, with an elephant’s head, removes obstacles."],
["Worship","Hindu worship, called puja, can take place at home shrines or in temples. Worshippers offer flowers, food, incense and light to images of the gods. Many Hindus practice yoga and meditation. Cows are especially respected, and many Hindus do not eat beef."],
["Avatars and epics","Vishnu is believed to come to Earth in different forms, called avatars, to restore order. Two of the most loved are Rama, hero of the Ramayana, and Krishna, who advises the warrior Arjuna in the Bhagavad Gita. The Ramayana is also retold across Southeast Asia, from Thailand’s Ramakien to the shadow puppet plays of Java."],
["Hindu influence in Southeast Asia","From the early centuries CE, Hindu ideas spread across Southeast Asia through trade. Angkor Wat in Cambodia was built as a temple to Vishnu, and Bali in Indonesia is still mainly Hindu. Many words in Thai, Malay, Indonesian and other languages of the region come from Sanskrit."]],
k:[["Followers","About 1.2 billion"],["Origin","Indian subcontinent"],["Oldest texts","The Vedas"],["Key ideas","Dharma, karma, samsara, moksha"],["Major festivals","Diwali, Holi, Navaratri"]],
d:[["c. 1500 BCE","Rigveda composed"],["c. 800–500 BCE","Upanishads written"],["c. 400 BCE–400 CE","Mahabharata and Ramayana take shape"],["c. 1113–50","Angkor Wat built for Vishnu"],["1893","Swami Vivekananda speaks in Chicago"],["2015","UN declares International Day of Yoga"]]},
islam:{l:"Islam is a monotheistic religion founded on belief in one God, called Allah in Arabic, and the teachings of the prophet Muhammad. Its followers, called Muslims, make up about a quarter of humanity, and Islam has shaped great civilizations from Spain and West Africa to Persia, India and Southeast Asia.",
s:[["The Prophet","Muhammad was born in Mecca around 570 CE. Muslims believe that, from about 610, the angel Gabriel revealed God’s words to him. Facing opposition in Mecca, he and his followers moved to Medina in 622. By his death in 632, much of Arabia had accepted Islam."],
["The Quran","Muslims regard the Quran as the word of God, revealed in Arabic. It has 114 chapters, called surahs. Many Muslims memorize the whole Quran, and those who do are called hafiz. The sayings and actions of Muhammad, called hadith, also guide Muslim life."],
["Prayer and fasting","Muslims pray five times a day facing Mecca, often in a mosque, called to prayer by the adhan. During Ramadan, the ninth month of the Islamic calendar, Muslims fast from dawn to sunset. The end of Ramadan is celebrated with Eid al-Fitr, a public holiday in many countries."],
["Islam around the world","Islam spread from Arabia across the Middle East and North Africa within a century, then to Persia, Central Asia, India and Spain. Traders and missionaries carried it to West and East Africa and across the Indian Ocean to Southeast Asia, reaching the islands of Indonesia, Malaysia and the southern Philippines. Today Indonesia has the largest Muslim population of any country, followed by Pakistan and India."]],
k:[["Followers","About 2 billion"],["Prophet","Muhammad (c. 570–632)"],["Holy book","The Quran"],["Holiest city","Mecca"],["Main branches","Sunni and Shia"]],
d:[["c. 570","Muhammad is born in Mecca"],["610","First revelation (traditional)"],["622","The Hijra to Medina"],["632","Muhammad dies"],["1206","The Delhi Sultanate is founded in India"],["1453","The Ottomans capture Constantinople"]]},
christ:{l:"Christianity is a monotheistic religion based on the life and teachings of Jesus of Nazareth, whom Christians believe to be the Son of God. It is the world’s largest religion, with a history of two thousand years that has deeply shaped world culture.",
s:[["The life of Jesus","Jesus was a Jewish teacher in Roman-ruled Judea who preached about the kingdom of God, love of God and neighbor, and forgiveness. According to the Gospels he performed miracles and gathered twelve apostles. He was crucified by Roman authorities in Jerusalem, and Christians believe he rose from the dead, celebrated at Easter."],
["Beliefs","Most Christians believe in the Trinity, one God in three persons: the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit. Central practices include prayer, reading the Bible and sacraments such as baptism and Holy Communion. Christmas celebrates the birth of Jesus."],
["Spreading the faith","The apostle Paul carried Christianity across the eastern Mediterranean. It became the official religion of the Roman Empire in 380 CE. Missionaries later brought it to Europe, Africa, the Americas and Asia. Today most Christians live in Latin America, Africa and Asia."],
["Christianity around the world","Christianity spread from the Middle East through the Roman Empire, then to Europe, and with European explorers and missionaries to the Americas, Africa and Asia. Today Brazil, Mexico and the Philippines have the largest Catholic populations, the United States the most Protestants, Russia and Ethiopia large Orthodox churches, and Nigeria and the Democratic Republic of the Congo some of the fastest-growing churches."]],
k:[["Followers","About 2.4 billion"],["Founder figure","Jesus of Nazareth"],["Holy book","The Bible"],["Main branches","Catholic, Orthodox, Protestant"],["Largest Catholic population","Brazil"]],
d:[["c. 30","Crucifixion of Jesus"],["313","Edict of Milan"],["380","Official religion of the Roman Empire"],["1054","Great Schism"],["1517","Reformation begins"],["1054","The Great Schism divides the Catholic and Orthodox churches"]]},
judaism:{l:"Judaism is the religion of the Jewish people and one of the world’s oldest monotheistic faiths. Its belief in one God and its sacred texts and ethics became the foundation from which Christianity and Islam also grew, and Jewish communities have kept their traditions alive for more than 3,000 years.",
s:[["Abraham and Moses","Jewish tradition traces its origins to Abraham, who made a covenant, or agreement, with God. Later, Moses led the Israelites out of slavery in Egypt, and at Mount Sinai received the Ten Commandments. These events are told in the Torah, the first five books of the Hebrew Bible."],
["Synagogue and home","Jews pray in synagogues, led by rabbis, who are teachers of Jewish law. Much religious life also happens at home. The Sabbath, from Friday sunset to Saturday nightfall, is a day of rest, prayer and family meals. Many Jews follow dietary laws called kashrut, eating food that is kosher."],
["Festivals","Rosh Hashanah is the Jewish New Year, and Yom Kippur, the Day of Atonement, is the holiest day, marked by fasting and prayer. Hanukkah, the festival of lights, recalls the rededication of the Temple in Jerusalem. Passover recalls the Exodus from Egypt with a ritual meal called the Seder."],
["Persecution and survival","Jewish communities often faced persecution, including expulsions from England in 1290 and Spain in 1492. In the Holocaust, Nazi Germany murdered six million Jews. The State of Israel was founded in 1948."]],
k:[["Followers","About 15 million"],["Holy text","Torah (Hebrew Bible) and Talmud"],["Place of worship","Synagogue"],["Holiest day","Yom Kippur"],["Language","Hebrew"]],
d:[["c. 1000 BCE","Kingdom of David and Solomon (traditional)"],["586 BCE","First Temple destroyed by Babylon"],["70 CE","Second Temple destroyed by Rome"],["c. 500","Babylonian Talmud completed"],["1492","Jews expelled from Spain"],["1948","State of Israel founded"]]},
stoic:{l:"Stoicism is a philosophy founded in ancient Greece that teaches how to live a good life by developing virtue, reason and calm in the face of life’s difficulties. Embraced by slaves and emperors alike, it remains one of the most practical and popular philosophies today.",
s:[["Four virtues","The Stoics named four cardinal virtues: wisdom, courage, justice and self-control. They believed these virtues are the only true good, while things such as wealth, health and reputation are indifferent, preferable perhaps, but not needed for a good life."],
["What we control","Epictetus taught that some things are up to us, such as our judgments, choices and actions, and some are not, such as other people’s opinions, the weather or illness. Peace of mind comes from focusing on the first and accepting the second calmly."],
["Living with nature","Stoics believed the universe is ordered by reason, called the logos, and that humans should live in agreement with nature. They also taught that all people are citizens of one world, an early idea of cosmopolitanism and equality."],
["Stoic exercises","Stoics practiced daily habits, such as reviewing their actions each evening, imagining possible misfortunes to prepare for them and reminding themselves that life is short. Seneca wrote letters full of such advice, and modern readers still use these exercises to manage stress."]],
k:[["Founder","Zeno of Citium, c. 300 BCE"],["Named after","The Stoa Poikile (painted porch), Athens"],["Famous Stoics","Epictetus, Seneca, Marcus Aurelius"],["Cardinal virtues","Wisdom, courage, justice, self-control"],["Famous book","Meditations by Marcus Aurelius"]],
d:[["c. 300 BCE","Zeno teaches at the Stoa"],["c. 230 BCE","Chrysippus systematizes Stoicism"],["c. 65 CE","Seneca’s Letters to Lucilius"],["c. 108 CE","Epictetus’ teachings recorded"],["c. 170–180","Marcus Aurelius writes the Meditations"],["1950s–60s","Stoic ideas inspire cognitive therapy"]]},
tao:{l:"Taoism, also spelled Daoism, is a Chinese philosophy and religion that teaches living in harmony with the Tao, the natural way of the universe. Emphasizing simplicity, balance and spontaneity, it has influenced Chinese thought, art and medicine for more than two thousand years.",
s:[["Laozi","Little is known for certain about Laozi, whose name means old master. According to legend, he was a record keeper at the Zhou court who, disappointed with society, rode west on a water buffalo. At the border a guard asked him to write down his wisdom, and the result was the short Tao Te Ching, about 5,000 characters long."],
["Yin and yang","Yin and yang are two opposite but connected forces, such as dark and light, cold and hot, soft and hard. In the yin-yang symbol each half contains a small dot of the other, showing that each holds the seed of its opposite. Health and harmony come from keeping them in balance."],
["Religious Taoism","From the 2nd century CE, Taoism developed into an organized religion with priests, temples, scriptures and many gods and immortals. Followers sought long life through diet, breathing exercises, meditation and alchemy. Chinese communities around the world visit Taoist temples."],
["Taoism in daily life","Taoist ideas influenced feng shui, the arrangement of buildings and furniture in harmony with natural forces, and practices such as tai chi and qigong. Its love of nature inspired Chinese landscape painting, in which tiny human figures are dwarfed by mountains and water."]],
k:[["Key concept","The Tao, the way"],["Founding figure","Laozi"],["Key texts","Tao Te Ching, Zhuangzi"],["Symbol","Yin and yang"],["Key idea","Wu wei, effortless action"]],
d:[["c. 6th–4th century BCE","Tao Te Ching composed (dates debated)"],["c. 300 BCE","Zhuangzi writes"],["142 CE","Celestial Masters movement founded"],["c. 440","Taoism becomes a state religion under the Northern Wei"],["1400s","Taoist Canon compiled under the Ming"],["2020","UNESCO lists tai chi as intangible heritage"]]}
});

