// Longer, DK-style material for Languages articles: a lead, extra sections, key facts and key dates.
window.DEEP=window.DEEP||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP,{
langevo:{l:"Languages are never fixed. Over generations, the sounds, words and grammar of every language slowly change, and languages split, mix, spread and sometimes die. Linguists study these changes to trace the histories of peoples and to understand how human communication works.",
s:[["How languages split","When groups of speakers become separated, their speech changes in different ways. Over centuries, dialects become separate languages. Latin split into Spanish, French and Italian in this way, and the Austronesian languages spread and diverged as people sailed from Taiwan across the islands of Southeast Asia and beyond."],
["Sound changes","Sounds change in regular patterns. For example, many words that began with p in early Indo-European languages came to begin with f in Germanic languages, so Latin pater became English father. By comparing such patterns, linguists reconstruct ancestor languages that were never written down."],
["Pidgins and creoles","When people without a shared language need to communicate, they may create a simplified pidgin. When children grow up speaking it as a first language, it becomes a full creole. Haitian Creole, Tok Pisin in Papua New Guinea and Chavacano in the Philippines are creoles."],
["Endangered languages","Around 40% of the world’s languages are endangered, many with only a few elderly speakers. When a language dies, knowledge, songs and stories may be lost. Communities around the world are recording elders, teaching children and creating dictionaries to keep their languages alive."]],
k:[["Languages in the world","About 7,000"],["Largest family by speakers","Indo-European"],["Largest family by number of languages","Niger–Congo"],["Most languages","Papua New Guinea, over 800"],["Endangered share","About 40%"]],
d:[["c. 4000 BCE","Proto-Indo-European spoken (estimated)"],["c. 3000 BCE","Austronesian expansion from Taiwan begins"],["1786","William Jones notes links between Sanskrit, Greek and Latin"],["1822","Grimm’s law of sound change"],["1950s","Modern linguistics expands"],["2022","UN Decade of Indigenous Languages begins"]]},
alphabet:{l:"An alphabet is a set of letters, each standing for a sound of a language. Invented more than 3,000 years ago in the Middle East, the alphabet made writing far easier to learn than earlier systems with hundreds of signs, and it spread to become the most widely used way of writing in the world.",
s:[["Before the alphabet","Early writing systems such as Egyptian hieroglyphs and Mesopotamian cuneiform used hundreds of signs for words and syllables, so only trained scribes could write. Around 1800 BCE, workers in Egypt’s Sinai region created a simpler script based on sounds, borrowing pictures such as an ox head for the sound a."],
["From alpha to A","The Phoenicians, a seafaring trading people of Lebanon, spread their alphabet around the Mediterranean. The Greeks borrowed it, and the name alphabet comes from their first two letters, alpha and beta. Our letter A began as a picture of an ox head turned on its side."],
["Baybayin","Many scripts of South and Southeast Asia, descended from India’s Brahmi script, are abugidas: each character stands for a consonant with a built-in vowel, and marks change the vowel. Devanagari for Hindi, Thai, Khmer and the old Baybayin script of the Philippines all work this way."],
["Writing systems today","The Latin alphabet is used by more people than any other script. Cyrillic is used for Russian, Arabic script runs from right to left, and Korea’s Hangul, created in 1443 under King Sejong, is praised as one of the most scientific alphabets. Alphabets vary in size: Hawaiian has 13 letters and Khmer more than 70."]],
k:[["First alphabet","Proto-Sinaitic, c. 1800 BCE"],["Spread by","The Phoenicians"],["Word origin","Greek alpha + beta"],["Most used script","Latin alphabet"],["Shortest alphabet","Rotokas of Papua New Guinea, 12 letters"]],
d:[["c. 1800 BCE","Proto-Sinaitic script"],["c. 1050 BCE","Phoenician alphabet"],["c. 800 BCE","Greek alphabet with vowels"],["c. 700 BCE","Latin alphabet develops"],["1443","Hangul created in Korea"],["1928","Türkiye switches from Arabic script to the Latin alphabet"]]},
english:{l:"English is a West Germanic language that began in England about 1,500 years ago and has become the most widely spoken language on Earth, counting both native and second-language speakers. It is an official language of dozens of countries.",
s:[["Old English","After Roman rule ended in Britain, Angles, Saxons and Jutes from northern Germany and Denmark settled there in the 400s and 500s. Their language, Old English, looked very different from today’s. The epic poem Beowulf was written in it. Viking settlers later added Norse words such as sky, egg and they."],
["A borrowing language","English has borrowed words from more than 350 languages. Norman French gave words for law and government, Latin and Greek gave scientific terms, and trade and empire brought words from around the world, such as shampoo from Hindi, tea from Chinese, safari from Swahili and boondocks from Tagalog."],
["English as a world language","English spread first with the British Empire and later through American influence in business, science, films and the internet. Today about 1.5 billion people speak it, but only about 400 million as a first language. It is the main language of international air travel, science and diplomacy."],
["Many Englishes","English varies around the world. American and British English differ in spelling, such as color and colour, and in words such as elevator and lift. Indian, Nigerian, Singaporean, Australian, Philippine and South African English each have their own accents and vocabulary."]],
k:[["Family","Indo-European, West Germanic"],["Speakers","About 1.5 billion (native and second language)"],["Native speakers","About 380 million"],["Letters","26"],["Official in","More than 50 countries"]],
d:[["c. 450","Anglo-Saxons settle in Britain"],["c. 1000","Beowulf manuscript written"],["1066","Norman Conquest"],["c. 1400","Chaucer’s Canterbury Tales"],["1755","Johnson’s Dictionary"],["1755","Samuel Johnson publishes his Dictionary of the English Language"]]},
spanish:{l:"Spanish, also called Castilian, is a Romance language that began in medieval Spain and spread across the Americas, parts of Africa and Asia. With around 500 million native speakers, it is one of the most widely spoken languages in the world.",
s:[["From Latin to Castilian","As Roman rule faded, the Latin spoken in Iberia developed into several languages, including Castilian, Catalan, Galician and Portuguese. Castile’s power made its language dominant. In 1492, Antonio de Nebrija published the first grammar of Spanish, one of the first grammars of any modern European language."],
["Arabic influence","Muslim rulers governed much of Spain from 711 to 1492, and Spanish absorbed thousands of Arabic words, many beginning with al-, such as almohada (pillow), azúcar (sugar) and algodón (cotton). Some of these words later traveled with Spanish around the world."],
["Spanish in the Americas","Spanish arrived in the Americas with Columbus in 1492 and spread through the Spanish Empire. Today about 90 percent of Spanish speakers live in the Americas. Latin American Spanish absorbed words from Indigenous languages, such as chocolate and tomate from Nahuatl, and the United States has more than 40 million Spanish speakers."],
["A global language","Mexico has the most Spanish speakers of any country. Spanish is the second most spoken language in the United States and an official language of the United Nations. The Real Academia Española, founded in 1713, and similar academies in other countries guide its standard form."]],
k:[["Family","Indo-European, Romance"],["Native speakers","About 500 million"],["Country with most speakers","Mexico"],["Official in","About 20 countries"],["Special letter","Ñ"]],
d:[["c. 900s","Earliest written traces of Spanish"],["1492","Nebrija’s grammar published"],["1492","Antonio de Nebrija publishes the first grammar of Spanish"],["1605","Don Quixote published"],["1713","Real Academia Española founded"],["1713","The Royal Spanish Academy is founded"]]},
mandarin:{l:"Mandarin Chinese is the most widely spoken first language in the world and the official language of China and Taiwan. With its tones, characters and thousands of years of written history, it is the language of one of the world’s oldest continuous civilizations.",
s:[["Four tones","In Mandarin, the pitch of a syllable changes its meaning. The syllable ma said with a high level tone means mother, with a rising tone hemp, with a falling-rising tone horse and with a falling tone scold. Learners must master tones to be understood."],
["Characters","Chinese characters began as pictures more than 3,000 years ago, carved on oracle bones. Many combine a part that hints at meaning with a part that hints at sound. A well-educated reader knows several thousand characters. Mainland China uses simplified characters, introduced in the 1950s, while Taiwan and Hong Kong use traditional ones."],
["Grammar","Mandarin grammar is quite different from English. Verbs do not change for tense; instead, words such as le show that an action is complete. There are no plural endings. Measure words are used when counting, much as English says a sheet of paper."],
["Chinese loanwords around the world","Chinese words have travelled far. English took tea, ketchup and kowtow from Chinese languages, Japanese borrowed thousands of characters, and Indonesian, Thai and other Southeast Asian languages contain many words from Hokkien and other southern Chinese languages, brought by traders and settlers over a thousand years."]],
k:[["Native speakers","About 900 million"],["Family","Sino-Tibetan"],["Tones","4 main tones plus a neutral tone"],["Romanization","Pinyin (1958)"],["Writing","Chinese characters (hanzi)"]],
d:[["c. 1200 BCE","Oracle bone inscriptions"],["221 BCE","Qin standardizes the script"],["c. 100","Shuowen Jiezi dictionary"],["1913","Conference on a national pronunciation"],["1956","Simplified characters introduced"],["1958","Pinyin adopted"]]},
latin:{l:"Latin was the language of ancient Rome and, for more than a thousand years after, the international language of learning, law and religion in Europe. Although no longer anyone’s first language, it lives on in the Romance languages, in scientific names and in thousands of English words.",
s:[["Classical Latin","The polished Latin of writers such as Cicero, Virgil and Julius Caesar is called Classical Latin. Ordinary people spoke Vulgar Latin, a more relaxed everyday form that developed into the Romance languages. Latin nouns change their endings to show their role in a sentence, so word order is flexible."],
["The language of learning","After Rome fell, Latin remained the language of the Church, universities and science. Newton’s Principia and Copernicus’s great book were written in Latin. Until the 1900s, many schools required students to learn it, and the Catholic Church still uses it for official documents."],
["Latin in names","Scientists give every species a two-part Latin name, such as Homo sapiens, wise human, or Panthera leo, the lion. Many mottos are in Latin, such as e pluribus unum on US coins and the mottos of many schools and universities."],
["Latin around us","About half of English words come from Latin, directly or through French. Abbreviations such as a.m. (ante meridiem), p.m. (post meridiem), e.g. (exempli gratia) and etc. (et cetera) are Latin. Months such as July and August are named after Julius Caesar and Augustus."]],
k:[["Family","Indo-European, Italic"],["Language of","Ancient Rome"],["Descendants","Spanish, Portuguese, French, Italian, Romanian"],["Official today","Vatican City"],["English words from Latin","About half"]],
d:[["c. 600 BCE","Earliest Latin inscriptions"],["c. 70–19 BCE","Life of Virgil, Rome’s great poet"],["c. 400","Jerome’s Latin Bible, the Vulgate"],["1687","Newton’s Principia in Latin"],["1753","Linnaeus’s Latin species names"],["1962–65","Catholic Mass moves to local languages"]]},
arabic:{l:"Arabic is a Semitic language spoken by more than 400 million people across the Middle East and North Africa. As the language of the Quran, it is also used in worship by Muslims worldwide, and its script is one of the most widely used writing systems on Earth.",
s:[["The Arabic script","The Arabic alphabet has 28 letters, all consonants or long vowels. Short vowels are usually left out but can be shown with small marks. Most letters change shape depending on whether they come at the start, middle or end of a word, and words are joined in flowing lines."],
["A language of science","During the Islamic Golden Age, scholars translated Greek, Persian and Indian works into Arabic and added new discoveries. Many star names, such as Aldebaran and Betelgeuse, and scientific words, such as alcohol, alkali and zenith, come from Arabic."],
["Arabic around the world","Arabic is an official language of more than twenty countries and the language of worship in Islam, so Muslims everywhere study it, including in madrasas, or Islamic schools, from Indonesia to Nigeria. Many languages have borrowed Arabic words, among them Spanish, Persian, Turkish, Swahili, Malay and Hindi. Arabic is also an important language of trade, diplomacy and the economies of the Gulf."],
["Calligraphy styles","Calligraphers developed many styles of Arabic writing, from the angular Kufic, used in early Qurans, to the rounded Naskh used in printing and the elegant Thuluth seen on mosque walls. Calligraphy is considered one of the highest Islamic arts."]],
k:[["Family","Afro-Asiatic, Semitic"],["Speakers","More than 400 million"],["Letters","28"],["Direction","Right to left"],["UN official language","Since 1973"]],
d:[["c. 300s","Early Arabic inscriptions"],["c. 610–632","The Quran is revealed"],["c. 800s","Baghdad’s translation movement"],["1973","Arabic becomes a UN official language"],["2012","World Arabic Language Day first marked (18 December)"],["2021","Arabic calligraphy listed by UNESCO"]]},
signlang:{l:"Sign languages are natural languages that use the hands, face and body instead of sound. Developed by deaf communities around the world, they have their own vocabulary and grammar and can express anything a spoken language can, from everyday chat to poetry and science.",
s:[["Not just gestures","Sign languages are not mime or signed versions of spoken languages. American Sign Language, for example, is unrelated to British Sign Language, even though both countries speak English. Each sign is made of a handshape, location, movement and palm direction, much as spoken words are made of sounds."],
["Fingerspelling","Most sign languages have a manual alphabet for spelling names and words that have no sign. Some, such as American Sign Language, use one hand, while British Sign Language uses two. Auslan, the sign language of Australia, uses a two-handed alphabet like British Sign Language."],
["A new language is born","In the 1970s and 1980s, deaf children brought together in new schools in Nicaragua created their own sign language, Nicaraguan Sign Language. Linguists watched it develop a complete grammar within a generation, offering a rare look at how languages are born."],
["Deaf culture and rights","Many deaf people see themselves as a cultural and language community. The UN Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities, adopted in 2006, recognizes sign languages. Many countries now require sign language interpreters in courts and on television news."]],
k:[["Number of sign languages","About 300"],["Deaf people worldwide","About 70 million"],["First public school for the deaf","Paris, 1760"],["FSL law","Republic Act 11106 (2018)"],["International Day","23 September"]],
d:[["1755","First public school for deaf people in Paris"],["1817","American School for the Deaf founded"],["1880","Milan congress bans signing in many schools"],["1960","Stokoe shows ASL is a full language"],["2006","UN disability rights convention"],["2010","Educators formally reject the 1880 Milan ban on sign language teaching"]]}
});

