// In-depth material (part 1 of the Philippines): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
manila:{s:[
["Life along the Pasig","The Pasig River links Laguna de Bay with Manila Bay and was once the city’s main highway. Barges carried rice, fish and goods, and wealthy families built riverside mansions. In the 1900s factories and crowded settlements polluted it badly, and by the 1990s parts of the river were declared biologically dead. Clean-up campaigns, riverbank parks and a ferry service have tried to bring it back to life, and some fish and birds have returned."],
["The galleon port","For 250 years Manila was one of the world’s great trading cities. Chinese junks brought silk, porcelain and tea to the Parian, the Chinese market district outside the walls, and the goods were loaded onto huge galleons bound for Acapulco. Mexican silver flowed back, much of it ending up in China. This made Manila one of the first truly global cities, connecting Asia, the Americas and Europe in a single trade network."],
["War and destruction","During the Battle of Manila in February and March 1945, American and Filipino forces fought Japanese troops street by street. About 100,000 civilians were killed, many in massacres, and the city was shelled into ruins. Intramuros lost almost all its churches and buildings except San Agustin. Manila was among the most heavily damaged Allied capitals of World War II, after Warsaw, and much of its grand prewar architecture was lost forever."],
["The American city","After 1898 the Americans hired the planner Daniel Burnham, who designed a city of broad avenues, parks and government buildings in the classical style. The Manila Hotel opened in 1912, and the Metropolitan Theater, with its Art Deco designs, opened in 1931. Escolta Street became the fashionable shopping district, with the country’s first department stores, cinemas and office buildings."],
["Districts of the city","The City of Manila is divided into districts, each with its own character. Tondo is the most crowded and the birthplace of Andrés Bonifacio. Quiapo is famous for its church and busy markets, Malate and Ermita for restaurants and nightlife, and Sampaloc for its many universities, earning it the name University Belt. Malacañang Palace, the official residence of the president, stands on the Pasig in San Miguel."],
["Manila Bay","Manila Bay is one of the finest natural harbors in Asia, sheltered by the Bataan Peninsula and guarded by Corregidor Island. Its sunsets are famous, painted by artists and celebrated in songs. The bay has suffered from pollution and reclamation, and a court ordered government agencies to clean it up in 2008. In 2020 a stretch of shoreline was covered with crushed white dolomite rock, a project that drew both praise and criticism."]],
sp:["The Black Nazarene","Every 9 January, millions of devotees fill the streets of Quiapo for the Traslación, a procession of the Black Nazarene, a dark wooden statue of Christ carrying the cross brought from Mexico in 1606. Barefoot men in maroon shirts struggle to touch the image or pull its ropes, believing it brings healing and blessings. The procession can last more than 20 hours and is one of the largest religious events in the world."],
g:[["Intramuros","The old walled city of Spanish Manila"],["Parian","The Chinese market district outside the walls"],["Galleon","A large sailing ship used in the Manila–Acapulco trade"],["Malacañang","The official residence of the President of the Philippines"]],
f:["Manila is one of the most densely populated cities on Earth, with over 40,000 people per square kilometer.","The Manila Metro Rail’s LRT-1, opened in 1984, was the first light rail line in Southeast Asia.","Manila’s Chinatown, Binondo, was founded in 1594, making it older than any Chinatown in the Americas or Europe."]},
spanph:{s:[
["The encomienda","In the early years, the Spanish crown rewarded conquistadors with encomiendas, grants giving them the right to collect tribute from people in an area in return for protecting them and teaching them Christianity. In practice many encomenderos abused their power. Bishop Domingo de Salazar, the first bishop of Manila, protested these abuses, and the system was gradually replaced by direct royal government."],
["Reducción and the town","To make it easier to convert, tax and govern people, the Spanish moved scattered villages into new towns, a policy called reducción, bajo de la campana, under the church bells. Each town, or pueblo, was led by a Filipino official called the gobernadorcillo, assisted by barrio heads called cabezas de barangay. These local leaders, known as the principalía, became a powerful native elite."],
["Galleons and silver","The Manila galleon trade made a few merchants rich but did little for most Filipinos. Building and sailing the galleons required forced labor, and thousands of Filipino men cut timber and served as sailors. Some Filipino sailors settled in Mexico, and Mexican and Filipino influences flowed in both directions, bringing maize, cacao, tomatoes and chili to the islands and Filipino words and coconut wine to Mexico."],
["Tobacco and monopoly","In 1782 Governor-General José Basco y Vargas set up a royal tobacco monopoly. Farmers in northern Luzon were forced to grow tobacco and sell it only to the government at low prices. The monopoly made the colony profitable but caused great hardship and revolts until it was abolished in 1882. Manila’s cigar factories employed thousands of workers, many of them women."],
["The ilustrados","By the late 1800s some Filipino families had grown wealthy from sugar, tobacco and trade. They sent their sons to universities in Manila and Europe, and these educated Filipinos, called ilustrados, absorbed liberal ideas. Living in Madrid, Barcelona and Paris, men such as Rizal, Marcelo H. del Pilar and Graciano López Jaena began to demand reforms and equal rights for Filipinos."],
["Gomburza","In 1872 a mutiny of Filipino soldiers at the arsenal in Cavite was quickly crushed. Spanish authorities used it as an excuse to arrest Filipino priests who had campaigned for the rights of Filipino clergy. Fathers Mariano Gómez, José Burgos and Jacinto Zamora, known together as Gomburza, were executed by garrote. Their deaths shocked Filipinos and inspired the young Rizal, who dedicated El Filibusterismo to them."]],
sp:["The Manila–Acapulco Galleon","For 250 years, one or two great galleons a year crossed the Pacific between Manila and Acapulco. The eastward voyage could take six months, battling storms, scurvy and hunger. Ships such as the Nuestra Señora de la Concepción carried fortunes in silk and porcelain, making them targets for English pirates. The route ended in 1815, after Mexico’s war of independence. UNESCO marks it as a key chapter in world history."],
g:[["Encomienda","A grant of the right to collect tribute from a population"],["Polo y servicio","Forced labor required of Filipino men under Spanish rule"],["Principalía","The native elite who held local offices"],["Ilustrado","An educated Filipino of the late 1800s"]],
f:["Spain ruled the Philippines for 333 years, longer than the United States has existed as a country.","The Philippines was named after King Philip II of Spain, one of the few countries named after a person.","Around 4,000 Spanish loanwords are found in Tagalog, including words for days, months and numbers."]},
japocc:{s:[
["Why Japan invaded","Japan wanted to build an empire in Asia it called the Greater East Asia Co-Prosperity Sphere, gaining oil, rubber and other resources. The Philippines, then an American commonwealth, had US bases that threatened Japan’s plans. Hours after attacking Pearl Harbor, Japanese planes bombed Clark Field in Pampanga, destroying much of the American air force on the ground."],
["Manila, an open city","To spare Manila from destruction, General MacArthur declared it an open city in late December 1941 and withdrew his forces to Bataan. Japanese troops entered Manila on 2 January 1942. President Quezon and Vice President Osmeña were evacuated to Corregidor, then to Australia and the United States, where they formed a government in exile."],
["The guerrilla war","After the surrender, thousands of Filipino soldiers and civilians took to the mountains and formed guerrilla units on Luzon, Panay, Negros, Mindanao and other islands. They ambushed Japanese patrols, protected villagers and radioed intelligence to the Allies. Submarines secretly brought them weapons and supplies. Leaders included Wenceslao Vinzons, who was captured and executed, and Macario Peralta on Panay."],
["The Battle of Leyte Gulf","In October 1944 the largest naval battle in history was fought in the waters around Leyte. The Japanese navy tried to destroy the American landing fleet but was crushed, losing four aircraft carriers and many battleships and cruisers. It was in this battle that Japanese kamikaze pilots, who deliberately crashed their planes into ships, first appeared in organized attacks."],
["Liberation of prisoners","As American forces advanced, raids were launched to free prisoners before they could be killed. In January 1945 American Rangers and Filipino guerrillas rescued more than 500 prisoners from the Cabanatuan camp. In February, paratroopers and guerrillas freed over 2,000 civilian internees at Los Baños. In Manila, troops liberated thousands held at the University of Santo Tomas."],
["Cost and aftermath","More than a million Filipinos are estimated to have died during the war. Cities, bridges, railways and farms were destroyed. War crimes trials held in Manila condemned General Tomoyuki Yamashita and General Masaharu Homma. Japan later paid reparations to the Philippines under a 1956 agreement, and the two countries are now close partners in trade and aid."]],
sp:["Josefa Llanes Escoda","Josefa Llanes Escoda founded the Girl Scouts of the Philippines in 1940. During the occupation she and her husband, Antonio, secretly brought food, medicine and messages to Filipino and American prisoners of war at Camp O’Donnell and in Manila. Both were arrested by the Japanese, and both were executed. She appears on the paper 1,000-peso banknote alongside Vicente Lim and José Abad Santos, heroes of the war."],
g:[["Guerrilla","A fighter in a small, irregular force that uses surprise attacks"],["Open city","A city declared undefended to spare it from attack"],["Kamikaze","A Japanese pilot who crashed his plane into an enemy ship on purpose"],["Reparations","Payments made by a country to make up for war damage"]],
f:["Araw ng Kagitingan, the Day of Valor, on 9 April remembers the fall of Bataan.","The Battle of Leyte Gulf in 1944 is often considered the largest naval battle in history.","Japanese soldier Hiroo Onoda kept fighting on Lubang Island until 1974, not believing the war had ended."]},
edsa:{s:[
["The Marcos years","Ferdinand Marcos was elected president in 1965 and re-elected in 1969, the first president to win a second full term. His government built roads, schools and the Cultural Center of the Philippines, but corruption and debt grew. Protests, called the First Quarter Storm, erupted in 1970. Barred by the constitution from a third term, Marcos declared martial law in September 1972, citing threats from communists and violence."],
["Life under martial law","Under martial law, curfews were imposed, the media came under government control and critics such as journalists, students and opposition politicians were jailed. The military gained great power. A 1973 constitution let Marcos rule by decree. Economic growth slowed in the 1980s, and by 1984 the Philippines faced a debt crisis, high inflation and unemployment."],
["The quick count","During the snap election, the citizens’ group NAMFREL organized volunteers to guard ballot boxes and conduct a quick count. When the official tally by the election commission diverged, 35 computer workers walked out of the counting center on 9 February. The walkout showed the world that the results were being manipulated and helped turn public opinion against the government."],
["Radio Veritas and the crowds","Radio Veritas, a Catholic station, broadcast calls for people to go to EDSA and updates on the standoff. When its transmitter was damaged, broadcaster June Keithley continued on another station in secret. Ordinary people brought food, water and flowers to soldiers. Crowds estimated at up to two million filled the avenue between Camp Aguinaldo and Camp Crame."],
["The final day","On 25 February 1986 two inaugurations took place: Corazon Aquino was sworn in at Club Filipino, and Marcos took his oath at Malacañang. That night, as crowds approached the palace, Marcos and his family were flown by US helicopters to Clark Air Base and then to Hawaii. Crowds entered the palace, where they later found Imelda Marcos’s famous collection of shoes."],
["Lessons and memory","The People Power Monument and the EDSA Shrine, a church at the corner of EDSA and Ortigas Avenue, commemorate the revolution, and 25 February is a holiday. The Presidential Commission on Good Government was created to recover the Marcos wealth. Historians and citizens continue to debate the legacy of the Marcos years and the promises of EDSA."]],
sp:["Corazon Aquino","Corazon Cojuangco Aquino had never held office before 1986 and described herself as a plain housewife. As president, she restored democracy, freed political prisoners and oversaw a new constitution. She survived several coup attempts by military rebels. In 1986 Time magazine named her Woman of the Year. After stepping down in 1992, she remained a symbol of democracy until her death in 2009."],
g:[["Martial law","Rule by the military, with ordinary laws and rights suspended"],["Snap election","An election called earlier than scheduled"],["Laban","Filipino for fight; the opposition’s rallying cry and hand sign"],["Coup","A sudden, illegal seizure of power, often by the military"]],
f:["The EDSA Revolution is often called the first People Power revolution, inspiring movements in Eastern Europe in 1989.","EDSA is about 24 km long and passes through six cities of Metro Manila.","Yellow became the color of the opposition after supporters tied yellow ribbons to welcome Ninoy Aquino home."]},
phlang:{s:[
["Words from many places","Philippine languages have absorbed words from many cultures. Sanskrit, through Malay, gave words such as guro (teacher), bathala and diwa (spirit). Chinese Hokkien gave pansit, susi (key) and kuya. Arabic gave salamat. Spanish gave thousands, and English continues to add words, sometimes respelled, such as dyip for jeep and kompyuter."],
["Focus and verbs","Philippine languages are known for their verb system. A Tagalog verb can take different forms depending on whether the focus is on the doer, the object or the place. Bumili ang bata ng tinapay means the child bought bread, focusing on the child, while binili ng bata ang tinapay focuses on the bread. Verbs also change to show whether an action has begun or been completed."],
["Respect in language","Tagalog speakers show respect using the words po and opo, especially when talking to elders or strangers. Plural pronouns, such as kayo and sila, are also used to address one person politely. Children greet elders with mano po, pressing the elder’s hand to their forehead. Other Philippine languages have their own ways of showing respect."],
["Baybayin today","Baybayin nearly disappeared after the Spanish era, but it is enjoying a revival. Artists use it in logos, tattoos and street art, and the script appears on Philippine banknotes and the seal of some government agencies. In Mindoro and Palawan, the Hanunó’o, Buhid and Tagbanwa peoples still write in related scripts, including on bamboo."],
["Sign and Indigenous languages","Filipino Sign Language is used by the deaf community and was recognized by law in 2018. Many smaller languages are endangered as young people shift to Filipino, English or larger regional languages. Ayta and Agta languages spoken by small groups of Negrito peoples are among the most threatened, and linguists and communities are working to record them."],
["Philippine languages abroad","With millions of Filipinos living overseas, Tagalog is one of the most spoken languages in the United States, Canada and parts of the Middle East. Tagalog words have also entered English, such as boondocks and yo-yo, which came from the Philippines. Many universities abroad teach Filipino."]],
sp:["The Laguna Copperplate Inscription","In 1989 a worker digging sand near the mouth of the Lumbang River in Laguna found a thin, crumpled copper sheet. When scholars read it, they discovered it was a legal document dated 900 CE, recording the cancellation of a debt. Written in Kawi script in a mix of Old Malay, Old Javanese and Old Tagalog, it is the oldest known written document from the Philippines and is now kept at the National Museum."],
g:[["Austronesian","The language family that includes most Philippine languages"],["Creole","A language that develops from a mix of languages and becomes a mother tongue"],["Abugida","A script in which each sign stands for a consonant plus a vowel"],["Loanword","A word borrowed from another language"]],
f:["Kumusta, the Filipino greeting, comes from the Spanish ¿cómo está?","Tagalog has no grammatical gender; siya means both he and she.","The Philippines celebrates Buwan ng Wika, the national language month, every August."]},
phbio:{s:[
["The Philippine eagle","The Philippine eagle is one of the largest and most powerful eagles in the world, with a wingspan of about 2 m. A pair raises only one chick every two years, and the young stay with their parents for well over a year. They hunt flying lemurs, monkeys and large birds. Killing one is punishable by up to 12 years in prison, and the Philippine Eagle Foundation in Davao has bred them in captivity."],
["The tarsier","The Philippine tarsier is a tiny primate that fits in the palm of a hand. Each of its huge eyes is about as large as its brain. It can turn its head almost all the way around and leap several meters between branches to catch insects. Tarsiers are shy and easily stressed by noise and handling, so sanctuaries in Bohol protect them in their natural forest."],
["The tamaraw and other mammals","The tamaraw, a small, wild buffalo, lives only on the island of Mindoro, with only a few hundred remaining. Other unique mammals include the Philippine warty pig, the Visayan spotted deer, the Calamian deer of Palawan and many cloud rats, large tree-dwelling rodents. Scientists continue to discover new mammals in the forests of Luzon and Mindanao."],
["Reptiles and amphibians","The Philippines is home to the Philippine crocodile, a small freshwater crocodile that is among the world’s most endangered, and the huge saltwater crocodile. Lolong, a saltwater crocodile captured in Agusan del Sur in 2011, was measured at 6.17 m, the largest crocodile ever held in captivity. There are also many endemic lizards, snakes and frogs."],
["Life in the sea","The Coral Triangle has the greatest variety of marine life on Earth, and the Verde Island Passage, between Luzon and Mindoro, has been called the center of the center of marine shorefish biodiversity. Philippine waters shelter dugongs, five species of sea turtle and many sharks and rays. Tubbataha Reefs is so remote that it can only be visited a few months each year."],
["Saving forests","Forests once covered most of the Philippines, but by the late 1900s logging and farming had reduced them to a small fraction. Forests protect watersheds, prevent landslides and shelter wildlife. Reforestation programs, community forest management and the protection of remaining old-growth forests in places such as the Sierra Madre and Palawan are vital for the country’s future."]],
sp:["The Sierra Madre","The Sierra Madre is the longest mountain range in the Philippines, stretching about 540 km along the eastern side of Luzon. Its forests are home to the Philippine eagle, the golden-crowned flying fox and many plants found nowhere else. The mountains also shield central Luzon from typhoons arriving from the Pacific, weakening them before they reach Manila. The Northern Sierra Madre Natural Park is the country’s largest protected area."],
g:[["Endemic","Found naturally in only one place"],["Megadiverse","One of the countries holding most of Earth’s species"],["Watershed","An area of land that drains into a river or reservoir"],["Critically endangered","Facing an extremely high risk of extinction"]],
f:["The Philippine eagle was once called the monkey-eating eagle, but it was renamed in 1978.","More than half of the Philippines’ reptiles and amphibians are found nowhere else.","Mount Hamiguitan in Davao Oriental has a pygmy forest of trees over 100 years old that stand only about 1.5 m tall."]}
});

