/* data/deep-more-ph2.js */
// In-depth material (part 2 of the Philippines): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
banaue:{s:[
["How the terraces were built","Ifugao builders cut level steps into the mountainside using wooden tools and, later, iron implements. They piled stones or packed clay to form retaining walls, some more than 6 m high. Every terrace is slightly lower than the one above, so water can flow from field to field. The work was done by families and neighbors working together, a custom called bayanihan, in exchange for food and shared labor."],
["Water engineering","The terraces depend on a clever system of canals, bamboo pipes and sluices. Water from forest springs high on the slopes is guided along the mountainside and let into the topmost terrace. When it is full, the water spills down to the next, and so on to the bottom. Farmers open and close small gates to share water fairly, and disputes were settled by village elders."],
["A year of rice","The farming year begins after the harvest, with fields cleaned and walls repaired. Seedlings are grown in nursery plots and transplanted by hand, mostly by women, in the cool months. Farmers weed and watch the water level for months. Harvest comes in the middle of the year, when rice bundles are cut with small knives and carried home to dry in granaries."],
["The Ifugao people","The Ifugao live in the mountains of north-central Luzon. Their society traditionally had ranks based on wealth in rice fields, with the richest families, the kadangyan, holding feasts called hagabi. They are famous woodcarvers, making the bulul, carved figures of rice guardians kept in granaries. Their languages and customs are distinct from those of the lowlands."],
["Resisting conquest","The rugged mountains protected the Cordillera peoples from Spanish conquest for most of the colonial period. The Spanish set up a few military posts but never fully controlled the area. As a result, Ifugao traditions survived largely intact into the 20th century, unlike many lowland customs. American rule brought roads, schools and missionaries to the region."],
["Tourism and the future","Tourism brings income to Banaue and Batad, where visitors hike between terraces and stay in homestays. But too many visitors and new buildings can damage the landscape. Programs pay farmers to keep growing traditional rice, help sell heirloom rice abroad and train young people in terrace repair, so the knowledge is passed on to the next generation."]],
sp:["The bulul","A bulul is a carved wooden figure, usually a seated man or woman with arms crossed on the knees. The Ifugao believe these figures guard the rice harvest and help it multiply. They are made from narra wood and blessed in a ceremony by a mumbaki priest, who bathes them in the blood of a sacrificed pig or chicken. Old bulul are prized by museums and collectors around the world."],
g:[["Terrace","A flat step cut into a slope for farming"],["Mumbaki","An Ifugao ritual priest"],["Muyong","A privately owned forest that protects the water supply"],["Hudhud","Ifugao chanted narratives, recognized by UNESCO"]],
f:["The Banaue terraces appeared on the old Philippine 1,000-peso note.","The Ifugao word for the terraces is payoh.","Ifugao was made a province in 1966."]},
phtyph:{s:[
["Why the Philippines is hit so often","The Philippines faces the western Pacific, the most active area for tropical cyclones on Earth. Storms form over warm water east of the islands and are steered west by winds. Many cross the country before heading toward China, Vietnam or Taiwan. Northern Luzon, Bicol and Eastern Visayas are hit most often, while southern Mindanao is rarely struck."],
["Naming storms","PAGASA names every tropical cyclone that enters the Philippine Area of Responsibility, using four sets of Filipino names that rotate yearly. If a storm is especially deadly or destructive, its name is retired and replaced. Yolanda, Ondoy, Pablo and Sendong are among the retired names. Internationally, storms receive separate names chosen by countries around the Pacific."],
["Super Typhoon Haiyan","Haiyan made landfall in Guiuan, Eastern Samar, on 8 November 2013 with sustained winds estimated at over 300 km/h by some agencies. It swept across the Visayas in one day. A storm surge up to about 6 m high swept into Tacloban City, destroying homes and the airport. More than 4 million people were displaced, and countries around the world sent aid."],
["Building to survive","Traditional bahay kubo houses of bamboo and nipa are light and easy to rebuild, and some peoples, such as the Ivatan of Batanes, build thick stone houses with heavy thatched roofs to withstand strong winds. Modern building codes require strong roofs and anchoring. After Haiyan, a no-build zone was proposed along vulnerable coasts."],
["Before, during and after","Families are urged to prepare a go-bag with water, food, medicine, flashlights and documents, and to know the way to the nearest evacuation center. During a storm, people should stay indoors away from windows and avoid floodwaters, which may carry disease such as leptospirosis. After the storm, they should watch for fallen power lines and damaged buildings."],
["Help and recovery","The National Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council coordinates the national response. Local governments run evacuation centers in schools and gyms. The military, the Red Cross and volunteers deliver relief goods. Recovery can take years, as families rebuild homes and farmers replant coconut trees, which take several years to bear fruit again."]],
sp:["Batanes and the Ivatan","Batanes, the northernmost province, lies in the path of many typhoons. Its people, the Ivatan, have adapted with houses made of limestone and coral walls up to a meter thick, topped by thick roofs of cogon grass that are tied down before storms. They wear the vakul, a headdress of palm fibers that protects against sun and rain. The province’s rolling hills and stone houses make it one of the country’s most beautiful places."],
g:[["Typhoon","A tropical cyclone in the western Pacific with very strong winds"],["Storm surge","A rise of seawater pushed ashore by a storm"],["PAR","Philippine Area of Responsibility, the zone PAGASA monitors"],["Lahar","A flow of volcanic mud, often set off by heavy rain"]],
f:["Typhoon is thought to come from the Chinese tai fung, meaning great wind.","PAGASA stands for Philippine Atmospheric, Geophysical and Astronomical Services Administration.","Typhoon Haiyan’s name was retired, and Yolanda will never be used again in the Philippines."]},
phfood:{s:[
["Adobo","Adobo is meat, usually chicken or pork, simmered in vinegar, soy sauce, garlic, bay leaves and peppercorns. The vinegar helped preserve food in the tropical heat before refrigerators. The Spanish named it after their own adobar marinade, but the cooking method was already Filipino. Every family has its own version, from adobong puti without soy sauce to adobo sa gata with coconut milk."],
["Kakanin and sweets","Kakanin are sweets made from rice, often glutinous, cooked with coconut and sugar. They include suman wrapped in banana leaves, sapin-sapin in colorful layers, puto, bibingka baked in clay pots with charcoal above and below, and puto bumbong, purple and steamed in bamboo tubes, a Christmas favorite eaten after Simbang Gabi."],
["Eating together","Filipino meals are shared. Dishes are placed in the middle of the table for everyone, and guests are urged to eat more. In a boodle fight, a tradition from the military, food is laid on banana leaves along a long table and eaten by hand. Eating with the hands, called kamayan, is still common at home and in some restaurants."],
["Fruits of the islands","The Philippines grows sweet mangoes, especially the carabao mango of Guimaras, as well as bananas, pineapples, papayas and many tropical fruits less known abroad, such as lanzones, rambutan, mangosteen, santol and durian from Davao. Fruits are eaten fresh, dried, pickled as atchara or blended into shakes."],
["Drinks","Popular drinks include fresh buko juice from young coconuts, sago’t gulaman, a sweet drink with tapioca pearls and gelatin, and calamansi juice. Tsokolate made from tablea is enjoyed on rainy days. Traditional alcoholic drinks include tuba, coconut wine, lambanog, a strong coconut liquor from Quezon, and basi, a sugarcane wine from Ilocos."],
["Filipino food abroad","Filipino restaurants have spread across the United States, Canada, the Middle East and Europe, and chefs have won attention for dishes such as sisig, kare-kare and ube desserts. Ube, with its bright purple color, became a worldwide trend in ice cream, cakes and doughnuts. Jollibee opened branches in many countries, often to long lines of Filipinos abroad."]],
sp:["Lechon de Cebu","For a Filipino fiesta, the lechon is the centerpiece. In Cebu the whole pig is stuffed with lemongrass, garlic, onions, salt and pepper, then roasted slowly over charcoal for hours while being turned by hand. The skin becomes thin, golden and crackling, so tasty that no sauce is needed. In Luzon lechon is usually served with a liver sauce. The American chef and television host Anthony Bourdain famously praised Cebu’s lechon as the best pig he had ever eaten."],
g:[["Ulam","Any dish eaten with rice"],["Kakanin","Rice cakes and sweets"],["Merienda","A snack between main meals"],["Kamayan","Eating with the hands"]],
f:["Sinigang is soured with fruits such as tamarind, guava, kamias or green mango.","Ube is a purple yam, different from taro.","Calamansi, a small citrus fruit, is used in Filipino cooking like lemon or lime."]},
rizal:{s:[
["Family and childhood","Rizal’s parents, Francisco Mercado and Teodora Alonso, were prosperous farmers who leased land from Dominican friars. His mother taught him to read and pray. When he was a boy, she was jailed for over two years on false charges, an injustice that marked him deeply. The family changed its surname from Mercado to Rizal partly to protect José after his brother Paciano became linked to the executed Gomburza priests."],
["A brilliant student","Rizal won top prizes at the Ateneo, where he also wrote poems and sculpted. At 18 his poem A la juventud filipina, To the Filipino Youth, won a literary contest, and it is often called the first great poem by a Filipino in Spanish. He studied medicine at the University of Santo Tomas but felt discriminated against by Spanish professors and left to study in Europe."],
["The Noli and the Fili","Noli Me Tángere, Latin for touch me not, tells the story of Crisóstomo Ibarra, a young man who returns from Europe hoping to reform his town but is destroyed by corrupt friars and officials. Its darker sequel, El Filibusterismo, follows Ibarra in disguise as the jeweler Simoun, plotting revenge. Rizal paid for printing the Noli with help from his friend Maximo Viola."],
["Travels and talents","Rizal traveled through Europe, Asia and the United States. He was an eye doctor who operated on his own mother’s eyes, a painter, sculptor, novelist, poet, essayist, historian, fencer and naturalist. He annotated Antonio de Morga’s 1609 history of the Philippines to show that Filipinos had a rich culture before the Spanish arrived."],
["The Dapitan years","During four years of exile in Dapitan, Rizal practiced medicine, farmed, taught local boys and built a relief map of Mindanao in the town plaza. He discovered a frog, a beetle and a lizard that were named after him, such as Draco rizali. He fell in love with Josephine Bracken, an Irish woman from Hong Kong, whom he married shortly before his execution, according to some accounts."],
["Hero of the nation","Rizal’s ideas of education, dignity and peaceful reform inspired the Katipunan, even though he refused to join their uprising. The Americans later promoted him as a national hero. Today his statue stands in nearly every town plaza, the Rizal Monument in Luneta is guarded by sentries, and his image appears on the one-peso coin."]],
sp:["Mi último adiós","On the night before his execution, Rizal wrote a farewell poem of 14 stanzas in Spanish, untitled at the time. He hid it in an alcohol lamp and gave it to his family, telling his sister in English that there was something inside. In it he bids farewell to his beloved country and offers his life gladly for her. The poem was later translated into many languages and is among the best-known works of Filipino literature."],
g:[["Ilustrado","An educated Filipino of the late 1800s"],["Propaganda Movement","A reform campaign by Filipinos in Spain"],["La Liga Filipina","A civic organization founded by Rizal in 1892"],["Filibustero","A Spanish term for a subversive or rebel"]],
f:["Rizal’s pen names included Laong Laan and Dimasalang.","Rizal Day, 30 December, is a national holiday in the Philippines.","Rizal Park in Manila was formerly called Bagumbayan and also known as Luneta."]},
phpower:{s:[
["Early electricity in Manila","Electric lighting first came to Manila in the 1890s. In 1903 the American-owned Manila Electric Railroad and Light Company, later Meralco, won a franchise to supply power and run electric streetcars, called tranvías. By the 1930s Manila had a network of streetcar lines, which were destroyed in World War II and never rebuilt."],
["The age of NAPOCOR","For decades the government-owned National Power Corporation, NAPOCOR, built and ran most of the country’s power plants and transmission lines, including large hydroelectric dams such as Ambuklao, Binga and Angat in Luzon and the Agus and Pulangi plants in Mindanao. Rural electric cooperatives, formed from the 1970s, brought power to the provinces."],
["The power crisis","In the early 1990s, demand grew faster than supply, and Luzon suffered rolling blackouts of up to 12 hours a day, crippling businesses. The government responded by inviting private companies to build power plants quickly, often under contracts that guaranteed their payments. The blackouts ended by 1993, but some contracts left lasting costs for consumers."],
["How the market works","In the wholesale electricity spot market, generators offer to sell power every five minutes, and the lowest-priced offers are dispatched first until demand is met. Prices can spike when plants break down or demand peaks on hot days. Reserves are kept ready for emergencies. The market operator, IEMOP, publishes prices and schedules."],
["Geothermal power","The Philippines sits on the Ring of Fire, with hot rock close to the surface. Geothermal plants drill wells to tap underground steam, which spins turbines. The Tiwi plant in Albay, opened in 1979, and the Makban plant in Laguna were among the first, followed by plants in Leyte, Negros and Mindanao. Geothermal power runs day and night regardless of weather."],
["Electrifying every home","Most Filipino households now have electricity, but some remote villages and islands still lack reliable power. The government’s goal is total electrification. Small solar home systems, mini-grids and new transmission links to islands such as Mindoro and Palawan are part of the plan, along with net metering, which lets homeowners sell rooftop solar power back to the grid."]],
sp:["The Agus–Pulangi hydro complex","Lake Lanao, in Mindanao, drains through the Agus River, which falls steeply on its way to the sea. A chain of hydroelectric plants on the river, together with plants on the Pulangi River in Bukidnon, has long supplied a large share of Mindanao’s electricity. Maria Cristina Falls, on the Agus, powers one of the plants and is called the twin falls. Low rainfall during El Niño can reduce output and cause shortages."],
g:[["Generation","The production of electricity at power plants"],["Transmission","Carrying power over high-voltage lines"],["Distribution","Delivering power to homes and businesses"],["Spot market","A market where power is bought and sold for immediate delivery"]],
f:["Meralco began in 1903 by running electric streetcars in Manila.","Maria Cristina Falls in Iligan powers a hydroelectric plant.","The Philippines is one of the world’s top three producers of geothermal electricity, behind the United States and Indonesia."]},
cebu:{s:[
["Geography of the island","Cebu is about 225 km long but only about 40 km across at its widest. A mountainous spine runs down its middle, with coastal plains where most people live. The province includes more than 160 smaller islands, such as Mactan, Bantayan, Camotes, Malapascua and Olango, an important stopover for migrating birds."],
["Magellan’s Cross","Magellan’s Cross is said to have been planted by the expedition in April 1521 when Humabon and his followers were baptized. Today a cross encased in tindalo wood stands in a small chapel near the Basilica del Santo Niño. Some say the original lies inside the newer cross. Its ceiling shows a painting of the baptism."],
["Fort San Pedro","Fort San Pedro, begun by Legazpi in 1565 and rebuilt in stone in the 1700s, is the oldest and smallest triangular bastion fort in the Philippines. It defended Cebu against pirates and raiders. It has served as a military post, a prison, an American school and a zoo, and it is now a museum and park."],
["The Sinulog festival","Sinulog comes from the Cebuano word sulog, meaning current, describing the forward and backward dance step, like the flow of the Pahina River. Dancers move two steps forward and one back, holding up images of the Santo Niño. The Grand Parade on the third Sunday of January features contingents from across the country in elaborate costumes."],
["Cebuano language and culture","Cebuano, also called Bisaya or Binisaya, is spoken by about 20 million people across Cebu, Bohol, Negros Oriental, Siquijor and much of Mindanao. Cebuanos are proud of their language and culture, and Cebuano music, films and literature are thriving. Cebu also produces guitars, furniture made of rattan and dried mangoes."],
["Mactan and the islands","Mactan Island, linked to mainland Cebu by bridges, holds the international airport, beach resorts and the Mactan Shrine. Bantayan Island is famous for white beaches and dried fish, and Camotes for caves and lakes. Island hopping takes visitors to coral reefs, sandbars and small fishing villages."]],
sp:["Basilica del Santo Niño","The Basilica Minore del Santo Niño was founded in 1565, making it the oldest Roman Catholic church in the Philippines. The present stone church was completed in the 1700s. It houses the small image of the Child Jesus given to Juana, wife of Rajah Humabon, in 1521. Millions of pilgrims visit, and devotees light candles and dance the sinulog outside. The 2013 earthquake damaged its bell tower, which has since been restored."],
g:[["Sugbu","The pre-Spanish name for Cebu"],["Rajah","A ruler or king in pre-colonial Southeast Asia"],["Santo Niño","An image of the Child Jesus"],["Bisaya","The Visayan peoples and their languages, especially Cebuano"]],
f:["Cebu was the capital of the Spanish Philippines from 1565 to 1571.","The Marcelo Fernan Bridge and the Sergio Osmeña Bridge link Mactan to mainland Cebu, with a third bridge opened in 2022.","Cebu’s dried mangoes are among the Philippines’ best-known food exports."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-ph3.js */
// In-depth material (part 3 of the Philippines): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
mindanao:{s:[
["Regions and provinces","Mindanao is divided into six administrative regions: Zamboanga Peninsula, Northern Mindanao, Davao Region, Soccsksargen, Caraga and the Bangsamoro Autonomous Region in Muslim Mindanao. Major cities include Davao, Cagayan de Oro, Zamboanga, General Santos, Iligan and Butuan. About a quarter of all Filipinos live on the island and its neighboring islands."],
["The Lumad peoples","Lumad is a Cebuano word meaning native, used for the many non-Muslim Indigenous groups of Mindanao, such as the Manobo, Bagobo, T’boli, Subanen, Higaonon and B’laan. The T’boli of Lake Sebu are famous for t’nalak, a cloth woven from abaca fibers in patterns said to come to weavers in their dreams. Many Lumad communities struggle to protect their ancestral lands."],
["Lake Lanao and the Maranao","Lake Lanao, the largest lake in Mindanao, is the homeland of the Maranao, the people of the lake. Marawi, on its shore, is the only city in the Philippines with a mostly Muslim population. The Maranao are famous for okir, flowing designs of scrolls and leaves carved into wood and brass, and for the torogan, the great house of a datu."],
["Butuan and early history","Archaeologists in Butuan, Agusan del Norte, have found the remains of balangay, large wooden boats built by sewing planks together, some dated to around 320 CE and others to the 1200s. Gold ornaments, Chinese ceramics and other finds show that Butuan was a wealthy trading kingdom that sent missions to China around 1001."],
["The Battle of Marawi","In 2017 militants linked to the Islamic State seized parts of Marawi City. Government forces fought them for five months, and the battle destroyed much of the city center. About 360,000 people fled their homes. Rebuilding has been slow, and many residents have still been waiting to return to the most damaged area."],
["Mindanao’s riches","Mindanao is called the Land of Promise. It produces most of the country’s bananas and pineapples for export, as well as durian, mangosteen, rubber, coffee, cacao and palm oil. General Santos is a major tuna port. The island also has large forests, rivers that generate hydroelectric power and deposits of gold in places such as Mount Diwata."]],
sp:["Mount Apo","Mount Apo, a potentially active volcano straddling Davao City, Davao del Sur and Cotabato, is the highest mountain in the Philippines. Its name may mean grandfather. It is sacred to several Lumad peoples, and it shelters the Philippine eagle and hundreds of plant species. Climbers pass steaming sulfur vents and boulder fields to reach the summit, where three peaks surround a crater lake. It is a national park and an ASEAN Heritage Park."],
g:[["Moro","A term for the Muslim peoples of the southern Philippines"],["Lumad","Non-Muslim Indigenous peoples of Mindanao"],["Balangay","An ancient plank-built boat found in Butuan"],["Okir","Maranao and Tausug flowing decorative designs"]],
f:["The Rio Grande de Mindanao is the second longest river in the Philippines.","Davao City covers more than 2,400 km², making it one of the largest cities in the world by land area.","The T’boli dream weavers of Lake Sebu weave t’nalak cloth from abaca."]},
palawan:{s:[
["The Underground River","The Puerto Princesa Subterranean River flows for about 8 km through a cave beneath a limestone mountain before emerging into the sea. Visitors paddle in boats through vast chambers with stalactites, stalagmites and thousands of bats and swiftlets. In 2010 explorers found a second level of the cave system. In 2012 the site was named one of the New 7 Wonders of Nature."],
["Tubbataha Reefs","Tubbataha Reefs Natural Park lies in the middle of the Sulu Sea, about 150 km southeast of Puerto Princesa. Its two atolls and a smaller reef have some of the healthiest coral in the world, with hundreds of species of fish and coral, sharks, turtles and manta rays. Rangers live on a small station there year-round to protect it from illegal fishing."],
["Palawan wildlife","Palawan has animals found nowhere else in the country, such as the Palawan bearcat, or binturong, a furry tree-dweller that smells like popcorn; the Palawan peacock-pheasant, with shimmering blue feathers; the Philippine pangolin; and the Calamian deer, found only in the Calamian Islands. The Philippine cockatoo survives in protected areas such as Rasa Island."],
["Puerto Princesa","Puerto Princesa, the capital, is one of the largest cities in the country by area, much of it covered by forest. It is known for its clean streets and environmental programs, including tree planting and bans on illegal fishing. The city is the gateway to the Underground River and Honda Bay, with its island beaches and sandbars."],
["Islands of the north","The Calamianes islands, including Busuanga, Coron and Culion, are known for clear lakes, hot springs and shipwreck diving. Culion was once a leper colony, set up in 1906, and it is now a town with a museum telling its history. Calauit Island has a wildlife sanctuary where giraffes and zebras from Africa were introduced in 1977."],
["Balancing tourism and nature","Tourism has grown rapidly in Palawan, bringing jobs but also pressure. El Nido has limited visitor numbers to some lagoons, and Coron has introduced rules to protect its lakes. Nickel mining in the south has caused concern among conservationists. Palawan’s Strategic Environmental Plan, a special law passed in 1992, aims to manage development."]],
sp:["The Manunggul Jar","The Manunggul Jar is a burial jar found in Manunggul Cave, part of the Tabon Caves complex, in 1964. Its lid shows two figures in a boat: a boatman steering and a passenger with arms crossed on his chest, believed to be a soul journeying to the afterlife. Made around 890 to 710 BCE, it is a National Treasure and appeared on the old 1,000-peso banknote. It is displayed at the National Museum in Manila."],
g:[["Karst","A landscape of limestone shaped by water into cliffs and caves"],["Atoll","A ring-shaped coral reef around a lagoon"],["Biosphere reserve","A UNESCO-recognized area that balances conservation and people’s needs"],["Binturong","The Palawan bearcat"]],
f:["Palawan has been voted the world’s best island several times by readers of travel magazines.","The Underground River was named one of the New 7 Wonders of Nature in 2012.","Palawan has more than 1,700 islands and islets."]},
bohol:{s:[
["Boholanos and their language","Boholanos speak Bol-anon, a form of Cebuano, and are known for their thrift, hard work and strong family ties. Many have moved to Cebu, Mindanao and abroad for work. Bohol has produced presidents, including Carlos P. Garcia, who served from 1957 to 1961 and promoted the Filipino First policy."],
["Old churches","Bohol has some of the oldest stone churches in the country, built by Jesuit and Recollect missionaries using coral stone and egg-white mortar. Baclayon Church dates from the early 1700s. The Loboc Church, with its painted ceilings, and Loon Church, once one of the grandest, were badly damaged in the 2013 earthquake, and Loon’s was later rebuilt."],
["The 2013 earthquake","The magnitude 7.2 earthquake struck on 15 October 2013, a public holiday, which may have saved lives because offices and schools were closed. Over 200 people died. The quake raised the ground along a newly found fault, the North Bohol Fault, creating a long, wall-like scarp that is now preserved as a geological monument."],
["Tarsier sanctuaries","The Philippine Tarsier Sanctuary in Corella protects tarsiers in their forest habitat. Visitors must stay quiet and must not use camera flash, since stress can harm the tiny animals. Some roadside places once kept tarsiers in cages for tourists, but conservationists urge visitors to see them only in proper sanctuaries."],
["Panglao and the sea","Panglao Island, linked to Bohol by bridges, is famous for Alona Beach and nearby dive sites, with walls of coral, turtles and schools of jacks. Pamilacan Island is known for dolphin and whale watching, as former whale hunters now work as guides. Bohol also has the Danajon Bank, one of the rare double barrier reefs in the world."],
["Sweets and crafts","Bohol is known for kalamay, a sticky sweet of coconut milk and sugar sold in coconut shells, and for peanut kisses, cookies shaped like the Chocolate Hills. Antequera is famous for baskets woven from vines and grasses. The Sandugo Festival every July celebrates the blood compact with parades and street dancing."]],
sp:["The Danajon Bank","Off Bohol’s northern coast lies the Danajon Bank, one of only a handful of double barrier reefs in the world, where two parallel lines of reef formed over thousands of years. It stretches about 130 km and supports many fishing communities. Overfishing and destructive fishing damaged it badly, but marine protected areas and community projects are helping fish and corals recover."],
g:[["Sandugo","The 1565 blood compact between Legazpi and Sikatuna"],["Scarp","A steep slope or wall formed by faulting"],["Limestone","A rock formed mainly from the remains of corals and shells"],["Kalamay","A sticky sweet made from coconut milk and sugar"]],
f:["Bohol is the tenth largest island in the Philippines.","The Chocolate Hills were declared the country’s third National Geological Monument in 1988.","Former President Carlos P. Garcia was a Boholano poet as well as a politician."]},
taal:{s:[
["How Taal formed","Taal is part of the Macolod Corridor, a zone of volcanoes in southern Luzon. Over hundreds of thousands of years, enormous explosive eruptions emptied the magma chamber beneath it, and the ground collapsed to form a vast caldera. Water filled the caldera, creating Taal Lake. Volcano Island grew later from smaller eruptions in the middle."],
["Eruptions through history","Taal has erupted more than 30 times since records began in 1572. The 1754 eruption lasted about six months and buried the towns of Taal, Lipa, Tanauan, Sala and Bauan, which were rebuilt on higher ground. The 1911 eruption sent deadly blasts of hot ash and gas across the island and lake shore. The 1965 eruption created a new crater."],
["Base surges","Taal is known for base surges, ground-hugging clouds of hot ash, gas and steam that race outward at high speed during explosions involving water and magma. Scientists studying Taal’s 1965 eruption were among the first to describe them in detail. They are extremely dangerous because they move faster than people can run."],
["Living around the lake","Towns around Taal Lake, such as Talisay, Agoncillo, San Nicolas and Laurel, depend on fishing, farming and tourism. Fish cages in the lake raise tilapia and bangus. Tagaytay, on the ridge above, is a popular cool-weather resort with views of the lake and volcano. Tens of thousands of people live within the volcano’s danger zone."],
["Watching the volcano","PHIVOLCS monitors Taal with seismometers, ground-deformation sensors, gas measurements and cameras. Alert levels run from 0 to 5. Rising sulfur dioxide emissions and volcanic smog, called vog, have sometimes forced schools in nearby towns to close. Residents are taught evacuation routes, and Volcano Island is a permanent danger zone."],
["Heritage of Taal town","The town of Taal, rebuilt after 1754, is famous for its well-preserved ancestral houses and the Basilica of St. Martin of Tours, one of the largest churches in Asia. It is known for balisong, folding knives made in nearby Balisong, for embroidery called burda and for tapang Taal, cured meat."]],
sp:["The 2020 eruption","When Taal erupted on 12 January 2020, residents watched a towering column of ash light up with volcanic lightning. Ashfall blanketed Batangas, Cavite and parts of Metro Manila, and flights were canceled. Fissures opened in towns such as Lemery and Agoncillo, and the main crater lake disappeared. Over 300,000 people were evacuated. The eruption reminded the country how close a dangerous volcano lies to the capital."],
g:[["Caldera","A large basin formed when a volcano collapses"],["Base surge","A fast, ground-hugging cloud of hot ash and gas"],["Vog","Volcanic smog containing sulfur dioxide"],["Fissure","A long crack in the ground"]],
f:["Taal is one of the smallest active volcanoes in the world.","Taal Lake is the third largest lake in the Philippines.","Taal’s Basilica of St. Martin of Tours is about 96 m long."]},
mayon:{s:[
["Eruption history","Mayon has erupted around 50 times since 1616, about once every decade on average. Its eruptions range from lava fountains and slow lava flows to violent explosions and pyroclastic flows. The 1814 eruption was the deadliest, and the 1897 eruption, which lasted about a week, killed hundreds of people. Recent eruptions include 2006, 2009, 2014, 2018 and 2023."],
["Pyroclastic flows and lahars","Mayon’s steep slopes allow pyroclastic flows, avalanches of hot ash and rock, to rush down quickly. In 1993 such flows killed 77 farmers working on the slopes. After eruptions, heavy rains turn loose ash into lahars. In 2006, Typhoon Durian set off lahars from Mayon that buried villages and killed about 1,000 people."],
["Legazpi and Albay","Legazpi City, the capital of Albay, stands at the foot of Mayon and is the main gateway to the Bicol Region. Other towns around the volcano, such as Daraga, Camalig, Guinobatan and Tabaco, have long histories of eruptions and evacuations. Daraga Church, built on a hill in 1773, offers a view of the volcano."],
["Monitoring Mayon","PHIVOLCS runs an observatory on Lignon Hill and monitors Mayon’s earthquakes, swelling, gas and glow. When magma rises, the crater may glow red at night, and rocks tumble down the slopes. Alert levels determine how large the evacuation zone becomes, extending to 8 km on the southeastern side during major unrest."],
["Abaca and pili","The fertile volcanic soil around Mayon supports abaca, a banana relative whose strong fibers are used for rope, paper and textiles, and the pili tree, whose nuts are made into candies. Bicol cooking uses plenty of coconut milk and chili, as in the famous Bicol Express. Visitors can buy pili nut treats and abaca crafts in Legazpi."],
["Climbing and adventure","Climbing Mayon is difficult and allowed only with guides and permits when activity is low. In 2013 a sudden steam explosion killed five climbers near the summit. More visitors enjoy all-terrain-vehicle tours across old lava fields, visits to the Cagsawa ruins and views of the cone from Lignon Hill and the town of Sto. Domingo."]],
sp:["Albay’s zero-casualty approach","Albay province became known worldwide for its disaster planning. Instead of waiting for disaster, officials evacuate people early, using maps of danger zones, prepared evacuation centers and drills in every barangay. Schools teach disaster preparedness, and climate change is part of the curriculum. In several eruptions and typhoons the province recorded no deaths, and the United Nations recognized its work as a model for others."],
g:[["Stratovolcano","A steep volcano built of layers of lava and ash"],["Pyroclastic flow","A fast avalanche of hot gas, ash and rock"],["Abaca","A plant whose strong fiber is used for rope and cloth"],["Pili","A tree whose nuts are a Bicol delicacy"]],
f:["Mayon is often said to have the most perfect cone of any volcano in the world.","Mayon Volcano Natural Park covers about 5,800 hectares.","The Cagsawa ruins appear in countless photographs as Mayon’s most famous foreground."]},
malolos:{s:[
["Why Malolos?","Malolos, in Bulacan, was chosen as the revolutionary capital because it was close to Manila but outside American control, with good roads and rail. Its wealthy families supported the revolution. Twenty women of Malolos had earlier petitioned for the right to study, and Rizal praised them in a famous letter in 1889."],
["The inauguration","On 23 January 1899, bells rang and crowds gathered as Aguinaldo took his oath at Barasoain Church. The Malolos Constitution was proclaimed, and foreign countries were informed of the new republic. However, no country recognized it. The government also set up schools, including the Universidad Literaria de Filipinas, and a newspaper."],
["The government at work","The republic created ministries for foreign affairs, the interior, finance, war and other matters. It issued its own money and stamps and organized an army. Provincial and town governments were elected in many areas. The Philippine Revolutionary Army, led by generals such as Antonio Luna, prepared to defend the republic."],
["Antonio Luna","General Antonio Luna, a scientist and pharmacist who had studied in Europe, was the republic’s most capable military commander. He was strict and hot-tempered and made many enemies. In June 1899 he was killed by soldiers in Cabanatuan, a loss that weakened the republic. His life was told in the popular 2015 film Heneral Luna."],
["The fight continues","After the main army was defeated, Filipino forces turned to guerrilla war. Fighting continued in many provinces until 1902, and some leaders, such as Macario Sakay, fought on until 1906. The war cost the lives of an estimated 200,000 or more Filipino civilians, mainly from famine and disease, along with about 20,000 Filipino and over 4,000 American soldiers."],
["Remembering Malolos","Barasoain Church, the cradle of democracy in the East, appears on the old ten-peso bill and is a national shrine. Several presidents, including Joseph Estrada in 1998, chose it for their inauguration. The Malolos Republic is remembered every 23 January as First Philippine Republic Day."]],
sp:["Gregorio del Pilar and Tirad Pass","On 2 December 1899, 24-year-old General Gregorio del Pilar and about 60 soldiers held the narrow Tirad Pass in Ilocos Sur to delay American troops pursuing Aguinaldo. They fought until nearly all were killed, including del Pilar. Their stand allowed Aguinaldo to escape north. Del Pilar, called the boy general, became a symbol of youthful courage, and his story was told in the 2018 film Goyo."],
g:[["Republic","A state governed by elected representatives, not a monarch"],["Constitution","The basic law that sets up a government and protects rights"],["Congress","An assembly of representatives that makes laws"],["Sovereignty","Supreme authority over a territory"]],
f:["Barasoain Church appeared on the old ten-peso banknote.","Malolos City is the capital of Bulacan province.","First Philippine Republic Day, 23 January, was declared a special working holiday by law in 2018."]}
});

;
