// In-depth material (part 4 of the Philippines): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
independence:{s:[
["The Spanish–American War","In April 1898 the United States went to war with Spain over Cuba. On 1 May Commodore George Dewey destroyed the Spanish fleet in Manila Bay. Dewey helped bring Aguinaldo back from Hong Kong, and Filipino forces quickly took control of much of Luzon, surrounding Manila. Many Filipinos believed the Americans had come as allies in their fight for freedom."],
["The mock battle","On 13 August 1898 Spanish and American commanders staged a mock battle for Manila, arranged so that Spain could surrender to the Americans rather than to the Filipinos. Filipino troops were kept out of the city. In December, under the Treaty of Paris, Spain sold the Philippines to the United States for 20 million dollars, without asking Filipinos."],
["The Philippine–American War","Fighting broke out on 4 February 1899 when an American sentry fired on Filipino soldiers in Manila. The war lasted officially until 1902, with guerrilla resistance continuing for years after. American forces used harsh methods, including burning villages and moving civilians into camps. The Balangiga incident in Samar in 1901 and the brutal reprisals that followed are among its most remembered events."],
["Steps toward self-rule","Americans promised to prepare Filipinos for self-government. The Philippine Assembly, elected in 1907, was led by Sergio Osmeña and Manuel Quezon. The Jones Law of 1916 promised independence once a stable government existed. The Tydings–McDuffie Act of 1934 set a ten-year transition under the Commonwealth, ending on 4 July 1946."],
["Independence at last","On 4 July 1946, at the Luneta in Manila, the American flag was lowered and the Philippine flag raised as Manuel Roxas became the first president of the Republic of the Philippines. The country was devastated by war. Agreements gave the United States military bases and trade privileges, which some Filipinos felt limited true independence."],
["Independence Day today","President Diosdado Macapagal moved Independence Day to 12 June in 1962 to honor the revolutionaries of 1898. Each year, the president leads a flag-raising ceremony, often at Kawit or the Rizal Monument, and towns hold parades, cultural shows and fireworks. Families display flags at home during the national flag days from 28 May to 12 June."]],
sp:["The Aguinaldo Shrine","Emilio Aguinaldo’s family home in Kawit, Cavite, where independence was proclaimed, became a national shrine in 1964. Aguinaldo himself redesigned the house, adding a tall tower and secret passages, and its ceiling is carved with a map of the Philippines. He lived there until his death in 1964 at the age of 94, having seen the country move from Spanish colony to independent republic. The famous balcony is a symbol of Philippine freedom."],
g:[["Independence","Freedom from control by another country"],["Treaty of Paris (1898)","The agreement by which Spain ceded the Philippines to the US"],["Commonwealth","The self-governing transition government of 1935 to 1946"],["Lupang Hinirang","The Philippine national anthem"]],
f:["Emilio Aguinaldo was 29 when he proclaimed independence and lived to be 94.","The Philippine flag is one of the few in the world flown upside down, red on top, in time of war.","The bells of Balangiga, taken by American soldiers in 1901, were returned to the Philippines in 2018."]},
phfest:{s:[
["Sinulog","Sinulog in Cebu City is one of the largest festivals in the country, drawing millions of visitors each January. It begins with a fluvial procession at sea, followed by a solemn procession of the Santo Niño and then the grand parade of dancers, drummers and giant floats. Its dance step imitates the current of a river: two steps forward and one back."],
["Dinagyang","Dinagyang in Iloilo City, held in January, honors the Santo Niño with tribes of dancers who paint their bodies and perform powerful, synchronized routines to thunderous drums. It began in 1968, when a replica of the Santo Niño was brought from Cebu. Dinagyang has won awards as one of the best tourism events in Asia."],
["Pahiyas and harvest festivals","Every 15 May, the town of Lucban in Quezon honors San Isidro Labrador, the patron of farmers. Houses are covered with colorful kiping, leaf-shaped rice wafers, along with fruits, vegetables and handicrafts. Similar harvest festivals take place in other towns of Quezon, and visitors are free to take some of the decorations after the procession."],
["Moriones","During Holy Week on the island of Marinduque, men dress as Roman soldiers wearing carved, painted wooden masks called morion. They roam the streets for days, playing tricks on children. The festival retells the story of Longinus, the Roman soldier who, according to tradition, was cured of blindness by Christ’s blood and converted."],
["Kaamulan and Indigenous festivals","Kaamulan in Malaybalay, Bukidnon, gathers the province’s seven Indigenous tribes, the Bukidnon, Higaonon, Talaandig, Manobo, Matigsalug, Tigwahanon and Umayamnon, for rituals, dances and traditional sports. Similar festivals, such as Lang-ay in Mountain Province and T’nalak in South Cotabato, celebrate local cultures and crafts."],
["The longest Christmas","The Philippines is famous for celebrating the world’s longest Christmas season, starting as early as September. Simbang Gabi, nine dawn masses from 16 to 24 December, ends with Noche Buena, a midnight feast on Christmas Eve. Children go caroling from house to house, and the season ends with the Feast of the Three Kings in early January."]],
sp:["The Giant Lantern Festival","In San Fernando, Pampanga, the Ligligan Parul, or Giant Lantern Festival, is held each December. Barangays compete with lanterns as tall as a three-story building, made of steel frames and thousands of colored lights. Inside each lantern, operators work rotors made of large steel barrels to switch lights on and off in dazzling, changing patterns set to music. San Fernando is called the Christmas Capital of the Philippines."],
g:[["Fiesta","A celebration, usually honoring a town’s patron saint"],["Parol","A star-shaped Christmas lantern"],["Simbang Gabi","Nine days of dawn masses before Christmas"],["Kiping","Colorful rice wafers used to decorate houses at Pahiyas"]],
f:["Nearly every one of the country’s more than 42,000 barangays holds its own fiesta.","Kalibo’s Ati-Atihan is often called the mother of all Philippine festivals.","Noche Buena, the Christmas Eve feast, often includes ham, queso de bola and fruit salad."]},
phfolk:{s:[
["Gods of the old religion","Before Christianity, many Filipino peoples worshipped a supreme being as well as lesser gods and ancestral spirits called anito or diwata. Among the Tagalogs, Bathala was the creator. Visayans honored Kaptan and Magwayen, gods of the sky and the sea. Offerings were made through babaylan, priestesses who led rituals and healing."],
["Biag ni Lam-ang","The Ilocano epic tells of Lam-ang, a hero who could speak at birth and chose his own name. He sets out to avenge his father, who was killed by enemies, defeats them single-handedly and then woos the beautiful Ines Kannoyan. When he is swallowed by a giant fish, his faithful rooster and dog help bring him back to life from his bones."],
["Hinilawod","The Hinilawod, from central Panay, is one of the longest epics in the world. Sung in the Sulod language over several days, it tells of the demigod brothers Labaw Donggon, Humadapnon and Dumalapdap and their adventures to win wives and defeat monsters. Much of it was recorded in the 1950s from elderly chanters by the anthropologist F. Landa Jocano."],
["Legends of places","Many places have legends explaining their names or shapes. The Chocolate Hills are said to be the tears of a heartbroken giant. Mount Makiling is the home of the beautiful goddess Maria Makiling. The legend of the pineapple tells of a lazy girl named Pinang whose mother wished she had a thousand eyes to find things, and she turned into a fruit covered with eyes."],
["The Darangen","The Darangen is the great epic of the Maranao people of Lake Lanao. It has 17 cycles and about 72,000 lines, telling of the kingdom of Bembaran and its heroes, such as Prince Bantugan. It is chanted at weddings and celebrations, often over several nights. UNESCO proclaimed it a Masterpiece of the Oral and Intangible Heritage of Humanity in 2005."],
["Folklore and daily life","Folk beliefs still shape everyday customs. Many Filipinos will not sweep the floor at night, believing it sweeps away luck, or will avoid traveling soon after a funeral. Albularyo, folk healers, use herbs, prayers and massage. Pamahiin, superstitions, are often followed lightly as a mix of tradition and fun."]],
sp:["Maria Makiling","Mount Makiling, a dormant volcano in Laguna, is said to be guarded by Maria Makiling, a beautiful diwata, or forest spirit. In the stories, she helps poor farmers and hunters, gives them gifts of ginger that turn to gold, and punishes those who harm the forest. She withdrew from people after being betrayed. Rizal wrote down one version of her legend. Some say the mountain’s shape looks like a woman lying down."],
g:[["Anito","An ancestral spirit or the image representing it"],["Diwata","A nature spirit or deity"],["Babaylan","A priestess and healer in pre-colonial society"],["Epic","A long narrative poem about heroic deeds"]],
f:["Biag ni Lam-ang is considered the oldest recorded Philippine folk epic.","The Hinilawod has been recorded with about 29,000 verses.","In some folk tales the duwende can bring good or bad luck depending on how they are treated."]},
phecon:{s:[
["The economy since independence","After World War II, the Philippines was one of the more prosperous countries in Asia. But in later decades it fell behind neighbors such as Thailand and Malaysia, hurt by political instability, debt and corruption. Growth picked up strongly from the 2010s, averaging around 6% a year, and the country is aiming to become an upper-middle-income economy."],
["Manufacturing and exports","Electronics, especially semiconductors assembled and tested in economic zones, make up the largest share of Philippine exports. Other exports include machinery, wiring harnesses for cars, coconut oil, bananas, pineapples and copper. Special economic zones run by the Philippine Economic Zone Authority offer tax incentives to attract foreign companies."],
["Tourism","Tourism brings income to places such as Boracay, Palawan, Cebu, Bohol and Siargao, and supports many jobs in hotels, restaurants and transport. Before the pandemic, more than 8 million foreign tourists visited in 2019. The Department of Tourism promoted the slogan It’s More Fun in the Philippines, launched in 2012."],
["Agriculture and fishing","About a fifth to a quarter of Filipino workers are in farming and fishing, though these sectors contribute only around a tenth of the economy. Many farmers are poor, farming small plots. Rice, corn, coconuts, sugar, bananas and pineapples are major crops, and the country is a big exporter of bananas and pineapples. Land reform programs have redistributed millions of hectares."],
["Building the country","The government has invested heavily in infrastructure, such as new expressways, airports, bridges and railways, including the Metro Manila Subway and the North–South Commuter Railway. Better transport aims to reduce traffic, lower costs and spread growth beyond Metro Manila to regions such as Central Luzon, Cebu and Davao."],
["Poverty and opportunity","Poverty has fallen over recent decades, but millions of Filipinos remain poor, especially in rural areas and parts of Mindanao. Programs such as the Pantawid Pamilyang Pilipino Program, 4Ps, give cash grants to poor families who keep their children in school and take them for health checkups. Education, jobs and access to credit are seen as key to further progress."]],
sp:["The Philippine Stock Exchange tower","In 2018 the Philippine Stock Exchange moved to a new tower in Bonifacio Global City, Taguig, bringing together the trading floors once divided between Makati and Ortigas. Bonifacio Global City, built on a former military base, Fort Bonifacio, has grown since the 2000s into a modern business district with offices, shops, parks and the headquarters of many companies, a symbol of the country’s economic rise."],
g:[["GDP","Gross domestic product, the value of everything an economy produces"],["Remittance","Money sent home by workers abroad"],["BPO","Business process outsourcing"],["Economic zone","An area with special incentives for business"]],
f:["Overseas Filipino workers are often called bagong bayani, new heroes.","The Philippines is one of the world’s largest exporters of bananas.","It’s More Fun in the Philippines became a famous tourism slogan in 2012."]},
baguio:{s:[
["Burnham’s plan","In 1904 Daniel Burnham, the famous Chicago architect, drew up a plan for Baguio with parks, a government center and a market, carefully fitted to the hills. The plan included what is now Burnham Park and Session Road. The Mansion, built in 1908 as the summer residence of the American governor-general, is now the official summer residence of the Philippine president."],
["The 1990 earthquake","On 16 July 1990 a magnitude 7.8 earthquake struck central Luzon. In Baguio, hotels and buildings collapsed, including the Hyatt Terraces Plaza, and landslides cut off the city. Over 1,600 people died across Luzon, with hundreds in Baguio. Rescuers, including miners from nearby Benguet, dug through rubble for days. The city rebuilt within a few years."],
["The Panagbenga festival","Panagbenga, from a Kankanaey word meaning season of blooming, began in 1996 to lift spirits after the earthquake. Held through February, it features street dancing in costumes inspired by Cordillera culture and a grand parade of floats covered in thousands of fresh flowers. Session Road is closed to cars for Session in Bloom, filled with stalls and performers."],
["Pines and climate","Baguio’s cool weather comes from its altitude. Temperatures often fall to around 10°C in January and February, and the lowest recorded was 6.3°C in 1961. Benguet pine trees grow on its hills. But rapid growth has crowded the city and cut many trees, and traffic and water shortages are challenges for a city planned for far fewer people."],
["Markets and food","The Baguio City Public Market sells fresh vegetables grown in the highlands of Benguet, such as cabbage, carrots, potatoes and lettuce, along with strawberry jam, peanut brittle, ube jam and woven goods. Nearby La Trinidad is famous for strawberry picking. Baguio is also known for its cafés and the famous Good Shepherd ube jam, sold by nuns."],
["Mines Camp and the mountains","Baguio grew alongside the gold and copper mines of Benguet, one of the country’s major mining areas. Nearby attractions include Mines View Park, with views of old mining towns; the Bell Church, a Chinese-style temple; and Camp John Hay, once an American military base and now a resort and forest park. Hikers explore trails to Mount Santo Tomas and beyond."]],
sp:["The Philippine Military Academy","The Philippine Military Academy, at Fort del Pilar in Baguio, trains cadets to become officers of the army, navy and air force. Founded in 1936 and moved to Baguio, it is the country’s equivalent of West Point. Cadets study for four years under strict discipline. Graduation in March is a major event, often attended by the president. Its alumni include many generals and some national leaders."],
g:[["Hill station","A mountain town developed as a cool retreat"],["Ibaloi","An Indigenous people of Benguet and Baguio"],["Panagbenga","Kankanaey for season of blooming"],["Benguet pine","A pine tree native to the mountains of Luzon"]],
f:["The Mansion in Baguio is the official summer residence of the President of the Philippines.","Mateo Cariño’s land case reached the US Supreme Court, which ruled in his favor in 1909.","Baguio’s Burnham Park has a lake where visitors rent boats."]},
vigan:{s:[
["Calle Crisologo","Calle Crisologo, named after the writer and politician Mena Crisologo, is Vigan’s most famous street. Cobblestones laid in the Spanish era run between rows of tall ancestral houses with wooden balconies and shuttered windows. Cars are banned, and calesas, horse-drawn carriages, clatter along. Many houses now hold shops, cafés and small museums."],
["Churches and plazas","Vigan has two main plazas, Plaza Salcedo and Plaza Burgos. St. Paul’s Metropolitan Cathedral, built in an earthquake baroque style with thick walls and a separate bell tower, stands beside them. The bell tower was built apart from the church so that if it fell in an earthquake it would not damage the cathedral."],
["Burnay jars","Vigan’s burnay pottery tradition dates back to Chinese settlers. Potters shape large, dark earthen jars using clay from the Abra River and fire them in long wood-fired kilns called pugon. The jars are used to ferment and store basi, sugarcane wine, and bagoong, fish paste. Visitors can watch potters at work and try making pots themselves."],
["Abel Iloco","Ilocano weavers create abel, a strong cotton cloth woven on wooden looms in patterns such as binakul, which is said to confuse evil spirits. Abel is made into blankets, towels and clothing. The craft is passed from mother to daughter, and modern designers have revived it for fashion."],
["Elpidio Quirino","Elpidio Quirino, president of the Philippines from 1948 to 1953, was born in Vigan, in a jail where his father worked as a warden. His presidency focused on rebuilding after the war. A museum in Vigan, Syquia Mansion, the home of his wife’s family, displays his belongings and shows how wealthy Vigan families lived."],
["Vigan by night","In the evening, the Plaza Salcedo comes alive with a dancing fountain show set to music and lights. Visitors stroll Calle Crisologo under the glow of lamps, eat empanada and okoy at food stalls, and explore the Baluarte, a nature park on the city outskirts. The Viva Vigan Festival of the Arts in May celebrates the city’s heritage."]],
sp:["The Silang revolt","Diego Silang led a revolt in 1762 while British forces occupied Manila, hoping to end Spanish abuses in Ilocos. He was assassinated in 1763 by a former friend paid by the Spanish. His wife, Gabriela Silang, took command and fought on for months on horseback. She was captured and hanged in Vigan in September 1763. Gabriela is remembered as one of the first women to lead a revolt in the Philippines."],
g:[["Bahay na bato","A house with a stone lower floor and wooden upper floor"],["Calesa","A horse-drawn carriage"],["Burnay","Traditional earthenware jars of Vigan"],["Abel","Handwoven Ilocano cloth"]],
f:["Vigan is one of the few Hispanic towns in the Philippines whose layout survives largely intact.","President Elpidio Quirino was born in Vigan in 1890.","Vigan longganisa is small, garlicky and slightly sour."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-ph5.js */
// In-depth material (part 5 of the Philippines): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
boracay:{s:[
["Getting there","Most visitors fly to Caticlan or Kalibo on Panay, then take a short boat ride across the narrow channel to Boracay’s jetty port. From there, tricycles and electric vehicles carry them to their hotels. The island is small enough to cross on foot in less than an hour at its narrowest point, near the middle."],
["Beaches beyond White Beach","Boracay has more than a dozen beaches. Bulabog Beach, on the eastern side, is one of Asia’s best spots for kitesurfing and windsurfing during the northeast monsoon, called amihan. Puka Shell Beach in the north was once covered with tiny shells used for necklaces. Diniwid, Ilig-Iligan and Balinghai are quieter coves."],
["Under the sea","The waters around Boracay have coral gardens, reef fish, sea turtles and colorful nudibranchs. Popular dive sites include Crocodile Island, Yapak, where divers sometimes see sharks along a deep wall, and Friday’s Rock. Snorkeling trips, glass-bottomed boats and helmet diving let non-divers see the reefs as well."],
["The Ati of Boracay","The Ati are among the earliest inhabitants of Panay and nearby islands. As tourism grew, many Ati families were pushed off the best land. In 2018 the government awarded the Ati community land titles to a few hectares on the island, recognizing their ancestral claim. The Ati are also honored in the Ati-Atihan festival of nearby Kalibo."],
["Why it closed","By 2018 Boracay’s rapid growth had overwhelmed it. Untreated sewage flowed into the sea, causing algae blooms near the beach, and many buildings had been built illegally on wetlands and too close to the water. President Rodrigo Duterte called the island a cesspool and ordered it closed for six months, from April to October 2018, for rehabilitation."],
["The new Boracay","After reopening, Boracay enforced a 30-meter easement along White Beach, where no structures are allowed, and new sewer and drainage systems were built. The number of tourists allowed on the island at one time was capped, hotels must be accredited and visitors need confirmed bookings. Electric tricycles replaced many gasoline vehicles."]],
sp:["The paraw","A paraw is a traditional Visayan double-outrigger sailboat with a narrow hull and two bamboo floats on either side for balance. Its large sail, once woven from palm leaves, is now usually made of cloth. Fishermen sailed paraw for generations, and on Boracay they now carry tourists across the water at sunset. Iloilo holds the annual Paraw Regatta, one of the oldest sailing events in Asia."],
g:[["Amihan","The cool northeast monsoon"],["Habagat","The rainy southwest monsoon"],["Easement","A strip of land kept free of buildings, here along the shore"],["Paraw","A traditional Visayan outrigger sailboat"]],
f:["Boracay’s White Beach has been named the world’s best beach by several travel publications.","The island covers only about 10 km².","Boracay is part of the municipality of Malay in Aklan."]},
siargao:{s:[
["The towns of Siargao","Siargao has nine municipalities, including General Luna, the surfing and tourism center; Dapa, the main port; Del Carmen, with its vast mangroves; and Pilar, home to the Magpupungko rock pools. Many visitors fly into Sayak Airport in Del Carmen, while others arrive by ferry from Surigao City."],
["How the waves form","Siargao faces the open Philippine Sea, with deep water running right up to the reefs. Swells generated by distant storms in the Pacific travel thousands of kilometers before hitting the island’s reefs, where they rise suddenly and break. The best surfing season is from about August to November, when typhoon swells and offshore winds combine."],
["Mangroves of Del Carmen","Del Carmen has one of the largest continuous mangrove forests in Mindanao, covering more than 4,000 hectares. Mangroves protect the coast from storm surges, filter water and serve as nurseries for fish and crabs. Visitors paddle among the roots, and a crocodile sanctuary helps protect the saltwater crocodiles that live there."],
["Island life and food","Before tourism, most Siargao families lived by fishing and growing coconuts. Today many also run guesthouses, restaurants and surf schools. Local dishes include fresh kinilaw, raw fish cured in vinegar or coconut vinegar with ginger and onion, and grilled seafood. Coconut palms line the famous winding road toward Pacifico."],
["Surf culture","Siargao has produced its own surfing champions, many of whom grew up on the island’s waves. Surf schools teach beginners on gentler breaks, while experienced surfers ride Cloud 9, Stimpy’s, Jacking Horse and Pacifico. The annual international surfing competition draws professional surfers from around the world, and local surfers often take home prizes."],
["Growing pains","Siargao’s popularity has brought rising land prices, traffic, waste and pressure on fresh water. Residents and local governments work to manage growth, protect the protected landscape and seascape and keep the relaxed island character that draws visitors. Building rules and waste programs aim to keep the island from repeating the problems of other tourist hot spots."]],
sp:["Typhoon Odette","Super Typhoon Odette, known internationally as Rai, made its first landfall on Siargao on 16 December 2021 with winds of about 195 km/h. It flattened homes, tore roofs from resorts, snapped coconut trees and cut power and communications for weeks. The storm went on to devastate parts of Dinagat, Bohol, Cebu, Negros and Palawan, killing over 400 people. Within a year, much of Siargao had reopened to visitors."],
g:[["Reef break","A surf spot where waves break over a coral reef"],["Swell","Ocean waves created by distant winds"],["Mangrove","A salt-tolerant tree that grows in coastal waters"],["Kinilaw","Raw fish cured in vinegar or citrus"]],
f:["Siargao is about 437 km² in area.","Cloud 9 got its name from a chocolate bar.","Siargao was declared a protected landscape and seascape in 1996."]},
pinatubo:{s:[
["A forgotten volcano","Before 1991 most people did not even know Pinatubo was a volcano. Covered in forest, it was home to Aeta communities and lay near the American Clark Air Base. Geologists later found that it had erupted about 500 years earlier and even more violently around 35,000 years ago. Its awakening in 1991 surprised the world."],
["A successful forecast","Scientists installed seismometers and tracked thousands of small earthquakes and rising sulfur dioxide. They issued alert levels and produced hazard maps showing which areas were in danger. About 60,000 people were evacuated before the main eruption, and Clark Air Base was emptied. Experts estimate the warnings saved at least 5,000 lives."],
["Effects on the world","The eruption threw about 15 million tonnes of sulfur dioxide into the stratosphere. There it formed a haze of tiny droplets that circled the globe, reflecting sunlight. Sunsets around the world glowed red and purple for months, global temperatures dipped by about 0.5°C in 1992, and the ozone layer thinned temporarily."],
["The Aeta people","The Aeta, one of the Negrito peoples and among the earliest inhabitants of the Philippines, had lived on Pinatubo’s slopes for generations, hunting, gathering and farming. They believed the mountain was the home of their god Apo Namalyari. The eruption destroyed their villages, and many lived for years in resettlement camps before some returned to the mountain."],
["The Americans leave","Ash heavily damaged Clark Air Base, and the United States decided not to repair it. In September 1991 the Philippine Senate voted not to renew the treaty that allowed American bases, and Subic Naval Base, also covered in ash, was handed over in 1992. Clark and Subic were turned into special economic zones and an airport."],
["Lahar control","Over the following decade, lahars buried large areas of farmland and whole towns. Rivers such as the Pasig-Potrero and the O’Donnell filled with volcanic sediment. Engineers built a long dike, the Megadike, to protect towns in Pampanga. In 2001 the crater lake threatened to overflow, and a channel was cut to drain it safely."]],
sp:["The church of Bacolor","San Guillermo Parish Church in Bacolor, Pampanga, was built in the 1500s and rebuilt in the 1800s. In October 1995, lahars from Pinatubo swept through the town, burying the church up to half its height. Rather than abandon it, the people dug out the interior and raised the floor, so today visitors enter through what were once high windows. It is a striking reminder of the eruption’s lasting effects."],
g:[["Stratosphere","The layer of the atmosphere above the weather, about 10 to 50 km up"],["Lahar","A fast-flowing mudflow of volcanic debris and water"],["Caldera","A large crater formed when a volcano collapses"],["Aeta","An Indigenous people of central Luzon"]],
f:["The 1991 Pinatubo eruption was about ten times larger than the 1980 eruption of Mount St. Helens.","Pinatubo’s summit lost about 260 m in height in the eruption.","The Pinatubo crater lake is one of the deepest lakes in the Philippines."]},
lapulapu:{s:[
["Mactan before Magellan","Mactan was a small island of fishing and trading communities across a narrow channel from Cebu. It was divided among several chiefs. Lapulapu ruled one part, while another chief, Zula, was willing to submit to the Spaniards. Rivalries among datus and the powerful Rajah Humabon of Cebu shaped the events of April 1521."],
["Magellan’s demand","After converting Humabon, Magellan demanded that other chiefs accept the Spanish king and the Christian faith. He sent a message to Lapulapu demanding tribute. Lapulapu refused. Magellan, confident that Spanish weapons and armor would win easily, declined Humabon’s offer of warriors and insisted on attacking with his own men."],
["Weapons of the battle","Lapulapu’s warriors carried spears, bamboo lances hardened in fire, kampilan swords and shields. The Spaniards had muskets, crossbows, swords and steel armor. But firing from boats far out in shallow water, the Spanish shots had little effect. When the warriors learned that armor did not cover the legs, they aimed there, wounding Magellan in the leg with a poisoned arrow, according to Pigafetta."],
["After the battle","With Magellan dead, the expedition was shaken. A few days later, Rajah Humabon invited Spanish officers to a feast, where many were killed, possibly because Humabon’s attitude changed after the defeat. The survivors burned one ship and sailed on. Only one, the Victoria, under Juan Sebastián Elcano, returned to Spain in 1522."],
["Legends of Lapulapu","Because so little is known about Lapulapu, many legends grew. Some stories say he was an old man in 1521, others that he was young. Some Muslim communities in Mindanao and Sulu consider him a Muslim hero, while others question this. A popular legend says he turned into a stone that stands in the sea near Mactan. Historians treat these stories with caution."],
["Lapu-Lapu City today","Opon, the main town of Mactan, was renamed Lapu-Lapu City in 1961. Today it holds the Mactan–Cebu International Airport, beach resorts, an economic zone with factories and the Mactan Shrine, where the Lapulapu Monument and the Magellan Shrine stand near each other. The fish called lapu-lapu, a type of grouper, shares his name."]],
sp:["The Kadaugan sa Mactan","Each 27 April, Mactan celebrates Kadaugan sa Mactan, the Victory at Mactan, with a reenactment of the battle on the shore near the Mactan Shrine. Actors in traditional costume play Lapulapu’s warriors and Magellan’s men, wading through the water as the story unfolds. In 2021, for the 500th anniversary, the celebration was part of a national program marking the quincentennial of the battle and of Christianity in the Philippines."],
g:[["Datu","A chief or ruler of a community"],["Kampilan","A long, single-edged sword"],["Tribute","Payment made to show submission to a ruler"],["Quincentennial","A 500th anniversary"]],
f:["The grouper fish called lapu-lapu is a popular dish in Filipino restaurants.","Lapulapu is often called the first Filipino hero.","Lapulapu appears on the seal of the Philippine National Police."]},
bonifacio:{s:[
["Tondo beginnings","Bonifacio was born in a house near the Tutuban railway area of Tondo, then a crowded district of workers and fishermen. His father, Santiago, was a tailor and local official, and his mother, Catalina de Castro, worked in a cigarette factory. Their deaths from tuberculosis left Andrés, the eldest, responsible for five younger siblings."],
["Actor and reader","Bonifacio loved the theater and acted in plays, especially the moro-moro, dramas of battles between Christians and Muslims, which helped him become a confident speaker. He read widely, including the Bible, novels and books on law and revolution. He worked for British and German trading firms, learning about the outside world."],
["Founding the Katipunan","On 7 July 1892, the night after Rizal was arrested and deported, Bonifacio, Deodato Arellano, Ladislao Diwa, Teodoro Plata and others met in a house on Azcarraga Street in Tondo and founded the Kataastaasang, Kagalanggalangang Katipunan ng mga Anak ng Bayan, the Highest and Most Respected Society of the Children of the Nation."],
["Secrets and rituals","Members underwent a frightening initiation, were blindfolded and asked questions about Spanish rule, then signed an oath in their own blood. They used code names and passwords. Bonifacio’s code name was Maypagasa, meaning one with hope. Women joined in a women’s chapter, and Gregoria de Jesús kept the society’s seal and documents safe."],
["Kalayaan and the Decalogue","In 1896 the Katipunan printed one issue of its newspaper, Kalayaan, meaning freedom, on a secret press. It inspired thousands to join, and membership grew to perhaps 30,000 or more. Bonifacio wrote the Duties of the Sons of the People, and Emilio Jacinto wrote the Kartilya, which taught members to live with honor, kindness and courage."],
["The debate over his legacy","Some historians and citizens argue that Bonifacio deserves to be called the first president, since the Katipunan formed a revolutionary government with him as its head in 1896. His trial and execution by fellow revolutionaries remain a painful and debated episode. His remains were never found with certainty, but his memory lives in streets, schools and monuments throughout the country."]],
sp:["Gregoria de Jesús","Gregoria de Jesús, called Oriang, married Bonifacio in 1893 at age 18 and became the Lakambini, or muse, of the Katipunan. She guarded secret documents, carried messages and nursed the wounded. After Bonifacio’s death she searched for his body in the mountains. She later married Julio Nakpil, a composer and revolutionary, and wrote her memoirs. She died in 1943 and is honored as a heroine of the revolution."],
g:[["Katipunan","The secret revolutionary society founded in 1892"],["Supremo","Bonifacio’s title as head of the Katipunan"],["Cedula","A Spanish-era tax certificate and identity card"],["Lakambini","Muse; a title given to Gregoria de Jesús"]],
f:["Bonifacio Day, 30 November, is a national holiday.","The Katipunan’s flag went through several designs, including a red flag with a white K.","Bonifacio and Apolinario Mabini appear together on the Philippine ten-peso coin."]},
commonwealth:{s:[
["The 1935 Constitution","Delegates to a constitutional convention wrote the 1935 Constitution, approved by Filipino voters in a plebiscite. It created a presidential system modeled on that of the United States, with a president elected for six years, a one-house National Assembly, later changed to a two-house Congress, and a Supreme Court. It remained in force until 1973."],
["Manuel L. Quezon","Quezon was born in Baler, in what is now Aurora province, in 1878. He fought in the revolution, became a lawyer and rose to lead the Senate. He was famous for his passionate speeches and once said he would prefer a government run like hell by Filipinos to one run like heaven by Americans. Quezon City and Quezon province are named after him."],
["Social justice and land","The Commonwealth faced deep inequality, as many farmers worked as tenants for large landowners. Quezon’s social justice program set minimum wages, an eight-hour workday and courts to settle labor disputes. Peasant unrest in central Luzon, led by groups such as the Sakdalistas in 1935, showed the depth of rural anger."],
["A national language","In 1936 the National Language Institute was created to study Philippine languages and recommend one as the basis for a national language. It chose Tagalog in 1937, and from 1940 it was taught in schools. Quezon is called the Father of the National Language, and Buwan ng Wika in August ends on his birthday, 19 August."],
["Preparing for defense","Expecting war in Asia, Quezon asked General Douglas MacArthur to build a Philippine army. The plan called for training reservists, but there was too little time and money. When Japan attacked in 1941, Filipino and American troops fought together as the United States Army Forces in the Far East, but they were poorly equipped."],
["Refuge for Jewish families","Between 1937 and 1941, Quezon, working with the Frieder brothers and US High Commissioner Paul McNutt, helped about 1,300 Jewish refugees from Germany and Austria settle in the Philippines. Quezon donated land in Marikina for refugee housing. A monument in Israel, the Open Doors memorial, honors the Philippines for this rescue."]],
sp:["Quezon City","Quezon dreamed of a new capital away from crowded Manila. In 1939 Quezon City was founded on land in the hills to the northeast, planned with wide avenues, a central park and government buildings. It was officially the capital of the Philippines from 1948 to 1976. Today it is the country’s most populous city, home to the House of Representatives, the University of the Philippines Diliman and the Quezon Memorial Circle."],
g:[["Commonwealth","A self-governing transition government under US sovereignty"],["Plebiscite","A direct vote of the people on an important question"],["Tenant farmer","A farmer who works land owned by someone else"],["High Commissioner","The US representative in the Commonwealth"]],
f:["Manuel Quezon’s birthday, 19 August, is a holiday in Quezon City and nearby provinces.","The Commonwealth lasted just under eleven years.","Sergio Osmeña became president in 1944 when Quezon died in office."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-ph6.js */
// In-depth material (part 6 of the Philippines): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
sulu:{s:[
["The islands of Sulu","The Sulu Archipelago is a chain of hundreds of islands stretching from the tip of the Zamboanga Peninsula toward Borneo. The main islands are Jolo, Basilan, Siasi, Pata and the Tawi-Tawi group. Sea and land have always been closely linked for its peoples, who include the Tausug, Sama, Yakan and Badjao."],
["Government of the sultanate","The sultan ruled with the help of a council of datus, or chiefs, who controlled their own followers and lands. Islamic law and local custom guided justice. Sulu sent diplomatic missions to China; in 1417 a Sulu ruler, Paduka Batara, traveled to the Ming court, died there and was buried in Dezhou, where his tomb still stands."],
["Wars with Spain","For more than two centuries, Spain fought a series of wars against the Muslim sultanates of the south, which the Spanish called the Moro Wars. Spanish forces attacked Jolo several times, and Moro raiders struck Spanish-held towns in return, leading to the building of watchtowers and stone churches along Visayan coasts. Spain finally occupied Jolo in 1876."],
["Under American rule","In 1899 the Americans signed the Kiram–Bates Treaty with Sultan Jamalul Kiram II, promising not to interfere in Sulu’s religion and customs. They later abrogated the treaty and fought resistance fighters. The Battle of Bud Dajo in 1906 killed hundreds of Tausug men, women and children and was condemned by critics, including Mark Twain."],
["Peoples of the sea","The Sama Dilaut, often called Badjao, are known as sea nomads who traditionally lived on houseboats called lepa, diving for fish, pearls and sea cucumbers. Many now live in stilt villages over the water. The Yakan of Basilan are famous for brightly colored woven cloth. Each group has its own language and customs."],
["Sulu today","Sulu is now a province of the Philippines, with Jolo as its capital. It has faced poverty and armed conflict, but its people continue traditions of fine weaving, boat building, music and dance. In 2024 the Supreme Court ruled that Sulu was not part of the Bangsamoro region, after voters there had rejected joining it in 2019."]],
sp:["The vinta","The vinta is a traditional boat of the Sulu and Zamboanga region, with an outrigger on each side and a striking rectangular sail striped in bright colors such as red, yellow, green and blue. The prow is often carved with curling designs. Vintas were used for fishing and trading among the islands. Today they are a symbol of Zamboanga City, where they race and parade in the Hermosa Festival."],
g:[["Sultan","A Muslim ruler"],["Tausug","People of the current, the main people of Sulu"],["Badjao","Sea-dwelling Sama people of the southern seas"],["Vinta","A colorful sailboat of the southern Philippines"]],
f:["The tomb of Sulu’s Paduka Batara, who died in 1417, still stands in Dezhou, China.","Sulu pearls were once among the most prized in Asia.","Tausug women perform the pangalay, a graceful dance with long metal fingernails."]},
phsport:{s:[
["Boxing’s golden history","Boxing arrived with the Americans, and Filipinos soon excelled. Francisco Guilledo, known as Pancho Villa, became the first Asian world champion in 1923. Gabriel Flash Elorde held a world title for seven years in the 1960s and was elected to the International Boxing Hall of Fame. Others include Nonito Donaire, Donnie Nietes and Jerwin Ancajas."],
["Billiards","Filipinos are among the best pool players in the world. Efren Bata Reyes, called the Magician for his amazing shots, won world championships in different games and is considered one of the greatest players ever. His partner Francisco Django Bustamante also won world titles, and the Philippines has produced many champions in nine-ball."],
["Volleyball fever","Women’s volleyball has become one of the country’s most watched sports, with college rivalries in the UAAP and professional leagues such as the Premier Volleyball League filling arenas. The Philippines hosted the men’s Volleyball World Championship in 2025. Star players such as Alyssa Valdez became household names."],
["The Southeast Asian Games","The Philippines has competed in the Southeast Asian Games since 1977 and hosted them several times, most recently in 2019, when it topped the medal table for the first time since 2005. Filipino athletes win medals in boxing, wushu, taekwondo, athletics, swimming, gymnastics, cycling and dancesport."],
["Esports and chess","Filipino teams and players have won international esports tournaments in games such as Mobile Legends and Dota 2. In chess, Eugene Torre played in a record number of Chess Olympiads, and Wesley So, born in Bacoor, became one of the world’s top players before moving to the United States."],
["Traditional games","Before modern sports, Filipino children played traditional games, called larong Pinoy, such as patintero, a tag game on a grid; sipa, kicking a small ball or rattan disc; luksong tinik, jumping over hands; and tumbang preso, knocking down a can with slippers. Schools and festivals keep these games alive."]],
sp:["Hidilyn Diaz","Hidilyn Diaz grew up in Zamboanga City, where she began lifting using a homemade barbell of concrete. She won silver at the 2016 Rio Olympics, the country’s first Olympic medal in 20 years. In Tokyo in 2021, she lifted an Olympic record in the clean and jerk to win gold in the women’s 55 kg class, ending a 97-year wait. She became a national hero and was promoted in the Philippine Air Force."],
g:[["PBA","Philippine Basketball Association, Asia’s oldest pro league"],["Larong Pinoy","Traditional Filipino games"],["Arnis","The national martial art and sport"],["SEA Games","The Southeast Asian Games"]],
f:["Efren Reyes is nicknamed Bata, meaning kid, and the Magician.","The Philippine Arena in Bulacan can hold about 55,000 people.","Sipa was the national sport of the Philippines before arnis replaced it in 2009."]},
phdance:{s:[
["Tinikling","Tinikling began in Leyte. Two people sit facing each other and clap two long bamboo poles together and on the ground in rhythm, while dancers hop nimbly between and around them, avoiding getting their ankles caught. The dance imitates the tikling bird stepping between grass stems and avoiding farmers’ traps. It gets faster and faster as it goes on."],
["Kulintang music","Kulintang is played on a row of small bronze gongs laid horizontally on a wooden frame, struck with wooden beaters. It is accompanied by larger hanging gongs, called agung and gandingan, and a drum. Found among the Maguindanao, Maranao and other southern peoples, it is used at weddings, festivals and celebrations, and its players improvise within traditional patterns."],
["Cordillera music","Mountain peoples of northern Luzon play gangsa, flat bronze gongs held in the hand and struck with sticks or palms, in groups that dance in circles. Bamboo instruments include the kalaleng, a nose flute, the tongatong, bamboo tubes stamped on the ground, and the kolitong, a bamboo zither. Songs accompany rituals for harvest and healing."],
["Spanish influences","Spanish-era dances include the cariñosa, a flirtatious dance with a fan and handkerchief, often called the national dance; the rigodon, a formal court dance performed at state events; and the pandanggo, from the Spanish fandango. Rondalla ensembles of plucked string instruments accompany them, and the harana, serenading a woman under her window, became a courtship tradition."],
["American era and jazz","The Americans brought brass bands, jazz, swing and Broadway songs. Filipino musicians became famous across Asia, playing in hotels and nightclubs from Shanghai to Tokyo. Music schools such as the University of the Philippines Conservatory of Music, founded in 1916, trained classical musicians and composers."],
["Dance companies today","The Bayanihan and the Ramon Obusan Folkloric Group perform Philippine folk dances on stages worldwide. Ballet Philippines and Ballet Manila combine classical ballet with Filipino themes. Street dance crews have won world hip hop dance championships, and festival street dances keep traditional and new movements alive."]],
sp:["The kundiman","The kundiman is the classic Filipino love song, slow and tender, often expressing longing and devotion. During the revolution and American rule, kundiman lyrics sometimes hid patriotic messages, with the beloved woman standing for the motherland. Composers such as Francisco Santiago, called the Father of the Kundiman Art Song, and Nicanor Abelardo turned it into concert music. Famous kundiman include Bayan Ko and Nasaan Ka Irog."],
g:[["Kulintang","A set of small gongs played in the southern Philippines"],["Rondalla","An ensemble of plucked string instruments"],["Harana","A traditional serenade"],["Gangsa","Flat bronze gongs of the Cordillera"]],
f:["The cariñosa is often called the national dance of the Philippines, though it has no official status.","The tikling is a rail bird that lives in rice fields.","Bayan Ko, a patriotic kundiman, became an anthem of protest during the martial law years."]},
jeepney:{s:[
["Building a jeepney","A jeepney body is built around a truck chassis and engine, often a reconditioned Japanese diesel engine. Craftsmen cut, bend and rivet sheets of stainless steel and galvanized iron by hand. The front often has a grille, chrome hood ornaments shaped like horses and many side mirrors. Painters add names, saints, cartoon characters and landscapes."],
["Jeepney art","Every jeepney is unique. Owners decorate them with airbrushed murals, religious images, slogans, family names and bright colors. Some carry plastic flowers, tassels and extra lights. This folk art has been displayed in museums and at world fairs, and jeepney designs inspire Filipino fashion, products and graphic design."],
["Routes and drivers","Jeepneys run on franchised routes set by the Land Transportation Franchising and Regulatory Board. Many drivers rent the vehicle from an operator for a daily fee, called boundary, and keep what they earn above it. They work long hours in traffic and heat. Barkers sometimes call out the destination to attract passengers."],
["Jeepney culture","Riding a jeepney is a shared experience. Passengers squeeze together on side benches, pass fares and change, and help each other. Jeepneys appear in Filipino films, songs and jokes. For many Filipinos, the jeepney is a symbol of resourcefulness, turning war leftovers into something useful and joyful."],
["Problems and pollution","Old jeepneys can be unsafe, with no seat belts and only an open back door. Their diesel engines produce smoke that adds to air pollution, a serious health problem in Metro Manila. Traffic jams worsen as jeepneys stop anywhere to load passengers. Modernization aims to address these issues."],
["Modern jeepneys","Modern public utility vehicles look more like minibuses, with side doors, air conditioning, cameras, GPS and automatic fare collection, and engines that meet Euro 4 emission standards or run on electricity. Supporters say they are safer and cleaner. Critics say they are too expensive for small operators and drivers, and that the classic jeepney should be preserved as heritage."]],
sp:["Sarao Motors","Leonardo Sarao started building jeepneys in 1953 with a loan of 700 pesos and a small team. Sarao Motors in Las Piñas grew into the most famous jeepney maker, producing thousands of vehicles a year at its peak in the 1970s. Its jeepneys, with their trademark horse hood ornaments, became icons. As demand fell and modern vehicles arrived, the company scaled back production, but its jeepneys remain beloved symbols of Filipino ingenuity."],
g:[["Jitney","An old word for a cheap shared taxi"],["Boundary","The daily rental fee a driver pays the operator"],["Barker","A person who calls out a vehicle’s destination for passengers"],["Franchise","Official permission to operate a transport route"]],
f:["Jeepneys are often called the king of the road in the Philippines.","A jeepney was displayed at the 1964 New York World’s Fair.","Many jeepneys have a hand-painted name on the front, often of the owner’s children."]},
pulag:{s:[
["Getting to the summit","Most hikers start from the Ambangeg trail in Kabayan, Benguet, the easiest route, which passes through pine and mossy forest before reaching the grassy summit. Hikers usually camp overnight and climb before dawn to watch the sunrise. The Akiki and Tawangan trails are longer and harder, and the Ambaguio trail approaches from Nueva Vizcaya."],
["The mossy forest","Between the pine forest and the summit grassland lies a mossy forest, always damp and cool, where trees are covered in thick moss, ferns, lichens and orchids. Mist drifts through the trees, and the air smells of earth and leaves. This forest catches moisture from clouds and releases it slowly into streams."],
["The Kabayan mummies","In the caves of Kabayan, near Mount Pulag, the Ibaloi people once preserved their dead as mummies, called fire mummies. Bodies were dried slowly over fires for weeks, then smoked and rubbed with herbs before being placed in coffins in caves. The mummies are hundreds of years old and are considered sacred. They are among the Philippines’ national cultural treasures."],
["Plants of Pulag","Pulag is home to the Benguet pine, dwarf bamboo, rhododendrons and hundreds of other plants, including many orchids found nowhere else. The dwarf bamboo of the summit, about knee-high, gives the grasslands their soft, golden-green look. Some plants of the summit are more like those of temperate countries than tropical lowlands."],
["Weather and safety","The weather on Pulag can change quickly, with fog, rain and strong winds. Hikers can suffer from hypothermia if they get wet and cold. Rangers require guides, good gear and a briefing on safety and respect for the mountain. Climbs are sometimes suspended during storms or when the trails need to recover."],
["Communities around Pulag","Farmers in the valleys around Pulag grow vegetables such as cabbage, potatoes and carrots on steep terraced fields, supplying markets in Baguio and Manila. Some work as guides and porters. Balancing farming, tourism and forest protection is a challenge, since clearing land for vegetables can threaten the park’s forests and water."]],
sp:["The sea of clouds","On clear mornings, the valleys below Mount Pulag fill with low clouds, so the peak seems to float above a white ocean. As the sun rises, the clouds turn pink, orange and gold. The phenomenon happens because cool, moist air settles in the valleys overnight, forming a layer of cloud below the summit. It is the reason thousands of hikers climb Pulag each year, often waking at 2 or 3 in the morning to reach the summit in time."],
g:[["Mossy forest","A cool, wet mountain forest with trees covered in moss"],["Hypothermia","A dangerous drop in body temperature"],["Ibaloi","An Indigenous people of Benguet"],["Dwarf bamboo","A short bamboo that covers Pulag’s summit"]],
f:["Mount Pulag is the third highest mountain in the Philippines, after Mount Apo and Mount Dulang-dulang.","The Kabayan mummy caves are listed by UNESCO on its tentative World Heritage list.","Temperatures near Pulag’s summit can drop to around 0°C in January and February."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-hist1.js */
// In-depth material (History, part 1): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
egypt:{s:[
["Writing and scribes","Hieroglyphs combined pictures that stood for sounds, whole words and ideas, and there were several hundred common signs. Scribes trained for years in temple schools, learning to write with reed pens on papyrus and on fragments of pottery. Being a scribe was a respected job, free from hard labor, and scribes recorded everything from harvests and taxes to prayers and stories."],
["Mummies","Mummification took about 70 days. Embalmers removed the brain through the nose and took out the internal organs, which were stored in canopic jars, though the heart was left in place. The body was dried with natron salt, then wrapped in hundreds of meters of linen with protective charms. The richest mummies were placed in nested coffins inside a stone sarcophagus."],
["Gods and temples","Egyptians worshipped hundreds of gods, many shown with animal heads: Horus with a falcon’s head, Anubis with a jackal’s head and Thoth with an ibis head. Great temples, such as Karnak and Luxor at Thebes, were homes for the gods, where priests cared for their statues daily. Ordinary people prayed at home shrines and wore amulets for protection."],
["The Valley of the Kings","From about 1500 BCE, New Kingdom pharaohs were buried in tombs cut deep into the cliffs of a desert valley near Thebes, rather than in pyramids, hoping to hide them from robbers. More than 60 tombs have been found, their walls covered with paintings and texts to guide the king through the underworld. Almost all were robbed in ancient times, except Tutankhamun’s."],
["Women in Egypt","Egyptian women had more rights than women in many ancient societies. They could own and inherit property, run businesses, sign contracts and go to court. Some became priestesses, and a few, such as Hatshepsut and Cleopatra VII, ruled the country. Queen Nefertiti, wife of Akhenaten, was shown in art with great power and beauty."],
["Egypt’s later history","After the New Kingdom, Egypt was ruled at times by Libyans, Nubians, Assyrians and Persians. Alexander the Great conquered it in 332 BCE, and his general Ptolemy founded a Greek-speaking dynasty based in Alexandria, with its famous lighthouse and library. Cleopatra VII, the last Ptolemaic ruler, allied with Julius Caesar and Mark Antony before Rome took control."]],
sp:["Tutankhamun’s tomb","On 4 November 1922 Howard Carter, working for Lord Carnarvon, found steps leading to a sealed tomb in the Valley of the Kings. Inside were more than 5,000 objects: gilded shrines, chariots, jewelry, games, furniture and, in the burial chamber, the young king’s mummy in three nested coffins, the innermost of solid gold. His gold mask weighs about 10 kg. Tutankhamun, who died at about 19, became the most famous pharaoh of all."],
g:[["Pharaoh","The king of ancient Egypt"],["Hieroglyph","A picture sign used in Egyptian writing"],["Papyrus","A writing material made from river reeds"],["Sarcophagus","A stone coffin"]],
f:["Egyptians invented an early form of toothpaste made from crushed rock salt, mint and pepper.","Cleopatra VII was the first Ptolemaic ruler to learn the Egyptian language.","The Great Pyramid was originally covered with polished white limestone that made it shine in the sun."]},
rome:{s:[
["The Roman Republic","In the Republic, two consuls were elected each year to lead the state and the army, so no one person held power for long. The Senate, made up of former officials, advised them. Citizens voted in assemblies. Over time, ordinary citizens, called plebeians, won rights from the aristocratic patricians, including their own officials called tribunes."],
["Julius Caesar","Julius Caesar was a brilliant general who conquered Gaul, roughly modern France, between 58 and 50 BCE. Ordered to give up his army, he crossed the Rubicon River into Italy in 49 BCE, starting a civil war. He won and became dictator for life, but senators who feared he would make himself king stabbed him to death on the Ides of March, 15 March 44 BCE."],
["Augustus and the Pax Romana","Caesar’s adopted heir, Octavian, defeated his rivals and in 27 BCE took the name Augustus, becoming the first emperor while keeping the forms of the Republic. His rule began the Pax Romana, about 200 years of relative peace and prosperity, when trade flourished and roads and cities spread across the empire."],
["Roman religion and Christianity","Romans worshipped many gods, such as Jupiter, Juno, Mars and Venus, and honored emperors as divine. They often accepted the gods of conquered peoples. Christians, who refused to worship the emperor, were sometimes persecuted. In 313 Constantine’s Edict of Milan allowed Christian worship, and by 380 Christianity had become the official religion."],
["Roman law and language","Roman law, gradually written down and finally compiled under the Byzantine emperor Justinian in the 530s, became the basis of legal systems in much of Europe and Latin America. Latin became the language of the western empire and developed into the Romance languages. The Roman calendar, reformed by Caesar, is the ancestor of the one we use today."],
["Why Rome fell","Historians give many reasons for the fall of the Western Empire: civil wars, weak leaders, economic problems, plague, overreliance on hired soldiers and pressure from Germanic peoples and the Huns. In 410 the Visigoths sacked Rome, and in 476 the Germanic leader Odoacer deposed the last western emperor, Romulus Augustulus."]],
sp:["Pompeii","On 24 August 79 CE, by the traditional date, Mount Vesuvius erupted and buried the town of Pompeii under several meters of ash and pumice. Thousands died. Rediscovered in the 1700s, Pompeii offers a snapshot of Roman life: streets with stepping stones, bakeries with loaves in ovens, graffiti on walls, painted villas and plaster casts of people caught in the disaster. It is one of the most visited archaeological sites in the world."],
g:[["Republic","A state in which citizens elect their leaders"],["Senate","Rome’s council of elders and former officials"],["Legion","A unit of the Roman army of about 5,000 soldiers"],["Aqueduct","A channel built to carry water over long distances"]],
f:["The word salary may come from salt, which was valuable in Roman times.","The Colosseum could be flooded for mock sea battles, according to ancient writers.","Roman roads were so well built that some are still used today."]},
indrev:{s:[
["The textile revolution","Before the Industrial Revolution, spinning and weaving were done at home. A series of inventions changed this: John Kay’s flying shuttle of 1733 sped up weaving, James Hargreaves’ spinning jenny let one worker spin many threads, and Richard Arkwright’s water frame of 1769 spun strong thread using water power. Arkwright’s mill at Cromford is often called the first modern factory."],
["Coal and iron","Coal powered steam engines and furnaces. In 1709 Abraham Darby began smelting iron with coke, made from coal, instead of charcoal from wood, making iron cheaper. Iron was used for machines, bridges, rails and ships. Coal mining grew enormously, and miners, including children, worked in dangerous, dark tunnels."],
["Canals and railways","Canals carried heavy coal and goods cheaply; the Bridgewater Canal, opened in 1761, halved the price of coal in Manchester. Then came railways. George Stephenson’s Locomotion No. 1 ran on the Stockton and Darlington Railway in 1825, and the Liverpool and Manchester Railway of 1830 became the first intercity line, carrying passengers and goods by steam."],
["Changing society","The Industrial Revolution created a new middle class of factory owners, merchants and professionals, and a large working class. Cities such as Manchester and Birmingham grew from towns into cities. Pollution, disease and crowded slums were common. Writers such as Charles Dickens described the hardships of industrial life."],
["Reform and rights","Over time, reformers pushed for change. Laws limited working hours for women and children, required schooling and improved public health. Trade unions became legal, and the right to vote was gradually extended. The Chartists of the 1830s and 1840s campaigned for votes for all men."],
["The second Industrial Revolution","From about 1870, new technologies spread: cheap steel from the Bessemer process, electricity, chemicals, the telephone and the internal combustion engine. Germany and the United States became industrial leaders. Assembly lines, such as Henry Ford’s in 1913, made mass production possible. Japan rapidly industrialized after the Meiji Restoration of 1868."]],
sp:["The Luddites","Between 1811 and 1816, skilled textile workers in the English Midlands and north, fearing that new machines would take their jobs and lower wages, broke into factories at night and smashed knitting frames and power looms. They claimed to follow a mysterious leader named Ned Ludd. The government sent thousands of soldiers, and machine-breaking was made punishable by death. Today a Luddite means someone who opposes new technology."],
g:[["Factory","A building where goods are made by machines"],["Mechanization","Replacing hand work with machines"],["Urbanization","The growth of towns and cities"],["Trade union","An organization of workers formed to protect their interests"]],
f:["Manchester was nicknamed Cottonopolis because of its many cotton mills.","In 1800 about 20% of people in Britain lived in towns; by 1900 it was about 75%.","The world’s first public steam railway opened in England in 1825."]},
ww2:{s:[
["Appeasement and the road to war","In the 1930s Britain and France tried to avoid war by giving in to some of Hitler’s demands, a policy called appeasement. At Munich in 1938 they agreed that Germany could take the Sudetenland from Czechoslovakia. In August 1939 Germany and the Soviet Union signed a pact not to fight each other, secretly agreeing to divide Poland."],
["The Battle of Britain","After France fell in 1940, Germany tried to win control of the skies over Britain in preparation for an invasion. Through the summer and autumn, the Royal Air Force fought the German Luftwaffe, helped by radar. Germany then bombed British cities in the Blitz. Britain’s survival, led by Prime Minister Winston Churchill, kept a base for later Allied attacks."],
["The Eastern Front","In June 1941 Germany invaded the Soviet Union in Operation Barbarossa, the largest invasion in history. The fighting was immense and brutal, with millions of soldiers and civilians killed. The siege of Leningrad lasted almost 900 days. The Soviet victory at Stalingrad in early 1943 and at Kursk later that year turned the tide."],
["The Holocaust","Nazi Germany carried out the systematic murder of about six million Jews, as well as Roma, disabled people and others. Jews were stripped of rights, forced into ghettos and sent to death camps such as Auschwitz-Birkenau, Treblinka and Sobibor. The Holocaust is remembered so that such crimes are never repeated, and 27 January is International Holocaust Remembrance Day."],
["Technology of war","The war drove rapid advances in technology: radar, jet aircraft, rockets such as the German V-2, codebreaking computers and the atomic bomb, developed by the United States in the secret Manhattan Project. Penicillin was mass-produced to treat wounded soldiers. Many of these technologies shaped the world after the war."],
["Founding the United Nations","The Allies planned a new organization to keep the peace. In June 1945 representatives of 50 nations signed the United Nations Charter in San Francisco, and Poland signed soon after as the 51st founding member. The UN officially came into being on 24 October 1945."]],
sp:["D-Day","On 6 June 1944 about 156,000 Allied troops from the United States, Britain, Canada and other nations landed on five beaches in Normandy, France, code-named Utah, Omaha, Gold, Juno and Sword. Thousands of ships and aircraft supported them. Despite heavy losses, especially at Omaha Beach, the Allies gained a foothold. Within three months they liberated Paris, and the landings opened the way to the defeat of Germany."],
g:[["Axis","The alliance of Germany, Italy and Japan"],["Allies","The nations that fought the Axis"],["Blitzkrieg","German for lightning war, fast attacks with tanks and aircraft"],["Holocaust","The Nazi genocide of Jews and others"]],
f:["The Soviet Union lost about 27 million people in World War II, more than any other country.","The United Nations Charter was signed by 50 nations in San Francisco in June 1945.","The atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki are the only uses of nuclear weapons in war."]},
renais:{s:[
["Why Italy?","Italy was full of reminders of ancient Rome, from ruins to manuscripts. Its city-states, such as Florence, Venice and Milan, were rich from trade and banking, and their rulers competed to show off their wealth and taste by hiring artists and scholars. The Black Death of 1348 had also shaken old ideas and, by reducing the population, left survivors with more money to spend."],
["The Medici","The Medici family of Florence grew rich as bankers to the popes and rulers of Europe. Cosimo de’ Medici and his grandson Lorenzo the Magnificent were great patrons, supporting artists such as Botticelli, Donatello and the young Michelangelo. The family later produced popes and queens of France."],
["Sculpture","Renaissance sculptors revived the classical nude and lifelike portraits. Donatello’s bronze David, made around the 1440s, was the first freestanding nude statue since ancient times. Lorenzo Ghiberti spent decades on the golden bronze doors of the Florence Baptistery, which Michelangelo called the Gates of Paradise."],
["Venice and color","Venetian painters such as Giovanni Bellini, Giorgione and Titian were famous for rich color and soft light, using oil paint on canvas. Titian painted portraits of emperors and kings and dramatic religious and mythological scenes. Venice was also a center of printing, with presses such as that of Aldus Manutius publishing classical texts."],
["Women of the Renaissance","Women had limited opportunities, but some won fame. Isabella d’Este, Marchioness of Mantua, was a powerful patron and collector. Christine de Pizan, an earlier writer, defended women’s abilities. Painters such as Sofonisba Anguissola and later Artemisia Gentileschi gained international reputations."],
["Explorers and the wider world","The Renaissance spirit of curiosity encouraged exploration and science. Maps improved, and ancient geography was challenged by new discoveries. Explorers such as Columbus, an Italian sailing for Spain, and Magellan expanded European knowledge of the world. Anatomist Andreas Vesalius published accurate drawings of the human body in 1543."]],
sp:["Brunelleschi’s dome","For decades, Florence Cathedral had a huge hole where its dome should be, because no one knew how to cover the 45-meter-wide space. In 1418 Filippo Brunelleschi won a competition with a daring plan: a double-shelled dome built without wooden supports underneath, using bricks laid in a herringbone pattern. He even invented hoisting machines to lift the materials. Completed in 1436, it remains the largest brick dome in the world."],
g:[["Renaissance","French for rebirth"],["Humanism","The study of classical learning and human potential"],["Patron","A wealthy person who pays for art or learning"],["Perspective","A technique to show depth on a flat surface"]],
f:["The word Renaissance was popularized in the 1800s by historians such as Jacob Burckhardt.","The Mona Lisa has no visible eyebrows.","Raphael died at 37 on his birthday, Good Friday, in 1520."]},
maya:{s:[
["Tikal","Tikal, in the rainforest of northern Guatemala, was one of the most powerful Maya cities, with perhaps 60,000 or more people at its height. Its temples rise above the treetops, the tallest about 65 m high. Inscriptions record a long rivalry with Calakmul. Tikal was abandoned in the 900s and is now a national park and World Heritage Site."],
["Kings and queens","Maya rulers, called k’uhul ajaw, holy lords, performed ceremonies, led armies and commissioned monuments recording their deeds. Pakal the Great ruled Palenque for 68 years in the 600s and was buried in a richly decorated tomb inside the Temple of the Inscriptions, with a jade mask. Some women, such as Lady Six Sky of Naranjo, ruled powerful cities."],
["Mathematics and astronomy","The Maya used a base-20 number system written with dots for one, bars for five and a shell symbol for zero. Their astronomers tracked the movements of the Sun, Moon and Venus with great accuracy and predicted eclipses. Buildings such as El Castillo at Chichén Itzá were aligned with the Sun, creating light-and-shadow effects at the equinoxes."],
["Religion and rituals","Maya religion included many gods, such as Itzamna, the creator; Chaak, the rain god; and K’inich Ajaw, the sun god. Rituals included offerings, dances, fasting and bloodletting, in which rulers offered their own blood. Caves and cenotes, natural sinkholes filled with water, were seen as entrances to the underworld, called Xibalba."],
["Discoveries with lasers","In recent years, archaeologists have used lidar, laser scanning from aircraft, to see through the rainforest canopy. Surveys in Guatemala revealed tens of thousands of previously unknown structures, roads, farms and defenses, showing that Maya cities were larger and more connected than once thought, possibly supporting millions of people."],
["The Maya today","More than six million Maya people live in Guatemala, Mexico, Belize and Honduras, speaking about 30 Mayan languages such as K’iche’, Yucatec and Q’eqchi’. They keep traditions of weaving, farming and religious practice, often blended with Christianity. Maya activist Rigoberta Menchú won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1992 for her work for Indigenous rights."]],
sp:["Chichén Itzá","Chichén Itzá, on Mexico’s Yucatán Peninsula, flourished from about 900 to 1200 CE. Its great pyramid, El Castillo, has 91 steps on each of its four sides, plus a top platform, making 365, one for each day of the year. At the spring and autumn equinoxes, shadows create the image of a serpent slithering down the stairway. The site also has a huge ball court and the Sacred Cenote. It was named one of the New 7 Wonders of the World in 2007."],
g:[["Stela","A carved stone slab recording events"],["Cenote","A natural sinkhole filled with water"],["Codex","A folding book of bark paper"],["Long Count","The Maya calendar that counts days from a starting date"]],
f:["Chocolate comes from a Maya and Aztec tradition of drinking cacao.","Maya writing was largely deciphered only in the second half of the 20th century.","El Castillo at Chichén Itzá is also called the Temple of Kukulcán, the feathered serpent god."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-hist2.js */
// In-depth material (History, part 2): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
silk:{s:[
["The secret of silk","Silk is made from the cocoons of silkworms, caterpillars that feed on mulberry leaves. The Chinese guarded the secret of silk-making for centuries, and smuggling silkworm eggs out of China was punishable by death. According to legend, monks smuggled eggs to the Byzantine Empire in hollow canes around 550 CE, starting a European silk industry."],
["Caravans and camels","Merchants traveled in caravans of dozens or hundreds of camels for safety. The two-humped Bactrian camel could carry heavy loads across cold deserts and mountains and survive on little water. Caravans moved about 30 km a day. They faced sandstorms, bandits, freezing passes in the Pamir Mountains and scorching heat in the Taklamakan Desert."],
["Oasis cities","Cities grew up around oases and river valleys along the route. Samarkand, Bukhara and Merv in Central Asia, Dunhuang and Kashgar in China, and Palmyra in Syria became rich trading hubs filled with markets, mosques, temples and inns. Merchants from many lands mixed there, and many people spoke several languages."],
["The Sogdians","The Sogdians, an Iranian people from around Samarkand, were among the greatest Silk Road traders between the 400s and 800s. They set up merchant communities across Central Asia and China, and their language served as a common tongue of trade. Some Sogdians rose to high positions at the Tang court in China."],
["Dunhuang’s caves","Near the oasis town of Dunhuang, Buddhist monks carved nearly 500 decorated caves, the Mogao Caves, between the 300s and 1300s. Their murals and statues show travelers, merchants and Buddhist stories. In 1900 a hidden library was found inside one cave, holding tens of thousands of manuscripts, including the Diamond Sutra of 868, the oldest dated printed book."],
["Diseases and exchanges","The Silk Road spread not only goods and ideas but also disease. The Black Death, a plague probably caused by bacteria carried by fleas on rodents, is thought to have spread from Central Asia along trade routes to Europe in the 1340s, killing a third or more of Europe’s population. Foods such as grapes, walnuts and carrots also traveled along it."]],
sp:["Marco Polo","Marco Polo, a merchant from Venice, traveled to China with his father and uncle in 1271 and stayed for about 17 years, serving the Mongol emperor Kublai Khan. Back in Europe, he was captured in a war and dictated his story in prison to a writer named Rustichello. The book described paper money, coal, huge cities and riches that amazed Europeans. Some scholars have doubted parts of his account, but it inspired explorers, including Columbus."],
g:[["Caravan","A group of traders traveling together"],["Caravanserai","A roadside inn for caravans"],["Oasis","A fertile spot in a desert with water"],["Sericulture","The raising of silkworms to make silk"]],
f:["China’s modern Belt and Road Initiative is named after the old Silk Road.","A single silk cocoon can be unwound into a thread nearly 1 km long.","The Silk Roads of the Chang’an–Tianshan corridor were listed as a World Heritage Site in 2014."]},
space:{s:[
["Sputnik shock","When Sputnik 1, a metal sphere with four antennas, began beeping from orbit in October 1957, Americans were shocked that the Soviets had taken the lead. The United States responded by creating NASA in 1958, increasing funding for science education and launching its own satellite, Explorer 1, which discovered the Van Allen radiation belts."],
["Animals in space","Before people, animals tested whether living creatures could survive spaceflight. The dog Laika orbited Earth in 1957 but died during the flight. In 1960 the Soviet dogs Belka and Strelka orbited and returned safely. The United States sent chimpanzees, including Ham in 1961. These flights proved that humans could survive weightlessness."],
["The Mercury and Gemini programs","America’s first astronauts, the Mercury Seven, became national heroes. Alan Shepard made the first American spaceflight in 1961, and John Glenn orbited Earth in 1962. The two-person Gemini missions of 1965 and 1966 practiced spacewalks, long flights and docking two spacecraft, skills needed for Apollo."],
["Tragedy and courage","Spaceflight was dangerous. In January 1967 three astronauts died in a fire during a test of Apollo 1. Months later, Soviet cosmonaut Vladimir Komarov died when his Soyuz 1 capsule’s parachute failed. In 1970 Apollo 13 was crippled by an explosion on the way to the Moon, but the crew returned safely after a tense four days."],
["The Soviet Moon program","The Soviet Union also tried to land cosmonauts on the Moon, but its giant N1 rocket exploded on all four test launches between 1969 and 1972. The Soviets instead sent robotic missions: Luna 16 brought back lunar soil in 1970, and Lunokhod 1, the first rover on another world, explored the Moon’s surface the same year."],
["Space stations and beyond","After the Moon race, both countries turned to space stations. The Soviets launched Salyut 1 in 1971 and later Mir, while the Americans launched Skylab in 1973. The Space Shuttle flew from 1981 to 2011. Today many countries, including China and India, have space programs, and private companies launch rockets and plan trips to the Moon and Mars."]],
sp:["Apollo 11","On 16 July 1969 a Saturn V rocket launched Neil Armstrong, Buzz Aldrin and Michael Collins toward the Moon. On 20 July the lunar module Eagle landed in the Sea of Tranquility with less than a minute of fuel to spare. Hours later Armstrong stepped onto the surface, followed by Aldrin, while Collins orbited above. About 600 million people watched on television. They planted a flag, collected rocks and left a plaque reading “We came in peace for all mankind.”"],
g:[["Cosmonaut","A Russian or Soviet astronaut"],["Lunar module","The spacecraft that landed on the Moon"],["Orbit","The curved path of an object around a planet or star"],["Satellite","An object that orbits a planet"]],
f:["The Apollo Guidance Computer had less memory than a modern pocket calculator.","Gagarin’s flight in 1961 lasted 108 minutes.","Footprints left on the Moon could last millions of years because there is no wind to erase them."]},
greece:{s:[
["Minoans and Mycenaeans","Before classical Greece, the Minoans built palaces on Crete, such as Knossos, around 2000 to 1450 BCE, decorated with colorful frescoes of dolphins and bull-leaping. On the mainland, the warlike Mycenaeans built fortress cities such as Mycenae. Their world collapsed around 1200 BCE, leading to a dark age before Greek civilization revived."],
["Democracy in Athens","In Athenian democracy, all male citizens could speak and vote in the assembly, which met about 40 times a year on a hill called the Pnyx. Many officials were chosen by lot. Citizens could vote to exile a dangerous politician for ten years by writing his name on pieces of pottery called ostraka, the origin of the word ostracism. Women, slaves and foreigners could not vote."],
["Life in Sparta","Spartan boys left home at seven to train in the agoge, a tough military school. They learned to endure hunger, cold and pain and became full-time soldiers. Spartan women were unusually free, exercising in public and running households. Most work was done by helots, an enslaved population who greatly outnumbered the Spartans."],
["The Golden Age of Athens","After defeating Persia, Athens became rich and powerful, leading the Delian League of allied cities. Under the statesman Pericles in the 400s BCE, the city built the Parthenon and other temples on the Acropolis. Playwrights, historians such as Herodotus and Thucydides, sculptors such as Phidias and philosophers made it a center of learning."],
["Alexander the Great","Alexander became king of Macedon at 20 in 336 BCE. In about ten years he conquered the Persian Empire, Egypt and lands as far as India, never losing a battle. He founded many cities named Alexandria. He died in Babylon at 32 in 323 BCE, and his generals divided his empire, spreading Greek language and culture across the Hellenistic world."],
["Greek legacy","Greek ideas shaped the Western world: democracy, philosophy, theater, history writing, geometry, medicine and the Olympic Games. Greek architecture inspired buildings from the US Capitol to the British Museum in London. Many English words in science and medicine, such as biology, telephone and democracy, come from Greek roots."]],
sp:["The Parthenon","The Parthenon, built between 447 and 432 BCE on the Acropolis of Athens, was a temple to Athena, the city’s protector. Designed by Ictinus and Callicrates, with sculpture overseen by Phidias, it held a 12-meter statue of the goddess made of gold and ivory. Its columns bulge slightly and lean inward, subtle refinements that make it look perfectly straight. In 1687 an explosion of stored gunpowder badly damaged it."],
g:[["Polis","A Greek city-state"],["Democracy","Government by the people"],["Acropolis","A high citadel of a Greek city"],["Hellenistic","The Greek-influenced period after Alexander"]],
f:["The marathon race recalls a legendary runner who carried news of victory from Marathon to Athens.","Greek doctors still take a version of the Hippocratic Oath.","The ancient Olympic Games were held every four years for more than a thousand years."]},
mongol:{s:[
["Mongol warfare","Each Mongol warrior had several horses, so he could switch to a fresh one and ride great distances. Armies used signal flags and drums to coordinate movements. They learned to besiege cities using catapults and engineers from conquered lands, and they used terror to make cities surrender, though they rewarded those who submitted."],
["The Yam postal system","To control their vast empire, the Mongols built the Yam, a network of relay stations a day’s ride apart, where messengers could get food, rest and fresh horses. Messages could travel hundreds of kilometers a day. Officials carried a paiza, a metal tablet that gave them the right to use the system."],
["Religion and tolerance","The Mongols followed traditional beliefs in Tengri, the eternal blue sky, and shamans, but they generally tolerated other religions. Christians, Muslims, Buddhists and Taoists lived and worked at the Mongol court. Over time, Mongol rulers in different regions converted, with the Ilkhans and the Golden Horde becoming Muslim and the Yuan emperors favoring Tibetan Buddhism."],
["Kublai Khan and the Yuan","Kublai Khan, Genghis’s grandson, completed the conquest of China in 1279, defeating the Song dynasty. He built a new capital, Khanbaliq, on the site of modern Beijing, and a summer palace at Xanadu. He tried twice to invade Japan and also attacked Vietnam and Java, with little success. The Yuan dynasty ruled China until 1368."],
["The Golden Horde","In the west, Batu Khan, another grandson of Genghis, conquered the Russian principalities and founded the Golden Horde. Russian princes paid tribute to the khans for about 240 years. Moscow gradually grew strong by collecting this tribute and in 1480 finally stopped paying, ending the so-called Tatar yoke."],
["Mongolia today","Modern Mongolia is an independent democratic country of about 3.5 million people, with Ulaanbaatar as its capital. Many Mongolians still herd animals and live in gers. Genghis Khan is honored as the father of the nation, and the Naadam festival each July features wrestling, horse racing and archery, the three manly sports."]],
sp:["Genghis Khan’s rise","Temüjin’s father was poisoned when Temüjin was about nine, and his family was abandoned by their clan. They survived by fishing and gathering roots. As a young man Temüjin was captured and enslaved but escaped. He built loyal friendships and alliances, defeated rival chiefs and united the Mongol, Tatar, Merkit and other tribes. At a great assembly in 1206 he was proclaimed Genghis Khan. His burial place remains unknown."],
g:[["Khan","A Mongol ruler or chief"],["Steppe","A vast grassland plain"],["Ger","A round felt tent, also called a yurt"],["Khanate","A state ruled by a khan"]],
f:["At about 24 million km², the Mongol Empire was roughly the size of North America.","Some studies suggest millions of people today may descend from Genghis Khan.","The Mongols used gunpowder weapons learned from the Chinese in some of their campaigns."]},
frrev:{s:[
["The Old Regime","Before 1789 France was an absolute monarchy. King Louis XVI ruled from the grand palace of Versailles, built by Louis XIV. The clergy and nobility, together only a small part of the population, owned much of the land and paid few taxes. Peasants paid taxes, church tithes and dues to their lords, and many lived in poverty."],
["The storming of the Bastille","On 14 July 1789, Parisians fearing that the king would use troops to crush the National Assembly stormed the Bastille, a fortress prison that symbolized royal tyranny, seeking gunpowder. They freed the few prisoners inside. The event became the symbol of the revolution, and 14 July is France’s national holiday, Bastille Day."],
["Rights of Man","In August 1789 the National Assembly adopted the Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen, which said that men are born free and equal in rights, including liberty, property, security and resistance to oppression. Olympe de Gouges answered with a Declaration of the Rights of Woman in 1791, demanding equality for women."],
["The fall of the king","In 1791 the royal family tried to flee Paris in disguise but was recognized and stopped at Varennes. Trust in the king collapsed. In 1792 crowds stormed the Tuileries Palace, the monarchy was abolished and France became a republic. Louis XVI was tried for treason and guillotined in January 1793, and Marie Antoinette followed in October."],
["Napoleon","Napoleon Bonaparte, a young general from Corsica, won fame in Italy and Egypt and seized power in 1799. He crowned himself emperor in 1804. His Napoleonic Code organized French law and spread across Europe. His armies conquered much of Europe until defeat in Russia in 1812 and final defeat at Waterloo in 1815, after which he was exiled to Saint Helena."],
["Symbols of the revolution","The revolution created lasting symbols: the blue, white and red tricolor flag; the anthem La Marseillaise, composed in 1792; Marianne, a woman representing the republic; and the motto Liberty, Equality, Fraternity. The revolutionary calendar renamed months after nature and started counting years from 1792, though it was abandoned in 1806."]],
sp:["The guillotine","The guillotine, named after Dr. Joseph-Ignace Guillotin, who proposed a more humane, equal method of execution, was first used in April 1792. A heavy, angled blade dropped between two posts. During the Terror it became a grim symbol, used to execute the king and queen, nobles, revolutionaries who fell from favor and ordinary people accused of disloyalty. France continued to use the guillotine until 1977 and abolished the death penalty in 1981."],
g:[["Estates-General","An assembly of the three estates of France"],["Bastille","A Paris fortress prison stormed in 1789"],["Jacobins","A radical political club during the revolution"],["Republic","A state without a monarch"]],
f:["The metric system of meters and kilograms was introduced during the French Revolution.","La Marseillaise got its name from volunteers from Marseille who sang it as they marched to Paris.","Marie Antoinette probably never said “Let them eat cake.”"]},
wwi:{s:[
["The assassination","On 28 June 1914 Archduke Franz Ferdinand, heir to the Austro-Hungarian throne, and his wife Sophie visited Sarajevo in Bosnia. After a failed bomb attack, their driver took a wrong turn, and a young Bosnian Serb, Gavrilo Princip, shot them both. Within six weeks, Europe’s great powers were at war."],
["Life in the trenches","Soldiers lived in muddy, rat-infested trenches, often knee-deep in water, under constant threat of shells and snipers. Disease, lice and trench foot were common. Between the opposing lines lay no man’s land, full of barbed wire and craters. Attacks over the top often ended in slaughter by machine guns."],
["New weapons","World War I introduced deadly new technology. Poison gas, first used on a large scale at Ypres in 1915, caused choking and blindness. Tanks first appeared at the Somme in 1916. Airplanes were used for scouting and then for dogfights and bombing. Submarines sank ships, and zeppelins bombed cities."],
["The home front","Governments mobilized whole economies for war. Women took jobs in factories, farms and offices, replacing men at the front. Food was rationed, and propaganda posters urged people to enlist and buy war bonds. In many countries, women’s war work helped win them the right to vote after the war."],
["The Christmas truce","On Christmas Eve and Christmas Day 1914, along parts of the Western Front, British and German soldiers stopped fighting. They sang carols, climbed out of their trenches, exchanged gifts and cigarettes and, in some places, played football in no man’s land. Officers later banned such truces."],
["Aftermath and memory","The Treaty of Versailles of 1919 forced Germany to accept blame, give up territory and pay reparations, which caused resentment that helped lead to World War II. New countries, such as Poland, Czechoslovakia and Yugoslavia, appeared. The red poppy, from the poem In Flanders Fields, became a symbol of remembrance on 11 November."]],
sp:["The Battle of the Somme","On 1 July 1916 British and French forces attacked German lines along the Somme River after a week-long artillery bombardment meant to destroy the enemy defenses. It failed. On the first day alone, the British army suffered about 57,000 casualties, including nearly 20,000 dead, the worst day in its history. The battle dragged on until November, with over a million soldiers killed or wounded on both sides, for an advance of only about 10 km."],
g:[["Armistice","An agreement to stop fighting"],["Trench","A long ditch dug to shelter soldiers"],["No man’s land","The ground between opposing trenches"],["Reparations","Payments for war damage"]],
f:["About 65 million soldiers were mobilized during World War I.","The war was called the war to end all wars, but another world war followed 21 years later.","More than a million soldiers from India fought for Britain in World War I."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-hist3.js */
// In-depth material (History, part 3): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
phrev:{s:[
["Roots of discontent","By the late 1800s many Filipinos resented Spanish rule. Friars controlled vast estates and local life, Filipino priests were denied parishes, taxes and forced labor were heavy, and Filipinos had no voice in government. Revolts had broken out for centuries, but the execution of the Gomburza priests in 1872 helped awaken a sense of shared Filipino identity."],
["The outbreak","When a Katipunan member revealed the society’s existence to a Spanish priest in August 1896, arrests began. Bonifacio called the Katipuneros to rise up. Their first major attack, on the Spanish powder magazine at San Juan del Monte on 30 August, failed. Spain declared a state of war in eight provinces, which are honored by the eight rays of the sun on the Philippine flag."],
["Martyrs and arrests","Spanish authorities arrested and executed many suspected rebels. In Cavite, thirteen men, the Thirteen Martyrs of Cavite, were executed in September 1896. In Bagumbayan, Rizal was executed in December, though he had not supported the revolt. In Bicol, fifteen men known as the Quince Mártires were also executed in January 1897. These deaths angered Filipinos and drove more to join the fight."],
["Women of the revolution","Women played vital roles. Melchora Aquino, called Tandang Sora, aged 84, fed and nursed wounded Katipuneros and was exiled to Guam. Gabriela Silang had led an earlier revolt. Trinidad Tecson, the Mother of Biak-na-Bato, fought in battles and cared for the wounded. Teresa Magbanua, a schoolteacher from Iloilo, led troops in the Visayas."],
["The revolution spreads","After the 1898 return of Aguinaldo, the revolution spread rapidly across Luzon and to the Visayas and Mindanao. Revolutionary governments were formed in Panay, Negros and Cebu. By the time the Americans took Manila in August 1898, Filipino forces controlled most of the archipelago outside the capital."],
["The meaning of the revolution","The Philippine Revolution was the first nationalist revolution in Asia against a Western colonial power. Although the republic it created was defeated by the United States, it gave Filipinos a shared history, heroes, a flag and an anthem, and proved that Filipinos of many regions and languages could unite as one nation."]],
sp:["Apolinario Mabini","Apolinario Mabini was born to a poor family in Tanauan, Batangas, in 1864. He became a lawyer, but in 1896 polio left him paralyzed from the waist down. Carried in a hammock, he became Aguinaldo’s chief adviser, writing decrees and the plan for the government. Called the Sublime Paralytic and the brains of the revolution, he refused to swear allegiance to the United States and was exiled to Guam. He died of cholera in 1903."],
g:[["Katipunero","A member of the Katipunan"],["Martyr","A person who dies for a cause"],["Revolution","A forceful overthrow of a government"],["Cedula","A Spanish-era tax certificate"]],
f:["The eight rays of the sun on the Philippine flag stand for the first eight provinces placed under martial law by Spain in 1896.","Tandang Sora is called the Mother of the Katipunan.","The Thirteen Martyrs of Cavite are honored by a monument in Cavite City."]},
coldwar:{s:[
["The Iron Curtain falls","After World War II, the Soviet Union installed communist governments in Eastern Europe, including Poland, Hungary, Czechoslovakia, Romania and Bulgaria. The United States responded with the Truman Doctrine of 1947, promising to help countries resist communism, and the Marshall Plan, which gave billions of dollars to rebuild Western Europe."],
["The Berlin Airlift","In 1948 the Soviets blocked all road and rail routes into West Berlin, hoping to force the Western Allies out. Instead, American and British planes flew in food, coal and supplies for nearly a year, at times landing every few minutes, about 2.3 million tonnes in all. The Soviets lifted the blockade in May 1949."],
["The nuclear arms race","The Soviet Union tested its first atomic bomb in 1949, ending the American monopoly. Both sides then built far more powerful hydrogen bombs and missiles that could reach the other’s cities in minutes. The idea of mutually assured destruction, that any nuclear war would destroy both sides, made leaders cautious. People built fallout shelters and practiced drills."],
["Wars in Asia","The Cold War turned hot in Asia. In the Korean War of 1950 to 1953, United Nations forces from 16 countries fought North Korean and Chinese troops. In Vietnam, the United States and its allies fought communist forces until 1973, and Saigon fell in 1975."],
["The space race","The rivalry spread to space. The Soviet Union launched Sputnik 1, the first satellite, in 1957 and sent Yuri Gagarin into orbit in 1961. The United States answered with the Apollo program and landed astronauts on the Moon in 1969. The race ended in cooperation: in 1975 American and Soviet spacecraft docked in orbit, and their crews shook hands."],
["How it ended","In the 1980s Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev and US President Ronald Reagan held summits and signed treaties to reduce nuclear weapons. The Soviet economy was failing, and Eastern Europeans demanded freedom. In 1989 the Berlin Wall fell, Germany reunited in 1990, and on 26 December 1991 the Soviet Union was formally dissolved."]],
sp:["The Cuban Missile Crisis","In October 1962 American spy planes discovered Soviet nuclear missiles being installed in Cuba, about 150 km from Florida. President John F. Kennedy ordered a naval blockade of Cuba and demanded the missiles be removed. For 13 tense days the world feared nuclear war. Finally, Soviet leader Nikita Khrushchev agreed to remove the missiles in exchange for an American promise not to invade Cuba and the secret removal of US missiles from Turkey."],
g:[["Superpower","An extremely powerful nation"],["Communism","A system in which the state controls property and the economy"],["Proxy war","A war fought through allies rather than directly"],["Iron Curtain","The boundary dividing Western and Eastern Europe"]],
f:["Pieces of the Berlin Wall are displayed in cities around the world.","The term Cold War was popularized in 1947 by American journalist Walter Lippmann.","The Cold War space race put the first person in orbit in 1961."]},
aztec:{s:[
["The Mexica migration","According to their own accounts, the Mexica came from a homeland called Aztlán in the north, from which the name Aztec comes. They wandered for generations, guided by their god Huitzilopochtli, before settling on the marshy island in Lake Texcoco. Neighboring peoples at first looked down on them, but they became skilled warriors and allies."],
["Society and classes","At the top of Aztec society was the huey tlatoani, the great speaker or emperor, followed by nobles, priests and warriors. Most people were commoners, macehualtin, who farmed, fished and made crafts, organized into neighborhoods called calpulli. Merchants, pochteca, traveled far and also acted as spies. There were also enslaved people, often debtors or captives."],
["Education","Aztec children were educated at home and at school. Sons of nobles attended the calmecac, where priests taught history, religion, writing and astronomy. Commoners’ sons went to the telpochcalli, which trained them to be warriors. Aztec education was unusual for its time in that nearly all boys attended some kind of school."],
["Religion and sacrifice","The Aztecs believed the gods had sacrificed themselves to create the world and keep the sun moving, and that humans must repay them with offerings, including human blood. Captives taken in war were sacrificed at temples, especially the Templo Mayor. Wars called flower wars were fought partly to capture prisoners for sacrifice."],
["Writing and art","The Aztecs used pictorial writing, painted in codices on deerskin or bark paper, to record history, tribute and calendars. Artisans made feathered headdresses, gold jewelry, turquoise mosaics and huge stone sculptures, such as the Sun Stone. After the conquest, Spanish friars worked with Aztec scholars to record their culture in works such as the Florentine Codex."],
["Legacy in Mexico","Aztec heritage lives on in Mexico. About 1.6 million people still speak Nahuatl, the Aztec language, and words such as chocolate, tomato, avocado and chili come from it. Mexico City stands on the site of Tenochtitlan, and the ruins of the Templo Mayor lie beside its cathedral. The eagle on a cactus appears on the Mexican flag."]],
sp:["Moctezuma II","Moctezuma II became huey tlatoani in 1502 and ruled the empire at its height. He was a warrior and priest who expanded the empire through conquest. When Cortés arrived in 1519, Moctezuma welcomed him with gifts, possibly hoping to avoid war or unsure whether the Spaniards were men or gods. The Spaniards took him hostage in his own city, and he died in 1520 during an uprising, by Spanish accounts struck by stones thrown by his own people."],
g:[["Mexica","The people who founded Tenochtitlan, often called Aztecs"],["Chinampa","A floating garden built on a lake"],["Tlatoani","Ruler, literally speaker"],["Nahuatl","The language of the Aztecs"]],
f:["Tenochtitlan was larger than any city in Spain when Cortés arrived.","The Aztec Sun Stone weighs about 24 tonnes.","The Aztecs played a ball game called ullamaliztli with a rubber ball."]},
ottoman:{s:[
["The fall of Constantinople","In April 1453 Sultan Mehmed II, aged 21, besieged Constantinople with a huge army and giant cannons, one of which fired stone balls weighing hundreds of kilograms. To get around a chain blocking the harbor, his men dragged ships overland on greased logs. On 29 May the city fell, ending the Byzantine Empire after more than 1,000 years. Mehmed became known as the Conqueror."],
["Suleiman the Magnificent","Suleiman I, who ruled for 46 years, expanded the empire into Hungary, Iraq and North Africa, and his navy dominated the Mediterranean. Europeans called him the Magnificent; Ottomans called him Kanuni, the Lawgiver, for his legal reforms. His reign saw great achievements in architecture, poetry and art. His wife, Hürrem Sultan, was unusually influential."],
["The janissaries","The janissaries were an elite corps of infantry, among the first standing armies in Europe. Boys from Christian families in the Balkans were taken through the devshirme, converted to Islam and trained as soldiers or officials. Over time the janissaries became powerful and resisted reform. In 1826 Sultan Mahmud II abolished them."],
["Daily life and culture","Ottoman cities had markets called bazaars, public baths called hammams, coffeehouses and mosques with charitable kitchens and schools. The Grand Bazaar of Istanbul, begun in the 1450s, is one of the oldest and largest covered markets in the world. Ottoman cuisine, music, poetry and shadow puppet theater influenced lands from the Balkans to the Middle East."],
["The sick man of Europe","In the 1800s the empire lost territory to nationalist uprisings, such as Greek independence in 1830, and to Russia and other powers. Europeans called it the sick man of Europe. Reforms modernized the army and created a constitution in 1876, briefly. The Young Turks seized power in 1908, and the empire joined World War I on Germany’s side."],
["The end of the empire","During World War I, the Ottoman government carried out mass deportations and killings of Armenians, which many historians and countries recognize as genocide. After defeat, the empire was occupied and divided. Mustafa Kemal led a war of independence and founded the Republic of Turkey in 1923, abolishing the sultanate in 1922 and the caliphate in 1924."]],
sp:["Topkapı Palace","For about 400 years, Topkapı Palace in Istanbul was the residence of the sultans and the center of Ottoman government. Built after 1459 on a hill overlooking the Bosphorus and the Golden Horn, it had four courtyards, kitchens that fed thousands, a treasury, a library and the harem, where the sultan’s family lived. Today it is a museum displaying jewels such as the Spoonmaker’s Diamond and the Topkapı Dagger."],
g:[["Sultan","The ruler of the Ottoman Empire"],["Grand vizier","The sultan’s chief minister"],["Janissary","A soldier of the elite Ottoman infantry"],["Millet","A self-governing religious community in the empire"]],
f:["Tulips were brought to Europe from the Ottoman Empire in the 1500s.","The Ottoman Empire lasted about 623 years.","Istanbul has been the capital of three empires: Roman, Byzantine and Ottoman."]},
china:{s:[
["The Han dynasty","The Han dynasty, ruling for about 400 years, was so important that the main ethnic group in China still calls itself the Han people. Han emperors adopted Confucianism as the state philosophy, opened the Silk Road and expanded the empire. Cai Lun is traditionally credited with improving paper-making in 105 CE. The Han capital, Chang’an, rivaled Rome."],
["The Tang and Song","The Tang dynasty was a golden age of poetry, with poets such as Li Bai and Du Fu, and of trade, art and Buddhism. Its capital, Chang’an, had perhaps a million people. The Song dynasty that followed saw great advances in technology, such as movable type, gunpowder weapons and the magnetic compass, and the first paper money issued by a government."],
["Zheng He’s voyages","Between 1405 and 1433, the Ming admiral Zheng He led seven great voyages with fleets of hundreds of ships, some of them huge treasure ships. They sailed through Southeast Asia, around India and as far as Arabia and East Africa, bringing back exotic gifts, including a giraffe. Then the Ming court stopped the voyages and turned inward."],
["The Forbidden City","The Forbidden City in Beijing was the palace of the Ming and Qing emperors from 1420 to 1912. Surrounded by walls and a moat, it has about 980 buildings. Ordinary people could not enter without permission, which gave it its name. Its yellow roof tiles were reserved for the emperor. Today it is the Palace Museum, visited by millions each year."],
["Contact with the West","In the 1700s China traded tea, silk and porcelain to Europe but wanted little in return. Britain began selling opium from India to China, causing addiction. When China tried to stop it, Britain won the First Opium War from 1839 to 1842 and took Hong Kong. Other unequal treaties followed, weakening the Qing dynasty."],
["Revolution and the end of empire","Rebellions such as the Taiping Rebellion, which killed millions, and the Boxer Rebellion of 1900 shook the Qing. Reformers and revolutionaries, led by Sun Yat-sen, sought change. The Wuchang Uprising of October 1911 triggered a revolution, and in 1912 the Republic of China was founded, ending over 2,000 years of imperial rule."]],
sp:["The Terracotta Army","In 1974 farmers digging a well near Xi’an discovered fragments of clay figures. Archaeologists uncovered an army of more than 7,000 life-size terracotta soldiers, along with horses and chariots, buried to guard the tomb of Qin Shi Huang, who died in 210 BCE. Each soldier has a different face and hairstyle, and they were once brightly painted. The emperor’s own burial mound has not yet been excavated."],
g:[["Dynasty","A series of rulers from the same family"],["Mandate of Heaven","The belief that heaven grants the right to rule"],["Civil service examination","Tests used to choose government officials"],["Porcelain","A fine, white ceramic first made in China"]],
f:["The Great Wall, with all its branches, is about 21,000 km long, according to a 2012 survey.","Chinese porcelain was so prized in Europe that it came to be called china.","Tea drinking became widespread in China during the Tang dynasty."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-hist4.js */
// In-depth material (History, part 4): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
explor:{s:[
["Prince Henry and Portugal","Prince Henry of Portugal, called the Navigator though he rarely sailed, funded expeditions down the west coast of Africa from the 1420s. Portuguese sailors reached Madeira, the Azores and Cape Verde, and pushed past Cape Bojador, which sailors had feared. In 1488 Bartolomeu Dias rounded the southern tip of Africa, opening the way to the Indian Ocean."],
["Columbus’s voyages","Christopher Columbus, an Italian navigator, believed he could reach Asia by sailing west across the Atlantic. Spain’s Queen Isabella and King Ferdinand funded his voyage, and his three ships, the Niña, the Pinta and the Santa María, reached the Bahamas in October 1492. He made four voyages and died believing he had reached Asia."],
["Life at sea","Long voyages were dangerous and miserable. Sailors ate hard biscuits, salted meat and dried fish, often infested with insects, and drinking water turned foul. Scurvy, caused by a lack of vitamin C, killed many. Storms, shipwrecks, mutinies and unknown coasts added to the risks. Fewer than one in ten of Magellan’s crew completed the voyage."],
["The Columbian Exchange","Contact between the Americas and the rest of the world led to an exchange of plants, animals and diseases called the Columbian Exchange. Maize, potatoes, tomatoes, cacao and chili went to Europe, Africa and Asia, while wheat, rice, sugarcane, horses, cattle and pigs went to the Americas. European diseases such as smallpox killed a large share of the Indigenous peoples of the Americas."],
["Conquest and colonies","Spanish conquistadors toppled the Aztec Empire in 1521 and the Inca Empire in the 1530s, taking enormous quantities of gold and silver. Portugal built trading posts across the Indian Ocean and colonized Brazil. Colonial rule brought new religions, languages and cities, but also forced labor, slavery and great suffering for Indigenous peoples and enslaved Africans."],
["Maps and the shape of the world","Exploration transformed maps. The Waldseemüller map of 1507 was the first to use the name America, after the navigator Amerigo Vespucci. Gerardus Mercator’s map projection of 1569 made navigation easier. By the 1600s, Europeans knew the outlines of most continents, though Australia, Antarctica and the Pacific remained partly unknown."]],
sp:["Vasco da Gama","In 1497 Vasco da Gama sailed from Lisbon with four ships, rounded the Cape of Good Hope and crossed the Indian Ocean with the help of a local pilot, reaching Calicut in India in May 1498. He returned with spices worth many times the cost of the voyage, though more than half his crew died. His voyage created a direct sea route from Europe to Asia and made Portugal a great trading power for a century."],
g:[["Caravel","A small, fast sailing ship used by explorers"],["Navigator","A person who plans and directs a ship’s route"],["Columbian Exchange","The transfer of plants, animals and diseases between hemispheres"],["Circumnavigation","Sailing all the way around the world"]],
f:["The Santa María ran aground on Hispaniola on Christmas Day 1492.","Magellan named the Pacific Ocean, meaning peaceful, because its waters seemed calm.","About 18 of the roughly 270 men who set out with Magellan returned to Spain on the Victoria."]},
usrev:{s:[
["The thirteen colonies","By the 1770s about 2.5 million people lived in the thirteen British colonies along the Atlantic coast, from New Hampshire to Georgia. They had their own elected assemblies and a strong sense of their rights as British subjects. About a fifth of the population were enslaved Africans, mainly in the southern colonies."],
["Lexington and Concord","On 19 April 1775 British troops marched from Boston to seize colonial weapons stored at Concord. Riders, including Paul Revere, warned local militias. At Lexington, shots were fired, and fighting spread. The British were harassed by colonial militiamen all the way back to Boston. These were the first battles of the war."],
["Common Sense","In January 1776 Thomas Paine published Common Sense, a pamphlet arguing in plain language that the colonies should break free from the king. It sold more than 100,000 copies within months, an enormous number at the time, and helped persuade many colonists to support independence."],
["Women and enslaved people in the war","Women ran farms and businesses, made supplies and sometimes followed armies as cooks and nurses. A few, such as Deborah Sampson, disguised themselves as men and fought. Enslaved people fought on both sides; Britain promised freedom to those who escaped to its lines, and thousands did. Yet slavery continued in the new nation until 1865."],
["Foreign allies","After the American victory at Saratoga in 1777, France signed an alliance with the United States in 1778, sending soldiers, ships, money and supplies. Spain and the Netherlands also joined the war against Britain. The French navy’s victory at the Battle of the Chesapeake in 1781 helped trap the British army at Yorktown."],
["The revolution’s influence","The American Revolution inspired other movements, including the French Revolution and independence struggles in Latin America. Its ideas of natural rights, government by consent and a written constitution spread worldwide, and many later constitutions, from Latin America to Liberia, borrowed from the United States Constitution."]],
sp:["George Washington","George Washington, a Virginia planter and former officer, was chosen to lead the Continental Army in 1775. Though he lost many battles, he kept the army together through hardships such as the winter at Valley Forge, and his surprise crossing of the icy Delaware River on Christmas night 1776 led to a victory at Trenton. After the war he became the first president of the United States, serving two terms and then stepping down voluntarily."],
g:[["Colony","A territory ruled by another country"],["Militia","Citizen soldiers who train part-time"],["Loyalist","A colonist who stayed loyal to Britain"],["Patriot","A colonist who supported independence"]],
f:["The Declaration of Independence was signed by 56 delegates.","Benjamin Franklin helped persuade France to join the war.","The Liberty Bell in Philadelphia became a symbol of independence."]},
viking:{s:[
["Why the Vikings raided","Historians suggest several reasons for the Viking raids: a growing population at home, a shortage of good farmland, the chance to win wealth and fame, and new ship technology. Monasteries were especially tempting targets because they held gold, silver and valuables and were poorly defended."],
["Raiders and rulers","Vikings raided coasts and rivers across Europe, from Britain and Ireland to France, Spain and the Mediterranean. In 845 they sailed up the Seine and attacked Paris. They founded Dublin, ruled a large area of England called the Danelaw, and in 911 their leader Rollo was granted land in France that became Normandy. The Norman conquest of England in 1066 was led by his descendant, William."],
["Rus and the east","Swedish Vikings, called Varangians or Rus, traveled along the rivers of Eastern Europe, trading furs, amber and slaves for silver. They helped found states centered on Novgorod and Kyiv, and the name Russia may come from the Rus. Some served as elite bodyguards to the Byzantine emperor, called the Varangian Guard."],
["Daily life","Viking families lived in longhouses, with a central hearth and space for people and animals. Women ran the household, managed farms while men were away and could own property and seek divorce. Vikings enjoyed board games, storytelling, music and feasts. They were skilled craftsmen in metal, wood and textiles."],
["Burials and treasure","Important Vikings were sometimes buried with ships, weapons, jewelry and even horses. The Oseberg ship burial in Norway held two women and rich goods, including a carved cart and sledges. Hoards of silver coins and jewelry buried for safekeeping, such as the Spillings hoard in Sweden, show how far Viking trade reached."],
["The end of the Viking Age","As Scandinavian kingdoms became Christian and more centralized in the 1000s, raiding declined. Kings such as Harald Bluetooth and Olaf Tryggvason promoted Christianity. The defeat of Harald Hardrada at Stamford Bridge in 1066 is often seen as the end of the Viking Age. Norse culture continued in Iceland, Scandinavia and the places the Vikings had settled."]],
sp:["L’Anse aux Meadows","At L’Anse aux Meadows, on the northern tip of Newfoundland in Canada, archaeologists Helge and Anne Stine Ingstad found the remains of Norse buildings in the 1960s, proving that Vikings reached North America about 500 years before Columbus. The site includes the remains of halls, workshops and a forge. Tree-ring studies of cut wood show that Norse people were there in the year 1021. It is a World Heritage Site."],
g:[["Norse","The people and language of medieval Scandinavia"],["Longship","A long, narrow Viking ship"],["Rune","A letter of the Viking alphabet"],["Saga","A long prose story from medieval Iceland"]],
f:["Bluetooth technology is named after the Viking king Harald Bluetooth.","Vikings in Greenland abandoned their settlements by the 1400s.","English words such as husband, knife, skull and window come from Old Norse."]},
reform:{s:[
["Indulgences","In the medieval Church, an indulgence was a pardon that reduced the punishment believed to await sinners after death. In the early 1500s the Church sold indulgences to raise money, including to rebuild St. Peter’s Basilica in Rome. A preacher named Johann Tetzel was especially famous for selling them, which provoked Luther’s protest."],
["The power of printing","The printing press made the Reformation possible on a large scale. Luther’s writings were printed in huge numbers; between 1518 and 1525 his works made up a large share of all books printed in German. Woodcut pictures and pamphlets carried reform ideas to people who could not read well. The Catholic Church also used printing to spread its responses."],
["The Peasants’ War","Inspired partly by reformers’ ideas of freedom, German peasants rose up in 1524 and 1525 against their lords, demanding fairer treatment. Luther condemned the revolt. The princes crushed it, and around 100,000 peasants were killed. The war showed how religious and social discontent could mix."],
["Calvin and Geneva","John Calvin, a French theologian, led the Reformation in Geneva from 1541. He taught predestination, the idea that God had already chosen who would be saved, and set up a strict church government. Calvinist churches spread to France, where followers were called Huguenots, the Netherlands, Scotland, where John Knox founded Presbyterianism, and later North America."],
["The English Reformation","When the Pope refused to annul Henry VIII’s marriage to Catherine of Aragon, Henry broke with Rome and made himself head of the Church of England in 1534. He closed monasteries and took their wealth. Under his children, England swung between Protestant and Catholic, until Elizabeth I established a moderate Protestant church."],
["Wars of religion","Religious divisions fueled conflict. In France, wars between Catholics and Huguenots included the St. Bartholomew’s Day Massacre of 1572. The Thirty Years’ War of 1618 to 1648, fought mainly in Germany, killed millions. The Peace of Westphalia allowed rulers to choose their territory’s religion and helped shape the modern system of states."]],
sp:["Martin Luther","Martin Luther was born in Eisleben in 1483. As a young man caught in a thunderstorm, he vowed to become a monk. Struggling with fear of God’s judgment, he found peace in the idea that faith alone saves. After the Diet of Worms, Frederick the Wise hid him at Wartburg Castle, where he translated the New Testament into German in about 11 weeks. He later married a former nun, Katharina von Bora, and wrote hymns still sung today."],
g:[["Indulgence","A pardon for sins sold by the medieval Church"],["Protestant","A member of a church that broke from Rome"],["Theses","Statements put forward for debate"],["Counter-Reformation","The Catholic Church’s response to Protestantism"]],
f:["Luther is said to have nailed his Ninety-five Theses to a church door in Wittenberg, though some historians doubt it.","The Jesuit order was founded in 1540 by Ignatius of Loyola.","The King James Bible of 1611 became one of the most influential books in English."]},
mesop:{s:[
["Sumer","The Sumerians, in southern Mesopotamia, built some of the world’s first cities, such as Uruk, Ur, Lagash and Eridu. Uruk may have had 40,000 to 80,000 people by around 3000 BCE, making it among the largest cities of its time. Each city was independent, ruled by a king or priest, and cities often fought over water and land."],
["The Epic of Gilgamesh","The Epic of Gilgamesh, one of the oldest surviving works of literature, tells of Gilgamesh, a king of Uruk, and his friend Enkidu. After Enkidu dies, Gilgamesh searches for the secret of eternal life, meeting Utnapishtim, who survived a great flood. The best-known version was found on clay tablets in the library of Ashurbanipal at Nineveh."],
["Hammurabi’s Babylon","Hammurabi, king of Babylon from about 1792 to 1750 BCE, united much of Mesopotamia. His law code, carved on a tall black stone stele now in the Louvre, contains 282 laws on trade, family, property and crime. It is famous for punishments based on an eye for an eye, though punishments depended on social class."],
["The Assyrians","The Assyrians, from northern Mesopotamia, built a powerful empire between about 900 and 600 BCE, using iron weapons, chariots and siege engines. Their capitals, such as Nineveh and Nimrud, had palaces decorated with carved stone panels showing battles and lion hunts. King Ashurbanipal collected a library of thousands of clay tablets."],
["Daily life","Most people were farmers who grew barley and dates and kept sheep and goats. Craftsmen made pottery, textiles and metal goods, and merchants traded with lands as far as the Indus Valley. Children of wealthy families attended schools called tablet houses, where they learned cuneiform. Beer brewed from barley was a common drink."],
["Mesopotamia today","The ruins of Mesopotamian cities lie in modern Iraq and parts of Syria and Turkey. Archaeologists have excavated Ur, Babylon, Nineveh and many other sites, but wars and looting have damaged some. In 2019 Babylon was added to the World Heritage list. Museums around the world hold Mesopotamian treasures."]],
sp:["Cuneiform","Cuneiform, from the Latin for wedge-shaped, began as simple pictures pressed into clay to record goods such as grain and sheep. Over time the signs became abstract wedges standing for words and syllables. Scribes used a reed stylus to press marks into wet clay, which was then dried or baked. Cuneiform was used for about 3,000 years for many languages. It was deciphered in the 1800s, partly using the Behistun Inscription in Iran."],
g:[["Ziggurat","A stepped temple tower"],["Cuneiform","Wedge-shaped writing on clay"],["Stele","An upright carved stone slab"],["Irrigation","Bringing water to fields through canals"]],
f:["Mesopotamians may have been among the first to brew beer, as early as 5,000 years ago.","The Sumerians divided the year into 12 months based on the Moon.","Hammurabi’s law code stele is about 2.25 m tall."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-geo1.js */
// In-depth material (Geography, part 1): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
ph:{s:[
["Luzon","Luzon, the largest island, holds Metro Manila and about half the country’s people. It has the Cordillera Central and Sierra Madre mountains, the broad Central Luzon plain, called the rice granary, the Cagayan Valley with the country’s longest river, and the volcanic Bicol peninsula in the south. Its northern provinces, such as Ilocos and Batanes, have their own distinct cultures."],
["The Visayas","The Visayas are the central islands, including Panay, Negros, Cebu, Bohol, Leyte and Samar. They are known for sugar plantations in Negros, the historic cities of Cebu and Iloilo, beaches such as Boracay and festivals such as Sinulog and Dinagyang. Most people speak Visayan languages such as Cebuano, Hiligaynon and Waray."],
["Early peoples","The first people to reach the islands may have arrived more than 40,000 years ago, ancestors of today’s Aeta, Ati and other Negrito peoples. Fossils of a small human species, Homo luzonensis, at least 50,000 years old, were found in Callao Cave in Cagayan. Austronesian-speaking farmers and seafarers arrived from Taiwan about 4,000 to 5,000 years ago, bringing rice, pottery and boats."],
["Barangays and trade","Before the Spanish, Filipinos lived in barangays, communities named after the balangay boats that carried early settlers. Each was led by a datu. Society included nobles, freemen and dependents. Barangays traded with China, Japan, Borneo and Southeast Asia, and kingdoms such as Tondo, Maynila, Cebu and Butuan grew rich from commerce."],
["Natural hazards","The Philippines is one of the most disaster-prone countries in the world. Besides typhoons, it faces earthquakes, volcanic eruptions, floods, landslides and tsunamis. The country has invested in early warning systems, evacuation centers and disaster education, and agencies such as PAGASA and PHIVOLCS monitor threats around the clock."],
["Filipinos around the world","About 10 million Filipinos live or work abroad, in the United States, the Middle East, Canada, Japan, Hong Kong, Singapore, Europe and on ships at sea. Large Filipino communities, such as in California, keep their traditions alive. Overseas Filipinos send money home and bring back new ideas, and many return to retire."]],
sp:["The national symbols","The Philippines has many national symbols. The flag carries the sun and three stars, the anthem is Lupang Hinirang and the national motto is Maka-Diyos, Maka-tao, Makakalikasan at Makabansa, for God, people, nature and country. The Philippine eagle is the national bird, the sampaguita the national flower, the narra the national tree and José Rizal the national hero. The carabao, a water buffalo, is often called the national animal."],
g:[["Archipelago","A group of islands"],["Barangay","The smallest unit of local government, and a pre-colonial community"],["Datu","A traditional chief"],["Overseas Filipino","A Filipino living or working abroad"]],
f:["The Philippines has the world’s fifth-longest coastline, about 36,000 km.","Homo luzonensis, a newly identified human species, was announced in 2019.","The Philippines is the only Asian country with a predominantly Catholic population, apart from Timor-Leste."]},
amazon:{s:[
["The Amazon River","The Amazon begins high in the Andes of Peru and flows about 6,400 km east to the Atlantic. It has more than 1,100 tributaries, including the Rio Negro and the Madeira. In places it is so wide that one bank cannot be seen from the other, and at its mouth it pours so much fresh water into the ocean that the sea is less salty far offshore."],
["The meeting of waters","Near Manaus in Brazil, the dark, tea-colored Rio Negro meets the sandy-colored Solimões, the upper Amazon. Because they differ in temperature, speed and density, the two rivers flow side by side without mixing for several kilometers, a spectacle called the Meeting of the Waters."],
["Seasonal floods","Each year the Amazon’s water level rises by up to 10 m or more during the rainy season, flooding huge areas of forest called várzea and igapó. Fish swim among the tree trunks, eating fruits and seeds and spreading them. When the waters fall, the land is enriched with silt, and people plant crops on the riverbanks."],
["Medicines and foods","Many medicines have come from rainforest plants. Quinine, used against malaria, comes from the cinchona tree, and curare, a plant poison used by hunters, led to drugs used in surgery. The Amazon also gave the world foods such as Brazil nuts, açaí berries, cacao and cassava, a staple root for millions of people."],
["Cities in the forest","Manaus, a city of over 2 million people, grew rich during the rubber boom of the late 1800s, when its famous opera house, the Teatro Amazonas, was built. Belém, near the river’s mouth, is a major port. Iquitos in Peru is the largest city in the world that cannot be reached by road, only by river or air."],
["Protecting the Amazon","Protected areas and Indigenous territories now cover a large share of the Brazilian Amazon. Satellites monitor deforestation, and governments, scientists and communities work against illegal logging and mining. Scientists warn that the forest may be approaching a tipping point beyond which large areas could turn to savanna."]],
sp:["The pink river dolphin","The Amazon river dolphin, or boto, is the largest river dolphin, reaching about 2.5 m. Adults can turn bright pink, especially males. Unlike ocean dolphins, it can turn its neck, which helps it hunt fish among flooded tree roots. Local legends say botos can turn into handsome men at night. River dolphins are threatened by pollution, dams and fishing nets."],
g:[["Canopy","The dense upper layer of the forest"],["Tributary","A river that flows into a larger one"],["Deforestation","Clearing forests for other uses"],["Biodiversity","The variety of living things in an area"]],
f:["Although often called the lungs of the planet, the Amazon uses up most of the oxygen it makes, so it adds little to the air overall.","The Amazon is home to an estimated 390 billion trees of about 16,000 species.","More than 3,000 kinds of fish live in the Amazon basin."]},
tect:{s:[
["Wegener’s idea","Alfred Wegener, a German meteorologist, noticed that the coasts of South America and Africa fit together like puzzle pieces. He found matching rocks and fossils, such as the reptile Mesosaurus, on both continents. In 1915 he published The Origin of Continents and Oceans. Most geologists rejected his theory because he could not explain what moved the continents."],
["Divergent boundaries","Where plates pull apart, magma rises to fill the gap. On the seafloor this forms mid-ocean ridges, such as the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, a 16,000 km mountain chain. On land, it forms rift valleys, such as the East African Rift. Iceland is one of the few places where a mid-ocean ridge rises above the sea."],
["Convergent boundaries","Where plates collide, one may sink beneath the other in a subduction zone, forming deep trenches and chains of volcanoes, such as those of Japan, Indonesia and the Andes. When two continents collide, neither sinks easily, so the crust crumples upward into great mountain ranges such as the Himalayas and the Alps."],
["Transform boundaries","Where plates slide past each other, they lock and then slip suddenly, causing earthquakes. The San Andreas Fault in California is a famous transform boundary. The North Anatolian Fault in Türkiye and the Alpine Fault in New Zealand also move sideways and have caused major earthquakes."],
["Magnetic stripes","In the 1950s and 1960s, scientists mapping the ocean floor found stripes of rock with alternating magnetic directions, recording the times Earth’s magnetic field had flipped. The stripes were symmetrical on both sides of mid-ocean ridges, showing that new seafloor forms at the ridges and spreads outward. This was strong evidence for plate tectonics."],
["Measuring the motion","Today GPS stations measure plate movements directly. The Pacific Plate moves northwest at about 7 to 10 cm a year, while the Atlantic widens by about 2.5 cm a year. Over millions of years these small movements add up, opening oceans and building mountains."]],
sp:["Pangaea","About 335 million years ago, nearly all the land on Earth was joined into one supercontinent called Pangaea, meaning all lands, surrounded by a single ocean, Panthalassa. Dinosaurs first appeared while Pangaea still existed. Around 200 million years ago it began splitting into Laurasia in the north and Gondwana in the south, which later broke into today’s continents. This is why fossils of the same land animals are found on continents now far apart."],
g:[["Lithosphere","Earth’s rigid outer layer"],["Subduction","One plate sinking beneath another"],["Fault","A crack in the crust where rocks move"],["Mid-ocean ridge","An underwater mountain range where new crust forms"]],
f:["The Atlantic Ocean widens by about the length of a fingernail each year.","Earth is the only planet known to have active plate tectonics.","Australia is moving north toward Asia at about 7 cm a year."]},
everest:{s:[
["The 1953 ascent","The British expedition of 1953, led by John Hunt, used hundreds of porters and set up a series of camps up the mountain. A first summit team turned back just short of the top. On 29 May Edmund Hillary of New Zealand and Tenzing Norgay, a Sherpa from Nepal, reached the summit at 11:30 a.m. News reached London on the morning of Queen Elizabeth II’s coronation."],
["Routes to the top","Most climbers use the South Col route from Nepal, starting at Base Camp at about 5,360 m and passing through the dangerous Khumbu Icefall, where towering blocks of ice can collapse without warning. Others climb the Northeast Ridge from Tibet. A typical expedition takes about two months, allowing climbers to acclimatize to the altitude."],
["Dangers on the mountain","Climbers face avalanches, falling ice, crevasses, storms, frostbite and altitude sickness. More than 300 people have died on Everest. In 2014 an avalanche in the Khumbu Icefall killed 16 Nepali guides, and in 2015 an earthquake triggered an avalanche that struck Base Camp. Many bodies remain on the mountain because recovering them is so dangerous."],
["Many names","The mountain has several names. In Tibetan it is Chomolungma, often translated as goddess mother of the world, and in Nepali it is Sagarmatha, roughly forehead of the sky. The English name honors George Everest, a British surveyor of India, though he never saw the peak. Sagarmatha National Park, which contains the Nepalese side, became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1979."],
["Records","Kami Rita Sherpa holds the record for the most ascents of Everest, more than 30. Apa Sherpa and Pasang Dawa Sherpa have also climbed it more than 20 times. Climbers have reached the summit without oxygen, in winter, from both sides and even skied down. The fastest ascents have taken less than a day from Base Camp."],
["Protecting the region","Everest lies within Sagarmatha National Park in Nepal, a World Heritage Site since 1979, and the Qomolangma reserve in Tibet. The park protects snow leopards, red pandas and Himalayan forests. Clean-up campaigns have removed tonnes of waste, and Nepal requires climbers to carry their trash down."]],
sp:["Tenzing Norgay","Tenzing Norgay grew up in the Khumbu region and worked as a porter on several British expeditions in the 1930s. By 1953 he had attempted Everest six times. After reaching the summit with Hillary, he buried small offerings of sweets in the snow as a gift to the gods. He became a hero in Nepal and India and later led the Himalayan Mountaineering Institute in Darjeeling, training new generations of climbers."],
g:[["Acclimatization","Adjusting the body to high altitude"],["Icefall","A steep, broken section of a glacier"],["Sherpa","A people of Nepal’s high valleys, famous as mountain guides"],["Crevasse","A deep crack in a glacier"]],
f:["Everest’s summit is made of limestone that formed on an ancient sea floor.","The jet stream can blow across Everest’s summit at over 160 km/h.","Mount Everest’s summit is the highest point on Earth above sea level, but Chimborazo’s summit is farthest from Earth’s center."]},
sahara:{s:[
["Desert peoples","The Tuareg, a Berber people known as the blue men because their indigo-dyed robes stain their skin, have lived in the Sahara for centuries. Men traditionally wear a veil called a tagelmust. Other desert peoples include the Toubou of Chad and the Moors of Mauritania. Many have settled in towns, but some still herd camels and goats."],
["Trans-Saharan trade","For over a thousand years, camel caravans crossed the Sahara, carrying gold, ivory and enslaved people north from West Africa and salt, cloth, horses and books south. Salt was so valuable it was traded weight for weight with gold. Cities such as Timbuktu, Gao and Agadez grew rich from this trade."],
["Timbuktu","Timbuktu, in Mali, became a great center of Islamic learning in the 1300s to 1500s. Its mosques, such as Djinguereyber, are built of mud brick. Scholars gathered tens of thousands of manuscripts on religion, law, astronomy and medicine. In 2012 and 2013 some manuscripts were destroyed by militants, but many were smuggled to safety."],
["Fossils and rock art","The Sahara holds remarkable fossils, such as Spinosaurus, a giant fish-eating dinosaur found in Egypt and Morocco, and Wadi Al-Hitan in Egypt, the valley of whales, where fossils of ancient whales with legs lie in the sand. Rock paintings and engravings at Tassili n’Ajjer in Algeria show giraffes, cattle herds and people swimming, from when the Sahara was green."],
["Life in the dunes","Despite the harsh conditions, many creatures live in the Sahara. Desert locusts can form swarms of billions. The addax, a rare antelope, rarely drinks, getting water from plants. Scorpions, sand vipers and fennec foxes hunt at night. Plants such as acacias have long roots that reach deep for water."],
["The Sahara and the world","Dust from the Sahara is blown across the Atlantic, fertilizing the Amazon rainforest and sometimes turning skies hazy in the Caribbean and Europe. The desert also holds oil and gas, especially in Algeria and Libya, and huge solar power potential. Some projects plan solar farms to supply electricity to Africa and Europe."]],
sp:["The Eye of the Sahara","In the Sahara of Mauritania lies the Richat Structure, a huge set of rings about 40 km across, so large that it is easiest to see from space. Astronauts used it as a landmark. Once thought to be a meteorite crater, it is now known to be an eroded dome of rock layers, pushed up from below and worn down by wind and water into concentric circles."],
g:[["Erg","A large sea of sand dunes"],["Oasis","A fertile spot in the desert with water"],["Desertification","The turning of land into desert"],["Caravan","A group of travelers with pack animals"]],
f:["Sand dunes in the Sahara can move several meters a year, pushed by the wind.","The Sahara is about the size of the United States or China.","Snow has fallen several times in the Algerian desert town of Aïn Séfra."]},
pacific:{s:[
["Polynesian voyagers","Polynesian navigators sailed double-hulled canoes across thousands of kilometers of open ocean, without instruments. They read the stars, the shape of ocean swells, the flight of birds and the color of clouds to find land. They reached Hawaii, New Zealand and Rapa Nui, settling the largest area ever colonized by one people before modern times."],
["European exploration","Vasco Núñez de Balboa was the first European to see the Pacific from the Americas in 1513. Magellan crossed it in 1520 and 1521, a voyage of more than three months on which many sailors starved. In the 1700s James Cook made three great voyages, mapping New Zealand, eastern Australia and Hawaii."],
["El Niño and La Niña","Normally, trade winds push warm water westward across the Pacific toward Asia. During El Niño, these winds weaken and warm water spreads east, bringing floods to Peru and droughts to Indonesia, Australia and Southeast Asia. La Niña is the opposite, often bringing more rain to the western Pacific. These cycles affect weather worldwide."],
["Deep-sea life","The deep Pacific is cold and dark, but it teems with strange life: glowing anglerfish, giant squid, tube worms living around hydrothermal vents that spew hot, mineral-rich water, and snailfish found at depths of over 8,000 m. Scientists discover new species on almost every deep-sea expedition."],
["Pacific nations","The Pacific is surrounded by the Pacific Rim, including China, Japan, Southeast Asia, Australia and the Americas, and dotted with island nations such as Fiji, Samoa, Tonga, Kiribati and Palau. Some low-lying nations, such as Tuvalu and Kiribati, are threatened by rising sea levels, and their leaders campaign for action on climate change."],
["Trade across the Pacific","From 1565 to 1815, Spanish galleons sailed between Manila and Acapulco, the first regular trade route across the Pacific, carrying Chinese silk and porcelain to the Americas and silver back to Asia. Today the Pacific carries much of the world’s trade, with giant container ships linking ports such as Shanghai, Busan, Singapore, Los Angeles and Vancouver."]],
sp:["The Challenger Deep","The Challenger Deep, at the southern end of the Mariana Trench, is the deepest known point in the ocean, about 10,935 m down. If Mount Everest were placed there, its peak would still be more than 2 km underwater. Jacques Piccard and Don Walsh reached it in the bathyscaphe Trieste in 1960, filmmaker James Cameron dove there alone in 2012, and since 2019 several crewed dives have explored it."],
g:[["Ring of Fire","The belt of volcanoes and earthquakes around the Pacific"],["Trench","A deep, narrow valley on the ocean floor"],["Atoll","A ring-shaped coral island"],["El Niño","A periodic warming of the eastern Pacific"]],
f:["The Pacific is wider than the Moon: at its widest it stretches about 19,800 km.","The Pacific holds more than half of Earth’s free water.","Point Nemo in the South Pacific is the place on Earth farthest from any land."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-geo2.js */
// In-depth material (Geography, part 2): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
nile:{s:[
["The Blue Nile","The Blue Nile rises at Lake Tana in the Ethiopian Highlands and plunges over the Blue Nile Falls, called Tis Issat, smoking water, before cutting a deep gorge through the mountains. Summer monsoon rains over Ethiopia swell it enormously, and it supplies most of the Nile’s water during the flood season, along with the rich silt that once fertilized Egypt."],
["The White Nile","The White Nile is the longer branch. It flows out of Lake Victoria in Uganda, passing through Lake Kyoga and Lake Albert and the vast Sudd wetlands of South Sudan, where much of its water evaporates. Its flow is steadier than the Blue Nile’s. The two branches meet at Khartoum, where the different colors of their waters can be seen side by side."],
["Cataracts","Between Khartoum and Aswan, the Nile flows over six cataracts, stretches of rapids and rocks where hard granite resists erosion. They made boat travel difficult and formed natural boundaries. Ancient Egypt’s southern border lay at the first cataract, near Aswan, beyond which lay Nubia, home of the Kingdom of Kush."],
["Nubia and Kush","South of Egypt, in what is now Sudan, the Kingdom of Kush flourished for centuries. Its kings conquered Egypt and ruled as pharaohs around 750 to 650 BCE. At Meroë, they built more than 200 small, steep pyramids, more pyramids than in all of Egypt. Kush was famous for gold, iron working and archers."],
["Boats on the Nile","Ancient Egyptians built boats from papyrus reeds and later from wood. Because the Nile flows north while the wind usually blows south, boats could float downstream with the current and sail upstream with the wind. Today feluccas, cruise ships and ferries carry people and goods, and the river remains a vital transport route."],
["Sharing the waters","Eleven countries share the Nile basin, and about 300 million people depend on it. Egypt relies on the Nile for almost all its water. Ethiopia’s Grand Ethiopian Renaissance Dam, Africa’s largest hydroelectric project, has caused tension with Egypt and Sudan, who fear it will reduce their water supply. The countries have held long negotiations over how to share it."]],
sp:["Abu Simbel","Ramesses II carved two great temples into a sandstone cliff at Abu Simbel in Nubia around 1250 BCE. Four colossal statues of the king, each about 20 m tall, guard the entrance of the main temple. Twice a year, sunlight reaches deep inside to light statues of the gods. When Lake Nasser threatened to flood them in the 1960s, an international effort led by UNESCO cut the temples into blocks and rebuilt them on higher ground."],
g:[["Cataract","A stretch of rapids on a river"],["Delta","A fan-shaped area of land at a river’s mouth"],["Silt","Fine soil carried by water"],["Basin","The area of land drained by a river"]],
f:["The Nile flows north, unlike most of the world’s big rivers.","Lake Nasser is about 550 km long.","Recent studies suggest the Amazon may be slightly longer than the Nile, depending on how the source is measured."]},
antarc:{s:[
["A continent discovered","For centuries geographers imagined a great southern land, but no one saw Antarctica until 1820, when Russian, British and American expeditions sighted it. James Cook had crossed the Antarctic Circle in the 1770s without seeing land. In the 1800s sealers and whalers explored its islands and coasts."],
["The heroic age","Between about 1897 and 1922 explorers made daring expeditions in what is called the heroic age of Antarctic exploration. Ernest Shackleton came within 180 km of the South Pole in 1909. On his 1914 expedition, his ship Endurance was crushed by ice, and he led all his men to safety after a long journey in a small lifeboat across stormy seas. The wreck of Endurance was found in 2022."],
["Ice shelves and icebergs","Glaciers flow from the interior to the coast, where many spread over the sea as floating ice shelves. The Ross Ice Shelf is about the size of France. When pieces break off, they form icebergs, some larger than small countries. Scientists watch ice shelves closely because their loss can speed up the flow of glaciers into the sea."],
["Antarctic wildlife","Antarctica’s coasts and islands teem with life in summer. Penguins nest by the millions, seals such as Weddell and leopard seals rest on the ice and whales feed on krill. Albatrosses with wingspans over 3 m glide over the Southern Ocean. On land, only tiny insects, mosses and lichens survive, along with two flowering plants on the Antarctic Peninsula."],
["Science stations","Research stations study climate, ice, wildlife, the ozone layer and space. Amundsen–Scott South Pole Station sits at the pole itself. Telescopes there and at other sites take advantage of the dry, clear air. Ice cores drilled at stations such as Vostok and Dome C reveal Earth’s climate history going back hundreds of thousands of years."],
["Antarctica and climate change","Parts of Antarctica, especially the Antarctic Peninsula and West Antarctica, are warming and losing ice. Large glaciers such as Thwaites, sometimes called the doomsday glacier, are being studied because their collapse could raise sea levels significantly. Sea ice around Antarctica reached record lows in 2023."]],
sp:["The South Pole race","In October 1911 Roald Amundsen set out from the Bay of Whales with four men and 52 dogs, reaching the South Pole on 14 December. Robert Falcon Scott’s British party, relying partly on ponies and man-hauling sledges, arrived on 17 January 1912 to find a Norwegian tent and flag. On the return, all five of Scott’s party died from cold, hunger and exhaustion, only about 18 km from a supply depot. Scott’s diary was found with his body."],
g:[["Ice shelf","A floating platform of ice attached to land"],["Ice core","A long cylinder of ice drilled for study"],["Krill","Small shrimp-like animals"],["Antarctic Treaty","The 1959 agreement setting Antarctica aside for peace and science"]],
f:["Antarctica has no time zone of its own; stations use the time of their home country or supply base.","About 98% of Antarctica is covered by ice.","Antarctica has a few dry valleys where no rain or snow has fallen for a very long time."]},
reef:{s:[
["Kinds of reef","The Great Barrier Reef includes fringing reefs close to the shore, platform reefs on the shelf and long ribbon reefs at the edge of the continental shelf. Between them are sandy cays, islands made of coral sand, some covered with trees and nesting seabirds. Its lagoons and channels are home to seagrass meadows and mangroves."],
["Colors of the reef","Coral reefs are among the most colorful places on Earth. Clownfish live among stinging anemones, protected by a layer of mucus. Parrotfish, angelfish, butterflyfish and wrasse dart among the corals. Giant clams can grow more than 1 m wide. Many reef creatures use bright colors as warnings, camouflage or to attract mates."],
["Aboriginal and Torres Strait connections","Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples have lived along the reef for tens of thousands of years. More than 70 Traditional Owner groups have connections to its land and sea country, with stories, songs and sacred sites linked to it. They work with the marine park authority to manage and protect it."],
["Tourism and science","The reef supports a tourism industry worth billions of dollars a year and about 60,000 jobs. Visitors dive, snorkel and fly over it. Research stations on islands such as Heron and Lizard study coral biology, fish and the effects of climate change. Citizen scientists help by recording what they see."],
["Bleaching events","Mass coral bleaching struck the reef in 1998, 2002, 2016, 2017, 2020, 2022 and 2024, driven by marine heat waves. In 2016 about a third of shallow-water corals in the northern reef died. Corals can recover if the water cools and they have time, but repeated bleaching gives them little chance."],
["Hope for reefs","Scientists are working on ways to help coral survive, such as breeding heat-tolerant corals, growing coral fragments in nurseries and spreading coral larvae onto damaged reefs. Cutting greenhouse gas emissions, improving water quality and controlling crown-of-thorns starfish give reefs the best chance."]],
sp:["Reefs of the Coral Triangle","The richest coral reefs on Earth lie in the Coral Triangle, the waters between Indonesia, Malaysia, the Philippines, Papua New Guinea, the Solomon Islands and Timor-Leste. It holds about three-quarters of all known coral species. Its reefs protect coasts from waves, provide fish for more than 100 million people and attract divers from around the world. Like the Great Barrier Reef, they face bleaching, overfishing and pollution, and marine protected areas help them recover."],
g:[["Coral polyp","A tiny animal that builds coral reefs"],["Bleaching","The loss of algae that makes coral turn white"],["Cay","A small, low island of coral sand"],["Zooxanthellae","Algae living inside coral"]],
f:["The Great Barrier Reef is larger than Italy.","Coral reefs cover less than 1% of the ocean floor but support about a quarter of all marine species.","Some coral colonies on the reef are hundreds of years old."]},
arctic:{s:[
["The Inuit","The Inuit live across the Arctic from Alaska to Canada and Greenland. For thousands of years they hunted seals, whales, caribou and fish, using kayaks, harpoons and dog sleds. They built snow houses, igloos, for winter hunting trips. Their clothing of caribou skin and sealskin kept them warm in temperatures far below freezing."],
["Other Arctic peoples","The Sámi of northern Norway, Sweden, Finland and Russia traditionally herd reindeer. The Nenets of Siberia migrate with their reindeer across the tundra. Other peoples include the Chukchi, Yupik and Aleut. Today many Arctic peoples combine traditional ways with modern life and work to protect their lands and languages."],
["Sea ice","Arctic sea ice grows in winter and shrinks in summer, reaching its smallest extent each September. Since 1979 the September ice has shrunk by roughly 40%, and the ice has also become thinner. Scientists predict the Arctic Ocean may be nearly ice-free in some summers before 2050, which would affect wildlife and weather far away."],
["Greenland","Greenland, the world’s largest island, is mostly covered by an ice sheet up to about 3 km thick. If it all melted, sea levels would rise about 7 m. Greenland is a self-governing territory of Denmark, with about 57,000 people, most of them Inuit. Its glaciers produce many of the icebergs that drift into the North Atlantic."],
["Exploring the Arctic","For centuries explorers searched for a Northwest Passage through the Arctic to Asia. John Franklin’s expedition vanished in 1845; its ships, Erebus and Terror, were found in 2014 and 2016. Roald Amundsen first sailed the passage in 1903 to 1906. Fridtjof Nansen let his ship Fram freeze into the ice to drift across the Arctic Ocean in the 1890s."],
["A changing Arctic","Warming threatens polar bears, which need sea ice to hunt seals. Thawing permafrost damages buildings and roads and releases greenhouse gases. Melting ice opens new shipping routes and access to oil, gas and minerals, raising concerns about pollution. The Arctic Council brings Arctic countries and Indigenous peoples together to cooperate."]],
sp:["The polar bear","The polar bear is the largest land carnivore, with males weighing up to about 700 kg. Its fur looks white but is actually made of hollow, transparent hairs over black skin, which absorbs heat. Polar bears hunt seals at breathing holes in the sea ice, waiting patiently for hours. They can swim for days. Their scientific name, Ursus maritimus, means sea bear. About 26,000 remain, and they are threatened by melting ice."],
g:[["Tundra","A treeless plain with frozen subsoil"],["Permafrost","Ground that stays frozen all year"],["Sea ice","Frozen seawater floating on the ocean"],["Aurora","Natural light displays in polar skies"]],
f:["The North Pole has no land beneath it, only floating sea ice.","The Arctic tern migrates from the Arctic to the Antarctic and back each year, about 70,000 km.","Svalbard holds a global seed vault deep inside a mountain to protect the world’s crops."]},
volc:{s:[
["Where volcanoes form","Most volcanoes form along plate boundaries. At subduction zones, such as around the Pacific, sinking plates release water that melts rock above them, feeding explosive volcanoes. At mid-ocean ridges, magma rises gently as plates pull apart. Hotspots, such as Hawaii and Iceland, form above plumes of hot rock rising from deep in the mantle."],
["Products of eruptions","Volcanoes produce lava flows, ash, gases and volcanic bombs, blobs of lava that cool in the air. Pumice is a light rock full of gas bubbles that can float on water. Obsidian is volcanic glass. Ash can fall far away, damaging crops, roofs and aircraft engines, and gases such as sulfur dioxide can affect the climate."],
["Supervolcanoes","A supervolcano can erupt more than 1,000 km³ of material at once. Yellowstone in the United States last erupted on this scale about 640,000 years ago. The Toba eruption in Sumatra about 74,000 years ago was one of the largest in Earth’s history, and scientists still debate how much it affected early humans. Such eruptions are extremely rare."],
["Volcanoes beyond Earth","Volcanoes exist on other worlds. Olympus Mons on Mars, about 22 km high, is the largest known volcano in the Solar System. Jupiter’s moon Io is the most volcanically active place known, with hundreds of volcanoes erupting sulfur. Venus is covered in volcanic plains, and icy moons such as Enceladus have cryovolcanoes that spray water."],
["Living with volcanoes","Hundreds of millions of people live near active volcanoes, attracted by fertile soil, water and geothermal energy. Volcanic regions grow coffee, rice, grapes and vegetables. Hot springs draw tourists. Communities learn to live with the risk through monitoring, hazard maps and evacuation plans."],
["Famous eruptions","Vesuvius buried Pompeii in 79 CE. Krakatoa’s 1883 explosion was heard nearly 5,000 km away and caused deadly tsunamis. Mount Pelée on Martinique destroyed the town of Saint-Pierre in 1902, killing about 28,000 people. Mount St. Helens erupted sideways in 1980, flattening forests. Pinatubo in 1991 was the second-largest eruption of the 20th century."]],
sp:["Tambora 1815","In April 1815 Mount Tambora on the island of Sumbawa in Indonesia exploded in the largest eruption in recorded history. It killed about 70,000 or more people, mainly from starvation afterward, and reduced the mountain’s height by over 1 km. Its ash and gases cooled the Northern Hemisphere, causing the 1816 Year Without a Summer, with snow in June in New England, failed harvests and famine in Europe."],
g:[["Magma","Molten rock underground"],["Lava","Molten rock that reaches the surface"],["Crater","A bowl-shaped opening at a volcano’s top"],["Pyroclastic flow","A fast-moving cloud of hot ash and gas"]],
f:["The word volcano comes from Vulcan, the Roman god of fire.","Indonesia has about 130 active volcanoes, more than any other country.","Most of Earth’s volcanic eruptions happen underwater along mid-ocean ridges."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-geo3.js */
// In-depth material (Geography, part 3): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
himal:{s:[
["The great peaks","Besides Everest, the Himalayas include Kangchenjunga, the third highest mountain, on the Nepal–India border; Lhotse and Makalu near Everest; Cho Oyu, Dhaulagiri, Manaslu and Annapurna in Nepal; and Nanga Parbat in Pakistan. K2, the second highest, is in the neighboring Karakoram range. Annapurna has one of the highest death rates for climbers."],
["Three ranges in one","The Himalayas are made up of parallel ranges. The Sivalik Hills form the low southern foothills. The Lesser Himalaya rise behind them, with hill stations such as Shimla and Darjeeling. The Great Himalaya contains the highest peaks, permanently covered in snow and ice. North of them lies the high, dry Tibetan Plateau."],
["Monsoon and weather","The Himalayas act as a giant wall. They block cold, dry winds from Central Asia, keeping India warm, and force moist monsoon winds from the Indian Ocean to rise and drop heavy rain on the southern slopes. Cherrapunji and Mawsynram, in the hills of northeast India, are among the wettest places on Earth."],
["Himalayan wildlife","The rare snow leopard, with its long tail and thick fur, hunts blue sheep and ibex on rocky slopes. Red pandas live in bamboo forests, and Himalayan tahr, musk deer and yaks roam the heights. The bar-headed goose migrates over the mountains at altitudes above 7,000 m. Many species are protected in parks such as Sagarmatha and Annapurna."],
["Earthquakes","Because India is still pushing into Asia, the Himalayas are one of the most earthquake-prone regions on Earth. The 2015 Gorkha earthquake in Nepal, of magnitude 7.8, killed about 9,000 people and damaged ancient temples in Kathmandu. Scientists warn that larger earthquakes are possible along the range."],
["Kingdoms of the mountains","The Himalayas are home to Nepal, the kingdom of Bhutan and parts of India, Pakistan and China. Bhutan, a Buddhist kingdom, measures its success by Gross National Happiness and keeps most of its land under forest. Sikkim and Ladakh in India and Tibet in China have rich Buddhist traditions, with monasteries perched on cliffs."]],
sp:["The yeti","Himalayan legends tell of the yeti, a large, hairy, ape-like creature said to roam the snowy heights. Western explorers in the 20th century reported giant footprints in the snow, and expeditions searched for the creature. Scientists who tested hair and bone samples said to be from yetis found that they came from bears, especially Himalayan brown bears. The yeti, or abominable snowman, remains a popular legend."],
g:[["Glacier","A slowly moving mass of ice"],["Monsoon","A seasonal wind that brings heavy rain"],["Sherpa","A people of Nepal known as mountain guides"],["Plateau","A high, flat area of land"]],
f:["The Himalayas contain more than 15,000 glaciers.","The Himalayas rise by about 1 cm a year in places.","Bhutan’s highest mountain, Gangkhar Puensum, is one of the highest unclimbed peaks in the world."]},
med:{s:[
["Phoenicians and Carthage","The Phoenicians, from cities such as Tyre and Sidon in modern Lebanon, were the great sailors and traders of the Mediterranean from about 1200 BCE. They founded colonies across North Africa and Spain, including Carthage in modern Tunisia. Carthage became a rich empire and fought Rome in three Punic Wars, until Rome destroyed it in 146 BCE."],
["Venice and Genoa","In the Middle Ages, the Italian sea republics of Venice and Genoa grew rich from Mediterranean trade, carrying silks and spices from the eastern Mediterranean to Europe. Venice, built on islands in a lagoon, had a powerful navy and an empire of ports and islands. Its traders, such as Marco Polo, traveled far."],
["Islands of the sea","The Mediterranean has thousands of islands. Sicily, the largest, has Mount Etna and a mix of Greek, Arab and Norman heritage. Crete was home to the Minoans. Malta has prehistoric temples older than the pyramids. Cyprus, Sardinia, Corsica, the Balearic Islands and the Greek islands each have their own history and culture."],
["Marine life","The Mediterranean holds about 17,000 marine species, many found nowhere else, though it covers less than 1% of the world’s ocean area. Posidonia seagrass meadows, sea turtles, monk seals, dolphins and tuna live there. Overfishing, pollution and warming waters are serious threats, and the Mediterranean monk seal is one of the most endangered seals."],
["Modern challenges","Millions of tourists visit Mediterranean beaches each year. Busy shipping lanes, plastic pollution and rising temperatures affect the sea. It is warming faster than the global average, causing heat waves and wildfires around its shores. Many migrants have tried to cross the sea from Africa and the Middle East to Europe, often in dangerous boats."],
["The Suez Canal","The Suez Canal in Egypt, opened in 1869, links the Mediterranean with the Red Sea, letting ships sail between Europe and Asia without going around Africa. About 12% of world trade passes through it. In 2021 the giant container ship Ever Given got stuck across the canal for six days, blocking hundreds of ships."]],
sp:["Mount Etna","Mount Etna, on the east coast of Sicily, is the highest active volcano in Europe, about 3,300 m tall, and one of the most active in the world. It erupts frequently, with lava fountains and flows that have destroyed villages over the centuries, including part of the city of Catania in 1669. Yet the fertile volcanic soil on its slopes grows grapes, oranges and pistachios. It became a World Heritage Site in 2013."],
g:[["Strait","A narrow sea passage between two landmasses"],["Mare Nostrum","Latin for our sea, the Roman name for the Mediterranean"],["Canal","An artificial waterway"],["Peninsula","Land almost surrounded by water"]],
f:["The Strait of Gibraltar is only about 14 km wide at its narrowest.","The Mediterranean has almost no tides.","Its name comes from Latin words meaning in the middle of the land."]},
quake:{s:[
["Faults","A fault is a fracture in Earth’s crust along which rocks move. In normal faults, rocks are pulled apart and one side drops. In reverse or thrust faults, rocks are pushed together and one side rises. In strike-slip faults, rocks slide past each other sideways. Most faults move only during earthquakes."],
["Foreshocks and aftershocks","Some large earthquakes are preceded by smaller foreshocks, though they can only be recognized afterward. Almost all large earthquakes are followed by aftershocks, smaller quakes that can continue for weeks or years as the crust adjusts. Aftershocks can topple buildings already weakened by the main shock."],
["Predicting earthquakes","Scientists cannot yet predict exactly when an earthquake will strike. Instead, they estimate the chances of earthquakes in each region over the coming decades and map areas likely to shake strongly. Early warning systems in countries such as Japan and Mexico detect the first waves and send alerts seconds before strong shaking arrives."],
["Building for earthquakes","Most deaths in earthquakes are caused by collapsing buildings. Engineers design buildings with flexible steel or reinforced concrete frames, cross-bracing and base isolators, pads that let a building move separately from the ground. Japan, Chile, New Zealand and California are known for their strict building codes."],
["Liquefaction and landslides","Strong shaking can make wet, sandy soil behave like a liquid, a process called liquefaction, causing buildings to tilt and sink. Earthquakes can also trigger landslides in mountainous areas. In the 2011 Christchurch earthquake in New Zealand, liquefaction damaged thousands of homes."],
["Famous earthquakes","The 1755 Lisbon earthquake, followed by a tsunami and fire, destroyed much of the Portuguese capital. The 1906 San Francisco earthquake and fire killed about 3,000 people. The 2010 Haiti earthquake killed over 200,000. The 2011 Tōhoku earthquake in Japan, of magnitude 9.0, triggered a tsunami that caused the Fukushima nuclear accident."]],
sp:["The Big One","Scientists warn that the San Andreas Fault in California, the Nankai Trough off Japan and faults near cities such as Istanbul, Tehran and Manila could produce very large earthquakes in coming decades. Nobody can predict exactly when. To prepare, many countries hold regular drills, teaching people to drop, cover and hold on, strengthen schools, hospitals and bridges, and build early warning systems that give a few precious seconds of notice."],
g:[["Epicenter","The point on the surface above an earthquake’s origin"],["Seismograph","An instrument that records ground shaking"],["Magnitude","A measure of the energy released by an earthquake"],["Aftershock","A smaller quake following a main earthquake"]],
f:["Earth has about 20,000 earthquakes a year strong enough to be recorded and located by global networks.","The deepest earthquakes occur about 700 km below the surface.","Earthquakes have been recorded on the Moon and on Mars, called moonquakes and marsquakes."]},
alps:{s:[
["Peaks and passes","The Alps have more than 80 peaks above 4,000 m. Famous ones include Mont Blanc, the Matterhorn, with its pyramid shape, the Eiger, with its dangerous north face, and the Jungfrau. For centuries, travelers crossed the mountains through passes such as the Great St. Bernard, the Brenner and the St. Gotthard, which linked northern Europe with Italy."],
["Rivers of ice","The Aletsch Glacier in Switzerland, about 20 km long, is the largest glacier in the Alps. Glaciers carved the valleys and left behind moraines, piles of rock, and lakes. Since the mid-1800s Alpine glaciers have lost more than half of their volume, and the loss has sped up in recent years. Some smaller glaciers have disappeared entirely."],
["Alpine wildlife","Ibex, wild goats with huge curved horns, climb steep cliffs. Chamois leap across rocks, and marmots whistle warnings from their burrows. Golden eagles and bearded vultures soar overhead. The edelweiss, a white, woolly flower, grows in rocky meadows and is a symbol of the Alps."],
["Switzerland and the Alps","About 60% of Switzerland is covered by the Alps. The Swiss built remarkable railways, tunnels and bridges through the mountains, and mountain railways climb to high stations such as the Jungfraujoch, at about 3,450 m, the highest railway station in Europe. Swiss watches, cheese and chocolate are famous worldwide."],
["Mountaineering","Modern mountaineering began in the Alps. The first ascent of Mont Blanc in 1786 is often seen as its birth. In 1865 Edward Whymper’s team made the first ascent of the Matterhorn, but four of the climbers fell to their deaths on the way down. The Alps remain a training ground for climbers worldwide."],
["Tourism and the future","Tens of millions of tourists visit the Alps each year to ski, hike and enjoy the scenery. Climate change threatens snow cover at lower ski resorts, and melting permafrost can make rock walls unstable. Alpine countries cooperate through the Alpine Convention, signed in 1991, to protect the region."]],
sp:["Hannibal’s crossing","In 218 BCE, the Carthaginian general Hannibal led an army of tens of thousands of soldiers, horses and 37 war elephants from Spain across the Pyrenees and the Alps to attack Rome. The crossing, in autumn snow, was terrible, and many men and animals died. Hannibal reached Italy and won great victories, including at Cannae in 216 BCE, but he never captured Rome. Historians still debate which pass he used."],
g:[["Pass","A route through a mountain range"],["Moraine","Rock and soil deposited by a glacier"],["Transhumance","Seasonal movement of livestock between pastures"],["Edelweiss","A woolly white Alpine flower"]],
f:["The Alps formed from rock layers once on an ocean floor, which is why some peaks contain marine fossils.","The Jungfraujoch railway station sits about 3,450 m above sea level.","St. Bernard dogs were famous for rescuing travelers lost in snow in the Alps."]},
andes:{s:[
["Ancient civilizations","Long before the Inca, the Andes were home to many civilizations. Caral in Peru, about 5,000 years old, is one of the oldest cities in the Americas. The Chavín, Nazca, Moche, Tiwanaku and Wari cultures built temples, irrigation systems and roads. The Nazca made giant drawings in the desert, the Nazca Lines, visible from the air."],
["Machu Picchu","Machu Picchu, built around 1450 for the Inca emperor Pachacuti, stands on a mountain ridge above the Urubamba River. Its stone walls fit so tightly that a knife cannot slip between the blocks, and they have survived earthquakes. The Spanish never found it, and it became known worldwide after Hiram Bingham’s visit in 1911. It is one of the New 7 Wonders of the World."],
["Lake Titicaca","Lake Titicaca, on the border of Peru and Bolivia, is the largest lake in South America by volume. According to Inca legend, the first Inca rulers emerged from its waters. The Uros people live on floating islands made of totora reeds, which they constantly renew. The lake also has a unique giant frog."],
["The Atacama","On the western side of the Andes lies the Atacama Desert in Chile, one of the driest places on Earth, where some weather stations have never recorded rain. Its clear, dry skies make it ideal for astronomy, and many of the world’s largest telescopes are built there. It is also rich in copper and lithium."],
["Glaciers and water","Andean glaciers supply drinking water, irrigation and hydroelectricity for cities such as La Paz, Lima and Quito. Many are shrinking quickly as the climate warms. Bolivia’s Chacaltaya glacier, once a ski area, disappeared by 2009. Communities are building reservoirs and finding new ways to save water."],
["Andean cultures today","Millions of people in the Andes speak Quechua and Aymara, the languages of the Inca and their neighbors. They weave colorful textiles from alpaca and llama wool, celebrate festivals such as Inti Raymi, the Inca sun festival in Cusco, and chew coca leaves to ease altitude sickness. Andean music, with panpipes and charangos, is famous worldwide."]],
sp:["The Inca road","The Qhapaq Ñan, the great Inca road system, stretched about 40,000 km through the Andes, from Colombia to Chile and Argentina. Roads climbed mountains with stone steps, crossed rivers on rope suspension bridges and passed through deserts. Relay runners called chasquis carried messages and goods, covering up to about 240 km a day. Rest houses called tambos stood along the way. It became a World Heritage Site in 2014."],
g:[["Altiplano","A high plateau in the central Andes"],["Quechua","The language of the Inca and many Andean people today"],["Llama","A domesticated South American camel"],["Terrace","A flat step cut into a hillside for farming"]],
f:["The Andes pass through seven countries: Venezuela, Colombia, Ecuador, Peru, Bolivia, Chile and Argentina.","The summit of Chimborazo in Ecuador is the point on Earth farthest from the planet’s center.","La Paz in Bolivia is the highest seat of government in the world, at about 3,600 m."]},
canyon:{s:[
["The Colorado River","The Colorado River begins in the Rocky Mountains and flows about 2,330 km to the Gulf of California. It supplies water to about 40 million people in the American West and Mexico. Dams such as the Glen Canyon Dam, upstream of the Grand Canyon, and the Hoover Dam, downstream, store water and generate electricity but have changed the river’s flow."],
["How the canyon formed","The Colorado Plateau slowly rose over millions of years while the river cut downward. Rain, frost and flash floods widened the canyon, and side streams carved branching canyons. Hard rock layers formed cliffs, and soft layers formed slopes, creating the canyon’s stepped walls. The work continues today, though slowly."],
["Fossils in the walls","The canyon’s rock layers hold fossils of ancient sea creatures, such as trilobites, brachiopods and corals, from times when the area was covered by shallow seas. Higher layers show footprints of early reptiles in ancient sand dunes. Remarkably, the canyon has no dinosaur fossils, because the rocks of the dinosaur age have been eroded away."],
["Animals and plants","The Grand Canyon is home to bighorn sheep, mule deer, mountain lions and many kinds of bats and birds. The rare California condor, with a wingspan of almost 3 m, soars over the canyon. Plants range from cacti near the river to ponderosa pines on the South Rim and spruce on the North Rim."],
["Visiting the canyon","About five million people visit the Grand Canyon each year. Most go to the South Rim, with viewpoints such as Mather Point. Hikers descend trails such as Bright Angel and South Kaibab to the river, and mule rides carry visitors into the canyon. The Grand Canyon Skywalk, a glass bridge on Hualapai land, juts out over the edge."],
["Protecting the canyon","President Theodore Roosevelt visited in 1903 and said the canyon should be left as it is. He made it a national monument in 1908. Today the park faces challenges from crowds, air pollution, mining near its borders and changes to the river. In 2023 a new national monument was created to protect lands around the canyon."]],
sp:["John Wesley Powell","John Wesley Powell, a geologist who had lost an arm in the American Civil War, led ten men in four wooden boats down the Green and Colorado rivers in 1869, through the unexplored Grand Canyon. They faced rapids, lost boats and food, and three men left the expedition near the end and were never seen again. Powell’s journey made him famous, and he later led the US Geological Survey and studied the American West."],
g:[["Canyon","A deep, narrow valley with steep sides"],["Erosion","The wearing away of rock by water, wind and ice"],["Rim","The edge at the top of a canyon"],["Rapids","A fast, rough stretch of river"]],
f:["The Grand Canyon is deep enough that it has different weather at the top and bottom.","Native American nations including the Havasupai, Hualapai, Hopi and Navajo have deep ties to the canyon.","Light from the canyon walls changes color throughout the day, from red to orange, pink and purple."]},
sea:{s:[
["Early kingdoms","Southeast Asia was home to great kingdoms. Srivijaya, based in Sumatra, controlled sea trade from the 600s to the 1200s. The Khmer Empire built Angkor. The Majapahit Empire of Java dominated the islands in the 1300s. Pagan in Myanmar and the kingdoms of Ayutthaya in Thailand and Champa in Vietnam also flourished."],
["Indian and Chinese influence","Trade brought Hindu and Buddhist ideas from India, which shaped art, writing and kingship. Temples such as Borobudur and Prambanan in Java show this influence. Chinese influence was strong in Vietnam, which was ruled by China for about a thousand years. Islam spread through trade from the 1200s, especially in the islands."],
["The colonial era","From the 1500s, European powers took control of most of the region. The Portuguese seized Malacca in 1511, Spain colonized the Philippines, the Dutch ruled Indonesia, Britain controlled Burma, Malaya and Singapore, and France ruled Vietnam, Laos and Cambodia. Only Thailand, then called Siam, remained independent, partly by playing rivals against each other."],
["Independence","After World War II, the countries of Southeast Asia won independence, some peacefully and some through war. The Philippines gained independence in 1946, Indonesia in 1949 after a war with the Netherlands, and Malaysia in 1957. The Vietnam War lasted until 1975. Timor-Leste became independent in 2002."],
["Economies","Southeast Asia has some of the world’s fastest-growing economies. Singapore is a global financial center. Vietnam, Thailand, Malaysia and the Philippines export electronics, cars and clothing. Indonesia, the largest economy, produces palm oil, coal and nickel. The region is also a major producer of rice, rubber, coffee and seafood."],
["Food and festivals","Southeast Asian food is famous for balancing sweet, sour, salty and spicy flavors, with ingredients such as rice, coconut, lemongrass, chili and fish sauce. Dishes include Thai pad thai, Vietnamese pho, Indonesian rendang and Filipino adobo. Festivals include Songkran, the Thai water festival, and Tết, the Vietnamese New Year."]],
sp:["Angkor Wat","Angkor Wat, built in the early 1100s by the Khmer king Suryavarman II, is the largest religious monument in the world, covering about 160 hectares. Built as a Hindu temple to Vishnu, it later became Buddhist. Its five towers represent Mount Meru, the home of the gods, and its walls are carved with kilometers of scenes from Hindu epics. The temple appears on Cambodia’s flag, and the surrounding Angkor area has hundreds of temples."],
g:[["ASEAN","The Association of Southeast Asian Nations"],["Monsoon","A seasonal wind bringing wet and dry seasons"],["Archipelago","A group of islands"],["Colonialism","Rule of one country by another"]],
f:["Thailand was never colonized by a European power.","Indonesia is the world’s largest archipelagic country.","Borobudur in Java is the world’s largest Buddhist temple."]},
iceland:{s:[
["The Althing","In 930, the Norse settlers of Iceland created the Althing, an assembly that met each summer at Þingvellir, a valley where the North American and Eurasian plates meet. Chieftains made laws, and a lawspeaker recited the laws from a rock. Iceland remained independent until it came under Norwegian rule in 1262 and later Danish rule."],
["The sagas","In the 1200s and 1300s, Icelanders wrote down sagas, long prose stories about the settlers, their feuds, voyages and families. The Saga of Erik the Red and the Saga of the Greenlanders describe voyages to Greenland and North America. Snorri Sturluson wrote the Prose Edda, a key source of Norse mythology. Icelanders still read these works today."],
["Volcanoes and fire","Iceland has about 30 active volcanic systems. Hekla, once called the gateway to hell, erupts frequently. The Laki eruption of 1783 released huge amounts of poisonous gas, killing much of Iceland’s livestock and causing famine that killed about a fifth of the population. Since 2021, a series of eruptions has occurred on the Reykjanes Peninsula near Grindavík."],
["Glaciers","Vatnajökull, Europe’s largest ice cap by volume, covers about 8% of Iceland and hides active volcanoes beneath it. When they erupt under the ice, they can cause sudden floods called jökulhlaups. Glacier lagoons such as Jökulsárlón are filled with floating icebergs. Iceland’s glaciers are shrinking, and in 2019 Icelanders held a funeral for Okjökull, the first glacier lost to climate change."],
["Life in Iceland","Icelanders have a high standard of living, long life expectancy and one of the highest rates of reading in the world. Many people use their father’s first name plus son or dóttir as a surname. Fishing, aluminum production and tourism are major industries. Iceland has no army and is often ranked as one of the most peaceful countries on Earth."],
["Midnight sun and long nights","Because Iceland lies just south of the Arctic Circle, summer days are very long. In June the Sun barely sets, and the sky stays bright all night. In December, daylight lasts only about four hours in Reykjavík. Long winter nights are perfect for watching the northern lights."]],
sp:["Surtsey","In November 1963 fishermen off Iceland’s south coast saw smoke rising from the sea. An underwater eruption had broken the surface, and over the next three and a half years it built a new island, Surtsey, named after the fire giant Surtur. Scientists have closely studied how plants, insects, birds and seals colonized the island. Only researchers may visit, and it became a World Heritage Site in 2008."],
g:[["Geyser","A hot spring that shoots water into the air"],["Jökulhlaup","A sudden flood from a glacier"],["Saga","A medieval Icelandic prose story"],["Ice cap","A dome of ice covering a large area of land"]],
f:["Iceland has no mosquitoes.","Iceland’s Strokkur geyser erupts every few minutes.","Icelanders are listed in the phone book by first name."]},
madag:{s:[
["Separate from Africa","Madagascar broke away from Africa about 165 million years ago, as part of a landmass with India, and then separated from India about 88 million years ago. Animals that reached it afterward, often by drifting across the sea on floating vegetation, evolved in isolation. This is why it has no native apes, big cats or antelopes, but has lemurs, tenrecs and fossas."],
["Other unique animals","Madagascar is home to about half of the world’s chameleon species, including some of the largest and smallest. The fossa, a cat-like predator, hunts lemurs. Tenrecs look like hedgehogs or shrews. The aye-aye taps on wood with a long, thin middle finger to find grubs. Many brightly colored frogs and geckos live nowhere else."],
["Lost giants","Until a few centuries ago, Madagascar had giant animals. The elephant bird, the largest bird that ever lived, stood about 3 m tall and laid eggs larger than those of any dinosaur. There were also giant lemurs and pygmy hippos. They died out after people arrived, probably because of hunting and habitat change."],
["History","Madagascar was ruled by several kingdoms. In the 1800s the Merina kingdom, based in the central highlands, united much of the island. Queen Ranavalona I resisted European influence. France conquered the island in 1895 and made it a colony. After an uprising in 1947, Madagascar gained independence in 1960."],
["Farming and the economy","Most Malagasy people are farmers, growing rice on terraced fields and raising zebu cattle. Madagascar produces most of the world’s natural vanilla, as well as cloves, coffee and lychees. Many people are poor, and the country faces challenges from cyclones, drought and deforestation."],
["Conservation","Madagascar has created many national parks, such as Ranomafana and Andasibe-Mantadia, to protect its forests and wildlife. Ecotourism brings income to local communities. Scientists continue to discover new species, especially frogs, chameleons and lemurs, and conservation groups work with villages to plant trees and protect forests."]],
sp:["The aye-aye","The aye-aye is one of the strangest primates on Earth. Active at night, it has large ears, big eyes and a long, skinny middle finger. It taps on tree bark and listens for the hollow sound of tunnels made by insect larvae, then gnaws a hole with its rodent-like teeth and pulls out the grub with its finger. Some local beliefs once considered it a bad omen, and it is endangered by habitat loss."],
g:[["Endemic","Found naturally in only one place"],["Lemur","A primate found only in Madagascar"],["Zebu","A humped breed of cattle"],["Baobab","A tree with a massive water-storing trunk"]],
f:["Madagascar produces about 80% of the world’s natural vanilla.","The ring-tailed lemur sunbathes with its arms spread out in the morning.","The Avenue of the Baobabs has trees hundreds of years old."]},
rift:{s:[
["How the rift began","The rift began forming about 25 to 30 million years ago as hot mantle rock rose beneath East Africa, lifting and stretching the crust. The Afar region, where three rifts meet at a triple junction, is one of the few places where the birth of a new ocean can be seen on land. Eventually the Horn of Africa may break away."],
["Lake Victoria","Lake Victoria, shared by Uganda, Kenya and Tanzania, is Africa’s largest lake and the second-largest freshwater lake in the world by area. It lies in a basin between the two arms of the rift. It once held hundreds of species of cichlid fish, but the introduction of the Nile perch in the 1950s caused many to die out."],
["Kilimanjaro","Kilimanjaro, Africa’s highest mountain, is a dormant volcano made of three cones. Climbers pass through rainforest, heathland, alpine desert and finally glaciers near the summit, Uhuru Peak. Its glaciers have shrunk dramatically and may disappear in the coming decades. Thousands of people climb it each year."],
["Wildlife and the savanna","The rift and surrounding plains are home to some of Africa’s greatest wildlife. The Serengeti and Maasai Mara see the great migration of over a million wildebeest, zebras and gazelles, followed by lions and hyenas. Ngorongoro Crater, a collapsed volcano, holds a remarkable concentration of animals."],
["Peoples of the rift","Many peoples live along the rift. The Maasai of Kenya and Tanzania herd cattle and are known for their red cloth and beadwork. The Afar herd camels in one of the hottest places on Earth. Farmers grow coffee and tea in the fertile highlands of Ethiopia and Kenya, and fishing communities live along the great lakes."],
["Geothermal energy","Heat from the rift is a valuable energy source. Kenya’s Olkaria geothermal plants, in the Rift Valley, generate a large share of the country’s electricity, making Kenya one of the world’s leading geothermal producers. Ethiopia and Djibouti are also developing geothermal power."]],
sp:["Lucy","In 1974 paleoanthropologist Donald Johanson and his team found about 40% of a skeleton of a small human ancestor in Hadar, Ethiopia. Named Lucy after a Beatles song playing at camp, she belonged to the species Australopithecus afarensis and lived about 3.2 million years ago. She stood just over 1 m tall and walked upright, though she also climbed trees. In Ethiopia she is called Dinkinesh, meaning you are marvelous."],
g:[["Rift","A long valley formed where the crust pulls apart"],["Triple junction","A place where three plate boundaries meet"],["Savanna","Grassland with scattered trees"],["Hominin","Humans and their close extinct relatives"]],
f:["The Danakil Depression in the Afar is one of the hottest places on Earth.","Lake Tanganyika is about 670 km long, the longest freshwater lake in the world.","The Great Rift Valley can be seen from space."]},
mekong:{s:[
["The upper Mekong","The Mekong rises on the Tibetan Plateau at over 5,000 m and flows through deep, narrow gorges in Yunnan, China, alongside the Yangtze and Salween rivers in a region called the Three Parallel Rivers, a World Heritage Site. China has built a series of large dams on this stretch."],
["The Golden Triangle","Where the borders of Thailand, Laos and Myanmar meet along the Mekong lies the Golden Triangle, once infamous for opium production. Today it is a tourist area, though drug trafficking remains a problem. Farther south, the river forms much of the border between Laos and Thailand, with towns such as Vientiane, the Lao capital, on its banks."],
["Khone Falls","In southern Laos, the Mekong spreads out among thousands of islands, called Si Phan Don, the Four Thousand Islands. Here the river plunges over the Khone Phapheng Falls, the largest waterfall in Southeast Asia by volume. The falls blocked river navigation, so in the 1890s the French built a short railway around them."],
["Cambodia and the Tonlé Sap","In Cambodia, the Mekong passes Phnom Penh, the capital, where it meets the Tonlé Sap River. The Tonlé Sap lake, the largest freshwater lake in Southeast Asia, is a huge fishery, and many people live in floating villages. The lake’s seasonal floods spread fertile soil and fish across surrounding fields."],
["The Mekong Delta","In Vietnam, the Mekong splits into many branches before reaching the South China Sea, forming a vast delta. Vietnamese call it Cửu Long, the Nine Dragons. The delta grows about half of Vietnam’s rice and much of its fruit and fish. Rising seas and sinking land are pushing saltwater into the delta, threatening farms."],
["Dams and the future","Dozens of hydroelectric dams have been built or planned on the Mekong and its tributaries, providing electricity for growing economies. But dams trap sediment, block fish migrations and change the flood cycle on which farmers and fishers depend. Countries along the river cooperate through the Mekong River Commission, though China is not a full member."]],
sp:["The Mekong giant catfish","The Mekong giant catfish is one of the largest freshwater fish in the world, reaching up to 3 m long and about 300 kg. It has no scales and no teeth and feeds on algae and plants. It migrates long distances along the river to breed. Overfishing and dams have made it critically endangered. Fishers in Thailand and Cambodia now release any they catch, and hatcheries raise young fish."],
g:[["Delta","A fan-shaped area where a river splits before the sea"],["Hydroelectric dam","A dam that generates electricity from water"],["Sediment","Soil and sand carried by water"],["Floodplain","Flat land that floods when a river overflows"]],
f:["The Mekong is the 12th longest river in the world.","About 60 million people depend on the Mekong for food and income.","Irrawaddy dolphins live in parts of the Mekong in Cambodia and Laos."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-tech1.js */
// In-depth material (Technology, part 1): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
internet:{s:[
["Packets and routers","When you send a message or load a page, the data is broken into small packets, each labeled with where it is going. Routers, special computers at junctions of the network, pass packets along the fastest available path. Packets from one message may take different routes and arrive out of order, and the receiving computer puts them back together."],
["Domain names","Every website has a numerical IP address, but people use names such as example.com. The Domain Name System, invented in 1983, works like a giant phone book, translating names into addresses. Endings such as .com, .org and .gov show the type of site, while country codes such as .jp for Japan or .br for Brazil show where it is registered."],
["Email and messaging","Ray Tomlinson sent the first email between computers on a network in 1971 and chose the @ symbol to separate the user’s name from the computer’s. Email became one of the internet’s most popular uses. Instant messaging, texting apps and video calls followed, letting people talk across the world for free."],
["Social media","Social media sites let people share posts, photos and videos with friends and the public. Facebook, launched in 2004, YouTube in 2005, Twitter in 2006 and Instagram in 2010 grew to billions of users, and today more than 5 billion people use social media."],
["The cloud and streaming","Instead of storing everything on one device, people now use the cloud, data saved in huge data centers and reached over the internet. Streaming services deliver music, films and games on demand. This requires enormous computing power and electricity, and data centers are now major users of energy."],
["The internet spreads","The internet grew from a few American universities to the whole world. Countries across Europe and Asia connected in the late 1980s and early 1990s, and most of Africa by the late 1990s. Internet cafés brought many people online for the first time. Today most people in Asia, Africa and Latin America go online through mobile phones, and bringing fast, affordable internet to rural areas remains a goal."]],
sp:["Tim Berners-Lee and the web","In 1989 Tim Berners-Lee, a British scientist at CERN, the European physics laboratory, proposed a system to share documents using hyperlinks. He wrote the first web browser and web server and invented HTML, the language of web pages, URLs and HTTP. The first website went live in 1991. CERN made the web free for everyone in 1993, a decision that helped it spread across the world. Berners-Lee was knighted in 2004."],
g:[["IP address","A number that identifies a device on the internet"],["Router","A device that directs data across networks"],["Browser","A program for viewing web pages"],["Hyperlink","A clickable link to another page"]],
f:["The first website, info.cern.ch, is still online.","About 5.5 billion people used the internet by the mid-2020s.","The internet and the World Wide Web are not the same: the web is one service that runs on the internet."]},
elec:{s:[
["Static electricity","When you rub a balloon on your hair, electrons move from one to the other, building up an electric charge that makes the balloon stick to walls and your hair stand up. This is static electricity. Lightning is a giant spark of static electricity between clouds or between a cloud and the ground."],
["Circuits","Electricity flows only around a complete loop called a circuit, from a power source through wires and devices and back. A switch breaks or completes the circuit. In a series circuit, current passes through each device in turn, so if one bulb breaks, all go out. In a parallel circuit, each device has its own path."],
["AC and DC","Direct current, or DC, flows steadily in one direction, as from a battery. Alternating current, or AC, reverses direction many times a second: 50 times a second, or 50 hertz, in most of the world, and 60 hertz in North America and some other countries. Phones and laptops use chargers that convert AC from the wall into DC."],
["Power stations","Most power stations spin a turbine connected to a generator, where magnets rotating near coils of wire induce an electric current. The turbine may be turned by steam from burning coal, gas or biomass or from nuclear reactors or geothermal wells, by falling water or by wind. Solar panels produce electricity directly from light."],
["Electricity in the body","Your body runs on electricity. Nerve cells send messages as tiny electrical pulses, letting you think, feel and move. The heart’s beat is controlled by electrical signals, measured by doctors with an electrocardiogram. Electric eels and some rays can produce shocks of hundreds of volts to stun prey."],
["Saving electricity","Using electricity wisely saves money and reduces pollution from power plants. Ways to save include switching off lights and appliances when not in use, using LED bulbs, which use far less power than old bulbs, choosing energy-efficient air conditioners and fridges and using natural light and breezes."]],
sp:["Michael Faraday","Michael Faraday, the son of a blacksmith, left school at 13 to work as a bookbinder in London, reading the science books he bound. He became assistant to the chemist Humphry Davy and went on to make great discoveries. In 1831 he found that moving a magnet near a coil of wire produces an electric current, the principle behind generators and transformers. He also invented an early electric motor and gave popular Christmas lectures for young people."],
g:[["Current","The flow of electric charge"],["Voltage","The push that drives current"],["Resistance","How much a material opposes current"],["Circuit","A complete path for electricity"]],
f:["An LED bulb can use about 75% less energy than an old incandescent bulb.","Lightning flashes about 40 to 50 times every second somewhere on Earth.","Electricity travels through wires at a large fraction of the speed of light."]},
comp:{s:[
["Mechanical calculators","Long before electronic computers, people built machines to calculate. Blaise Pascal built a mechanical adding machine in 1642, and Gottfried Leibniz built one that could multiply. In the 1800s, Joseph-Marie Jacquard used punched cards to control looms, an idea Babbage borrowed for his Analytical Engine, which was never fully built."],
["Codebreakers and ENIAC","During World War II, British engineers built Colossus to break German codes, and in the United States, ENIAC was built to calculate artillery tables. ENIAC filled a large room, used about 18,000 vacuum tubes and weighed about 27 tonnes. It was programmed by setting switches and plugging cables, a job done largely by six women programmers."],
["From mainframes to microchips","In the 1950s and 1960s large mainframe computers ran banks, governments and universities. Transistors and then integrated circuits made computers smaller and faster. In 1971 the Intel 4004 placed a whole processor on one chip. Home computers such as the Apple II of 1977 and the IBM PC of 1981 brought computing into homes and offices."],
["Graphical interfaces","Early computers were controlled by typing commands. Researchers at Xerox PARC developed windows, icons and the mouse in the 1970s. Apple’s Macintosh of 1984 made this style popular, and Microsoft Windows followed. Touchscreens, used on smartphones and tablets from 2007, made computers even easier to use."],
["Programming","A program is a list of instructions. Early programmers wrote in machine code, but languages such as FORTRAN, COBOL and BASIC made programming easier. Today, programmers use languages such as Python, JavaScript and C++. Many schools around the world teach coding, and software developers work from almost every country."],
["Computers in the future","Researchers are developing quantum computers, which use the strange rules of quantum physics to solve certain problems much faster than ordinary computers. Artificial intelligence is making computers better at recognizing images and language. Computers are becoming smaller and found in everything from watches to cars, an idea called the Internet of Things."]],
sp:["Alan Turing","Alan Turing was a British mathematician who, in 1936, described a simple imaginary machine that could carry out any calculation, the idea behind every modern computer. During World War II he led efforts at Bletchley Park to break the German Enigma code, helping the Allies win. He later designed early computers and asked whether machines could think. He was persecuted for being gay and died in 1954. Britain later apologized and put him on the £50 note."],
g:[["Hardware","The physical parts of a computer"],["Software","Programs that tell a computer what to do"],["Processor","The chip that carries out instructions"],["Binary","A number system using only 0 and 1"]],
f:["ENIAC used about 150 kilowatts of power, enough to dim lights in parts of Philadelphia, according to legend.","A modern smartphone is millions of times more powerful than the computers that guided Apollo 11.","The word computer once meant a person who did calculations."]},
print:{s:[
["Paper","Printing needed cheap paper. Paper was invented in China, improved around 105 CE, and spread along trade routes to the Islamic world by the 700s and to Europe by the 1100s. Before paper, Europeans wrote on parchment made from animal skin, which was costly. Paper mills in Italy and Germany made printing practical."],
["The Gutenberg Bible","Gutenberg’s great Bible, printed around 1455, had about 1,280 pages in two volumes, with 42 lines per page. About 180 copies were made, some on paper and some on vellum. Its letters looked like handwriting, and colored initials were added by hand. It is considered one of the most beautiful books ever printed."],
["Printing spreads","Printers trained in Mainz carried the craft across Europe. By 1500, presses in more than 200 towns had printed millions of books, called incunabula, from the Latin for cradle. Venice became a center of printing. William Caxton set up England’s first press in 1476, printing Chaucer’s Canterbury Tales."],
["Newspapers and the public","Printing allowed the first newspapers, which appeared in Germany in the early 1600s. Newspapers spread news, opinions and advertisements, helping people take part in public debate. France’s Gazette began in 1631, and the first daily newspaper in English, the Daily Courant, appeared in London in 1702."],
["Printing and science","Printed books allowed scientists to share accurate diagrams, tables and results. Copernicus, Vesalius, Galileo and Newton all reached readers through print. Because every copy was the same, errors could be corrected, and knowledge built upon. Printing helped make the Scientific Revolution possible."],
["Printing today","Modern presses print newspapers and books at enormous speed. Laser and inkjet printers let people print at home. 3D printers build objects layer by layer, from toys to machine parts and even houses. E-books and screens have reduced some printing, but billions of books are still printed each year."]],
sp:["Johannes Gutenberg","Johannes Gutenberg was born in Mainz, Germany, around 1400 and trained as a goldsmith. Using his metalworking skills, he developed a hand mold to cast large numbers of identical metal letters, an oil-based ink that stuck to metal and a press adapted from wine presses. His partnership with the financier Johann Fust ended in a lawsuit, and Gutenberg lost his workshop. He died in 1468, little known, but his invention changed the world."],
g:[["Movable type","Individual letters that can be rearranged and reused"],["Press","A machine that presses paper onto inked type"],["Incunabula","Books printed in Europe before 1501"],["Typeface","A design of letters, such as a font"]],
f:["About 49 copies of the Gutenberg Bible survive, some incomplete.","The word font comes from the French for something melted, because type was cast in metal.","The first printing press in the Americas was set up in Mexico City in 1539."]},
flight:{s:[
["Dreams of flight","For thousands of years people dreamed of flying. The Greek myth of Icarus told of wings made of feathers and wax. Leonardo da Vinci sketched flying machines with flapping wings. Many early inventors jumped from towers wearing wings and were hurt or killed. The secret of flight lay in fixed wings, not flapping."],
["The Wright brothers","Wilbur and Orville Wright ran a bicycle shop in Dayton, Ohio. They studied birds and tested gliders and model wings in a homemade wind tunnel. They built their own lightweight engine and propellers. On 17 December 1903, at Kitty Hawk, Orville flew for 12 seconds. By 1905 they could fly for more than half an hour."],
["Planes in war","Airplanes were first used in war for scouting in World War I and soon for dogfights and bombing. In World War II, fighters and bombers played huge roles, and jet engines were first used. Aircraft carriers became the most powerful warships. Military research led to faster, larger and more reliable aircraft."],
["Jet engines","A jet engine sucks in air, compresses it, mixes it with fuel and burns it, blasting hot gas out the back to push the plane forward. Frank Whittle in Britain and Hans von Ohain in Germany invented jet engines independently in the 1930s. The Boeing 707, introduced in 1958, made jet travel common."],
["Helicopters and drones","Helicopters use spinning rotors to lift straight up, hover and fly in any direction, making them useful for rescues and reaching remote places. Igor Sikorsky built the first practical helicopter in 1939. Today drones, small remote-controlled aircraft, are used for photography, mapping, deliveries and disaster surveys."],
["Air travel today","More than 4 billion airline passenger trips are made each year. The busiest airports, such as Atlanta, Dubai and London Heathrow, handle tens of millions of passengers. Aviation contributes to climate change, so engineers are developing more efficient planes, sustainable fuels made from waste and electric aircraft for short flights."]],
sp:["Amelia Earhart","Amelia Earhart was an American pilot who became the first woman to fly solo across the Atlantic in 1932 and set many records. In 1937 she attempted to fly around the world near the equator with navigator Fred Noonan. After stops in Southeast Asia and New Guinea, they disappeared on 2 July over the Pacific while searching for tiny Howland Island. Despite many searches, their fate remains one of aviation’s great mysteries."],
g:[["Lift","The upward force produced by wings"],["Thrust","The force that pushes a plane forward"],["Drag","Air resistance that slows a plane"],["Aileron","A movable part of a wing used to roll a plane"]],
f:["The Wrights’ first flight was shorter than the wingspan of a modern Boeing 747.","Commercial airliners usually cruise at about 10,000 to 12,000 m.","The Montgolfier brothers’ first balloon passengers were a sheep, a duck and a rooster."]},
renew:{s:[
["Why renewable energy matters","Burning coal, oil and gas releases carbon dioxide, the main gas causing climate change, along with air pollution that harms health. Renewable energy produces little pollution while running. It also helps countries that import most of their oil and coal rely more on their own resources."],
["How solar cells work","A solar cell is made of two layers of silicon treated with different elements. When sunlight strikes it, its energy knocks electrons loose, and the layers push them in one direction, creating a current. Many cells are wired together in a panel. Panels work even on cloudy days, though they produce less power."],
["Hydropower","Hydropower plants use flowing or falling water to spin turbines. Large dams store water in reservoirs, while run-of-river plants use the natural flow. Hydropower is the largest source of renewable electricity, with giant plants such as the Three Gorges Dam in China and Itaipu on the Brazil–Paraguay border. Dams can flood land and affect rivers, so their impacts must be weighed."],
["Geothermal energy","Deep underground, rocks are hot. Geothermal plants drill wells to bring up hot water and steam, which drive turbines. The United States, Indonesia, the Philippines, Türkiye, New Zealand, Kenya and Iceland are leading users. Geothermal plants produce power steadily, day and night, and take up little land."],
["Renewables at home","Homes and businesses can install rooftop solar panels, solar water heaters and small wind turbines. In many countries, net metering allows homeowners to sell extra solar electricity to the grid. Australia has one of the highest rates of rooftop solar in the world, on about one in three homes."],
["The energy transition","Many countries plan to replace fossil fuels with renewable energy over the coming decades. This requires building solar and wind farms, stronger grids, storage, electric cars and efficient buildings. In 2023 renewables made up about 30% of global electricity, and solar was the fastest-growing source."]],
sp:["Larderello","In 1904 Prince Piero Ginori Conti lit five light bulbs in Larderello, in Tuscany, Italy, using a generator driven by natural steam from the ground. In 1913 the world’s first commercial geothermal power plant opened there. Larderello still produces electricity today, and geothermal energy has spread to volcanic regions around the world, from Iceland and New Zealand to Kenya’s Rift Valley and Indonesia."],
g:[["Renewable","Naturally replenished and not used up"],["Photovoltaic","Turning light directly into electricity"],["Fossil fuel","Coal, oil or gas formed from ancient life"],["Net metering","Selling extra rooftop solar power to the grid"]],
f:["The world’s largest solar parks cover areas of tens of square kilometers.","Hydropower is the oldest form of renewable electricity, first used in 1882.","Iceland gets almost all its electricity from hydropower and geothermal energy."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-tech2.js */
// In-depth material (Technology, part 2): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
gps:{s:[
["Before satellite navigation","For centuries sailors found their way using the Sun, stars, compasses and maps. Measuring longitude was so difficult that in 1714 Britain offered a prize for a solution; John Harrison’s marine chronometers, accurate clocks for ships, won it. In the 1900s radio navigation systems such as LORAN helped ships and planes. GPS made navigation precise everywhere."],
["Transit and the first idea","When Sputnik was launched in 1957, scientists tracked it by listening to how its radio signal changed pitch as it passed overhead. They realized the process could be reversed: if a satellite’s position is known, a receiver could work out its own location. This led to Transit, the first satellite navigation system, used by the US Navy from 1964."],
["Atomic clocks","GPS depends on extremely accurate timing. Each satellite carries atomic clocks that use the vibrations of cesium or rubidium atoms to keep time to within billionths of a second. Because radio signals travel at the speed of light, an error of one millionth of a second in timing would put a receiver off by about 300 m."],
["Improving accuracy","Signals can be bent by the atmosphere or bounce off buildings, causing errors. Systems such as differential GPS use ground stations at known positions to calculate and broadcast corrections. Surveyors using special receivers can find positions to within centimeters. Modern phones combine signals from several satellite systems with Wi-Fi and mobile towers."],
["GPS in science","Scientists place GPS stations on the ground to measure tiny movements of Earth’s crust, the slow drift of tectonic plates, the swelling of volcanoes before eruptions and the sinking of land as groundwater is pumped out, a serious problem in cities such as Jakarta and Mexico City. GPS signals also help measure moisture in the atmosphere for weather forecasts."],
["Privacy and safety","Location data from phones can reveal where people live, work and travel, so many apps ask permission before using it. GPS signals are weak and can be jammed or faked, a threat to ships and planes. Countries are developing backup systems to protect navigation and timing for critical services."]],
sp:["Ride-hailing and delivery apps","When you book a ride or order food through an app, GPS shows where you, the driver and the restaurant are, and calculates the route and arrival time. Ride-hailing and delivery apps such as Uber, Didi, Grab and Ola depend on GPS and maps to match millions of passengers and drivers each day. Delivery riders use GPS to navigate crowded city streets, and customers can watch their orders move on a map."],
g:[["Trilateration","Finding a position from distances to known points"],["Atomic clock","An extremely precise clock using atoms"],["Receiver","A device that picks up satellite signals"],["Coordinates","Numbers that give a location, such as latitude and longitude"]],
f:["GPS satellites travel at about 14,000 km/h.","The full GPS constellation was declared operational in 1995.","Smartphones can use GPS, GLONASS, Galileo and BeiDou satellites together."]},
ai:{s:[
["What intelligence means for machines","Researchers distinguish narrow AI, which performs one task such as recognizing faces or translating text, from general AI, a hypothetical system as flexible as a human mind. All current AI is narrow, though some systems handle many tasks. AI systems do not think or feel as humans do; they find patterns in data."],
["Expert systems","In the 1970s and 1980s, researchers built expert systems, programs that followed large sets of rules written by human experts, for example to diagnose diseases or find minerals. They worked in narrow areas but were hard to update and could not learn on their own, which helped lead to an AI winter."],
["Deep learning","Deep learning uses neural networks with many layers. In 2012 a deep network called AlexNet won an image recognition contest by a wide margin, sparking an explosion of research. Powerful graphics processors and huge datasets of labeled images and text made it possible. Deep learning now powers speech recognition, translation and self-driving car research."],
["Games and AI","Games have been a testing ground for AI. Deep Blue beat Garry Kasparov at chess in 1997. In 2016 AlphaGo, by DeepMind, beat Lee Sedol at Go, a game far more complex than chess. AlphaZero later taught itself chess, shogi and Go from scratch. AI systems have also beaten top players in poker and video games."],
["AI in science and medicine","AI helps doctors read X-rays and scans, predict disease and discover new drugs. In 2020 DeepMind’s AlphaFold predicted the three-dimensional shapes of proteins with remarkable accuracy, a breakthrough that won a share of the 2024 Nobel Prize in Chemistry. AI also helps forecast weather, design materials and analyze telescope data."],
["Rules and responsibility","As AI spreads, governments are writing rules. The European Union passed its AI Act in 2024. Concerns include bias, when systems treat some groups unfairly because of the data they learned from; deepfakes, realistic fake images and videos; job changes; and the safety of very powerful systems. Many experts call for AI that is transparent, fair and accountable."]],
sp:["Chatbots and language models","A large language model is trained on enormous amounts of text to predict the next word in a sentence. By learning patterns of language, it can answer questions, summarize articles, translate, write code and hold conversations. ChatGPT, released in November 2022, made such chatbots famous, and many others followed. They can be very helpful, but they sometimes make up facts, called hallucinations, so their answers should be checked against reliable sources."],
g:[["Algorithm","A set of step-by-step instructions"],["Machine learning","Teaching computers to learn from data"],["Neural network","A computing system loosely inspired by the brain"],["Training data","Examples used to teach an AI system"]],
f:["The term artificial intelligence was coined by John McCarthy in 1955 for the 1956 Dartmouth workshop.","Geoffrey Hinton and John Hopfield won the 2024 Nobel Prize in Physics for work on neural networks.","AI systems use large amounts of electricity in data centers to train and run."]},
battery:{s:[
["Volta’s pile","Alessandro Volta stacked disks of zinc and copper separated by cloth soaked in salt water. The pile produced a steady electric current, the first battery. Volta was inspired by Luigi Galvani’s experiments, in which frog legs twitched when touched with metal. Napoleon was so impressed that he made Volta a count."],
["Sizes and shapes","Batteries come in many shapes. Cylindrical AA and AAA cells power remotes and toys, button cells power watches and hearing aids, and flat pouch cells fit inside phones. Car batteries are large lead-acid batteries. Electric vehicle battery packs contain thousands of small cells or hundreds of larger ones."],
["Energy density","Energy density measures how much energy a battery holds for its weight or size. Lithium-ion batteries hold several times more energy than lead-acid batteries of the same weight, which is why they are used in phones, laptops and electric cars. Gasoline still holds far more energy per kilogram, so researchers keep working to improve batteries."],
["Charging and aging","Every time a rechargeable battery is charged and discharged, tiny chemical changes slowly reduce its capacity. Heat speeds up aging. Fast charging is convenient but can strain batteries. Keeping devices out of hot places, such as cars in the sun, and avoiding fully draining the battery helps batteries last longer."],
["Battery safety","Damaged or poorly made lithium-ion batteries can overheat and catch fire, a process called thermal runaway. Swollen batteries should not be used. Batteries should never be thrown in the trash or into fires, because they can explode and leak harmful chemicals. Many shops collect used batteries for safe recycling."],
["Batteries for the grid","Huge battery systems store electricity from solar and wind farms and release it when needed, such as in the evening. They also respond within milliseconds to stabilize the grid. California, China and Australia have built some of the largest, and battery systems combined with solar panels bring power to remote villages and islands."]],
sp:["John Goodenough","John B. Goodenough, an American physicist, discovered in 1980 that a cobalt oxide cathode could greatly increase the power of lithium batteries, a key step toward lithium-ion batteries. He kept working into his late 90s on new battery designs. In 2019, at 97, he became the oldest person ever to win a Nobel Prize, sharing it with Stanley Whittingham and Akira Yoshino. He died in 2023 at the age of 100."],
g:[["Anode","The negative electrode of a battery"],["Cathode","The positive electrode of a battery"],["Electrolyte","The substance that lets ions move between electrodes"],["Capacity","How much charge a battery can store"]],
f:["The Baghdad Battery, a 2,000-year-old clay jar, has been claimed as an ancient battery, though most experts doubt it.","Volta’s name gives us the unit volt.","An electric car battery may contain several thousand individual cells."]},
nuclear:{s:[
["Discovering fission","In 1938 Otto Hahn and Fritz Strassmann in Berlin found that bombarding uranium with neutrons produced lighter elements. Lise Meitner and Otto Frisch explained that the uranium nucleus had split, releasing enormous energy, and named it fission. Scientists quickly realized that a chain reaction could produce either a bomb or a power source."],
["Chicago Pile-1","On 2 December 1942, a team led by Enrico Fermi under the stands of a sports field at the University of Chicago started the first controlled nuclear chain reaction. Their reactor, Chicago Pile-1, was a pile of graphite blocks and uranium. It produced only half a watt of power, but it proved that nuclear energy could be controlled."],
["Kinds of reactors","Most reactors are pressurized water reactors, in which water under high pressure carries heat from the core to make steam in a separate loop. Boiling water reactors make steam directly. Other designs use heavy water, gas or liquid metal as coolants. Fast breeder reactors can create more fuel than they use."],
["Chernobyl and Fukushima","In April 1986 a flawed test at the Chernobyl plant in Ukraine caused a reactor explosion and fire, spreading radioactive material across Europe and forcing hundreds of thousands to leave their homes. In March 2011 an earthquake and tsunami knocked out cooling at Japan’s Fukushima Daiichi plant, causing meltdowns in three reactors. Both led to stricter safety rules."],
["Fusion power","Fusion, the process that powers the Sun, joins light atoms such as hydrogen to make helium, releasing huge energy with little long-lived waste. It requires temperatures of over 100 million degrees. ITER, a giant international fusion experiment being built in France, aims to show that fusion can produce more energy than it uses."],
["Nuclear energy and climate","Nuclear power plants produce almost no carbon dioxide while running, and some countries see them as important for fighting climate change. France gets most of its electricity from nuclear power. Critics point to high costs, long construction times, waste and accident risks. Germany closed its last reactors in 2023, while countries such as China and India are building new ones."]],
sp:["Zwentendorf, the plant that never ran","Austria’s only nuclear power plant, at Zwentendorf on the Danube, was completed in 1978 at great cost. Weeks before it was due to start, Austrians voted in a referendum, and by a very narrow margin, about 50.5 percent, they rejected nuclear power. The plant never produced a single watt of nuclear electricity. Today it is used for training, film shoots and even as a solar power station."],
g:[["Fission","Splitting heavy atoms to release energy"],["Fusion","Joining light atoms to release energy"],["Reactor","A machine where a controlled nuclear chain reaction takes place"],["Radioactive","Giving off radiation as atoms decay"]],
f:["One kilogram of uranium-235 can release about as much energy as nearly 3,000 tonnes of coal.","There are about 440 nuclear power reactors operating in the world.","Nuclear submarines can travel for many years without refueling."]},
rail:{s:[
["Railway mania","In the 1840s Britain experienced railway mania, as investors poured money into new lines and thousands of kilometers of track were built. Railways cut travel times from days to hours, let people take day trips to the seaside and carried fresh food and newspapers quickly to cities. They also forced the adoption of standard time."],
["Railways across continents","The first transcontinental railroad across the United States was completed in 1869, linking the Atlantic and Pacific coasts. Canada’s railway was finished in 1885, and the Trans-Siberian Railway, completed in 1916, crossed Russia. Railways opened up interiors to settlement, farming and mining."],
["Underground and metro","London opened the first underground railway, the Metropolitan Railway, in 1863, using steam trains in tunnels. Electric trains later made subways cleaner. Today large cities such as Tokyo, Shanghai, Paris and New York have huge metro networks carrying millions of passengers a day, and cities such as Dhaka and Ho Chi Minh City opened their first metros in the 2020s."],
["High-speed rail","Japan’s Shinkansen, the bullet train, opened between Tokyo and Osaka in 1964 in time for the Tokyo Olympics. It has carried billions of passengers with an excellent safety record. France’s TGV, Spain’s AVE and China’s vast high-speed network followed. China now has more high-speed rail than the rest of the world combined."],
["Freight trains","Freight trains carry coal, grain, ore, cars and shipping containers across continents. Long trains in Australia and the United States can be more than 2 km long. Trains use far less fuel per tonne carried than trucks, making them more efficient and less polluting."],
["The future of rail","Engineers are developing faster maglev trains, hydrogen-powered trains and better signaling systems that allow trains to run closer together safely. New high-speed lines are being built in India, and Indonesia opened Southeast Asia’s first high-speed railway, between Jakarta and Bandung, in 2023."]],
sp:["The bullet train","Japan’s Shinkansen, the first high-speed railway, opened on 1 October 1964, just before the Tokyo Olympics, linking Tokyo and Osaka at up to 210 km/h. Its sleek trains were nicknamed bullet trains. In more than 60 years of service, no passenger has been killed in a crash, and trains are famously punctual, with average delays of under a minute. It inspired high-speed lines around the world."],
g:[["Locomotive","An engine that pulls a train"],["Gauge","The distance between the rails"],["Maglev","Magnetic levitation train"],["Metro","An urban rapid transit railway"]],
f:["Japan’s Shinkansen trains are known for being on time to within seconds on average.","The longest straight stretch of railway in the world, in Australia, runs 478 km without a curve.","The world’s busiest train station by passengers is Shinjuku in Tokyo."]},
phone:{s:[
["Bell’s experiments","Alexander Graham Bell, a teacher of deaf students, studied how sound and speech work. With his assistant Thomas Watson, he experimented with sending several telegraph messages along one wire, which led him to transmit voice. On 10 March 1876 he spoke into his device, and Watson heard him in another room."],
["Telephone networks","Early telephones were connected in pairs, but switchboards soon linked many lines. Operators, often young women, connected calls by plugging cables into sockets. In 1891 Almon Strowger invented the automatic exchange, which let callers dial numbers themselves. Long-distance lines and undersea cables gradually connected the world."],
["Mobile networks","The first commercial cellular network opened in Japan in 1979. Mobile networks divide areas into cells, each with its own tower. As phones move, calls pass from one cell to the next. Each generation, from 1G analog to 2G digital, 3G data, 4G broadband and 5G, made phones faster and more capable."],
["The texting boom","The first text message, Merry Christmas, was sent in 1992. Because texts were cheap, young people around the world adopted them enthusiastically in the late 1990s and 2000s, inventing new abbreviations. Billions of texts were sent each day, and the Philippines was nicknamed the texting capital of the world. Today messaging apps have largely replaced texts."],
["How smartphones changed life","Smartphones let people take photos, navigate, shop, bank, study and stay in touch on social media. In many countries they are used for mobile money and online learning and work. But they also raise concerns about screen time, sleep, privacy and online scams, so learning to use them wisely is important."],
["Undersea cables","Most international calls and internet traffic travel through fiber-optic cables laid along the ocean floor. The first transatlantic telephone cable, TAT-1, opened in 1956. Today more than 500 cables link continents, some stretching over 20,000 kilometers."]],
sp:["The first mobile phone call","On 3 April 1973, Martin Cooper, an engineer at Motorola, stood on a street in New York City and made the first call from a handheld mobile phone, to a rival at Bell Labs. The prototype, called the DynaTAC, weighed about 1.1 kg and was about 25 cm long. Its battery lasted only about 30 minutes of talk time and took ten hours to charge. The first commercial version went on sale in 1983 for about 4,000 US dollars."],
g:[["Switchboard","Equipment used to connect phone calls by hand"],["Cellular network","A mobile network divided into areas served by towers"],["SMS","Short Message Service, text messaging"],["Fiber optics","Thin glass strands that carry data as light"]],
f:["The first text message, sent in 1992, said Merry Christmas.","There are more mobile phone subscriptions in the world than people.","Bell refused to have a telephone in his study, considering it a distraction."]},
sat:{s:[
["Launching satellites","Satellites are carried into space by rockets. A rocket must reach about 28,000 km/h to place a satellite in low Earth orbit. Launch sites near the equator, such as Kourou in French Guiana, give rockets an extra boost from Earth’s rotation. Some rockets, such as SpaceX’s Falcon 9, land their first stage back on Earth to be reused."],
["Communications satellites","Communications satellites relay television, telephone and internet signals around the world. Geostationary ones appear fixed in the sky, so dishes can point at them. Satellite networks in low orbit, made of thousands of small satellites, can bring internet to remote islands, ships and villages without cables."],
["Earth observation","Satellites take pictures of Earth to monitor forests, farms, cities, oceans, ice and pollution. The Landsat program has photographed Earth since 1972, creating a record of changes over decades. After disasters such as typhoons and earthquakes, satellite images help responders see which areas are damaged."],
["Science satellites","Space telescopes, such as Hubble and James Webb, observe the universe without the blurring of the atmosphere. Other satellites study the Sun, Earth’s magnetic field, gravity and climate. GRACE satellites measured tiny changes in Earth’s gravity to track melting ice and groundwater."],
["Crowded orbits","Space around Earth is getting crowded. More than 10,000 active satellites and millions of pieces of debris orbit the planet. Collisions can create more debris. In 2009 an American satellite and a dead Russian satellite collided. Agencies track debris and plan ways to remove old satellites."],
["Small satellites","Tiny satellites called CubeSats, about the size of a loaf of bread or smaller, are cheap to build and launch. The first were launched in 2003. Universities and students around the world now build them, and several countries, including Ghana, Kenya and Bhutan, launched their first satellites as CubeSats."]],
sp:["Landsat","Since Landsat 1 was launched in 1972, the American Landsat satellites have photographed every part of Earth’s land again and again, building the longest continuous record of our planet from space. Scientists use the pictures to track deforestation in the Amazon, the growth of cities, shrinking glaciers and the spread of farmland. Since 2008 the images have been free for anyone to use."],
g:[["Orbit","The path of an object around a planet"],["Geostationary","Orbiting at the same speed as Earth’s rotation"],["CubeSat","A small, standardized satellite"],["Space debris","Junk orbiting Earth"]],
f:["The Moon is Earth’s only natural satellite.","The International Space Station is the largest human-made object in orbit.","The first CubeSats were launched in 2003."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-tech3.js */
// In-depth material (Technology, part 3): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
laser:{s:[
["The maser","Before the laser came the maser, which amplified microwaves instead of light. Charles Townes built the first maser in 1954. He and Arthur Schawlow then proposed extending the idea to visible light. Townes shared the 1964 Nobel Prize in Physics with two Soviet scientists, Nikolay Basov and Aleksandr Prokhorov, for this work."],
["The first laser","On 16 May 1960 Theodore Maiman at Hughes Research Laboratories in California fired the first working laser, using a synthetic ruby crystal and a powerful flash lamp. It produced brief pulses of deep red light. At first some people called the laser a solution looking for a problem, but uses were soon found everywhere."],
["Lasers in everyday life","Lasers read barcodes at supermarket checkouts, print documents, play and record discs and carry internet data through fiber-optic cables. Laser pointers are used in presentations. Lasers measure distances in construction and in cars that sense obstacles. Light shows at concerts use powerful colored lasers."],
["Lasers in medicine","Doctors use lasers to reshape the cornea to correct vision, remove cataracts, seal blood vessels, treat skin conditions and remove tattoos. Laser scalpels can cut tissue while sealing it at the same time, reducing bleeding. Dentists use lasers to treat gums and teeth."],
["Lasers in science","Scientists use lasers to cool atoms to temperatures near absolute zero, to build atomic clocks, to study chemical reactions that happen in quadrillionths of a second and to detect gravitational waves. Huge lasers at the National Ignition Facility in the United States are used for fusion research."],
["Lidar","Lidar sends out pulses of laser light and measures how long they take to bounce back, building detailed 3D maps. It is used by self-driving cars, surveyors, archaeologists who use it to find ruins hidden under forests, and scientists who measure forests, glaciers and coastlines. In 2018 lidar revealed more than 60,000 hidden Maya structures in the jungles of Guatemala."]],
sp:["Measuring the Moon","Apollo 11, 14 and 15 astronauts and two Soviet rovers left special reflectors on the Moon. Observatories on Earth fire laser pulses at them and time how long the light takes to return, about 2.5 seconds for the round trip. These measurements show the distance to the Moon to within a few millimeters and reveal that the Moon is moving away from Earth by about 3.8 cm a year. They have also tested Einstein’s theory of gravity."],
g:[["Coherent","Light waves in step with each other"],["Laser diode","A tiny semiconductor laser"],["Lidar","Measuring distance with laser pulses"],["Optical fiber","A thin glass strand that carries light"]],
f:["The word laser started as an acronym but is now an ordinary word.","Laser light can be focused into a spot smaller than a human hair.","The first laser was made with a ruby crystal."]},
chip:{s:[
["What a semiconductor is","Pure silicon conducts electricity poorly. Adding tiny amounts of other elements, a process called doping, changes this. Adding phosphorus gives extra electrons, making n-type silicon, while adding boron creates holes, making p-type silicon. Where the two meet, electricity can be controlled, which is how diodes and transistors work."],
["How chips are made","Chips are made on thin, round wafers of pure silicon. Layers of materials are added, patterned with light through masks, a process called photolithography, and etched away. This is repeated many times to build billions of transistors and wires. The most advanced machines use extreme ultraviolet light and cost hundreds of millions of dollars each."],
["Silicon Valley","The area south of San Francisco became known as Silicon Valley after companies such as Fairchild Semiconductor and Intel were founded there in the 1950s and 1960s. Many technology companies, including Apple, Google and Nvidia, are based there. The term now stands for the high-tech industry in general."],
["The chip industry today","Making the most advanced chips requires enormous investment. Taiwan’s TSMC is the world’s largest contract chipmaker. South Korea, the United States, Japan, the Netherlands and China are also major players. A shortage of chips during 2020 to 2022 slowed the production of cars, phones and game consoles worldwide."],
["Chips everywhere","Chips are found in phones, computers, cars, washing machines, medical devices, toys and credit cards. A modern car may contain over 1,000 chips. Special chips called graphics processing units, or GPUs, are used to train artificial intelligence. Tiny chips also power sensors in the Internet of Things."],
["The limits of shrinking","Transistors have shrunk so much that some features are only a few dozen atoms wide. At this scale, strange quantum effects cause problems, and making chips smaller is getting harder and more expensive. Engineers are stacking chips in 3D and developing new materials and designs to keep improving performance."]],
sp:["Moore’s law","In 1965 Gordon Moore, who later co-founded Intel, noticed that the number of transistors that could be placed on a chip was doubling about every year, a rate he later revised to every two years. This trend, called Moore’s law, held for about five decades and drove the rapid improvement of computers. The Intel 4004 of 1971 had about 2,300 transistors; today’s most advanced chips have tens of billions."],
g:[["Semiconductor","A material that partly conducts electricity"],["Transistor","A tiny electronic switch"],["Wafer","A thin slice of silicon on which chips are made"],["Photolithography","Printing circuit patterns with light"]],
f:["Silicon is the second most abundant element in Earth’s crust.","A single silicon wafer can hold hundreds of chips.","Taiwan makes most of the world’s most advanced chips."]},
radio:{s:[
["Maxwell and Hertz","In the 1860s James Clerk Maxwell, a Scottish physicist, wrote equations showing that electricity and magnetism are linked and that light is an electromagnetic wave. He predicted other, invisible waves. In 1887 Heinrich Hertz in Germany produced and detected radio waves with sparks, proving Maxwell right. The unit of frequency, hertz, is named after him."],
["Wireless telegraphy","Guglielmo Marconi, a young Italian, built on these ideas to send wireless signals in Morse code over longer and longer distances. He moved to Britain, set up a company and in 1901 received signals across the Atlantic. He shared the 1909 Nobel Prize in Physics with Karl Ferdinand Braun. Ships soon carried wireless operators."],
["The golden age of radio","In the 1920s to 1940s, radio was the center of family entertainment. People gathered around radio sets to hear news, music, comedy, sports, quiz shows and serial dramas. Leaders such as Franklin D. Roosevelt spoke directly to citizens in fireside chats. During World War II, radio carried news and propaganda."],
["Transistor radios","The invention of the transistor made small, cheap, battery-powered radios possible from 1954. People could carry radios to the beach or listen in their rooms, which helped spread rock and roll music among teenagers. Transistor radios also brought news and music to remote villages around the world."],
["Radio astronomy","Radio telescopes detect radio waves from space. In 1932 Karl Jansky discovered radio waves coming from the center of the Milky Way. Radio astronomers have discovered pulsars, quasars and the cosmic microwave background. Arrays of radio dishes, such as the Very Large Array in New Mexico, work together like one giant telescope."],
["Radio today","Radio is still popular, especially in cars and in rural areas. Digital radio offers clearer sound, and internet radio and podcasts let people listen anywhere. AM stations often broadcast news and talk, while FM stations play music."]],
sp:["The War of the Worlds","On 30 October 1938, Orson Welles and his Mercury Theatre performed H. G. Wells’s novel The War of the Worlds on American radio as a series of fake news bulletins about a Martian invasion of New Jersey. Some listeners who tuned in late believed it was real. Newspapers reported mass panic, though historians now think the panic was greatly exaggerated. The broadcast showed the power of radio to shape what people believe."],
g:[["Frequency","The number of wave cycles per second"],["AM","Amplitude modulation, varying a wave’s strength"],["FM","Frequency modulation, varying a wave’s frequency"],["Antenna","A device that sends or receives radio waves"]],
f:["Radio waves can be longer than a football field or shorter than a grain of rice.","The first radio broadcast of a sports event took place in 1921.","Radio waves from Earth have been traveling into space for about 100 years."]},
steam:{s:[
["Hero’s engine","About 2,000 years ago, Hero of Alexandria described the aeolipile, a hollow ball mounted on a boiler that spun as steam shot out of two bent pipes. It was a toy or curiosity rather than a useful machine, but it showed that steam could produce motion."],
["Pumping mines","By the 1600s English coal and tin mines were going deeper and flooding with water. In 1698 Thomas Savery patented a steam pump called the Miner’s Friend. Thomas Newcomen’s improved engine of 1712 used a piston and a large beam to pump water. Hundreds of Newcomen engines were built across Britain."],
["Boulton and Watt","James Watt, an instrument maker in Glasgow, partnered with the businessman Matthew Boulton in Birmingham. Their company made engines that could turn wheels as well as pump water, opening the door to factory machinery. Watt coined the term horsepower to compare his engines with the work of horses."],
["Steam locomotives","Richard Trevithick built the first working railway steam locomotive in 1804. George Stephenson’s Rocket of 1829 set the pattern for later designs. Steam locomotives grew more powerful over the next century. The British locomotive Mallard set a world speed record for steam of about 203 km/h in 1938, which still stands."],
["Steam at sea","Steamships replaced sailing ships during the 1800s, first with paddle wheels and later with screw propellers. Isambard Kingdom Brunel’s Great Eastern of 1858 was the largest ship of its time and laid the first lasting transatlantic telegraph cable. Steam turbines later powered fast ocean liners such as the Titanic and the Lusitania."],
["The end of steam","In the 1900s steam engines were gradually replaced by diesel and electric locomotives and by internal combustion engines in cars and ships. But steam is still everywhere: most electricity worldwide is made by steam turbines. Heritage steam railways around the world keep old locomotives running for visitors."]],
sp:["James Watt","James Watt was born in Greenock, Scotland, in 1736. While repairing a model Newcomen engine at the University of Glasgow in 1765, he realized that its cylinder wasted energy by being heated and cooled with each stroke. His solution, a separate condenser, made steam engines about four times more efficient. He also invented a governor to control speed and a copying machine. The SI unit of power, the watt, is named in his honor."],
g:[["Boiler","A container in which water is heated to make steam"],["Piston","A part that slides inside a cylinder"],["Condenser","A device that turns steam back into water"],["Horsepower","A unit of power coined by James Watt"]],
f:["Steam takes up about 1,700 times more space than the water it came from.","Mallard’s steam speed record of 1938 has never been broken.","Steam tractors were used on farms in the early 1900s."]},
scope:{s:[
["Galileo’s discoveries","Galileo built telescopes that magnified about 20 to 30 times. With them he discovered that the Moon has mountains and craters, that Venus shows phases like the Moon, that Jupiter has four large moons and that the Milky Way is made of countless stars. He published his findings in The Starry Messenger in 1610."],
["Bigger and better","Telescopes grew larger. William Herschel built large reflecting telescopes and discovered Uranus in 1781. In 1845 Lord Rosse built the Leviathan of Parsonstown, with a 1.8 m mirror, and saw the spiral shapes of galaxies. The Hooker telescope at Mount Wilson, with a 2.5 m mirror, let Edwin Hubble discover the expanding universe."],
["Mountaintop observatories","Earth’s atmosphere blurs and absorbs light, so observatories are built on high, dry mountains such as Mauna Kea in Hawaii, the Atacama Desert in Chile and La Palma in the Canary Islands. Adaptive optics, mirrors that change shape hundreds of times a second, correct for air turbulence and sharpen images."],
["Space telescopes","Telescopes in space avoid the atmosphere altogether. The Hubble Space Telescope, launched in 1990, has taken some of the most famous images of space. Chandra observes X-rays, Spitzer observed infrared light and Kepler and TESS searched for exoplanets. The James Webb Space Telescope, with a 6.5 m gold-coated mirror, sees farther back in time than ever before."],
["Radio telescopes","Radio telescopes use large dish antennas to collect radio waves. China’s FAST, with a dish 500 m across set in a natural hollow, is the largest single-dish radio telescope. Arrays such as ALMA in Chile combine many dishes. The Event Horizon Telescope linked radio telescopes across the globe to photograph black holes."],
["Amateur astronomy","Many people enjoy astronomy with small telescopes and binoculars. Amateurs have discovered comets, supernovae and asteroids. Clubs hold star parties to share views of the Moon, planets and nebulae. Light pollution from cities makes stargazing harder, so dark-sky parks protect places where the night sky can still be seen clearly."]],
sp:["The James Webb Space Telescope","The James Webb Space Telescope, launched on 25 December 2021, is the largest and most powerful space telescope ever built. Its primary mirror is made of 18 gold-coated hexagonal segments that unfolded in space. A sunshield the size of a tennis court keeps it extremely cold, so it can detect faint infrared light. It orbits about 1.5 million km from Earth and has photographed some of the earliest galaxies, star nurseries and the atmospheres of distant planets."],
g:[["Objective","The main lens or mirror of a telescope"],["Eyepiece","The lens you look through"],["Magnification","How much bigger an object appears"],["Observatory","A building or site for observing the sky"]],
f:["Hubble has made more than 1.5 million observations.","Isaac Newton’s first reflecting telescope was only about 15 cm long.","The Webb telescope can detect heat from objects as faint as a bumblebee at the distance of the Moon, NASA says."]},
robot:{s:[
["Robots in factories","Industrial robots weld, paint and assemble cars, electronics and appliances. They work quickly, precisely and without tiring, and they can handle dangerous jobs. Most are robotic arms fixed in place, guided by computer programs. Japan, South Korea, China, Germany and the United States use the most industrial robots."],
["Robots that explore","Robots go where humans cannot easily go. Rovers explore Mars, robotic submarines dive to the deepest ocean trenches, and robots inspect damaged nuclear plants such as Fukushima. Drones survey disaster zones, and robots defuse bombs. NASA’s Curiosity and Perseverance rovers have driven many kilometers across Mars."],
["Medical robots","Surgical robots, such as the da Vinci system, let surgeons perform delicate operations through tiny cuts using robotic arms controlled from a console. Robots also help in hospitals by delivering medicines, disinfecting rooms and helping patients with rehabilitation. Robotic prosthetic limbs can be controlled by signals from muscles."],
["Robots at home","Robot vacuum cleaners and lawn mowers do household chores. Smart speakers and toy robots respond to voices. Some robots are designed as companions for elderly people. Researchers are building humanoid robots that can walk, climb stairs and use tools, though they are still far less capable than humans."],
["Teaching robots to learn","Traditional robots follow exact instructions. Newer robots use artificial intelligence to recognize objects, navigate rooms and learn from practice or by watching humans. Self-driving cars are a kind of robot, using cameras, radar and lidar to sense the road. Making robots safe and reliable in the messy real world is a major challenge."],
["Robots and jobs","Robots and automation can make work safer and more productive, but they also replace some jobs, especially repetitive ones. New jobs are created in designing, programming and maintaining robots. Many experts say education and training are key to helping workers adapt as robots become more common."]],
sp:["R.U.R. and the word robot","In 1920 the Czech writer Karel Čapek wrote a play called R.U.R., short for Rossum’s Universal Robots. In it, a factory makes artificial workers to do people’s labor, but the robots eventually rebel. The word robot came from robota, a Czech word for forced labor, and was suggested by Čapek’s brother Josef. The play was performed around the world and gave the English language a new word."],
g:[["Robot","A machine that senses, decides and acts"],["Actuator","A motor or device that makes a robot move"],["Sensor","A device that detects light, sound, touch or other information"],["Automation","Using machines to do tasks without humans"]],
f:["The word robotics was first used by science fiction writer Isaac Asimov in 1941.","Some robots can move with insect-like legs or swim like fish.","More than 3 million industrial robots work in factories worldwide."]}
});

;
