// In-depth material (Technology, part 4): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
crypto:{s:[
["Scytales and substitution","Ancient Spartans wrapped a strip of leather around a rod called a scytale and wrote along it; unwrapped, the letters were jumbled until wound on a rod of the same thickness. Substitution ciphers replace each letter with another letter or symbol. Mary, Queen of Scots, used one to plot against Elizabeth I in 1586, but codebreakers cracked it and she was executed."],
["The Vigenère cipher","In the 1500s a cipher using a keyword to shift letters by different amounts was developed and later named after Blaise de Vigenère. Because each letter could be encrypted several ways, it resisted frequency analysis and was called le chiffre indéchiffrable, the unbreakable cipher. In the 1800s Charles Babbage and Friedrich Kasiski showed how to break it."],
["Navajo code talkers","In World War II, the United States Marines used Navajo speakers to send messages in a code based on the Navajo language, which was unwritten and almost unknown outside the Navajo Nation. The code talkers could encode, send and decode messages in seconds, and the Japanese never broke the code. Their work stayed secret until 1968."],
["How public keys work","In public-key cryptography, each person has two linked keys: a public key that anyone can use to lock a message and a private key, kept secret, that is the only way to unlock it. It is like a mailbox that anyone can drop letters into but only the owner can open. This lets strangers communicate securely over the internet."],
["Passwords and security","Websites store passwords as hashes, scrambled versions that cannot easily be turned back into the original. Strong passwords are long and hard to guess, and using a different one for each site limits damage if one is stolen. Two-factor authentication, such as a code sent to your phone, adds another layer of protection."],
["Cryptocurrencies","Cryptocurrencies such as Bitcoin, launched in 2009, use cryptography to record transactions in a shared public ledger called a blockchain, without a central bank. Digital signatures prove who owns coins. Cryptocurrencies are popular with some investors, but their prices swing wildly, and scams are common, so regulators urge caution."]],
sp:["Breaking Enigma","The German Enigma machine had rotors that changed the cipher with every key press, giving an astronomical number of possible settings, changed daily. In the early 1930s Polish mathematicians Marian Rejewski, Jerzy Różycki and Henryk Zygalski first broke Enigma using mathematics. In 1939 they shared their work with Britain. At Bletchley Park, thousands of people, many of them women, used machines called bombes, designed by Alan Turing and Gordon Welchman, to read German messages, a secret called Ultra."],
g:[["Cipher","A method of scrambling a message"],["Encryption","Turning readable data into code"],["Key","Information needed to encrypt or decrypt"],["Hash","A fixed-length fingerprint of data"]],
f:["The word cryptography means hidden writing in Greek.","Bletchley Park’s work remained secret for about 30 years after the war.","The Caesar cipher with a shift of 13, called ROT13, is still used online to hide spoilers."]},
camera:{s:[
["The daguerreotype","Louis Daguerre’s process, announced in 1839, produced a sharp, mirror-like image on a silver-coated copper plate. Each image was unique and could not be copied. Exposures took minutes at first, so sitters had to stay perfectly still, sometimes with hidden head braces. Daguerreotype studios soon opened around the world, making portraits affordable."],
["Negatives and prints","William Henry Fox Talbot in England invented the calotype in 1841, which produced a paper negative from which many positive prints could be made. This negative–positive idea became the basis of film photography for over 150 years. Later, glass plates and then flexible film made photography faster and easier."],
["You press the button","George Eastman’s Kodak camera of 1888 came loaded with film for 100 pictures. When it was full, owners sent the whole camera back to the factory, which developed the pictures and reloaded the camera. Eastman’s slogan was You press the button, we do the rest. His cheap Brownie camera of 1900 cost one dollar."],
["Instant and color photography","In 1948 Edwin Land introduced the Polaroid camera, which produced a finished print within a minute. Color film such as Kodachrome, introduced in 1935, made color slides popular. By the 1960s and 1970s, color prints became common for family photos."],
["The digital revolution","Digital cameras use a light-sensitive sensor made of millions of tiny pixels instead of film. Images are stored as files and can be viewed instantly, edited and shared. Camera phones appeared around 2000, and smartphones made them the most common cameras in the world. Kodak, slow to adapt, filed for bankruptcy in 2012."],
["Photography as art and record","Photographers have recorded wars, such as Mathew Brady’s images of the American Civil War, and great events, such as the Moon landing. Artists such as Ansel Adams photographed landscapes, and photojournalists show the world’s struggles and joys, from revolutions and famines to the civil rights movement."]],
sp:["The first photograph","Around 1826 or 1827, Nicéphore Niépce, a French inventor, placed a plate coated with bitumen, a kind of asphalt, inside a camera obscura at a window of his country house. After an exposure of perhaps several hours or even days, the bitumen hardened where light struck it, recording a blurry view of buildings and a courtyard. Called View from the Window at Le Gras, it is the oldest surviving photograph taken with a camera."],
g:[["Lens","Curved glass that focuses light"],["Aperture","The opening that lets light in"],["Shutter","The part that opens and closes to expose the image"],["Pixel","A tiny dot that makes up a digital image"]],
f:["The word photography means drawing with light in Greek.","Early portraits rarely show smiles partly because exposures were long.","More photos are now taken every year than were taken in the whole 1900s."]},
ev:{s:[
["Early electric cars","In the 1880s and 1890s inventors in Britain, France, Germany and the United States built electric cars. Around 1900, electric cars were popular in American cities because they were quiet, clean and easy to start, unlike gasoline cars that had to be cranked by hand. Some taxis in New York were electric."],
["Why gasoline won","Cheap oil discoveries, better roads for long trips, Henry Ford’s affordable Model T and the electric starter, which removed the need for hand-cranking, gave gasoline cars the advantage. By the 1930s electric cars had almost disappeared, except for vehicles such as milk floats and golf carts."],
["The electric comeback","Concern about air pollution, oil prices and climate change brought electric cars back. Hybrid cars such as the Toyota Prius, launched in 1997, combined engines and motors. Tesla’s Roadster of 2008 showed that lithium-ion batteries could give long range and speed. By the mid-2020s, electric cars made up a large share of new car sales in China and parts of Europe."],
["Charging","Electric vehicles can be charged at home from a wall socket or a home charger, at workplaces and at public charging stations. Fast chargers can add hundreds of kilometers of range in about half an hour. Building enough charging stations is important for EVs to spread, especially in countries where few people have their own garage."],
["Electric two- and three-wheelers","In Asia, electric scooters, motorcycles and tricycles are especially popular because they are cheap to run. India, Vietnam and Indonesia have growing numbers of electric two-wheelers. China has hundreds of millions of electric two-wheelers. They help cut air pollution and noise in crowded streets."],
["Electric buses and trains","Many cities are replacing diesel buses with electric ones. Shenzhen in China switched its entire bus fleet of more than 16,000 buses to electric. Electric trains and light rail are already common, and cities such as Santiago in Chile and Bogotá in Colombia now run large electric bus fleets."]],
sp:["Tesla Motors","Tesla was founded in 2003 by Martin Eberhard and Marc Tarpenning, and Elon Musk soon became its main investor and later chief executive. Its first car, the Roadster of 2008, used thousands of laptop-style lithium-ion cells. The Model S of 2012 and the cheaper Model 3 of 2017 made electric cars desirable and helped push the whole car industry toward electric vehicles. China’s BYD later became one of the world’s largest electric car makers."],
g:[["EV","Electric vehicle"],["Hybrid","A vehicle with both an engine and an electric motor"],["Range","How far a vehicle can travel on a full charge"],["Regenerative braking","Recovering energy when slowing down"]],
f:["An electric car named La Jamais Contente was the first car to pass 100 km/h, in 1899.","Electric motors can turn more than 85% of their energy into motion, compared with about 30% for gasoline engines.","Norway has the highest share of electric cars in new car sales of any country."]},
wind:{s:[
["Windmills around the world","Windmills spread from Persia to China, the Middle East and Europe. In the Netherlands, thousands of windmills drained land below sea level, creating polders for farms and towns. The windmills at Kinderdijk, built in the 1700s, are a World Heritage Site. In the American West, small windmills pumped water for farms and cattle."],
["Wind power for electricity","In 1887 James Blyth in Scotland and Charles Brush in the United States built early wind turbines to make electricity. Denmark became a pioneer, and in the 1970s oil crisis many countries began researching wind power. Modern three-bladed turbines, developed in Denmark, became the standard design."],
["Giant turbines","Modern wind turbines are among the largest machines ever built. The tallest stand more than 250 m high, taller than many skyscrapers, with blades longer than a football field. Bigger turbines catch stronger winds higher up and produce more power. A single large offshore turbine can supply electricity for thousands of homes."],
["Wind farms","Wind farms group many turbines together on land or at sea. The largest offshore wind farms, such as Hornsea in the North Sea, have hundreds of turbines. Engineers choose sites with strong, steady winds and connect them to the grid with underwater cables. Floating turbines can be anchored in deep water."],
["Wind leaders","Denmark built the first offshore wind farm, Vindeby, in 1991 and gets about half its electricity from wind. China has more wind power than any other country, and the United States, Germany, India and Spain follow. Britain’s Hornsea project in the North Sea is one of the largest offshore wind farms in the world."],
["Challenges","Wind power varies with the weather, so it must be combined with other sources and storage. Some people object to the look or noise of turbines, and they can harm birds and bats. Turbine blades are hard to recycle, though new designs are improving this. Despite challenges, wind is one of the cheapest sources of new electricity."]],
sp:["Vindeby","The world’s first offshore wind farm, Vindeby, was built in 1991 in shallow water off the Danish island of Lolland. Its 11 small turbines, each about 35 meters tall, produced just 5 megawatts in total, less than one of today’s giant turbines. It ran for 25 years and was taken down in 2017, having shown that wind farms could survive at sea. Offshore wind has since grown into a huge industry."],
g:[["Turbine","A machine with blades turned by wind or water"],["Nacelle","The housing at the top of a turbine"],["Offshore","At sea, away from the shore"],["Polder","Land reclaimed from the sea, kept dry by pumps"]],
f:["The power in the wind increases with the cube of wind speed.","Denmark gets about half of its electricity from wind.","Ancient sailors used wind power to cross seas thousands of years before windmills."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-sci1.js */
// In-depth material (Science, part 1): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
solar:{s:[
["The Sun","The Sun is a star, a giant ball of hot hydrogen and helium gas about 1.4 million km across, big enough to hold over a million Earths. In its core, at about 15 million °C, nuclear fusion turns hydrogen into helium, releasing the energy that lights and warms the planets. It is about halfway through its life of roughly 10 billion years."],
["Mercury and Venus","Mercury, the smallest planet and closest to the Sun, has almost no atmosphere, so its surface bakes at about 430°C by day and freezes at −180°C at night. Venus, similar in size to Earth, is wrapped in thick clouds of sulfuric acid and carbon dioxide that trap heat, making it the hottest planet at about 465°C, hot enough to melt lead."],
["Earth and Mars","Earth is the only planet known to have liquid water on its surface and life. Mars, the red planet, is a cold desert with the largest volcano in the Solar System, Olympus Mons, and a canyon, Valles Marineris, as long as the United States is wide. Evidence shows that Mars once had rivers and lakes, and robots search for signs of past life."],
["The giant planets","Jupiter, the largest planet, has a Great Red Spot storm bigger than Earth and at least 95 known moons. Saturn has spectacular rings of ice and rock. Uranus spins on its side, probably knocked over by an ancient collision, and Neptune has the fastest winds in the Solar System, over 2,000 km/h. All four have rings, though Saturn’s are by far the brightest."],
["Dwarf planets","In 2006 astronomers defined a planet as a body that orbits the Sun, is round and has cleared its orbit of other objects. Pluto, which shares its orbit with many Kuiper Belt objects, was reclassified as a dwarf planet. Other dwarf planets include Ceres in the asteroid belt and Eris, Haumea and Makemake beyond Neptune."],
["Scale of the Solar System","Distances in the Solar System are enormous. If the Sun were the size of a basketball, Earth would be a peppercorn about 26 m away, and Neptune would be about 780 m away. Light from the Sun takes about 8 minutes to reach Earth and about 4 hours to reach Neptune. Astronomers use the astronomical unit, the Earth–Sun distance of about 150 million km."]],
sp:["The Voyager probes","Voyager 1 and Voyager 2 were launched in 1977 to explore the outer planets. Using a rare planetary alignment, Voyager 2 visited Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune, the only spacecraft to visit the last two. Both carry a golden record with sounds and images of Earth, including greetings in 55 languages, in case they are ever found. Voyager 1 is now more than 24 billion km away, in interstellar space, still sending faint signals home."],
g:[["Planet","A large round body that orbits a star and has cleared its path"],["Dwarf planet","A round body orbiting the Sun that has not cleared its orbit"],["Astronomical unit","The average distance from Earth to the Sun"],["Asteroid belt","A region of rocky bodies between Mars and Jupiter"]],
f:["Saturn is so light for its size that it would float in a giant bathtub of water.","A year on Neptune lasts about 165 Earth years.","The Sun contains about 99.8% of all the mass in the Solar System."]},
photo:{s:[
["Discovering photosynthesis","In the 1640s Jan van Helmont grew a willow tree in a pot and found that the tree gained much weight while the soil lost very little, so plants must get their mass from somewhere else. In 1771 Joseph Priestley showed that a plant could restore air in which a candle had burned out, and Jan Ingenhousz showed in 1779 that plants need light to do this."],
["Chlorophyll","Chlorophyll is the green pigment that captures light energy. It absorbs mainly red and blue light and reflects green, which is why most plants look green. Plants also have other pigments, such as carotenoids, which are yellow and orange. In autumn, when chlorophyll breaks down in some trees, these other colors show, turning leaves gold and red."],
["Stomata and gas exchange","Tiny pores called stomata, mostly on the undersides of leaves, let carbon dioxide in and oxygen and water vapor out. Guard cells open and close them. On hot, dry days, plants close their stomata to save water, which also slows photosynthesis. A single leaf can have tens of thousands of stomata."],
["Food chains begin here","Almost all life on Earth depends on photosynthesis. Plants and algae are producers, making food from sunlight. Herbivores eat plants, and carnivores eat herbivores, so the energy in nearly every meal can be traced back to sunlight captured by photosynthesis. Even fossil fuels are the remains of ancient photosynthesizing life."],
["C4 and CAM plants","Some plants have special ways of photosynthesizing in hot or dry places. C4 plants, such as maize, sugarcane and many tropical grasses, concentrate carbon dioxide inside their leaves, which makes them very efficient in strong sunlight. CAM plants, such as pineapples and cacti, open their stomata only at night to save water, storing carbon dioxide to use during the day."],
["Artificial photosynthesis","Scientists are trying to copy photosynthesis to make fuels from sunlight, water and carbon dioxide. Devices called artificial leaves use catalysts to split water into hydrogen and oxygen. Researchers are also working to make crops photosynthesize more efficiently, which could help feed a growing population."]],
sp:["The Great Oxygenation Event","For the first two billion years of Earth’s history, the atmosphere had almost no oxygen. Around 2.4 billion years ago, tiny cyanobacteria in the oceans, which had begun photosynthesizing, released so much oxygen that it began to build up in the air. This Great Oxygenation Event changed the planet: it rusted iron in the oceans, wiped out many microbes for which oxygen was poisonous and eventually allowed complex, oxygen-breathing life, including animals, to evolve."],
g:[["Chlorophyll","The green pigment that captures light"],["Chloroplast","The part of a plant cell where photosynthesis happens"],["Stomata","Tiny pores in leaves"],["Glucose","A sugar made in photosynthesis"]],
f:["Phytoplankton in the oceans produce roughly half of the oxygen made on Earth each year.","Photosynthesis captures only about 1% of the sunlight that falls on most plants.","Some sea slugs steal chloroplasts from the algae they eat and use them to photosynthesize."]},
dna:{s:[
["Rosalind Franklin","Rosalind Franklin, a British chemist, used X-ray crystallography to take sharp images of DNA. Her famous Photo 51, taken in 1952, showed a pattern that revealed DNA’s helical shape. Without her knowledge, it was shown to James Watson, which helped him and Francis Crick build their model. Franklin died in 1958 and could not share the 1962 Nobel Prize."],
["Genes and chromosomes","A gene is a section of DNA that carries instructions for making a protein or another useful molecule. Humans have about 20,000 genes, packed into 46 chromosomes in most cells, arranged in 23 pairs, one set from each parent. Genes affect traits such as eye color and blood type, along with the risk of some diseases."],
["The Human Genome Project","Launched in 1990, the Human Genome Project brought together scientists from many countries to read all three billion letters of human DNA. It was declared complete in 2003, ahead of schedule. Today a whole human genome can be sequenced in a day for a few hundred dollars, compared with about three billion dollars for the first one."],
["Genetic engineering","Scientists can now cut and paste DNA to change living things. Bacteria have been engineered to make human insulin since 1982. Genetically modified crops, such as insect-resistant corn and golden rice, are grown in many countries. The gene-editing tool CRISPR, adapted from a bacterial defense system, has made precise editing faster and cheaper."],
["DNA and disease","Some diseases, such as cystic fibrosis and sickle-cell disease, are caused by changes in single genes. Genetic testing can diagnose them and help families plan. In 2023, the first CRISPR-based therapy was approved to treat sickle-cell disease. Doctors increasingly tailor treatments, such as cancer drugs, to a patient’s genes."],
["Ethics of DNA","DNA technology raises important questions. Should parents be allowed to edit the genes of future children? Who owns genetic information, and how should it be protected? In 2018 a Chinese scientist announced he had edited the genes of twin babies, provoking worldwide criticism and his imprisonment. Many countries have laws limiting such experiments."]],
sp:["Ancient DNA","DNA can survive for thousands of years in bones and teeth. Scientists have sequenced the genomes of Neanderthals and discovered the Denisovans, a previously unknown group of ancient humans, from a finger bone found in a Siberian cave. Svante Pääbo won the 2022 Nobel Prize for this work. Ancient DNA shows that many people in Southeast Asia and Oceania, including some Filipino Negrito groups such as the Ayta Magbukon, carry traces of Denisovan DNA."],
g:[["Gene","A section of DNA with instructions for a trait or molecule"],["Chromosome","A packaged strand of DNA"],["Genome","All the DNA in an organism"],["Mutation","A change in DNA"]],
f:["If all the DNA in one person’s body were stretched out end to end, it would reach to the Sun and back many times.","Identical twins have almost exactly the same DNA.","DNA from a 2-million-year-old ecosystem in Greenland is the oldest ever sequenced."]},
bh:{s:[
["Escape velocity","To escape a planet’s gravity, an object must travel faster than its escape velocity, 11.2 km/s for Earth. If an object is squeezed small enough, its escape velocity exceeds the speed of light, about 300,000 km/s, and nothing can escape. The boundary where this happens is the event horizon."],
["Stellar black holes","When a star at least about 20 times the Sun’s mass runs out of fuel, its core collapses in a fraction of a second while its outer layers explode as a supernova. If the remaining core is heavy enough, it collapses into a black hole. Our galaxy probably contains millions of stellar black holes."],
["Supermassive black holes","Nearly every large galaxy has a supermassive black hole at its center. The one in the galaxy M87 has about 6.5 billion times the Sun’s mass. Astronomers are still studying how such giants grew so large so early in the universe’s history, as the James Webb Space Telescope has found them in very young galaxies."],
["Quasars","When gas falls into a supermassive black hole, it swirls into a hot, glowing disk called an accretion disk and can shoot out jets of particles at nearly the speed of light. The brightest such objects, quasars, can outshine all the stars in their galaxy combined and be seen across billions of light-years."],
["Time near a black hole","Einstein’s general relativity predicts that time passes more slowly in strong gravity. Close to a black hole’s event horizon, time would slow dramatically compared with time far away. An observer watching something fall in would see it slow down and fade, never quite crossing the horizon."],
["Merging black holes","When two black holes orbit each other, they gradually spiral together and merge, sending out gravitational waves. LIGO first detected such waves in 2015 from black holes about 29 and 36 times the Sun’s mass merging 1.3 billion light-years away. Since then, scientists have detected dozens of mergers."]],
sp:["The first picture of a black hole","On 10 April 2019 the Event Horizon Telescope team released the first image of a black hole, the giant at the center of galaxy M87, 55 million light-years away. To capture it, they linked radio telescopes from Hawaii to Antarctica into one Earth-sized telescope. The image shows a bright ring of glowing gas around a dark shadow. In 2022 the team released an image of Sagittarius A*, the black hole at the center of our own Milky Way."],
g:[["Event horizon","The boundary around a black hole from which nothing escapes"],["Singularity","The point of infinite density thought to lie at a black hole’s center"],["Accretion disk","A disk of gas swirling into a black hole"],["Supernova","A powerful explosion of a dying star"]],
f:["The term black hole was popularized by physicist John Wheeler in 1967.","Black holes do not suck everything in; at a distance their gravity is like that of any object of the same mass.","The nearest known black hole to Earth is about 1,500 light-years away."]},
evo:{s:[
["Before Darwin","Long before Darwin, thinkers wondered whether species could change. In the early 1800s Jean-Baptiste Lamarck proposed that animals pass on traits they gained during life, such as giraffes stretching their necks. Geologists such as Charles Lyell showed that Earth was very old, giving time for slow change. Darwin’s grandfather Erasmus also wrote about evolution."],
["Natural selection explained","Darwin noticed that living things produce more young than can survive, that individuals vary and that some variations are passed on. Those with traits better suited to their environment are more likely to survive and reproduce, passing on those traits. Over many generations, this process, natural selection, can change a population and produce new species."],
["Alfred Russel Wallace","Alfred Russel Wallace, a British naturalist, independently came up with the idea of natural selection while collecting animals in the islands of Southeast Asia. In 1858 he sent Darwin an essay describing it. Their ideas were presented together that year, and Darwin hurried to publish his book. Wallace also identified the Wallace Line, a boundary between Asian and Australian animals."],
["Evidence from DNA","DNA provides powerful evidence for evolution. Closely related species have very similar DNA, and the pattern of similarities matches family trees built from fossils and anatomy. Humans and chimpanzees, for example, share about 98 to 99% of their DNA. Scientists can even estimate when species split apart from the number of differences."],
["Evolution in action","Evolution can be observed today. Bacteria evolve resistance to antibiotics, insects to pesticides and viruses change quickly, which is why flu vaccines are updated yearly. Peppered moths in England turned darker during the Industrial Revolution, when soot blackened tree trunks, and lighter again when air became cleaner."],
["Extinctions","Most species that have ever lived are now extinct. Earth has seen five mass extinctions, the most famous about 66 million years ago, when an asteroid impact helped wipe out the non-bird dinosaurs. Many scientists warn that human activity is now causing species to disappear far faster than normal."]],
sp:["Darwin’s finches","When Charles Darwin visited the Galápagos Islands in 1835 aboard HMS Beagle, he collected small birds that he later learned were different species of finch, each found on particular islands. Their beaks varied in shape: thick for cracking seeds, thin for catching insects. Darwin realized they had probably descended from one ancestor and changed to suit their food. Scientists Peter and Rosemary Grant later watched their beaks evolve within a few years during droughts."],
g:[["Evolution","The change in living things over generations"],["Natural selection","The process by which better-adapted individuals survive and reproduce"],["Adaptation","A trait that helps an organism survive"],["Species","A group of organisms that can breed together"]],
f:["Birds are living dinosaurs, descended from small feathered theropods.","Darwin waited about 20 years before publishing his theory.","The Wallace Line, between Bali and Lombok, roughly separates the animals of Asia from those of Australia."]},
atom:{s:[
["Early ideas","Around 400 BCE the Greek thinkers Leucippus and Democritus proposed that matter is made of tiny, uncuttable particles, which they called atomos. In 1808 John Dalton revived the idea with evidence from chemistry, suggesting that each element has its own kind of atom with a particular weight."],
["The electron and the nucleus","In 1897 J. J. Thomson discovered the electron, showing that atoms contain smaller particles. In 1911 Ernest Rutherford fired particles at thin gold foil and found that a few bounced back, showing that most of an atom’s mass is packed into a tiny, dense nucleus. If an atom were the size of a stadium, its nucleus would be the size of a pea."],
["Electron shells","Niels Bohr proposed in 1913 that electrons orbit the nucleus in fixed energy levels, or shells. The electrons in the outer shell determine how an atom reacts with others. Atoms with full outer shells, such as helium and neon, rarely react, which is why these noble gases are called inert."],
["Groups and periods","In the periodic table, rows are called periods and columns are called groups. Elements in the same group have similar properties. Group 1, the alkali metals, such as sodium and potassium, react violently with water. Group 17, the halogens, such as chlorine, are reactive nonmetals. Group 18 holds the noble gases."],
["Isotopes and radioactivity","Isotopes of an element have the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons. Carbon-14, a radioactive isotope, decays at a known rate, so scientists can use it to date ancient wood, bones and cloth up to about 50,000 years old. Radioactive isotopes are also used in medicine to diagnose and treat diseases."],
["Mendeleev’s table","Dmitri Mendeleev, a Russian chemist, wrote the names and properties of the elements on cards and arranged them in order of atomic weight, grouping similar ones together. He left gaps for undiscovered elements and predicted their properties. When gallium, scandium and germanium were discovered, they matched his predictions, proving the table’s power."]],
sp:["Marie Curie and new elements","Working in a cold, leaky shed in Paris, Marie and Pierre Curie processed tonnes of pitchblende ore to isolate tiny amounts of two new radioactive elements, polonium, named after Marie’s homeland Poland, and radium, which glowed in the dark. Their work showed that atoms could change and release energy, overturning the idea that atoms were unchangeable. Element 96, curium, is named in their honor."],
g:[["Atom","The smallest unit of an element"],["Element","A substance made of only one kind of atom"],["Proton","A positively charged particle in the nucleus"],["Isotope","A form of an element with a different number of neutrons"]],
f:["About 99.9999% of an atom is empty space.","Hydrogen is the most common element in the universe.","There are more atoms in a glass of water than glasses of water in all the oceans."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-sci2.js */
// In-depth material (Science, part 2): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
grav:{s:[
["Aristotle and Galileo","For nearly 2,000 years, people followed Aristotle’s idea that heavy objects fall faster than light ones. Galileo tested falling and rolling objects and found that, without air resistance, all objects fall at the same rate. In 1971 astronaut David Scott dropped a hammer and a feather on the Moon, where there is no air, and they landed together."],
["The law of universal gravitation","Newton showed that every object with mass pulls on every other object. The force grows with the masses and weakens with the square of the distance: twice as far away means one quarter of the pull. The same law explains a falling apple, the Moon’s orbit, the tides and the paths of planets and comets."],
["Gravity on other worlds","Gravity depends on a world’s mass and size. On Mars a person would weigh about 38% of their Earth weight, and on Jupiter about 2.5 times as much. On the Moon, astronauts bounded along in big leaps. The Sun’s gravity at its surface is about 28 times Earth’s."],
["Einstein’s curved spacetime","Einstein imagined space and time woven together into spacetime, which is bent by mass and energy. Objects move along the curves, like marbles rolling on a stretched rubber sheet dented by a bowling ball. This explains why light bends around the Sun, as observed during an eclipse in 1919, and why time runs slightly slower in stronger gravity."],
["Gravity and the tides","The Moon’s gravity pulls on Earth’s oceans, creating bulges of water that produce high tides as Earth rotates beneath them. The Sun also contributes, and when Sun and Moon line up, tides are stronger. Gravity from Jupiter stretches its moon Io so much that it heats the interior, powering volcanoes."],
["The mystery of dark matter","Galaxies spin so fast that the gravity of their visible stars and gas should not be enough to hold them together. Astronomers think an invisible substance, called dark matter, provides extra gravity. It makes up about 85% of the matter in the universe, but no one yet knows what it is. Scientists are searching for it with detectors deep underground."]],
sp:["Newton’s apple","In 1666, during a plague outbreak that closed Cambridge University, young Isaac Newton returned to his family’s farm at Woolsthorpe. According to a story he told later in life, seeing an apple fall from a tree made him wonder whether the force pulling the apple down might reach as far as the Moon and keep it in orbit. The idea grew into his law of universal gravitation, published in the Principia in 1687. A descendant of the tree still grows at Woolsthorpe Manor."],
g:[["Gravity","The force that attracts masses to each other"],["Mass","The amount of matter in an object"],["Weight","The force of gravity on a mass"],["Orbit","A curved path around a planet or star"]],
f:["Gravity is the weakest of the four fundamental forces but acts over the greatest distances.","You would weigh slightly less at the equator than at the poles.","A neutron star’s gravity is about 200 billion times stronger than Earth’s."]},
cell:{s:[
["Microscopes reveal cells","Cells are too small to see with the naked eye. In the 1670s Antonie van Leeuwenhoek, a Dutch cloth merchant, built simple microscopes that magnified over 200 times and saw bacteria, sperm cells and blood cells for the first time, which he called animalcules. Electron microscopes, invented in the 1930s, revealed the tiny structures inside cells."],
["The cell membrane","Every cell is surrounded by a thin, flexible membrane made of fatty molecules. It controls what enters and leaves the cell, letting in food and oxygen and letting out wastes. Plant cells also have a tough cell wall outside the membrane, made of cellulose, which gives plants their stiffness."],
["The nucleus","The nucleus is the cell’s control center, holding its DNA. It directs the cell’s activities by sending instructions to make proteins. Bacteria have no nucleus; their DNA floats freely. Cells with a nucleus, called eukaryotic cells, make up animals, plants, fungi and many single-celled organisms."],
["Energy in cells","Mitochondria are often called the powerhouses of the cell. They use oxygen to break down food molecules, releasing energy stored in a molecule called ATP. Muscle cells, which need a lot of energy, have many mitochondria. Mitochondria have their own DNA, passed down from mothers, which suggests they were once free-living bacteria."],
["How cells make proteins","Proteins do most of the work in cells, building structures, speeding up reactions and carrying messages. Instructions in DNA are copied into messenger RNA, which travels to ribosomes. Ribosomes read the instructions and link amino acids together into long chains that fold into proteins of precise shapes."],
["Cells and disease","Many diseases begin in cells. Cancer happens when cells grow and divide out of control because of changes in their DNA. Viruses invade cells and hijack them to make copies of themselves. Understanding cells helps scientists develop medicines, vaccines and treatments such as cell therapies."]],
sp:["Henrietta Lacks and HeLa cells","In 1951 doctors in Baltimore took cancer cells from Henrietta Lacks, a 31-year-old African American mother, without her knowledge. Her cells, named HeLa, were the first human cells that kept dividing in the laboratory. They have been used in research on polio vaccines, cancer, genetics and viruses, leading to many discoveries. Her story raised important questions about consent and fairness in medical research, and her family has since been recognized."],
g:[["Cell","The smallest unit of life"],["Nucleus","The control center of a cell"],["Membrane","The thin outer layer of a cell"],["Mitochondrion","The part of a cell that releases energy"]],
f:["The largest single cell is an ostrich egg yolk.","Your body makes about 2 million new red blood cells every second.","Some nerve cells can be more than a meter long."]},
light:{s:[
["Measuring the speed of light","In 1676 Ole Rømer noticed that the eclipses of Jupiter’s moons came later when Earth was farther from Jupiter, and he used this to estimate the speed of light. In 1849 Hippolyte Fizeau measured it on Earth using a spinning toothed wheel and a mirror. Today the speed of light in a vacuum is fixed by definition at exactly 299,792,458 m/s."],
["Newton and the prism","In the 1660s Isaac Newton passed sunlight through a prism and saw it spread into a rainbow of colors. Then he used a second prism to recombine the colors into white light, showing that white light is a mixture of all colors. He named the band of colors the spectrum and identified seven colors: red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo and violet."],
["Rainbows","A rainbow forms when sunlight enters raindrops, bends, reflects off the back of the drop and bends again as it leaves, splitting into colors. To see one, the Sun must be behind you. A fainter second rainbow, with colors reversed, sometimes appears above the first. From an airplane, rainbows can appear as complete circles."],
["Shadows and eclipses","Light travels in straight lines, so when an object blocks it, a shadow forms. A solar eclipse happens when the Moon’s shadow falls on Earth, and a lunar eclipse when Earth’s shadow falls on the Moon. During a total lunar eclipse, the Moon turns red because Earth’s atmosphere bends red light onto it."],
["Invisible light","Infrared light, just beyond red, is felt as heat; night-vision cameras and TV remote controls use it. Ultraviolet light, just beyond violet, causes sunburn and helps the skin make vitamin D; bees and many birds can see it. Beyond these lie microwaves, radio waves, X-rays and gamma rays, all forms of electromagnetic radiation."],
["Light and life","Light powers photosynthesis, the basis of most food chains. Animals use light to see, find food and navigate. Some creatures, such as fireflies, glowworms and many deep-sea fish, make their own light through chemical reactions, called bioluminescence. In parts of Southeast Asia, thousands of fireflies flash in time together in riverside mangrove trees."]],
sp:["The speed of light","Light travels about 300,000 km every second, fast enough to circle Earth more than seven times in one second. Even so, the universe is so vast that light from distant stars takes years, centuries or billions of years to reach us, so when we look at the night sky, we see stars as they were long ago. Nothing can travel faster than light in a vacuum, a limit at the heart of Einstein’s theory of relativity."],
g:[["Spectrum","The range of colors in light"],["Refraction","The bending of light as it passes between materials"],["Wavelength","The distance between wave peaks"],["Photon","A particle of light"]],
f:["Light from the Sun is about 8 minutes old when it reaches you.","Light slows down to about two thirds of its vacuum speed in glass.","A light-year is the distance light travels in a year, about 9.5 trillion km."]},
quantum:{s:[
["Max Planck’s quanta","In 1900 Max Planck was trying to explain the colors of light given off by hot objects. He found that he could only do so if energy came in tiny, separate packets, which he called quanta. He thought it was a mathematical trick, but it was the beginning of quantum physics. He won the Nobel Prize in 1918."],
["Einstein and photons","In 1905 Albert Einstein used Planck’s idea to explain the photoelectric effect, in which light knocks electrons out of metals. Bright red light would not do it, but faint blue light would. Einstein said light itself comes in packets, later called photons, each with energy depending on its color. This won him the 1921 Nobel Prize."],
["Wave-particle duality","Experiments show that light and matter behave both as waves and as particles. In the double-slit experiment, electrons fired one at a time through two slits build up a wave-like pattern on a screen, as if each electron passed through both slits. Yet when detectors watch which slit they pass through, the pattern disappears."],
["The uncertainty principle","Werner Heisenberg showed in 1927 that it is impossible to know both the exact position and the exact speed of a particle at the same time. The more precisely one is known, the less precisely the other can be. This is not due to clumsy measurement but is a basic feature of nature."],
["Quantum technology","Quantum physics underlies much modern technology. Transistors, lasers, LEDs, solar cells, MRI scanners and atomic clocks all depend on it. Scientists are now building quantum computers, which use qubits that can be in several states at once, and quantum encryption systems that could make communication extremely secure."],
["Strange interpretations","Physicists still debate what quantum mechanics means. The Copenhagen interpretation, linked to Niels Bohr, says a particle has no definite properties until measured. The many-worlds interpretation suggests that every possible outcome happens in a separate branch of reality. Einstein disliked the randomness of quantum theory, saying God does not play dice."]],
sp:["The Solvay Conference of 1927","In October 1927 the world’s leading physicists met in Brussels at the fifth Solvay Conference to discuss the new quantum theory. A famous photograph shows 29 scientists, 17 of whom had won or would win Nobel Prizes, including Albert Einstein, Niels Bohr, Marie Curie, Max Planck, Werner Heisenberg, Erwin Schrödinger and Paul Dirac. Einstein and Bohr debated whether quantum mechanics was complete, a friendly argument that continued for years."],
g:[["Quantum","The smallest possible packet of energy"],["Photon","A quantum of light"],["Qubit","A quantum bit used in quantum computers"],["Superposition","Being in several states at once"]],
f:["Quantum effects allow the Sun to shine, because fusion depends on quantum tunneling.","Planck’s constant is about 6.6 × 10⁻³⁴ joule-seconds.","The 2022 Nobel Prize in Physics honored experiments on quantum entanglement."]},
relat:{s:[
["The speed of light is constant","Special relativity starts from two ideas: the laws of physics are the same for everyone moving steadily, and the speed of light is the same for all observers, no matter how fast they move. These simple ideas lead to astonishing results, such as moving clocks running slow and moving objects shrinking in the direction of motion."],
["E = mc²","Einstein’s most famous equation says that mass and energy are two forms of the same thing. E is energy, m is mass and c is the speed of light. Because c is so large, a tiny amount of mass contains enormous energy. This explains how the Sun shines and how nuclear power plants and bombs release energy."],
["Einstein’s gravity","In 1915 Einstein extended his theory to include gravity. He realized that a person falling freely feels weightless, so gravity and acceleration are linked. General relativity describes gravity as the curving of spacetime by mass. It correctly explained a small wobble in Mercury’s orbit that Newton’s theory could not."],
["The 1919 eclipse","During a total solar eclipse in May 1919, the British astronomer Arthur Eddington and others photographed stars near the darkened Sun from the island of Príncipe and from Brazil. The stars appeared slightly shifted, exactly as Einstein predicted, because the Sun’s gravity bent their light. The result made Einstein world-famous overnight."],
["Relativity in daily life","Relativity is not just theory. GPS satellites must correct for both special and general relativity, or their positions would drift by kilometers a day. Particle accelerators speed particles close to the speed of light, where their behavior matches relativity exactly. Gravitational lensing lets astronomers use galaxies as giant magnifying glasses."],
["Black holes and the universe","General relativity predicted black holes, an expanding universe and gravitational waves, all now confirmed. Einstein himself doubted some of these predictions at first. Combining relativity with quantum mechanics into a single theory of quantum gravity is one of the great unsolved problems in physics."]],
sp:["Albert Einstein","Albert Einstein was born in Ulm, Germany, in 1879. As a young man working at the patent office in Bern, Switzerland, he published four groundbreaking papers in 1905, his miracle year, on light, atoms, special relativity and E = mc². He moved to the United States in 1933 to escape the Nazis and worked at Princeton until his death in 1955. He was offered the presidency of Israel in 1952 but declined. His name has become a byword for genius."],
g:[["Relativity","Einstein’s theories of space, time and gravity"],["Spacetime","Space and time combined into one fabric"],["Time dilation","Time passing at different rates for different observers"],["Gravitational lensing","The bending of light by gravity"]],
f:["Astronauts on the International Space Station age slightly more slowly than people on Earth.","Einstein did not fail math at school; that is a myth.","The 1919 eclipse expedition’s results were announced in London in November 1919."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-sci3.js */
// In-depth material (Science, part 3): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
brain:{s:[
["Neurons","The brain contains about 86 billion nerve cells called neurons. Each has a cell body, branching dendrites that receive signals and a long axon that sends them. Neurons connect at tiny gaps called synapses, where chemical messengers called neurotransmitters pass signals across. A single neuron can connect with thousands of others."],
["The cerebrum","The cerebrum is the largest part of the brain, with a wrinkled outer layer called the cortex. Its folds increase its surface area. It handles thinking, language, memory, planning and the senses. The left and right halves, or hemispheres, are linked by a thick bundle of fibers called the corpus callosum."],
["The cerebellum and brainstem","The cerebellum, at the back of the brain, coordinates movement, balance and posture, helping you ride a bike or catch a ball. The brainstem connects the brain to the spinal cord and controls automatic functions such as breathing, heartbeat and swallowing. The hypothalamus controls hunger, thirst and body temperature."],
["The nervous system","The brain and spinal cord form the central nervous system. Nerves branching out to every part of the body make up the peripheral nervous system. Sensory nerves carry information to the brain, and motor nerves carry commands to muscles. Reflexes, such as pulling your hand from a hot stove, are handled by the spinal cord before the brain is even aware."],
["Brain plasticity","The brain can change throughout life, forming new connections when we learn and practice skills. This ability is called neuroplasticity. Children’s brains are especially flexible, but adults can also learn languages, music and new skills. After injuries such as strokes, other parts of the brain can sometimes take over lost functions."],
["Keeping the brain healthy","Sleep, exercise, a healthy diet, social activity and learning new things help keep the brain healthy. Helmets protect against head injuries in sports and on bikes and motorcycles. Mental health is as important as physical health, and conditions such as depression and anxiety are common and treatable."]],
sp:["Phineas Gage","In 1848 Phineas Gage, an American railway worker, was injured when an explosion drove an iron rod over a meter long through his head, damaging the front of his brain. Amazingly, he survived and could walk and talk. But friends said his personality changed, and he became impulsive and rude. His case helped scientists understand that the frontal lobes play a role in personality and self-control."],
g:[["Neuron","A nerve cell"],["Synapse","The junction between two neurons"],["Cortex","The wrinkled outer layer of the brain"],["Neurotransmitter","A chemical that carries signals between neurons"]],
f:["The brain uses about 20% of the body’s energy, though it is about 2% of body weight.","The brain cannot feel pain itself, so some brain surgery can be done while patients are awake.","Signals can travel along some nerves at over 100 m per second."]},
immune:{s:[
["White blood cells","White blood cells are the immune system’s soldiers. Neutrophils and macrophages swallow and digest invaders, a process called phagocytosis. Lymphocytes include B cells, which make antibodies, and T cells, which kill infected cells and direct the immune response. A drop of blood holds thousands of white blood cells."],
["Antibodies","Antibodies are Y-shaped proteins that lock onto specific invaders, called antigens, like keys fitting locks. They mark germs for destruction and can block viruses from entering cells. Each B cell makes antibodies for only one antigen, so the body keeps millions of different B cells ready."],
["Immune memory","After fighting an infection, some B and T cells become memory cells that can last for years. If the same germ returns, they recognize it and respond quickly, often before you feel ill. This is why people usually catch diseases such as chickenpox only once, and it is the basis of how vaccines work."],
["The lymphatic system","The lymphatic system is a network of vessels that carries a clear fluid called lymph. Lymph nodes, small bean-shaped organs in the neck, armpits and groin, filter the lymph and are packed with immune cells. They often swell when you are fighting an infection. The spleen, thymus and tonsils are also part of the immune system."],
["Autoimmune diseases","Sometimes the immune system mistakenly attacks the body’s own cells, causing autoimmune diseases such as type 1 diabetes, rheumatoid arthritis and lupus. Scientists do not fully understand why this happens, but genes and environment both play a part. Treatments calm the immune system to reduce damage."],
["Boosting the immune system","A healthy lifestyle supports immunity: getting enough sleep, eating plenty of fruits and vegetables, staying active, managing stress and keeping vaccinations up to date. Washing hands reduces the number of germs the immune system must fight. There is no magic pill that supercharges immunity."]],
sp:["Edward Jenner and Louis Pasteur","Edward Jenner’s smallpox vaccine of 1796 was the first scientific vaccine, though no one yet knew about germs. Nearly a century later, Louis Pasteur showed that weakened germs could protect against disease and developed vaccines against chicken cholera, anthrax and rabies. He named the method vaccination in Jenner’s honor. Their work laid the foundation of immunology, the science of the immune system."],
g:[["Antigen","A substance that triggers an immune response"],["Antibody","A protein that targets a specific invader"],["Lymphocyte","A type of white blood cell"],["Immunity","Protection against a disease"]],
f:["The immune system can recognize billions of different antigens.","Fever helps fight infection by making the body less comfortable for germs.","Breast milk passes antibodies from mother to baby."]},
climate:{s:[
["The atmosphere","Earth’s atmosphere is a blanket of gases, mostly nitrogen and oxygen, held by gravity. The lowest layer, the troposphere, about 10 to 15 km thick, is where weather happens. Above it, the stratosphere holds the ozone layer, which absorbs harmful ultraviolet light. Air pressure and temperature fall as you climb higher."],
["Clouds and rain","Clouds form when warm, moist air rises and cools, and water vapor condenses into tiny droplets or ice crystals. Fluffy cumulus clouds form on sunny days, flat stratus clouds bring gray skies and towering cumulonimbus clouds bring thunderstorms. When droplets combine and grow heavy, they fall as rain, snow or hail."],
["Winds and pressure","Wind is air moving from areas of high pressure to low pressure. The Sun heats the equator more than the poles, driving large wind patterns such as the trade winds. Earth’s rotation curves winds, an effect called the Coriolis effect. Monsoons are seasonal winds that bring wet and dry seasons to much of Asia, Africa and northern Australia."],
["Climate in cities","Cities make their own climates. Concrete, asphalt and buildings soak up the Sun’s heat and release it at night, and cars, factories and air conditioners add more, so a big city can be several degrees warmer than the countryside around it, an effect called the urban heat island. Parks, trees, water and pale roofs help cool cities down."],
["The greenhouse effect","Gases such as carbon dioxide, methane and water vapor trap heat in the atmosphere, keeping Earth warm enough for life. Without them, Earth’s average temperature would be about −18°C. But burning fossil fuels and cutting forests has raised carbon dioxide levels by about 50% since the 1700s, trapping more heat and warming the planet."],
["Effects of climate change","Earth has warmed by about 1.2 to 1.3°C since the late 1800s. Effects include stronger heat waves, heavier rainfall, rising sea levels, melting glaciers and ice sheets and damage to coral reefs. Low-lying coastal and island nations are among the most at risk. In 2015 nearly all countries signed the Paris Agreement to limit warming."]],
sp:["The Keeling Curve","In 1958 scientist Charles David Keeling began measuring carbon dioxide in the air at Mauna Loa Observatory, high on a volcano in Hawaii, far from cities. His measurements, continued to this day, form a rising zigzag line called the Keeling Curve. The zigzag shows plants absorbing carbon dioxide in the northern summer and releasing it in winter. The steady rise, from about 315 parts per million in 1958 to over 420 today, is clear evidence of human impact."],
g:[["Weather","Day-to-day conditions of the atmosphere"],["Climate","Average weather over many years"],["Greenhouse gas","A gas that traps heat in the atmosphere"],["Humidity","The amount of water vapor in the air"]],
f:["The hottest air temperature ever recorded was 56.7°C in Death Valley in 1913.","2024 was the warmest year on record globally.","The World Meteorological Organization, a UN agency, was founded in 1950."]},
sound:{s:[
["What sound is","Sound is a vibration that travels as a wave through air, water or solids. When a guitar string is plucked, it pushes air molecules together and then pulls them apart, creating areas of high and low pressure that spread outward. Sound cannot travel through empty space, so space is silent."],
["Speed of sound","Sound travels about 343 m per second in air at room temperature, about four times faster in water and even faster in steel. You can estimate how far away lightning is by counting the seconds between the flash and the thunder: every three seconds is about 1 km. Aircraft that fly faster than sound create a sonic boom."],
["Frequency and pitch","Frequency, measured in hertz, is how many vibrations happen each second. High frequencies sound high-pitched, like a whistle; low frequencies sound deep, like a drum. Humans hear about 20 to 20,000 hertz. Dogs hear higher pitches, bats and dolphins use ultrasound, and elephants and whales communicate with infrasound too low for us to hear."],
["Musical sound","Musical instruments make sound in different ways. Strings vibrate on guitars and violins, air vibrates in flutes and trumpets and drums vibrate when struck. The shape and material of an instrument add overtones, giving each its own tone, called timbre. Instruments such as the Indian sitar, the Indonesian gamelan gongs and the Australian didgeridoo each have distinctive sounds."],
["Noise and health","Very loud noise can damage the delicate hair cells in the inner ear, causing permanent hearing loss. Traffic, construction, loud music through earphones and fireworks can all harm hearing. Experts recommend keeping earphone volume low and taking breaks. Noise pollution can also disturb sleep and raise stress."],
["Uses of sound","Ships use sonar, sending sound pulses underwater and timing echoes to map the seafloor and find fish. Doctors use ultrasound to see babies in the womb and examine organs. Engineers use sound to detect cracks in metal. Noise-canceling headphones create sound waves that cancel out unwanted noise."]],
sp:["Breaking the sound barrier","As planes flew faster in the 1940s, pilots found that near the speed of sound, shock waves made planes shake violently, and some believed the sound barrier could not be broken. On 14 October 1947, US Air Force pilot Chuck Yeager flew the rocket-powered Bell X-1, dropped from a bomber, faster than sound over California. In 1997 the British ThrustSSC became the first car to break the sound barrier on land."],
g:[["Frequency","Number of vibrations per second, measured in hertz"],["Amplitude","The size of a sound wave, related to loudness"],["Decibel","A unit for measuring loudness"],["Echo","A reflected sound"]],
f:["The loudest sound in recorded history may have been the 1883 eruption of Krakatoa.","Sound travels faster in warm air than in cold air.","The blue whale makes some of the loudest sounds of any animal."]},
magnet:{s:[
["Lodestones","Long ago people discovered lodestones, natural rocks containing the mineral magnetite that attract iron. The ancient Greeks named magnets after Magnesia, a region where such stones were found. In China, lodestones were used in early compasses, called south-pointing spoons, more than 2,000 years ago, at first for fortune-telling and later for navigation."],
["Magnetic fields","Every magnet has a north and a south pole. Like poles repel and opposite poles attract. Around a magnet is an invisible magnetic field, strongest near the poles. Iron filings sprinkled near a magnet line up along the field lines, showing its shape. Cutting a magnet in half makes two smaller magnets, each with two poles."],
["Earth as a magnet","Earth behaves like a giant magnet because of molten iron swirling in its outer core, a process called the geodynamo. The magnetic poles are near, but not at, the geographic poles, and they slowly wander. Every few hundred thousand years, Earth’s magnetic field flips, swapping north and south."],
["The magnetosphere","Earth’s magnetic field extends into space, forming the magnetosphere. It shields the planet from the solar wind, a stream of charged particles from the Sun. Some particles are funneled toward the poles, where they collide with gases in the atmosphere and create auroras. Without the magnetosphere, the solar wind could strip away the atmosphere."],
["Electricity and magnetism","In 1820 Hans Christian Ørsted noticed that an electric current made a nearby compass needle move, showing that electricity creates magnetism. Michael Faraday later showed that moving magnets create electric currents. James Clerk Maxwell combined the two into one theory of electromagnetism. This link powers motors, generators, speakers and transformers."],
["Animals and magnetism","Many animals sense Earth’s magnetic field to navigate. Migrating birds, sea turtles, salmon and whales may use it like a built-in compass to travel thousands of kilometers. Sea turtles return to the beaches where they hatched, possibly by remembering the magnetic signature of the area."]],
sp:["MRI scanners","Magnetic resonance imaging, or MRI, uses a powerful magnet, tens of thousands of times stronger than Earth’s field, and radio waves to make detailed pictures of the inside of the body. The magnet lines up hydrogen atoms in the body’s water, and radio pulses make them give off signals that computers turn into images of the brain, muscles and organs. MRI uses no harmful radiation, but patients must remove metal objects before entering the scanner."],
g:[["Magnetic field","The area around a magnet where its force acts"],["Pole","One of the two ends of a magnet"],["Electromagnet","A magnet made by electric current"],["Compass","A device with a magnetic needle that points north"]],
f:["The strongest magnets made in laboratories are over a million times stronger than Earth’s field.","Magnetic north has been moving toward Siberia at tens of kilometers a year.","Fridge magnets often use a pattern of alternating poles so they stick on one side only."]},
stars:{s:[
["What a star is","A star is a huge ball of glowing gas, mostly hydrogen and helium, held together by its own gravity. In its core, nuclear fusion turns hydrogen into helium and releases enormous energy as light and heat. The Sun is an ordinary, medium-sized star. The nearest star beyond the Sun, Proxima Centauri, is about 4.2 light-years away."],
["Star colors","Stars come in different colors depending on their surface temperature. The coolest are red, at about 3,000°C, then orange, yellow, white and blue, the hottest at over 30,000°C. Betelgeuse in Orion is a cool red supergiant, while Rigel, in the same constellation, is a hot blue-white supergiant. The Sun is a yellow star."],
["How stars die","Stars like the Sun swell into red giants as they run out of fuel, then puff off their outer layers as glowing planetary nebulae, leaving a small, hot core called a white dwarf. Much bigger stars explode as supernovae, leaving neutron stars or black holes. The elements made in stars and supernovae are scattered into space."],
["We are made of stars","Hydrogen and helium formed soon after the Big Bang, but heavier elements, such as carbon, oxygen, iron and gold, were made in stars and spread by their explosions. The carbon in our bodies, the oxygen we breathe and the iron in our blood were made inside ancient stars. As astronomer Carl Sagan put it, we are made of star stuff."],
["The Milky Way and other galaxies","Our Sun is one of about 100 to 400 billion stars in the Milky Way, a spiral galaxy about 100,000 light-years across. There are perhaps 2 trillion galaxies in the observable universe. Galaxies are grouped into clusters and superclusters, separated by vast empty spaces called voids."],
["Navigating by the stars","For thousands of years people used the stars to tell time, plan farming and navigate. Sailors in the Northern Hemisphere used Polaris, the North Star, to find north, while those in the south used the Southern Cross. Farmers in many cultures, from ancient Greece to the Pacific islands and the Andes, watched the rising of the Pleiades to mark planting seasons."]],
sp:["Betelgeuse","Betelgeuse, the reddish star at Orion’s shoulder, is a red supergiant about 700 times wider than the Sun; if it replaced the Sun, it would swallow the orbits of Mercury, Venus, Earth and Mars. It is near the end of its life and will one day explode as a supernova, bright enough to see in daytime. In 2019 and 2020 it dimmed dramatically, which scientists later explained as a cloud of dust it had thrown off. Its explosion could happen any time in the next 100,000 years."],
g:[["Star","A glowing ball of gas powered by fusion"],["Nebula","A cloud of gas and dust in space"],["Light-year","The distance light travels in a year"],["Galaxy","A huge system of stars, gas and dust"]],
f:["On a clear, dark night you can see about 2,500 stars with the naked eye.","The brightest star in the night sky is Sirius.","Neutron stars are so dense that a teaspoon of their material would weigh billions of tonnes."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-sci4.js */
// In-depth material (Science, part 4): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
wcycle:{s:[
["Water on Earth","About 71% of Earth’s surface is covered by water, but about 97% of it is salty ocean water. Of the fresh water, most is frozen in glaciers and ice caps or hidden underground. Less than 1% of all water is fresh water in lakes, rivers and the atmosphere that people can easily use."],
["The Sun powers the cycle","The water cycle is driven by energy from the Sun, which heats oceans, lakes and land, turning water into vapor. Gravity then brings water back down as precipitation and pulls it downhill in rivers and underground. The same water has been cycling for billions of years, so the water you drink may once have been drunk by a dinosaur."],
["Clouds and fog","When water vapor rises and cools, it condenses on tiny particles of dust, salt or smoke, forming droplets that make clouds. Fog is a cloud at ground level, common in valleys and along coasts in the early morning. San Francisco is famous for summer fog rolling in from the Pacific, and in mountain regions fog often rolls in during the rainy season."],
["Ice and snow","In cold places, precipitation falls as snow, which can build up into glaciers and ice sheets that store water for thousands of years. When snow and ice melt in spring and summer, they feed rivers. Many of the world’s great rivers, such as the Ganges and the Yangtze, depend on meltwater from mountain glaciers."],
["Groundwater","Some rainwater soaks into the ground and fills spaces in soil and rock, forming groundwater. Layers of rock that hold large amounts of water are called aquifers. Wells and springs tap groundwater, which supplies drinking water to billions of people. Pumping too much can lower water tables and cause land to sink, as has happened in Mexico City, Jakarta and parts of California."],
["Water for cities","Big cities need enormous amounts of clean water. New York draws water from reservoirs in the Catskill Mountains, more than 150 kilometers away, and Los Angeles brings it hundreds of kilometers by aqueduct. Singapore collects rainwater, recycles wastewater and removes salt from seawater. Protecting watershed forests, fixing leaks and using water wisely help ensure there is enough for homes, farms and industry."]],
sp:["Transpiration and forests","Plants draw water from the soil through their roots and release it as vapor through tiny pores in their leaves, a process called transpiration. A single large tree can release hundreds of liters of water in a day. In rainforests such as the Amazon and the Congo, transpiration provides much of the moisture that forms clouds and rain. Cutting down forests can make regions drier and more prone to floods and landslides."],
g:[["Evaporation","Liquid water turning into vapor"],["Condensation","Vapor turning into liquid"],["Precipitation","Water falling from clouds"],["Aquifer","Underground rock that holds water"]],
f:["A water molecule may stay in the atmosphere for about nine days on average.","Antarctica holds about 70% of Earth’s fresh water as ice.","Plants release about 10 percent of the water vapor in the atmosphere."]},
newton:{s:[
["Before Newton","Before Newton, many people followed Aristotle’s idea that objects naturally come to rest unless something keeps pushing them. Galileo challenged this, showing that a ball rolling on a smooth surface would keep going if nothing slowed it. Newton built on Galileo’s ideas to create a complete set of laws describing motion."],
["The first law","Newton’s first law, the law of inertia, says that an object at rest stays at rest, and an object in motion keeps moving in a straight line at the same speed, unless a force acts on it. This is why passengers lurch forward when a bus brakes suddenly, and why seat belts are important."],
["The second law","The second law says that force equals mass times acceleration, F = ma. A greater force produces a greater acceleration, and a heavier object needs more force to accelerate the same amount. It explains why it is harder to push a loaded cart than an empty one and why rockets need enormous engines."],
["The third law","The third law says that for every action there is an equal and opposite reaction. When you jump, you push down on the ground, and the ground pushes you up. A rocket pushes hot gases out backward, and the gases push the rocket forward. Swimmers push water back to move forward."],
["Friction","Friction is a force that resists motion between surfaces in contact. It lets us walk without slipping, cars brake and nails hold. Too much friction wastes energy as heat, so machines use oil and ball bearings to reduce it. In space, with no air or surfaces, a spacecraft keeps moving without engines."],
["Momentum","Momentum is mass times velocity. A heavy truck moving slowly can have as much momentum as a fast-moving car. In a collision, momentum is conserved: the total stays the same. Engineers use this to design safer cars with crumple zones and air bags that spread out the force of a crash."]],
sp:["Isaac Newton","Isaac Newton was born in Woolsthorpe, England, on Christmas Day 1642 by the old calendar. A brilliant but solitary student at Cambridge, he developed calculus, explained gravity, discovered that white light contains all colors and built the first practical reflecting telescope. His Principia of 1687 is one of the most important science books ever written. He later ran the Royal Mint, chasing counterfeiters, and became president of the Royal Society. He died in 1727."],
g:[["Force","A push or pull"],["Inertia","The tendency of an object to resist changes in motion"],["Acceleration","A change in speed or direction"],["Friction","A force that resists motion"]],
f:["The unit of force, the newton, is named after Isaac Newton.","An apple weighs about one newton.","Newton’s laws are accurate enough to send spacecraft to other planets."]},
mendel:{s:[
["The monk and his garden","Gregor Mendel was an Augustinian friar at the monastery of St. Thomas in Brno, now in the Czech Republic. Trained in mathematics and science, he grew and crossbred about 28,000 pea plants between 1856 and 1863, carefully recording traits such as flower color, seed shape and plant height."],
["What Mendel found","Mendel discovered that traits are passed on as separate units, now called genes, one from each parent. Some forms of a trait are dominant and some recessive. When he crossed tall and short plants, all the offspring were tall, but in the next generation, about one in four was short. He described these patterns mathematically."],
["Ignored and rediscovered","Mendel presented his results in 1865 and published them in 1866, but few scientists noticed. He became abbot of his monastery and died in 1884. In 1900 three botanists, Hugo de Vries, Carl Correns and Erich von Tschermak, independently rediscovered his work, and Mendel became known as the father of genetics."],
["Punnett squares","A Punnett square is a simple grid used to predict the chances of offspring inheriting particular traits. If both parents carry one dominant and one recessive version of a gene, there is a one in four chance that a child will show the recessive trait. Reginald Punnett introduced the method in the early 1900s."],
["Genes and the environment","Most traits, such as height, are influenced by many genes as well as by environment, including food, exercise and health. Identical twins raised apart can differ in some ways because of their environments. Scientists say that genes and environment work together, not one or the other."],
["Genetics today","Genetics now helps diagnose diseases, develop crops, solve crimes and trace ancestry. Plant breeders, such as those at the International Rice Research Institute in Los Baños, use genetics to develop rice that resists pests, floods and drought. Genetic counseling helps families understand inherited conditions."]],
sp:["Thomas Hunt Morgan’s fruit flies","In 1910 Thomas Hunt Morgan, working in a crowded laboratory at Columbia University nicknamed the Fly Room, noticed a fruit fly with white eyes instead of the usual red. By breeding thousands of flies, he showed that genes are carried on chromosomes and that some traits, such as eye color in flies, are linked to sex chromosomes. Fruit flies, which breed quickly, became one of the most important animals in genetics, and Morgan won the Nobel Prize in 1933."],
g:[["Heredity","The passing of traits from parents to offspring"],["Gene","A unit of heredity"],["Dominant","A trait that shows when one copy is present"],["Recessive","A trait that shows only when two copies are present"]],
f:["Mendel chose peas because they grow quickly and have clear, easy-to-see traits.","A Punnett square is named after British geneticist Reginald Punnett.","Red hair is a recessive trait."]},
eco:{s:[
["Producers, consumers and decomposers","In an ecosystem, producers such as plants and algae make food from sunlight. Consumers eat other organisms: herbivores eat plants, carnivores eat animals and omnivores eat both. Decomposers, such as bacteria, fungi and earthworms, break down dead plants and animals, returning nutrients to the soil."],
["Energy pyramids","Energy flows through an ecosystem in one direction, from the Sun to producers to consumers. At each step, most energy is used for living or lost as heat, so only about 10% passes to the next level. This is why there are many more plants than herbivores and far fewer top predators."],
["Nutrient cycles","Unlike energy, nutrients are recycled. Carbon cycles between the air, living things, oceans and rocks. Nitrogen, needed for proteins, is turned by bacteria into forms plants can use. Phosphorus moves from rocks to soil to living things. Human activities, such as burning fuels and using fertilizers, have changed these cycles."],
["Mangroves and seagrass","Mangrove forests grow along tropical coasts around the world. Their tangled roots protect shores from storm surges and erosion and provide nurseries for fish, crabs and shrimp. Seagrass meadows feed dugongs and sea turtles and store carbon. Both ecosystems have been cleared for fish and shrimp ponds and development, and restoration efforts are under way."],
["Invasive species","When species are introduced to places where they do not naturally live, they can harm ecosystems. Rabbits brought to Australia in 1859 spread across the continent, cane toads poison native predators, and the golden apple snail became a major rice pest in Asia. Water hyacinth clogs lakes and rivers from Africa to Asia. Preventing new introductions is easier than removing them."],
["Ecosystem services","Healthy ecosystems provide services that people depend on: clean air and water, fertile soil, pollination of crops, fish, timber, protection from floods and storms and places for recreation. Economists estimate that these services are worth trillions of dollars each year. Protecting ecosystems protects people."]],
sp:["Wolves of Yellowstone","Wolves were wiped out from Yellowstone National Park by the 1920s. Without them, elk multiplied and overgrazed young trees along rivers. When wolves were reintroduced in 1995, elk numbers fell and their behavior changed, allowing willows and aspens to recover in some areas. Beavers, songbirds and other animals returned. Scientists call such a chain of effects a trophic cascade, though they still debate how large the wolves’ role was."],
g:[["Ecosystem","A community of living things and their environment"],["Habitat","The natural home of an organism"],["Food web","Interlinked food chains"],["Decomposer","An organism that breaks down dead matter"]],
f:["A single teaspoon of healthy soil can contain more microorganisms than there are people on Earth.","Mangroves can store several times more carbon per hectare than many rainforests.","About 75% of the world’s food crops depend at least partly on animal pollinators."]},
acid:{s:[
["Acids","Acids are substances that release hydrogen ions in water. They taste sour, can corrode metals and turn blue litmus paper red. Weak acids include vinegar, citric acid in calamansi and lemons and lactic acid in sour milk. Strong acids, such as sulfuric and hydrochloric acid, can burn skin and must be handled with great care."],
["Bases and alkalis","Bases are the chemical opposites of acids. They taste bitter and feel slippery, and they turn red litmus paper blue. Bases that dissolve in water are called alkalis. Examples include baking soda, soap, ammonia and drain cleaners. Strong alkalis, such as sodium hydroxide, are as dangerous as strong acids."],
["The pH scale explained","The pH scale runs from 0 to 14. Pure water has a pH of 7, which is neutral. Below 7 is acidic: lemon juice is about 2 and stomach acid about 1.5 to 3.5. Above 7 is alkaline: baking soda is about 8 to 9 and bleach about 12 to 13. Each step on the scale is ten times stronger than the next."],
["Indicators from nature","Some plant juices change color in acids and bases and can be used as indicators. Red cabbage juice turns red in acids and green or yellow in bases. Purple ube, turmeric and some flowers also change color. Litmus, made from lichens, has been used for centuries. Universal indicator paper shows a range of colors for different pH values."],
["Acid rain","When coal and oil are burned, sulfur dioxide and nitrogen oxides are released. They mix with water in the air to form acids, falling as acid rain. Acid rain can damage forests, harm fish in lakes and wear away stone buildings and statues. Laws limiting emissions from power plants and cars have reduced acid rain in many countries."],
["Ocean acidification","The oceans absorb about a quarter of the carbon dioxide released by burning fossil fuels. Carbon dioxide forms carbonic acid in seawater, making the ocean slightly more acidic. Since the 1800s ocean pH has dropped from about 8.2 to about 8.1. This makes it harder for corals, shellfish and plankton to build their shells and skeletons."]],
sp:["Søren Sørensen and pH","In 1909 the Danish chemist Søren Sørensen, working at the Carlsberg Laboratory, a brewery research lab in Copenhagen, introduced the pH scale to measure the acidity of solutions precisely. He was studying how acidity affected enzymes used in brewing beer. The letters pH are often said to stand for power of hydrogen. His simple scale is now used everywhere, from hospitals and swimming pools to farms and aquariums."],
g:[["Acid","A substance that releases hydrogen ions in water"],["Base","A substance that neutralizes acids"],["pH","A scale measuring acidity"],["Neutralization","An acid and base reacting to form salt and water"]],
f:["Your stomach makes hydrochloric acid strong enough to dissolve some metals.","Vinegar is about 4 to 8% acetic acid in water.","Ants make formic acid, named from the Latin formica, meaning ant."]},
tides:{s:[
["High and low tides","Most coasts have two high tides and two low tides about every 24 hours and 50 minutes, because the Moon rises about 50 minutes later each day. The difference in height between high and low tide is the tidal range. Some places, such as parts of the Gulf of Mexico, have only one high and one low tide a day."],
["Spring and neap tides","When the Sun, Moon and Earth line up, at new and full moon, their pulls combine to produce extra-high and extra-low tides called spring tides, which have nothing to do with the season. When the Sun and Moon pull at right angles, at the quarter moons, tides are smaller, called neap tides."],
["Predicting the tides","Because tides follow the movements of the Moon and Sun, they can be predicted years ahead. In 1872 the British scientist Lord Kelvin built a tide-predicting machine of gears and pulleys that added together the many cycles that make up the tide. Today computers do the same job, and tide tables are published for ports around the world to help sailors, fishers and coastal planners."],
["Tide pools","When the tide goes out, pools of seawater are left among rocks. These tide pools are home to sea stars, sea urchins, anemones, crabs, small fish and seaweeds that must survive changing temperatures and saltiness. In many coastal communities around the world, people gather shellfish and seaweed from tidal flats at low tide."],
["Tidal power","Tides can generate electricity. Tidal barrages, such as La Rance in France, opened in 1966, and Sihwa Lake in South Korea, opened in 2011, trap water at high tide and release it through turbines. Underwater turbines can also be placed in strong tidal currents, as in Scotland’s Pentland Firth. Tidal power is predictable, but suitable sites are few."],
["Tides on other worlds","Tides happen elsewhere in the Solar System. Jupiter’s gravity squeezes its moon Io, heating it so much that volcanoes erupt constantly. Tidal heating may keep oceans liquid beneath the icy crusts of Europa and Enceladus. Earth’s tides on the Moon long ago slowed its spin until the same face always points toward us."]],
sp:["The Bay of Fundy","The Bay of Fundy, between the Canadian provinces of New Brunswick and Nova Scotia, has the highest tides in the world, with a range of up to about 16 m. Twice a day, about 100 billion tonnes of seawater flow in and out of the bay, more than the flow of all the world’s rivers combined. At low tide, visitors can walk on the seafloor around the Hopewell Rocks, which become islands when the tide returns."],
g:[["Tide","The regular rise and fall of the sea"],["Spring tide","An extra-large tide at new and full moon"],["Neap tide","A small tide at quarter moons"],["Tidal range","The difference between high and low tide"]],
f:["Even solid ground rises and falls slightly, by up to about 30 cm, because of Earth tides.","The Moon’s gravity causes about two thirds of the tide-raising force, the Sun about one third.","Tides are gradually slowing Earth’s rotation, making days longer by about 2 milliseconds per century."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-sci5.js */
// In-depth material (Science and Space, part 5): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
microbe:{s:[
["Where microbes live","Microbes live almost everywhere: in soil, water, air, ice, hot springs, deep rocks and inside other living things. Some, called extremophiles, thrive in boiling vents, salty lakes or acidic pools. A gram of soil can hold billions of bacteria. Microbes were the first life on Earth, appearing more than 3.5 billion years ago."],
["Bacteria up close","Bacteria are single cells without a nucleus, usually a few thousandths of a millimeter long. They come in shapes such as spheres, rods and spirals. Many have whip-like tails called flagella for swimming. Under good conditions, some can divide every 20 minutes, so one bacterium can become billions in a day."],
["Inside a virus","Viruses are much smaller than bacteria and are not cells. A virus is a strand of genetic material, DNA or RNA, inside a protein coat. It cannot grow or reproduce on its own; it must invade a living cell and hijack it to make copies. Viruses infect every kind of living thing, even bacteria."],
["Diseases","Some microbes cause disease. Bacteria cause tuberculosis, cholera, typhoid and strep throat. Viruses cause colds, flu, measles, dengue, rabies and COVID-19. Fungi cause athlete’s foot, and protozoa cause malaria. Clean water, hygiene, vaccines and medicines have greatly reduced deaths from infectious diseases."],
["Helpful microbes","Most microbes are harmless or helpful. Bacteria in soil recycle nutrients and help plants get nitrogen. Yeast makes bread rise. Bacteria turn milk into yogurt and cheese and help make vinegar, soy sauce and fish sauce. Around the world, microbes ferment foods such as Korean kimchi, German sauerkraut, Japanese miso and Ethiopian injera."],
["The microbiome","The human body hosts trillions of microbes, especially in the gut, together called the microbiome. They help digest food, make vitamins, train the immune system and protect against harmful germs. A varied diet rich in fiber supports a healthy microbiome, while overuse of antibiotics can disturb it."]],
sp:["Dengue, a spreading disease","Dengue is a viral disease spread by Aedes mosquitoes, which bite during the day and breed in clean, still water in containers, tires and flower pots. It causes high fever, severe headache, joint pain and sometimes dangerous bleeding. About half the world’s people now live where dengue can spread, and in 2024 more than 14 million cases were reported, a record. Removing standing water where mosquitoes breed is one of the best defenses."],
g:[["Microbe","A living thing too small to see without a microscope"],["Bacterium","A single-celled organism without a nucleus"],["Virus","A tiny particle that reproduces inside cells"],["Microbiome","The community of microbes living in a body"]],
f:["There are more bacterial cells in your body than there are stars in the Milky Way.","Nata de coco is made by bacteria fermenting coconut water.","The largest known virus, a giant virus, is bigger than some bacteria."]},
moon:{s:[
["The Moon’s orbit","The Moon orbits Earth about every 27.3 days at an average distance of about 384,400 km. Because Earth is also moving around the Sun, it takes about 29.5 days to go from one new moon to the next. The Moon’s orbit is slightly oval, so it sometimes appears larger, a supermoon."],
["One face toward us","The Moon always shows the same face to Earth because it spins once on its axis in the same time it takes to orbit Earth, a situation called tidal locking. The far side, often wrongly called the dark side, gets as much sunlight as the near side. It was first photographed by the Soviet Luna 3 in 1959."],
["Craters and maria","The Moon’s surface is covered with craters made by asteroid and comet impacts over billions of years. With no air or weather, craters stay sharp. The dark patches, called maria, Latin for seas, are plains of solidified lava, not water. Tycho, a bright crater with long rays, is easy to see with binoculars."],
["Apollo landings","Between 1969 and 1972, six Apollo missions landed 12 astronauts on the Moon. They collected 382 kg of rocks and soil, set up experiments and drove lunar rovers on the last three missions. Their footprints and flags remain. Apollo 17’s Eugene Cernan was the last person to walk on the Moon, in December 1972."],
["The Moon and culture","The Moon has inspired myths, calendars and festivals. Many calendars, including the Islamic and Chinese calendars, are based on the Moon’s phases. Ramadan begins and ends with the sighting of the new crescent Moon, and the Mid-Autumn Festival, celebrated by Chinese and Vietnamese communities around the world, honors the full moon with mooncakes and lanterns."],
["Returning to the Moon","NASA’s Artemis program aims to return astronauts to the Moon, including the first woman, and build a long-term presence near the south pole, where ice may provide water. In April 2026, Artemis II carried four astronauts, three Americans and a Canadian, around the Moon and back, the first crewed flight to the Moon since 1972. China has landed rovers on both sides of the Moon and brought back samples from the far side in 2024. India’s Chandrayaan-3 landed near the south pole in 2023."]],
sp:["Earthrise","On 24 December 1968, the Apollo 8 astronauts became the first humans to orbit the Moon. As their spacecraft came around from the far side, they saw Earth rising over the gray lunar horizon, a blue and white ball against black space. Astronaut William Anders grabbed a camera and took a photograph now called Earthrise. It showed how small and fragile Earth is and helped inspire the environmental movement."],
g:[["Phase","The changing shape of the lit part of the Moon"],["Mare","A dark lava plain on the Moon"],["Crater","A bowl-shaped hollow made by an impact"],["Tidal locking","When one body always shows the same face to another"]],
f:["The Moon is moving away from Earth by about 3.8 cm a year.","A person would weigh about one sixth as much on the Moon.","The Moon has moonquakes, some lasting for many minutes."]},
mars:{s:[
["Why Mars is red","Mars looks red because its surface is covered with iron oxide, or rust, in its rocks and dust. Fine red dust also fills the thin atmosphere, giving the sky a butterscotch color by day. Sunsets on Mars, strangely, look blue, because the dust scatters blue light toward the direction of the Sun."],
["Olympus Mons and Valles Marineris","Olympus Mons is the largest volcano in the Solar System, about 22 km high and about 600 km across. It is a shield volcano that grew huge because Mars has no moving plates. Valles Marineris is a system of canyons about 4,000 km long and up to 7 km deep, dwarfing the Grand Canyon."],
["A thin, cold world","Mars’s atmosphere is about 100 times thinner than Earth’s and mostly carbon dioxide. Average temperatures are about −60°C, though summer days near the equator can reach about 20°C. The planet has polar ice caps of water ice and frozen carbon dioxide that grow and shrink with the seasons."],
["Missions to Mars","After early flybys, NASA’s Viking landers touched down in 1976 and searched for life. Rovers followed: Sojourner in 1997, Spirit and Opportunity in 2004, Curiosity in 2012 and Perseverance in 2021, which carried the Ingenuity helicopter, the first aircraft to fly on another planet. China’s Zhurong rover landed in 2021."],
["Looking for life","Scientists search for signs that Mars once supported life. Rovers have found ancient riverbeds, lake sediments and minerals that form in water, as well as organic molecules. Perseverance is collecting rock samples that future missions may bring back to Earth for study. So far, no definite evidence of life has been found."],
["Humans to Mars","Space agencies and companies hope to send people to Mars. The journey would take about seven months each way, and astronauts would face radiation, low gravity and isolation. They would need to make water, oxygen and fuel from Martian resources. Some plans aim for the 2030s or later."]],
sp:["Ingenuity","Ingenuity was a small helicopter, weighing just 1.8 kg, carried to Mars under the belly of the Perseverance rover. On 19 April 2021, it made the first powered, controlled flight on another planet, hovering a few meters above the ground. Because Mars’s air is so thin, its blades spun about 2,400 times a minute. Planned for five test flights, it flew 72 times over nearly three years before a damaged blade ended its mission in January 2024."],
g:[["Rover","A vehicle that explores a planet’s surface"],["Shield volcano","A broad volcano with gentle slopes"],["Ice cap","A covering of ice at a planet’s pole"],["Orbiter","A spacecraft that circles a planet"]],
f:["A day on Mars, called a sol, lasts about 24 hours 39 minutes.","Mars has the largest dust storms in the Solar System, sometimes covering the whole planet.","Mars has two tiny moons, Phobos and Deimos."]},
exoplanet:{s:[
["The first discoveries","The first planets found outside the Solar System, in 1992, orbited a pulsar, a dead star. In 1995 Michel Mayor and Didier Queloz discovered 51 Pegasi b, the first planet orbiting a Sun-like star, a giant planet circling closer than Mercury does to the Sun. They won the Nobel Prize in Physics in 2019."],
["The wobble method","A planet’s gravity makes its star wobble slightly. As the star moves toward and away from us, its light shifts slightly toward blue and then red. By measuring these tiny shifts, astronomers can detect planets and estimate their mass. This radial velocity method found many of the first exoplanets."],
["Kepler and TESS","NASA’s Kepler space telescope, launched in 2009, stared at about 150,000 stars, watching for tiny dips in brightness as planets passed in front of them. It found thousands of planets and showed that planets are common. TESS, launched in 2018, surveys nearby bright stars across the whole sky."],
["Kinds of exoplanets","Exoplanets come in great variety: hot Jupiters orbiting very close to their stars, super-Earths larger than Earth but smaller than Neptune, mini-Neptunes, lava worlds and planets orbiting two suns, like Tatooine in Star Wars. Some planets drift alone through space without any star."],
["Habitable worlds","Astronomers search for rocky planets in the habitable zone, where it is neither too hot nor too cold for liquid water. Proxima b, orbiting the nearest star to the Sun, lies in its star’s habitable zone. The TRAPPIST-1 system, about 40 light-years away, has seven Earth-sized planets, several in the habitable zone."],
["Searching for life","The James Webb Space Telescope can study the atmospheres of some exoplanets by examining starlight that passes through them. It has detected water vapor, carbon dioxide and other molecules. Scientists hope future telescopes will be able to detect gases, such as oxygen and methane together, that could signal life."]],
sp:["TRAPPIST-1","TRAPPIST-1 is a small, cool red dwarf star about 40 light-years away, only slightly bigger than Jupiter. In 2017 astronomers announced that it has seven rocky, Earth-sized planets, all orbiting closer to their star than Mercury is to the Sun. Because the star is so dim, several of the planets may be at temperatures where liquid water could exist. Its planets are so close together that from the surface of one, the others would appear larger than our Moon."],
g:[["Exoplanet","A planet orbiting a star other than the Sun"],["Transit","A planet passing in front of its star"],["Habitable zone","The region around a star where liquid water could exist"],["Red dwarf","A small, cool, dim star"]],
f:["More than 5,000 exoplanets have been confirmed.","Some exoplanets are thought to rain glass or iron.","Most stars in our galaxy probably have planets."]},
comet:{s:[
["Dirty snowballs","A comet’s solid center, the nucleus, is a mixture of ice, dust and rock, usually a few kilometers across. Astronomer Fred Whipple described comets as dirty snowballs in 1950. As a comet nears the Sun, its ice turns to gas, creating a glowing cloud called a coma and long tails that can stretch millions of kilometers."],
["Where comets come from","Short-period comets, which return in less than 200 years, come from the Kuiper Belt beyond Neptune. Long-period comets come from the distant Oort Cloud, which may extend a light-year or more from the Sun. A passing star or the gravity of the galaxy can nudge comets toward the inner Solar System."],
["The asteroid belt","Asteroids are rocky or metallic bodies, mostly found in the asteroid belt between Mars and Jupiter. The largest, Ceres, is about 940 km across and is classed as a dwarf planet. Others, such as Vesta, are large enough to have layers. Some asteroids have their own tiny moons."],
["Meteors and meteorites","When small pieces of rock or dust enter Earth’s atmosphere at high speed, they burn up, creating streaks of light called meteors or shooting stars. Pieces that survive and land are meteorites. Most meteorites come from asteroids, but a few come from the Moon and Mars, knocked off by impacts."],
["Visiting comets and asteroids","Spacecraft have visited many of these small bodies. The European Rosetta probe orbited comet 67P from 2014 to 2016 and dropped the Philae lander on it. Japan’s Hayabusa2 and NASA’s OSIRIS-REx collected samples from asteroids Ryugu and Bennu and returned them to Earth, finding water-bearing minerals and organic molecules."],
["Protecting Earth","Large asteroid impacts are rare but dangerous. Astronomers track near-Earth objects to spot any threats early. In 2022 NASA’s DART spacecraft deliberately crashed into the small asteroid Dimorphos and changed its orbit, the first test of a way to deflect an asteroid. No known asteroid poses a significant threat in the next century."]],
sp:["Halley’s Comet","Halley’s Comet returns about every 76 years. In 1705 Edmond Halley realized that comets seen in 1531, 1607 and 1682 were the same object, and he predicted its return in 1758. It appeared on schedule after his death, and it was named in his honor. Records of the comet go back over 2,000 years, and it appears in the Bayeux Tapestry of 1066. It last passed in 1986 and will return in 2061."],
g:[["Comet","An icy body that grows a tail near the Sun"],["Asteroid","A rocky body orbiting the Sun"],["Meteor","A streak of light from a rock burning in the atmosphere"],["Meteorite","A space rock that reaches the ground"]],
f:["Tens of tonnes of space dust fall on Earth every day.","The Perseid meteor shower peaks every August.","A meteorite crater in Arizona, Meteor Crater, is about 1.2 km wide."]},
bigbang:{s:[
["An expanding universe","In the 1920s astronomers found that distant galaxies are moving away from us, and the farther away they are, the faster they recede. This means the universe is expanding. If you run time backward, everything must once have been packed together in an extremely hot, dense state. This idea is the Big Bang theory."],
["Lemaître’s primeval atom","In 1927 Georges Lemaître, a Belgian priest and physicist, proposed that the universe began from a single primeval atom that expanded. Many scientists were skeptical. The name Big Bang was coined in 1949 by Fred Hoyle, who supported a rival steady-state theory in which the universe had no beginning."],
["The first minutes","In the first fraction of a second, the universe may have expanded incredibly fast in a process called inflation. Within the first few minutes, it cooled enough for protons and neutrons to combine into the nuclei of hydrogen, helium and a little lithium. The amounts predicted match those observed today."],
["The afterglow","About 380,000 years after the Big Bang, the universe cooled enough for atoms to form, and light could travel freely. That light, stretched into microwaves by cosmic expansion, still fills the sky as the cosmic microwave background. It was discovered by accident in 1965 by Arno Penzias and Robert Wilson, who won the Nobel Prize."],
["Age of the universe","By measuring the cosmic microwave background and the expansion rate, scientists estimate the universe is about 13.8 billion years old. Satellites such as COBE, WMAP and Planck mapped tiny temperature differences in the background radiation, the seeds from which galaxies grew."],
["The future of the universe","In 1998 astronomers discovered that the expansion of the universe is speeding up, driven by a mysterious dark energy that makes up about 68% of the universe. If this continues, galaxies will drift ever farther apart, and the universe may grow cold and dark over trillions of years. Scientists are still trying to understand dark energy."]],
sp:["The cosmic microwave background","In 1964 and 1965 Arno Penzias and Robert Wilson, working at Bell Labs in New Jersey, were testing a large horn-shaped antenna and kept hearing a faint hiss coming from every direction. They even cleaned out pigeon droppings, suspecting the birds. The hiss turned out to be the oldest light in the universe, left over from the Big Bang. Their discovery strongly supported the Big Bang theory and earned them the 1978 Nobel Prize in Physics."],
g:[["Big Bang","The theory that the universe began in a hot, dense state"],["Inflation","A very rapid expansion in the first instant"],["Dark energy","An unknown force speeding up the expansion"],["Redshift","Light stretched to longer wavelengths"]],
f:["Some of the static on old analog TVs came from the cosmic microwave background.","The observable universe is about 93 billion light-years across.","Hydrogen and helium make up about 98% of the ordinary matter in the universe."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-space2.js */
// In-depth material (Space and Astronomy, part 2): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
jupiter:{s:[
["King of the planets","Jupiter is the largest planet, more than 11 times wider than Earth and more than twice as massive as all the other planets combined. About 1,300 Earths could fit inside it. It is the fifth planet from the Sun, about 778 million km away, and takes almost 12 Earth years to complete one orbit."],
["A fast spin","Despite its size, Jupiter spins faster than any other planet, turning once in under 10 hours. The rapid spin makes it bulge at the equator and flatten at the poles. It also stretches its clouds into the bands of color that make the planet easy to recognize through a small telescope."],
["The Great Red Spot","The Great Red Spot is a giant storm, a high-pressure system swirling counterclockwise, that has been observed for at least 150 years and possibly since the 1600s. It was once about three times as wide as Earth but has been shrinking; today it is a little wider than Earth. Winds at its edges blow at over 400 km/h."],
["Galileo’s moons","In January 1610 Galileo Galilei discovered Jupiter’s four largest moons, Io, Europa, Ganymede and Callisto, now called the Galilean moons. Seeing moons circling another planet showed that not everything orbits Earth, supporting the idea of a Sun-centered system. Jupiter is now known to have about 95 moons."],
["Visiting Jupiter","Pioneer 10 flew past Jupiter in 1973, followed by Pioneer 11, Voyager 1 and 2, Galileo, which orbited from 1995 to 2003, and Juno, which arrived in 2016 and studies the planet from a polar orbit. Europe’s JUICE, launched in 2023, and NASA’s Europa Clipper, launched in 2024, are on their way to study the icy moons."],
["Could Europa host life?","Europa, slightly smaller than our Moon, has a smooth icy crust covering a salty ocean that may hold twice as much water as all of Earth’s oceans. Tides from Jupiter keep the ocean liquid. Scientists think it is one of the best places in the Solar System to look for life beyond Earth."]],
sp:["Comet Shoemaker–Levy 9","In 1992 a comet passed so close to Jupiter that the planet’s gravity tore it into more than 20 pieces. Discovered in 1993 by Carolyn and Eugene Shoemaker and David Levy, the fragments slammed into Jupiter in July 1994, the first collision between two Solar System bodies ever observed. Each impact left dark scars in Jupiter’s clouds, some larger than Earth, visible for months through backyard telescopes."],
g:[["Gas giant","A huge planet made mostly of hydrogen and helium"],["Galilean moons","Jupiter’s four largest moons"],["Great Red Spot","A giant, long-lasting storm on Jupiter"],["Aurora","Glowing lights near a planet’s poles"]],
f:["Jupiter gives off more heat than it receives from the Sun.","Io is the most volcanically active world in the Solar System.","Ganymede is the largest moon in the Solar System, bigger than Mercury."]},
saturn:{s:[
["The ringed planet","Saturn is the sixth planet from the Sun and the second largest, about nine times wider than Earth. Like Jupiter, it is a gas giant made mostly of hydrogen and helium. It is so light for its size that its average density is less than water; in an impossibly huge bathtub, it would float."],
["What the rings are made of","Saturn’s rings are made of countless pieces of water ice, with a little rock, ranging from tiny grains to chunks as big as houses. The main rings stretch about 280,000 km across but are mostly only about 10 meters thick. Some scientists think they are relatively young, perhaps a few hundred million years old."],
["Discovering the rings","Galileo first saw Saturn’s rings in 1610, but his small telescope showed only lumps on either side, which he called ears. In 1655 Christiaan Huygens realized they were a ring, and he also discovered the moon Titan. In 1675 Giovanni Cassini found a gap in the rings, now called the Cassini Division."],
["Winds and weather","Saturn’s winds are among the fastest in the Solar System, reaching about 1,800 km/h near the equator. Every 20 or 30 years, huge white storms erupt in its atmosphere. A storm that began in 2010 grew so large that it wrapped all the way around the planet."],
["Moons of many kinds","Saturn has more known moons than any other planet, about 274 confirmed by 2025. They include Titan, the second-largest moon in the Solar System, and Enceladus with its icy geysers. Mimas has a huge crater that makes it look like the Death Star. Small shepherd moons orbit within the rings and keep them in shape."],
["The Dragonfly mission","NASA plans to send Dragonfly, a nuclear-powered drone helicopter, to Titan, with launch planned for 2028. It will fly from place to place in Titan’s thick atmosphere and low gravity, studying the chemistry of its dunes and craters. Titan’s lakes of liquid methane and ethane make it unique."]],
sp:["Cassini’s Grand Finale","The Cassini spacecraft orbited Saturn from 2004 to 2017. Its Huygens probe landed on Titan in 2005, the most distant landing ever made. Running low on fuel, Cassini made 22 daring dives through the gap between Saturn and its rings, sending back close-up data. On 15 September 2017 it plunged into Saturn’s atmosphere and burned up, so it could never crash into and contaminate moons that might hold life."],
g:[["Ring","A flat band of particles orbiting a planet"],["Density","How much mass is packed into a given space"],["Shepherd moon","A small moon that keeps a ring’s edge sharp"],["Geyser","A jet of liquid or gas bursting from the ground"]],
f:["Saturn takes about 29.5 Earth years to orbit the Sun.","Saturn’s rings seem to vanish when we see them edge-on.","Titan is the only moon with a thick atmosphere."]},
milky:{s:[
["A river of light","On a dark night far from city lights, the Milky Way appears as a faint band of light across the sky. Galileo’s telescope showed in 1610 that it is made of countless faint stars. Its name comes from Greek myth, in which it was milk spilled by the goddess Hera."],
["Size and stars","The Milky Way is a disk of stars about 100,000 light-years across but only about 1,000 light-years thick in the main disk. It contains an estimated 100 billion to 400 billion stars, along with vast clouds of gas and dust where new stars are born, and many planets."],
["The Sun’s journey","The Sun is about 26,000 light-years from the center of the galaxy, in a minor arm called the Orion Arm. It travels around the galactic center at about 230 km/s, completing one orbit in about 230 million years, a galactic year. The last time the Sun was in its current position, the first dinosaurs were appearing."],
["Dark matter","Stars in the outer parts of the Milky Way orbit faster than they should if the galaxy contained only the matter we can see. Astronomers think the galaxy is surrounded by a huge halo of invisible dark matter, which has gravity but does not give off light. Dark matter may make up about 85% of the matter in the universe."],
["The black hole at the center","At the heart of the Milky Way lies Sagittarius A*, a supermassive black hole about 4 million times the mass of the Sun. Andrea Ghez and Reinhard Genzel tracked stars orbiting it and shared the 2020 Nobel Prize in Physics. In 2022 the Event Horizon Telescope released the first image of its shadow."],
["Collision with Andromeda","The Andromeda Galaxy, about 2.5 million light-years away, is the nearest large galaxy. It is approaching at about 110 km/s. In several billion years, the two galaxies may collide and merge into a single large galaxy, though stars are so far apart that they would hardly ever hit one another."]],
sp:["The Magellanic Clouds","From the Southern Hemisphere, two fuzzy patches can be seen near the Milky Way: the Large and Small Magellanic Clouds, dwarf galaxies that orbit our own. They are named after Ferdinand Magellan, whose crew described them during the round-the-world voyage of 1519 to 1522, though Polynesian, Aboriginal Australian and Persian astronomers knew them long before. In 1987 a supernova in the Large Magellanic Cloud was the brightest seen for nearly 400 years."],
g:[["Galaxy","A huge system of stars, gas and dust held by gravity"],["Light-year","The distance light travels in a year"],["Dark matter","Invisible matter detected by its gravity"],["Supermassive black hole","A black hole millions or billions of times the Sun’s mass"]],
f:["The Milky Way is part of the Local Group of more than 80 galaxies.","Light from the far side of the galaxy takes about 100,000 years to cross it.","The galaxy has a bar of stars across its center."]},
iss:{s:[
["The biggest spacecraft","The International Space Station is the largest object humans have ever put into space, about the size of a football field and weighing about 420 tonnes. It has more living space than a six-bedroom house, with two bathrooms, a gym and a large bay window called the Cupola."],
["How it was built","The first piece, the Russian-built Zarya module, was launched in November 1998. Over the following years, more than 40 flights, mostly by the Space Shuttle, carried new modules, which astronauts joined together during spacewalks. People have lived aboard without a break since 2 November 2000."],
["Speed and orbit","The ISS orbits about 400 km above Earth at about 28,000 km/h, circling the planet about every 90 minutes. Its crew sees about 16 sunrises and 16 sunsets every day. It can often be seen from the ground with the naked eye as a bright, steadily moving point of light crossing the sky in a few minutes."],
["Living in microgravity","On the ISS, astronauts float because they and the station are falling around Earth together. Water forms floating balls, sleepers zip into bags fixed to walls, and toilets use suction. Without exercise, astronauts lose bone and muscle, so they work out about two hours a day."],
["Science in orbit","Thousands of experiments have been carried out on the ISS, studying how plants grow, how fire burns, how fluids behave and how the human body changes in space. Astronauts grew lettuce and other vegetables in orbit. The research helps plan longer missions to the Moon and Mars."],
["Many nations in orbit","People from more than 20 countries have visited the station. Besides the partner nations, astronauts from countries such as Brazil, Malaysia, South Africa, South Korea, the United Arab Emirates and Kazakhstan have flown there. Small satellites from countries with young space programs, including Kenya, Ghana, Bhutan and the Philippines, have been released into orbit from the station’s Japanese module."]],
sp:["First satellites from the station","Through a program run by the United Nations and Japan’s space agency, called KiboCUBE, countries without their own rockets can send tiny satellites to the ISS and release them into orbit from the Japanese Kibo module. Kenya’s first satellite, 1KUNS-PF, was released this way in 2018, and Ghana, Bhutan, Guatemala and others have used the station to launch their first satellites. For many countries it was their first step into space."],
g:[["Microgravity","The near-weightless condition in orbit"],["Module","One section of a space station"],["Spacewalk","Working outside a spacecraft in space"],["Satellite","An object that orbits a planet"]],
f:["Astronauts on the ISS recycle about 98% of their water, including sweat and urine.","More than 280 people from over 20 countries have visited the ISS.","The ISS travels from coast to coast across the United States in about 10 minutes."]},
eclipse:{s:[
["A lucky coincidence","Total solar eclipses are possible because the Sun is about 400 times wider than the Moon but also about 400 times farther away, so they appear nearly the same size in the sky. Because the Moon is slowly moving away from Earth, in hundreds of millions of years total solar eclipses will no longer happen."],
["The shadow’s path","During a total solar eclipse, the darkest part of the Moon’s shadow, the umbra, races across Earth in a narrow path usually less than 200 km wide. Outside it, people see a partial eclipse. Totality never lasts longer than about 7.5 minutes at any one place. On average, a total eclipse is seen from any given spot only once every 375 years or so."],
["What you see","Just before totality, the last beads of sunlight shining through lunar valleys create Baily’s beads and the diamond ring effect. During totality, the sky darkens, temperatures drop, birds go quiet and the Sun’s pearly outer atmosphere, the corona, appears. Pink flames called prominences may be seen at the edge."],
["Blood moons","During a total lunar eclipse, the Moon turns a coppery red. Sunlight passing through Earth’s atmosphere is bent toward the Moon, and the atmosphere filters out blue light, like a sunset. Lunar eclipses can last over an hour and are safe to watch with the naked eye from anywhere it is night."],
["Myths and legends","Many cultures explained eclipses as a creature devouring the Sun or Moon. In Philippine mythology, the bakunawa, a giant sea serpent, rises to swallow the Moon, and people banged pots and gongs to make it spit the Moon out. In Chinese tradition a dragon eats the Sun, and in Norse myth wolves chase the Sun and Moon."],
["Eclipses and science","Eclipses have helped scientists make discoveries. Helium was first detected in the Sun’s spectrum during an eclipse in 1868. In 1919 Arthur Eddington’s eclipse expedition measured starlight bending around the Sun, confirming Einstein’s general relativity. Today eclipses help scientists study the corona."]],
sp:["The 1919 eclipse","On 29 May 1919, a total solar eclipse crossed Brazil and the island of Príncipe off West Africa. British teams led by Arthur Eddington and Frank Dyson photographed stars near the darkened Sun. Einstein’s theory predicted that the Sun’s gravity would bend starlight by a specific amount, twice what Newton’s physics implied. The measurements agreed with Einstein, and the result made him world-famous almost overnight."],
g:[["Umbra","The darkest central part of a shadow"],["Penumbra","The lighter outer part of a shadow"],["Corona","The Sun’s faint outer atmosphere"],["Totality","The period when the Sun is completely covered"]],
f:["There are between two and five solar eclipses every year.","A total solar eclipse can be seen from somewhere on Earth about every 18 months.","A solar eclipse and a lunar eclipse often happen about two weeks apart."]},
pulsar:{s:[
["How stars die","Stars end their lives in different ways depending on their mass. Sun-like stars swell into red giants and then shrink into white dwarfs. Much more massive stars explode as supernovas, and their cores collapse into neutron stars or black holes."],
["Neutron stars","A neutron star is the collapsed core of a massive star, packing more mass than the Sun into a ball about 20 km across, roughly the size of a city. Its matter is so dense that a teaspoonful would weigh about a billion tonnes on Earth. Its gravity is so strong that light bends around it."],
["Lighthouses in space","Pulsars are spinning neutron stars that send out beams of radio waves from their magnetic poles. If a beam sweeps past Earth, we see a regular pulse, like a lighthouse. Some spin once every few seconds; millisecond pulsars spin hundreds of times a second. Their timing is so regular it rivals atomic clocks."],
["Jocelyn Bell Burnell","In 1967 Jocelyn Bell, a 24-year-old graduate student at Cambridge, noticed a strange, regular signal in her radio telescope data, a pulse every 1.33 seconds. It was the first pulsar. Her supervisor Antony Hewish shared the 1974 Nobel Prize; many felt Bell deserved a share. In 2018 she won a large prize and gave all the money to help students."],
["The Crab Nebula","In 1054 Chinese astronomers recorded a guest star so bright it could be seen in daylight for weeks. It was a supernova, and its remains are the Crab Nebula. At its heart is a pulsar spinning about 30 times a second, which powers the glowing cloud of gas around it."],
["Ripples in spacetime","When two neutron stars spiral together and merge, they send out gravitational waves, ripples in spacetime predicted by Einstein. On 17 August 2017, detectors recorded such a merger, and telescopes around the world saw its light. The explosion, called a kilonova, created heavy elements such as gold and platinum."]],
sp:["Magnetars","Magnetars are neutron stars with the strongest magnetic fields known, about a thousand trillion times stronger than Earth’s. Their fields are so powerful that at a distance of 1,000 km they could wipe the data from every credit card on Earth. Starquakes in their crusts can release enormous bursts of gamma rays. In 2004 a flare from magnetar SGR 1806−20, about 50,000 light-years away, briefly affected Earth’s upper atmosphere."],
g:[["Neutron star","The dense collapsed core of a massive star"],["Pulsar","A spinning neutron star that sends out regular pulses"],["Supernova","The explosion of a star"],["Gravitational wave","A ripple in spacetime"]],
f:["The first pulsar was nicknamed LGM-1, for little green men.","Pulsar timing arrays use dozens of pulsars to detect very slow gravitational waves.","Some pulsars have planets; the first exoplanets ever found orbit one."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-hos1.js */
// In-depth material (History of Science, part 1): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
scimeth:{s:[
["Observation","Science begins with careful observation: noticing something and asking why. Scientists use their senses and instruments such as microscopes, telescopes and thermometers to collect data. Good observations are recorded carefully and measured where possible, so that others can check them."],
["Ibn al-Haytham","The Arab scientist Ibn al-Haytham, who worked in Cairo around 1000 CE, is often called a pioneer of the scientific method. In his Book of Optics, he tested ideas about light and vision with experiments using dark rooms and pinholes, rather than relying only on the authority of ancient writers. He urged scholars to doubt and check everything."],
["Bacon and Descartes","In the early 1600s, the English thinker Francis Bacon argued that knowledge should be built from careful observations and experiments, reasoning step by step to general laws. The French philosopher René Descartes stressed doubt and logical reasoning. Together their ideas shaped the Scientific Revolution."],
["Variables and controls","In an experiment, scientists change one thing, the independent variable, and measure its effect on another, the dependent variable, while keeping everything else the same. A control group, which does not receive the change, gives a baseline for comparison. Repeating experiments makes results more reliable."],
["Bias and blind tests","People tend to see what they expect, so scientists use methods to reduce bias. In a blind test, participants do not know whether they are getting the real treatment or a placebo. In a double-blind test, neither the participants nor the researchers know. Large, random samples also help avoid misleading results."],
["Science in everyday life","The scientific method is not only for laboratories. People use it when they test which fertilizer grows better rice, which route to school is quicker, or why a phone battery drains. Asking questions, testing ideas and changing your mind when the evidence shows you were wrong are skills useful to everyone."]],
sp:["Semmelweis and handwashing","In the 1840s, Hungarian doctor Ignaz Semmelweis noticed that far more mothers died of childbed fever in a Vienna hospital ward run by doctors than in one run by midwives. Doctors often came straight from dissecting bodies. He made them wash their hands in chlorinated lime, and deaths dropped sharply. Many doctors rejected his idea, and he died in 1865, before germ theory proved him right."],
g:[["Hypothesis","A testable explanation or prediction"],["Variable","A factor that can change in an experiment"],["Control","A comparison group that is not changed"],["Placebo","A fake treatment used for comparison"]],
f:["The word scientist was coined in 1833 by William Whewell.","A result that cannot be repeated by others is treated with suspicion.","Galileo is often called the father of modern science."]},
copern:{s:[
["Ptolemy’s universe","For about 1,400 years, most scholars followed the model of Claudius Ptolemy, a Greek astronomer in Alexandria around 150 CE. He placed Earth at the center, with the Sun, Moon, planets and stars circling it. To explain why planets sometimes seem to move backward, he added small circles called epicycles."],
["An ancient idea","The idea of a Sun-centered universe was not entirely new. Around 270 BCE, the Greek astronomer Aristarchus of Samos suggested that Earth goes around the Sun, but his idea was not widely accepted. Copernicus knew of Aristarchus and mentioned him in an early draft of his work."],
["On the Revolutions","Nicolaus Copernicus, a Polish astronomer and church official, set out his Sun-centered system in De revolutionibus orbium coelestium, On the Revolutions of the Heavenly Spheres, published in 1543. Legend says he received a printed copy on the day he died. A preface added by another person presented the idea as merely a convenient calculation."],
["How it explained the sky","In the Copernican system, Earth spins once a day and orbits the Sun once a year. The backward loops of planets such as Mars are illusions caused by Earth overtaking them. The system also explained why Mercury and Venus always appear close to the Sun. However, Copernicus still used perfect circles."],
["Bruno and the infinite universe","The Italian philosopher Giordano Bruno went further than Copernicus, suggesting the universe is infinite and that stars are distant suns with their own planets. He was burned at the stake in Rome in 1600, mainly for religious ideas considered heretical. Today he is remembered as a symbol of free thought."],
["Newton completes the picture","In 1687 Isaac Newton showed that the same force of gravity that makes an apple fall keeps the Moon and planets in their orbits. His laws of motion and gravity explained Kepler’s laws and why the planets orbit the Sun. The Sun-centered system now had a physical explanation."]],
sp:["The Copernican Revolution","The shift from an Earth-centered to a Sun-centered universe is called the Copernican Revolution. It took more than a century, through the work of Copernicus, Tycho Brahe, Kepler, Galileo and Newton. It changed not just astronomy but how people saw their place in the cosmos: Earth was one planet among many. The phrase Copernican revolution is now used for any idea that completely changes how we think."],
g:[["Geocentric","With Earth at the center"],["Heliocentric","With the Sun at the center"],["Epicycle","A small circle on a larger orbit in old models"],["Retrograde motion","A planet’s apparent backward movement"]],
f:["Copernicus also studied law and medicine and worked as a doctor.","A chemical element, copernicium, is named after him.","Kepler discovered that orbits are ellipses, not circles."]},
galileo:{s:[
["Falling objects","Aristotle taught that heavier objects fall faster than light ones. Galileo argued that, without air resistance, all objects fall at the same rate. He tested this by rolling balls down slopes and timing them. Whether he really dropped balls from the Leaning Tower of Pisa is uncertain; the story comes from his student’s biography."],
["The pendulum","According to a famous story, the young Galileo watched a lamp swinging in Pisa Cathedral and timed it with his pulse. He found that a pendulum takes the same time to swing whether the swing is wide or narrow. This discovery later led to the invention of the pendulum clock by Christiaan Huygens in 1656."],
["Building telescopes","In 1609 Galileo heard of a Dutch spyglass and quickly built his own, improving it until it magnified about 20 times. He sold telescopes to merchants and the Venetian government, who saw their use for spotting ships. Then he pointed his instrument at the night sky, something few had done systematically before."],
["What he saw","Through his telescope Galileo saw mountains and craters on the Moon, countless stars in the Milky Way, four moons of Jupiter, the phases of Venus and dark spots on the Sun. The phases of Venus showed that it orbits the Sun, strong evidence against the Earth-centered model."],
["Conflict with the Church","In 1616 the Catholic Church declared the Sun-centered idea contrary to scripture and warned Galileo not to defend it. His 1632 Dialogue Concerning the Two Chief World Systems seemed to mock the Earth-centered view. In 1633 he was tried by the Inquisition, forced to recant and placed under house arrest for the rest of his life."],
["Two New Sciences","Under house arrest and going blind, Galileo wrote Two New Sciences, published in the Netherlands in 1638. It described his studies of motion and the strength of materials and laid the foundations of modern physics. He died in 1642, the year Isaac Newton was born. In 1992 Pope John Paul II acknowledged the Church had erred in condemning him."]],
sp:["“And yet it moves”","According to legend, after being forced to declare that Earth does not move, Galileo muttered, Eppur si muove, “And yet it moves.” There is no evidence he said it at the trial, which would have been very dangerous. The phrase appeared in writing more than a century later, though a painting from around the 1640s may include the words. True or not, it has become a symbol of standing by the truth under pressure."],
g:[["Pendulum","A weight swinging freely from a fixed point"],["Telescope","An instrument that makes distant things appear closer"],["Inquisition","A Church court that tried people for heresy"],["Recant","To publicly take back a belief"]],
f:["Galileo’s middle finger is displayed in a museum in Florence.","The Galileo spacecraft studied Jupiter, the planet whose moons he found.","Europe’s satellite navigation system is named Galileo."]},
goldage:{s:[
["Baghdad and beyond","From about the 8th to the 13th centuries, scholars across the Islamic world, from Spain to Central Asia, made great advances in science. Baghdad, founded in 762, became a center of learning under the Abbasid caliphs. Other centers included Cairo, Córdoba, Damascus, Samarkand and Bukhara."],
["Translation movement","Scholars translated works by Greek, Persian and Indian writers into Arabic, including Aristotle, Euclid, Ptolemy and Galen. Translators such as Hunayn ibn Ishaq were well paid. This saved many ancient works that might otherwise have been lost, and scholars added their own corrections and discoveries."],
["Al-Khwarizmi and numbers","Muhammad ibn Musa al-Khwarizmi, who worked in Baghdad in the 800s, wrote a book that gave us the word algebra, from al-jabr, meaning restoring. Another of his books spread the Hindu numerals, including zero, which became our Hindu-Arabic numerals. The word algorithm comes from his name."],
["Ibn Sina","Ibn Sina, known in Europe as Avicenna, was a Persian doctor and philosopher who lived from about 980 to 1037. His Canon of Medicine collected the medical knowledge of his time and was used as a textbook in European universities until the 1600s. It described contagious diseases and how to test new medicines."],
["Instruments and observatories","Muslim astronomers built large observatories, such as at Maragha in Iran and Samarkand in Uzbekistan, and improved the astrolabe, an instrument for measuring the positions of stars. Many stars still have Arabic names, such as Aldebaran, Betelgeuse and Altair. They made accurate tables of planetary motion."],
["Chemistry and engineering","Jabir ibn Hayyan and later scholars developed laboratory techniques such as distillation and crystallization. Words such as alcohol, alkali and alchemy come from Arabic. Engineer al-Jazari, around 1206, described ingenious machines, including water clocks, pumps and robots powered by water."]],
sp:["Al-Jazari’s elephant clock","Ismail al-Jazari was an engineer who worked for a ruler in what is now Turkey. In 1206 he completed The Book of Knowledge of Ingenious Mechanical Devices, describing about 100 machines with detailed drawings. His most famous design is the elephant clock, a water clock with a mechanical elephant, a scribe, a dragon and birds that moved and made sounds every half hour. It combined ideas from India, Persia, Egypt, Greece and China."],
g:[["Caliph","A ruler of the Islamic community"],["Astrolabe","An instrument for measuring star positions"],["Algorithm","A step-by-step method for solving a problem"],["Distillation","Separating liquids by boiling and cooling them"]],
f:["The first known university still operating, al-Qarawiyyin in Morocco, was founded in 859 by Fatima al-Fihri.","Paper-making spread from China to Baghdad in the 700s.","Many lunar craters are named after Muslim scholars."]},
alchemy:{s:[
["Ancient chemistry","Long before chemistry was a science, people used chemical skills to make metals, glass, dyes, perfumes, medicines and soap. Ancient Egyptians mummified bodies with salts and resins. Chinese alchemists searching for long life discovered gunpowder around the 9th century."],
["The philosopher’s stone","European alchemists sought the philosopher’s stone, a substance believed to turn cheap metals such as lead into gold and to give eternal life. They never succeeded, but their experiments developed equipment and methods, such as furnaces and flasks, that chemists still use. Isaac Newton spent years on alchemy."],
["Paracelsus","Paracelsus, a Swiss doctor of the 1500s, said alchemy should be used to make medicines rather than gold. He used chemicals such as mercury and argued that the dose makes the poison: any substance can be harmful in large amounts. He is sometimes called a father of toxicology."],
["Robert Boyle","In The Sceptical Chymist, published in 1661, the Irish-born scientist Robert Boyle attacked the old idea of four elements and argued that matter is made of tiny particles. He stressed careful experiments and published his methods. Boyle’s law describes how the pressure of a gas changes with its volume."],
["Lavoisier and oxygen","Antoine Lavoisier, a French chemist, showed in the 1770s that burning is a reaction with oxygen, not the release of an imaginary substance called phlogiston. He showed that mass is conserved in chemical reactions and created a modern system for naming chemicals. He was executed during the French Revolution in 1794."],
["Dalton and Mendeleev","In 1808 English teacher John Dalton proposed that each element is made of its own kind of atom, with its own weight. In 1869 Russian chemist Dmitri Mendeleev arranged the known elements by weight and properties in a periodic table, leaving gaps for elements not yet discovered and predicting their properties accurately."]],
sp:["Making gold for real","Alchemists dreamed of turning lead into gold. In the 20th century, physicists finally did it, in a way. Using particle accelerators, they knocked protons out of the nuclei of bismuth and other heavy atoms, creating tiny amounts of gold. In 2025, scientists at CERN reported that collisions of lead nuclei in the Large Hadron Collider briefly created gold atoms. The cost, however, is far more than the gold is worth."],
g:[["Element","A substance made of only one kind of atom"],["Compound","A substance of two or more elements bonded together"],["Phlogiston","An imaginary substance once thought released in burning"],["Periodic table","A chart of the elements arranged by properties"]],
f:["The chemical symbol for gold, Au, comes from the Latin aurum.","Lavoisier’s wife, Marie-Anne, helped with his experiments and drew his apparatus.","There are 118 known elements."]}
});

;
