// In-depth material (Animals and Nature, part 1): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
elephant:{s:[
["Ancestors","Elephants belong to a group called proboscideans, animals with trunks. Their ancestors first appeared about 60 million years ago in Africa as pig-sized creatures. Later relatives included woolly mammoths and mastodons. Woolly mammoths lived until about 4,000 years ago on Wrangel Island in the Arctic."],
["Ears and cooling","African elephants have huge ears shaped a little like the map of Africa, while Asian elephants have smaller, rounded ears. Their ears are full of blood vessels. Flapping them cools the blood, helping elephants stay cool in hot climates. Elephants also spray water and dust on their backs to protect their skin."],
["Tusks","Tusks are long incisor teeth that keep growing throughout life. Elephants use them to dig for water, strip bark, move objects and defend themselves. Like people who are left- or right-handed, elephants usually favor one tusk. Many Asian elephant females have no tusks or only tiny ones."],
["Communication","Elephants make many sounds, from trumpets and roars to low rumbles too deep for humans to hear, called infrasound. These rumbles can travel several kilometers, and elephants may sense them through their feet. Scientists have found that elephants seem to have name-like calls for individual members of their group."],
["Memory and mourning","Elephants have large brains and long memories. Matriarchs remember the locations of water holes from years before, which helps their herds survive droughts. Elephants show interest in the bones of dead elephants, touching them with their trunks and feet, behavior some scientists compare to mourning."],
["Ivory and poaching","Poachers kill elephants for their ivory tusks, which are carved into ornaments. In the 1970s and 1980s, African elephant numbers fell by about half. A worldwide ban on the ivory trade began in 1989. Poaching still threatens elephants, along with loss of habitat and conflict with farmers whose crops they eat."]],
sp:["Lost elephants of the islands","Long ago, elephants and their relatives reached many islands and often shrank over generations. Dwarf elephants about a meter tall lived on Mediterranean islands such as Sicily, Malta and Cyprus, and stegodons, relatives of elephants, lived on islands from Flores in Indonesia to the Philippines. Woolly mammoths survived on Wrangel Island in the Arctic until about 4,000 years ago."],
g:[["Trunk","An elephant’s long nose and upper lip"],["Tusk","A long tooth that grows out of the mouth"],["Matriarch","The oldest female who leads a herd"],["Infrasound","Sound too low for humans to hear"]],
f:["An elephant’s trunk has about 40,000 muscles.","Elephants can drink up to 200 liters of water a day.","An elephant pregnancy lasts about 22 months, the longest of any land mammal."]},
whale:{s:[
["Baleen and teeth","Whales and dolphins, the cetaceans, are divided into two groups. Baleen whales, such as blue and humpback whales, have plates of baleen, made of keratin like fingernails, to strain small animals from water. Toothed whales, including dolphins, porpoises, orcas and sperm whales, hunt fish and squid."],
["The blue whale","The blue whale is the largest animal ever known to have lived, reaching about 30 meters long and up to about 180 tonnes. Its heart is about the size of a small car. Despite its size, it eats tiny shrimp-like krill, gulping up to about 4 tonnes a day during feeding season."],
["Sperm whales","Sperm whales have the largest brains of any animal. They dive more than 1,000 meters deep to hunt giant squid and can stay underwater for over an hour. They communicate with patterns of clicks called codas, and different family groups have their own dialects. Moby-Dick was a white sperm whale."],
["Whale songs","Male humpback whales sing long, complex songs that can last up to 20 minutes and are repeated for hours. All males in a population sing the same song, which slowly changes over time. In 1970 a record of humpback songs became a best seller and helped inspire the Save the Whales campaigns."],
["Whaling","For centuries people hunted whales for meat, oil and baleen. Industrial whaling in the 1900s killed nearly 3 million whales, bringing many species close to extinction. In 1986 the International Whaling Commission banned commercial whaling. Some populations, such as humpbacks, have recovered strongly."],
["Dangers today","Whales and dolphins still face threats: entanglement in fishing nets, collisions with ships, ocean noise, pollution, plastic and climate change. Dolphins and porpoises often drown in fishing gear, and the vaquita, a tiny porpoise of Mexico’s Gulf of California, is close to extinction because of it."]],
sp:["The vaquita","The vaquita is the world’s smallest and rarest porpoise, about 1.5 meters long, found only in the northern Gulf of California in Mexico. It drowns in gillnets set for a large fish called the totoaba, whose swim bladder is illegally sold. From about 600 in the late 1990s, only about 10 vaquitas were left by the 2020s. Efforts to remove illegal nets continue."],
g:[["Cetacean","A whale, dolphin or porpoise"],["Baleen","Comb-like plates used to filter food"],["Krill","Small shrimp-like animals in the ocean"],["Blowhole","A nostril on top of a whale’s head"]],
f:["Dolphins sleep with one half of the brain at a time.","Bowhead whales can live more than 200 years.","Orcas, or killer whales, are the largest kind of dolphin."]},
insect:{s:[
["Six legs and three parts","Insects have six legs and bodies divided into three parts: head, thorax and abdomen. Most have two antennae, compound eyes and one or two pairs of wings. They have a hard outer skeleton, or exoskeleton, which they shed as they grow. Spiders, with eight legs, are not insects."],
["How insects breathe","Insects do not have lungs. Air enters through tiny holes along their sides, called spiracles, and travels through a network of tubes directly to the body’s cells. This system works only over short distances, one reason insects today stay fairly small."],
["Giant insects of the past","About 300 million years ago, when Earth’s air contained more oxygen, giant insects lived. Meganeura, a dragonfly-like insect, had a wingspan of about 70 centimeters. Some scientists think the higher oxygen levels allowed insects to grow larger."],
["Metamorphosis","Many insects change form as they grow, a process called metamorphosis. In complete metamorphosis, as in butterflies, beetles and flies, there are four stages: egg, larva, pupa and adult. In incomplete metamorphosis, as in grasshoppers and dragonflies, young nymphs look like small adults and grow by molting."],
["Pollinators","Bees, butterflies, moths, beetles and flies pollinate flowers, helping plants make fruits and seeds. About three-quarters of the world’s leading food crops benefit at least partly from animal pollination. Without pollinators, foods such as mangoes, coffee, cacao and many vegetables would become scarce."],
["Insects as food","About 2 billion people around the world regularly eat insects, which are rich in protein. Grasshoppers are popular in Mexico, where they are called chapulines, fried crickets are sold in Thailand, and mopane worms are eaten in southern Africa. Insect farming is being studied as a way to produce food with less land and water than livestock."]],
sp:["The atlas moth","The atlas moth, found in forests across South and Southeast Asia, is one of the largest moths in the world, with a wingspan of up to about 25 centimeters. Its wingtips look like snake heads, perhaps to scare predators. The adult moth has no working mouth and does not eat; it lives for only one or two weeks on fat stored as a caterpillar, just long enough to find a mate and lay eggs."],
g:[["Exoskeleton","A hard outer skeleton"],["Antennae","Feelers used for touch and smell"],["Larva","The young form of an insect that changes completely"],["Pupa","The stage between larva and adult"]],
f:["There are about 10 quintillion insects alive at any time, by one estimate.","Ants can carry many times their own body weight.","Monarch butterflies migrate up to about 4,000 km."]},
shark:{s:[
["Skeletons of cartilage","Sharks are fish with skeletons made of cartilage, the flexible material in human ears and noses, rather than bone. This makes them lighter and more flexible. Rays and skates are their close relatives. There are more than 500 known species of sharks."],
["Shark skin","Shark skin is covered in tiny tooth-like scales called dermal denticles, which make it feel rough like sandpaper when stroked the wrong way. The scales reduce drag, helping sharks swim efficiently. Engineers have copied the design for swimsuits and ship coatings."],
["Sensing electricity","Sharks can detect the weak electric fields made by the muscles of other animals, using jelly-filled pores on their snouts called ampullae of Lorenzini. This lets them find prey hidden in sand. Hammerhead sharks, with their wide heads, may sweep the seafloor like metal detectors."],
["Breathing and swimming","Sharks breathe by passing water over their gills. Some, such as great whites, must keep swimming to push water through their gills. Others, such as nurse sharks, can pump water while resting on the bottom. Sharks do not have swim bladders; their large, oily livers help them float."],
["Babies","Sharks reproduce in different ways. Some lay eggs in leathery cases, sometimes called mermaid’s purses. Others give birth to live young. In some species, such as sand tiger sharks, the strongest unborn pups eat their siblings inside the mother. Sharks grow slowly and have few young."],
["Sharks in danger","People kill an estimated tens of millions of sharks each year, many for their fins, used in shark fin soup, and many caught accidentally. Because sharks reproduce slowly, many species are threatened. Sharks are important predators that keep ocean ecosystems healthy. People are far more dangerous to sharks than sharks are to people."]],
sp:["Where whale sharks gather","The whale shark is the largest fish in the world, growing to about 12 to 18 meters long. Despite its size, it eats plankton and small fish and is harmless to people. It gathers at feeding sites such as Ningaloo Reef in Australia, the coast of Mexico’s Yucatán, the Maldives and the Philippines. Where sharks are fed to draw tourists, scientists worry that feeding changes their natural behavior."],
g:[["Cartilage","Flexible tissue that forms a shark’s skeleton"],["Gill","An organ for breathing underwater"],["Dermal denticle","A tiny tooth-like scale"],["Apex predator","An animal at the top of a food chain"]],
f:["The Greenland shark may live more than 270 years.","Sharks existed before trees appeared on land.","A shark may go through tens of thousands of teeth in its lifetime."]},
penguin:{s:[
["Where penguins live","There are about 18 species of penguins, all living in the Southern Hemisphere apart from the Galápagos penguin, which sometimes crosses just north of the equator. Only a few species, such as emperor and Adélie penguins, live on Antarctica itself. Others live in South America, southern Africa, Australia and New Zealand."],
["Underwater flight","Penguins cannot fly in the air, but they fly underwater. Their wings have become stiff flippers. Their bodies are streamlined like torpedoes, and their solid bones help them dive. Gentoo penguins can swim at up to about 36 km/h, and emperor penguins can dive over 500 meters deep."],
["Feathers and fat","Penguins have more feathers than most birds, short and densely packed, with a layer of fat underneath. They spread oil from a gland near their tails to waterproof their feathers. Once a year, penguins molt, replacing all their feathers at once, and must stay on land without eating for weeks."],
["Huddling","In Antarctic winter, temperatures can fall below −40°C, with fierce winds. Male emperor penguins huddle together in groups of thousands, taking turns moving from the cold edge to the warm center. Inside a huddle, temperatures can rise above 20°C."],
["Penguin food","Penguins eat fish, squid and krill, catching them with beaks and spiny tongues that grip slippery prey. They can drink seawater, as a special gland above their eyes removes the salt, which they sneeze or shake out. Their main predators are leopard seals, orcas and sea lions."],
["Threats","Many penguin species are declining. Climate change is melting the sea ice emperor penguins need for breeding; some colonies lost all their chicks in 2022 when ice broke up early. Overfishing reduces food, while oil spills and pollution harm penguins in South Africa and South America."]],
sp:["The emperor’s long winter","Emperor penguins breed during the Antarctic winter. After the female lays one egg, she passes it to the male and returns to the sea to feed. The male balances the egg on his feet under a warm flap of skin called the brood pouch for about two months, eating nothing and losing up to about 40% of his body weight. If the chick hatches before the mother returns, he feeds it a milky substance from his throat."],
g:[["Flipper","A flat limb used for swimming"],["Colony","A large group of animals living together"],["Molt","To shed and replace feathers"],["Brood pouch","A warm fold of skin for an egg"]],
f:["The smallest penguin, the little blue penguin, is about 30 cm tall.","Fossils show a giant extinct penguin about as tall as a person.","Many penguins return to the same nest and partner each year."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-ani2.js */
// In-depth material (Animals and Nature, part 2): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
octopus:{s:[
["Mollusks with brains","Octopuses are mollusks, related to snails and clams, in a group called cephalopods, meaning head-foot, which also includes squid and cuttlefish. Over millions of years, octopuses lost their shells. There are about 300 species, from the tiny star-sucker pygmy octopus to the giant Pacific octopus."],
["Three hearts and blue blood","An octopus has three hearts. Two pump blood through the gills, and one pumps it around the body. Its blood is blue because it uses a copper-based protein, hemocyanin, rather than iron-based hemoglobin, to carry oxygen. This works well in cold water with little oxygen."],
["Ink and jets","When threatened, many octopuses squirt a cloud of dark ink to confuse predators and dull their sense of smell. They can escape quickly by jet propulsion, shooting water out through a tube called a siphon. They can also squeeze through any gap larger than their beak, the only hard part of their body."],
["Using tools","Octopuses are among the few invertebrates known to use tools. The veined octopus of the western Pacific collects coconut shell halves and carries them along the seafloor, then assembles them into a shelter. In aquariums, octopuses have learned to open jars and have escaped from tanks."],
["Changing color","Octopuses can change color and texture in a fraction of a second. Their skin contains thousands of cells called chromatophores filled with pigment, which muscles expand or shrink. Other cells reflect light. Strangely, octopuses appear to be color-blind, and scientists are still studying how they match their surroundings."],
["Mothers that guard","A female octopus lays thousands of eggs in a den and guards them, cleaning them and blowing water over them, without eating. When the eggs hatch, she usually dies. A deep-sea octopus was observed guarding her eggs for about four and a half years, the longest known brooding period of any animal."]],
sp:["The mimic octopus","The mimic octopus, discovered off Sulawesi, Indonesia, in 1998, lives in shallow waters of Southeast Asia and can imitate other animals to scare off predators. It may flatten itself and glide like a poisonous flatfish, raise striped arms like a venomous lionfish, or bury itself with two arms waving like a sea snake. Scientists think it chooses which animal to copy depending on which predator threatens it."],
g:[["Cephalopod","A mollusk with arms on its head, such as an octopus"],["Chromatophore","A color-changing skin cell"],["Siphon","A tube used to squirt water"],["Invertebrate","An animal without a backbone"]],
f:["About two-thirds of an octopus’s neurons are in its arms.","Each sucker can taste what it touches.","The plural of octopus can be octopuses or octopodes."]},
wolf:{s:[
["Gray wolves and others","The gray wolf is the largest wild member of the dog family, with males weighing about 40 to 60 kg. It once lived across most of the Northern Hemisphere. Other wolves include the red wolf of the southeastern United States, one of the most endangered canids, and the Ethiopian wolf of Africa’s highlands."],
["Hunting together","Wolves hunt large animals such as deer, elk, moose and bison by working together. They chase prey over long distances, testing herds to find weak, young or old animals. A wolf can eat about 9 kg of meat at once. Most hunts fail, so wolves can go days without food."],
["Howling","Wolves howl to communicate over long distances, up to about 10 km in open country. Howling helps gather the pack, warn rival packs to keep away and keep in touch while traveling. Wolves do not howl at the Moon; they simply point their heads up so the sound carries farther."],
["From wolf to dog","Dogs are descended from ancient wolves, probably domesticated more than 15,000 years ago, possibly when wolves scavenged near human camps. Over time, friendlier animals were bred, and today there are hundreds of dog breeds, as well as countless free-roaming village dogs around the world."],
["Yellowstone’s wolves","Wolves were wiped out from Yellowstone National Park in the United States by the 1920s. Without them, elk multiplied and ate young trees along rivers. In 1995 and 1996, 31 wolves from Canada were released there. The elk changed their behavior, trees grew back, and beavers and songbirds returned, showing how one species can shape an ecosystem."],
["Fear and folklore","Wolves appear in many stories, often as villains, as in Little Red Riding Hood and The Three Little Pigs. In other traditions they are respected: Rome’s founders, Romulus and Remus, were said to be raised by a she-wolf, and many Native American peoples honor the wolf. Attacks on people are very rare."]],
sp:["The wolf’s comeback in Europe","Wolves were hunted to extinction in much of Western Europe by the early 1900s. With legal protection and growing forests, they have returned naturally from refuges in Italy, Spain and Eastern Europe. Wolves now live in every mainland country of the European Union, and there are estimated to be around 20,000 in Europe. Their return has caused conflict with farmers over attacks on sheep, and countries are debating how strictly to protect them."],
g:[["Pack","A family group of wolves"],["Canid","A member of the dog family"],["Domestication","Taming and breeding animals for human use"],["Keystone species","A species with a large effect on its ecosystem"]],
f:["Wolf packs usually have about 4 to 9 members.","Wolves can run at about 60 km/h in short bursts.","Wolf pups are born deaf and blind."]},
frog:{s:[
["What amphibians are","Amphibians are cold-blooded vertebrates that usually begin life in water and later live partly on land. The word amphibian means double life. There are three groups: frogs and toads; salamanders and newts; and caecilians, legless burrowers that look like worms. There are over 8,000 known species."],
["Breathing through skin","Many amphibians breathe partly through their thin, moist skin, which must stay damp. Some salamanders have no lungs at all. Because their skin absorbs water and chemicals easily, amphibians are very sensitive to pollution, making them useful indicators of a healthy environment."],
["Calls","Male frogs call to attract females and warn rivals, using vocal sacs that swell like balloons to make their calls louder. Each species has its own call. On rainy nights in the tropics, the croaking of frogs and toads fills forests and rice fields, and scientists can identify species by sound alone."],
["Unusual parents","Amphibians care for their young in surprising ways. Male Darwin’s frogs carry tadpoles in their vocal sacs. Female Surinam toads carry eggs embedded in the skin of their backs. Poison dart frogs carry tadpoles on their backs to tiny pools in plants. Some frogs lay eggs on land that hatch directly into tiny froglets."],
["Chytrid fungus","Since the 1980s, a disease caused by the chytrid fungus has spread around the world, killing amphibians by damaging their skin. It has caused the decline of hundreds of species and the extinction of dozens, the worst disease-caused loss of biodiversity ever recorded. Scientists are breeding threatened species in captivity to save them."],
["The cane toad","The cane toad, native to the Americas, was introduced to many places in the 1930s and after, including Australia, the Caribbean and Pacific islands, to eat pests in sugarcane fields. It spread widely and is poisonous to animals that eat it. In Australia it has become a serious invasive species."]],
sp:["Frogs without tadpoles","Many frogs skip the tadpole stage altogether. They lay eggs on land, in moss, leaf litter or tree holes, and tiny froglets hatch straight out of them. This is called direct development. The coquí frogs of Puerto Rico, the Platymantis frogs of Southeast Asia and the rain frogs of the Americas all breed this way, which frees them from needing ponds or streams."],
g:[["Amphibian","An animal that lives both in water and on land"],["Tadpole","The water-living young of a frog"],["Vocal sac","A stretchy pouch used to amplify calls"],["Invasive species","A species that spreads harmfully in a new place"]],
f:["The Goliath frog of West Africa can weigh over 3 kg.","Frogs swallow with help from their eyes, which push food down.","Some frogs can freeze solid in winter and thaw in spring."]},
tiger:{s:[
["Where tigers live","Tigers once roamed from Turkey to eastern Russia and down to Indonesia. Today they survive in only a few percent of their former range, in India, Nepal, Bhutan, Bangladesh, Russia, China and parts of Southeast Asia. They live in tropical forests, mangrove swamps, grasslands and snowy forests."],
["Bengal and Siberian tigers","The Bengal tiger of India is the most numerous. The Siberian, or Amur, tiger of the Russian Far East is the largest, with males weighing up to about 300 kg and thick fur for temperatures of −40°C. Sumatran tigers are the smallest surviving tigers. Scientists now group tigers into two subspecies: mainland and Sunda island tigers."],
["Solitary hunters","Tigers usually live and hunt alone, defending large territories marked with scent and scratches. They ambush deer, wild pigs and buffalo, stalking close before a final rush. A tiger can eat about 40 kg of meat in one meal. Most hunts fail, so tigers may hunt only every few days."],
["Swimmers","Unlike most cats, tigers love water. They swim well and often cool off in rivers and pools. In the Sundarbans mangrove forest of India and Bangladesh, tigers swim between islands and hunt fish and crabs as well as deer. Sundarbans tigers have a fearsome reputation for attacking people."],
["Cubs","Tigresses usually give birth to two to four cubs, which are born blind and helpless. The mother raises them alone, hiding them in dens and teaching them to hunt. Cubs stay with their mother for about two years before leaving to find their own territory. Many cubs die young."],
["Tigers in culture","Tigers are national animals of India, Bangladesh, Malaysia and South Korea. In Chinese tradition, the tiger is one of the 12 animals of the zodiac and a symbol of courage. Tigers appear in the folk tales of many Asian peoples, and the tiger in Rudyard Kipling’s The Jungle Book, Shere Khan, is one of the most famous in literature."]],
sp:["Counting tigers","In 2010 the 13 tiger range countries set a goal to double the number of wild tigers by 2022, the Chinese Year of the Tiger. The global count rose from about 3,200 to around 5,500 by 2023. India, home to about three-quarters of the world’s wild tigers, counts them using thousands of camera traps that photograph passing animals; each tiger can be identified by its unique stripes. Numbers have fallen, however, in parts of Southeast Asia."],
g:[["Territory","An area an animal defends"],["Camera trap","A camera triggered by passing animals"],["Subspecies","A distinct population within a species"],["Poaching","Illegal hunting"]],
f:["No two tigers have the same stripe pattern.","A tiger’s roar can be heard up to about 3 km away.","Tigers have stripes on their skin as well as their fur."]},
bat:{s:[
["More than 1,400 species","Bats make up about one-fifth of all mammal species, with more than 1,400 known. They live on every continent except Antarctica. The smallest, the bumblebee bat of Thailand and Myanmar, weighs about 2 grams. The largest fruit bats have wingspans of about 1.5 meters."],
["Fruit bats and insect bats","Most bats are small insect-eaters that use echolocation. Fruit bats, also called flying foxes, have large eyes and good noses, eat fruit and nectar and mostly do not echolocate. Some bats eat fish, frogs or even other bats. Three species feed on blood."],
["Seed spreaders and pollinators","Fruit bats spread seeds as they fly, helping forests regrow. Some bats pollinate plants, including durian, bananas, and the agave used to make tequila. In Southeast Asia, durian flowers open at night and are pollinated mainly by bats."],
["Pest control","A single insect-eating bat can eat hundreds or even thousands of insects in a night. Bats eat mosquitoes, moths and beetles that damage crops, saving farmers billions of dollars in pesticide costs worldwide. Bat droppings, called guano, are collected from caves as fertilizer."],
["Hibernation and migration","In cold places, many bats hibernate through winter in caves, lowering their body temperature and heart rate to save energy. Others migrate long distances. In the tropics, where it is warm all year, bats stay active throughout the year, though some move between roosts with the seasons."],
["Threats to bats","Bats face threats from habitat loss, hunting for food, disturbance of caves and wind turbines. In North America, a fungal disease called white-nose syndrome has killed millions of hibernating bats since 2006. Flying foxes are hunted in parts of Asia and the Pacific, and several species are endangered."]],
sp:["Giant flying foxes","The largest bats are the flying foxes of Asia, Australia and the Pacific islands. The giant golden-crowned flying fox of the Philippines and the large flying fox of Southeast Asia have wingspans of up to about 1.5 to 1.7 meters. They feed on fruit, nectar and leaves and roost in large colonies in tall trees, flying out at dusk in dramatic streams. Hunting and forest loss have made many species endangered."],
g:[["Echolocation","Finding objects by listening to echoes"],["Flying fox","A large fruit-eating bat"],["Guano","Bat or bird droppings used as fertilizer"],["Roost","A place where bats rest"]],
f:["Bracken Cave in Texas is home to about 15 million bats, the largest bat colony on Earth.","Bats are the only mammals capable of true flight.","Some bats live more than 40 years."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-eco1.js */
// In-depth material (Economics and Society, part 1): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
supdem:{s:[
["Scarcity","Economics begins with scarcity: people want more than there is. Land, time, money and materials are limited, so people, businesses and governments must make choices. Every choice has an opportunity cost, the value of the next best thing given up. Supply and demand help explain how markets allocate scarce goods."],
["Equilibrium","The point where the supply curve and demand curve cross is called equilibrium. At that price, the amount buyers want equals the amount sellers offer. If the price is higher, unsold goods pile up and sellers cut prices. If it is lower, shortages appear and prices rise. Markets tend to move toward equilibrium."],
["Prices as signals","Prices carry information. When the price of something rises, it tells producers to make more and consumers to use less or find substitutes. Economist Friedrich Hayek argued that prices coordinate the knowledge of millions of people that no single planner could gather."],
["Substitutes and complements","Substitutes are goods that can replace each other, such as rice and bread or trains and buses. If the price of one rises, demand for the other increases. Complements are used together, such as cars and gasoline or printers and ink. If gas prices rise sharply, demand for cars that use a lot of it may fall."],
["Price controls","Governments sometimes set price ceilings, maximum prices, to protect consumers, or price floors, minimum prices, to protect producers. Rent controls and minimum wages are common examples, and some governments freeze prices of basic goods during emergencies. Ceilings set below equilibrium can cause shortages, and floors above it can cause surpluses."],
["Onions in 2022–2023","In the 1970s, oil producers cut supply, and the price of oil quadrupled in a few months, causing long lines at gas stations in the United States and Europe. Food prices can swing in the same way: when bad weather ruins a harvest, prices of staples such as onions, coffee or cocoa can soar until new supplies arrive."]],
sp:["The diamond-water paradox","Water is essential to life but cheap; diamonds are useless for survival but expensive. Adam Smith puzzled over this paradox in 1776. Later economists explained it with marginal utility: what matters is the value of one more unit. Because water is usually plentiful, an extra glass is worth little, while diamonds are rare, so each one is valued highly. In a desert, however, a glass of water might be worth more than a diamond."],
g:[["Scarcity","Limited resources compared with wants"],["Equilibrium","The price where supply equals demand"],["Opportunity cost","The value of the next best choice given up"],["Price ceiling","A legal maximum price"]],
f:["Concert and plane tickets often use dynamic pricing that changes with demand.","A good whose demand rises as prices rise, such as luxury goods, is sometimes called a Veblen good.","Alfred Marshall popularized supply and demand diagrams in 1890."]},
money:{s:[
["Barter","Before money, people traded goods directly, a system called barter. A farmer might trade rice for a fisher’s catch. But barter requires a double coincidence of wants: each person must want what the other has. Money solves this problem by being accepted by everyone."],
["Commodity money","Many things have served as money: cattle, salt, shells, beads, cacao beans and metals. Cowrie shells were used in Africa and Asia for centuries. The Pacific island of Yap used huge stone discs, some taller than a person, which stayed in place while ownership changed hands."],
["Paper money","Paper money was first used widely in China during the Song dynasty, around 1000 CE. Marco Polo was amazed that the emperor’s paper was accepted as payment. In Europe, banks began issuing paper notes in the 1600s. Today’s money is fiat money, valuable because governments declare it legal tender and people trust it."],
["Interest","Banks pay interest to savers and charge interest to borrowers. Interest is the price of using money over time. Simple interest is paid only on the original amount; compound interest is paid on interest as well, so savings grow faster over long periods. High interest on debts can make them hard to repay."],
["The Bangko Sentral","Most countries have a central bank, which issues the currency, sets key interest rates to control inflation, supervises banks and manages foreign reserves. The Bank of England, founded in 1694, is one of the oldest, the US Federal Reserve was created in 1913, and the European Central Bank has managed the euro since 1998."],
["Going cashless","Mobile and card payments are replacing cash in many places. In Sweden, fewer than one in ten purchases are paid in cash, China’s Alipay and WeChat Pay are used by more than a billion people, and India’s UPI handles billions of instant payments a month. Digital payments became especially popular during the COVID-19 pandemic."]],
sp:["Rai stones of Yap","On the Pacific island of Yap, people used huge stone discs called rai as money for important exchanges such as marriages and land deals. The stones, some up to about 4 meters across, were carved from limestone on Palau and carried hundreds of kilometers by canoe. Because they were too heavy to move, ownership simply passed by agreement, with everyone remembering who owned which stone, an early example of money as a shared record."],
g:[["Barter","Trading goods directly without money"],["Fiat money","Money valuable because a government declares it so"],["Interest","Payment for borrowing or lending money"],["Legal tender","Money that must be accepted for debts"]],
f:["The euro is shared by 21 European Union countries.","The word salary may come from salt, once valuable to Romans.","Most money today exists as digital records, not cash."]},
trade:{s:[
["Comparative advantage","In 1817 economist David Ricardo explained comparative advantage: even if one country is better at making everything, both countries gain if each focuses on what it gives up least to produce, and they trade. This is why countries specialize, Taiwan in chips and Bangladesh in clothing, for example, and trade for other goods."],
["The Silk Road","For centuries, the Silk Road network linked China with Central Asia, the Middle East and Europe. Traders carried silk, spices, paper, glass and horses. Ideas, religions, technologies and diseases traveled along these routes too. Sea routes across the Indian Ocean also carried huge amounts of trade."],
["The galleon trade","From 1565 to 1815, Spanish galleons sailed between Manila and Acapulco in Mexico. They carried Chinese silk and porcelain, Asian spices and other goods to the Americas and returned with Mexican silver. Manila became one of the first global trading hubs, and Spanish silver coins circulated as far as China, an early sign of a truly global economy."],
["The World Trade Organization","After the Second World War, countries created the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade, GATT, in 1947 to lower trade barriers. In 1995 it was replaced by the World Trade Organization, which sets trade rules and settles disputes between its 166 members."],
["Remittances and services","Services are a growing share of trade. India exports software and IT services worth hundreds of billions of dollars, and call centers and back offices in countries such as India and the Philippines serve customers worldwide. Migrant workers send home more than 650 billion US dollars a year in remittances, a lifeline for many families."],
["Supply chains","Modern products often have parts made in many countries. A smartphone might be designed in one country, use chips from another and be assembled in a third. These global supply chains make goods cheaper but can break down, as during the COVID-19 pandemic, when factory closures and shipping delays caused shortages worldwide."]],
sp:["The container box","In 1956 the American trucking businessman Malcom McLean loaded 58 metal containers onto a ship in New Jersey. Standard steel boxes that could move straight from ship to truck to train cut the cost of loading cargo by more than 90 percent. Today tens of millions of containers cross the oceans each year, and cheap shipping made it possible for goods to be made in one country and sold in another."],
g:[["Export","A good sold to another country"],["Import","A good bought from another country"],["Tariff","A tax on imported goods"],["Comparative advantage","The ability to produce at lower opportunity cost"]],
f:["About 80% of world trade by volume travels by sea.","About 80 percent of world trade by volume travels by sea.","The Manila–Acapulco galleon route ran for 250 years."]},
inflation:{s:[
["Prices over time","Inflation is a general rise in prices over time, which means each unit of money buys less. If inflation is 4% a year, something that costs 100 this year will cost about 104 next year. Over many years, even low inflation adds up: at 4%, prices double in about 18 years."],
["Demand-pull and cost-push","Demand-pull inflation happens when people and businesses want to buy more than the economy can produce, pulling prices up. Cost-push inflation happens when costs rise, such as oil, wages or food after a typhoon, and businesses pass them on. Printing too much money can also cause inflation."],
["The consumer price index","Statistics offices measure inflation using the consumer price index, CPI. It tracks the prices of a basket of goods and services that typical households buy, such as food, rent, electricity, transport and school fees. In poorer countries, food makes up a larger share of the basket."],
["Central banks fight inflation","When inflation is too high, central banks raise interest rates. This makes borrowing more expensive, so people and businesses spend less, cooling demand. In 1979 to 1981, the US Federal Reserve under Paul Volcker raised rates to nearly 20% to break double-digit inflation."],
["Hyperinflation","Hyperinflation is extremely rapid inflation. In Germany in 1923, prices doubled every few days, and people carried money in wheelbarrows. In Zimbabwe in 2008, a 100 trillion dollar note was printed. In Venezuela in the late 2010s, inflation reached over a million percent. Hyperinflation can destroy savings."],
["Mickey Mouse money","Hyperinflation is extremely fast inflation. In Germany in 1923, prices doubled every few days, and people carried wages home in wheelbarrows. Hungary in 1946 had the worst hyperinflation ever recorded, with prices doubling about every 15 hours, and Zimbabwe printed a 100 trillion dollar note in 2009. During wartime occupations, such as Japan’s in Southeast Asia, occupation money also became nearly worthless."]],
sp:["The 2022 inflation surge","After the COVID-19 pandemic, inflation rose around the world. Supply chains were disrupted, demand recovered quickly, and Russia’s invasion of Ukraine in 2022 pushed up oil and food prices. Inflation reached about 9% in the United States and over 10% in the euro area and Britain in 2022, the highest in 40 years. Central banks raised interest rates sharply, and inflation fell back over the following two years."],
g:[["Inflation","A general rise in prices"],["CPI","Consumer price index, a measure of prices"],["Purchasing power","How much money can buy"],["Hyperinflation","Extremely rapid inflation"]],
f:["Hungary’s hyperinflation of 1946 is the worst ever recorded.","Some countries have experienced deflation, falling prices, for years.","The Rule of 72 estimates how long prices take to double: divide 72 by the inflation rate."]},
gdp:{s:[
["What GDP counts","Gross domestic product, GDP, is the total value of all final goods and services produced in a country in a year. It counts things such as cars, rice, haircuts and phone services, but not unpaid household work or most informal trade. It is the most widely used measure of the size of an economy."],
["Simon Kuznets","GDP-like measurements were developed in the 1930s, during the Great Depression, when governments needed to know how badly the economy had shrunk. Economist Simon Kuznets created the first national income accounts for the United States. He warned that such measures do not show the welfare of a nation."],
["GDP per person","Dividing GDP by population gives GDP per capita, a rough measure of average living standards. Small, rich countries such as Luxembourg and Singapore have very high GDP per capita. Large countries such as China and India have big total GDPs but lower GDP per capita."],
["The fastest growers","Some economies have grown astonishingly fast. China grew by about 10% a year for three decades after 1980, lifting hundreds of millions out of poverty, and South Korea, Taiwan and Singapore were called Asian tigers for their rapid growth from the 1960s. In most rich countries, services make up about 70% of the economy, industry about 25% and agriculture only a few percent."],
["Purchasing power","Comparing GDP between countries is tricky because prices differ. A meal in Hanoi costs less than in Tokyo. Economists use purchasing power parity, PPP, to adjust for price differences. Measured by PPP, China has the world’s largest economy; measured in US dollars, the United States does."],
["Happiness and well-being","GDP does not measure happiness, health, inequality or the environment. Bhutan famously measures gross national happiness. The UN’s Human Development Index combines income, education and life expectancy. The World Happiness Report ranks countries by how people rate their lives."]],
sp:["The broken window","Imagine a boy breaks a shop window. The shopkeeper pays a glazier to replace it, and GDP rises. But is the town better off? French economist Frédéric Bastiat used this parable in 1850 to show the hidden cost: the shopkeeper would have spent that money on something else, like new shoes. Rebuilding after typhoons may boost GDP, but it does not mean disasters are good. GDP measures activity, not well-being."],
g:[["GDP","The total value of goods and services produced in a year"],["Per capita","Per person"],["Recession","A period when the economy shrinks"],["PPP","Purchasing power parity, adjusting for price differences"]],
f:["The world’s total GDP is over 100 trillion US dollars.","Income earned by citizens working abroad counts toward gross national income, GNI, not GDP.","Most countries publish GDP figures every quarter."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-eco2.js */
// In-depth material (Economics and Society, part 2): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
stockm:{s:[
["Why companies sell shares","Companies sell shares to raise money to grow, build factories or develop products without borrowing. The first time a company sells shares to the public is called an initial public offering, IPO. Shareholders become part-owners and may share in profits through dividends."],
["Amsterdam 1602","The Dutch East India Company, founded in 1602, was the first company to sell shares to the general public that could be freely traded. Investors bought and sold them in Amsterdam, which became home to the first modern stock exchange. The company traded in spices from Southeast Asia."],
["Wall Street","The New York Stock Exchange traces its origins to 1792, when 24 brokers signed the Buttonwood Agreement under a buttonwood tree on Wall Street. It is now the world’s largest stock exchange by the value of companies listed. The Nasdaq, founded in 1971, was the first electronic stock market."],
["Dividends and prices","Investors can earn money in two ways: dividends, payments from company profits, and capital gains, when share prices rise. Prices change with company performance, interest rates, news and investors’ hopes and fears. Prices can also fall, and investors can lose money."],
["The 1929 crash","In October 1929 the US stock market crashed after a long boom fueled by borrowing. Prices fell by about 90% from 1929 to 1932. The crash helped trigger the Great Depression, a worldwide economic slump. New rules were introduced afterward to make markets safer and more honest."],
["Investing wisely","Experts advise investors to diversify, spreading money across many companies and types of investment, to reduce risk. Index funds, which track a whole market index, are a low-cost way to do this. Investing for the long term usually works better than trying to guess short-term ups and downs."]],
sp:["Tulip mania","In the 1630s, people in the Netherlands paid extraordinary prices for rare tulip bulbs. At the peak in early 1637, a single bulb of a prized variety reportedly sold for more than a house in Amsterdam. Then, in February 1637, buyers suddenly vanished and prices collapsed. Historians now think the damage was smaller than once claimed, but tulip mania remains a famous symbol of a speculative bubble."],
g:[["Share","A unit of ownership in a company"],["Dividend","A payment to shareholders from profits"],["IPO","Initial public offering, a first sale of shares"],["Bubble","Prices rising far above real value"]],
f:["Nasdaq, opened in 1971, was the first electronic stock market.","Stock exchanges once used trading floors full of shouting brokers.","Black Monday, 19 October 1987, saw the Dow fall about 22% in one day."]},
insur:{s:[
["Pooling risk","Insurance works by pooling risk. Many people pay small amounts, called premiums, into a shared fund. When one of them suffers a loss, such as a fire, accident or illness, the fund pays them. No one knows who will be unlucky, but the group can predict roughly how many losses will occur."],
["Marine insurance","Insurance for sea voyages developed in Italian city-states such as Genoa in the 1300s. Merchants paid to protect cargo against shipwreck and piracy. In the late 1600s, merchants and shipowners met at Edward Lloyd’s coffee house in London, which grew into Lloyd’s of London, still a major insurance market."],
["Life insurance","Life insurance pays money to a person’s family when they die, helping replace lost income. Modern life insurance used mortality tables showing how long people of different ages are likely to live. Today, many people also buy plans to save for education, retirement and funerals."],
["Health insurance","Health insurance helps pay for medical care. Germany created the first national health insurance system in 1883 under Otto von Bismarck. Today many countries cover all their citizens through public insurance or tax-funded systems, such as Britain’s National Health Service, while in others many people rely on private insurance through their employers."],
["Social insurance","Governments run social insurance programs for workers. The United States created Social Security in 1935, and most countries now provide pensions, sickness, maternity and disability benefits funded by workers’ and employers’ contributions. Some, like Singapore, require workers to save in individual accounts for housing, health and retirement."],
["Moral hazard","Insurance companies worry about moral hazard: insured people may take more risks because they are protected. To reduce it, policies often include deductibles, amounts the insured must pay first, and exclusions. Companies also check claims carefully to prevent fraud."]],
sp:["Typhoon insurance","Disasters can wipe out farmers’ crops and homes, and many poor families have no insurance. Governments and aid groups have experimented with catastrophe bonds and parametric insurance, which pays out automatically when a measured event, such as wind speed or rainfall, crosses a set level. Caribbean countries pooled their risk in 2007, and the World Bank has issued catastrophe bonds for countries such as Mexico, Chile and the Philippines."],
g:[["Premium","The regular payment for insurance"],["Claim","A request for payment from an insurer"],["Deductible","The amount paid before insurance covers the rest"],["Actuary","An expert who calculates insurance risks"]],
f:["Lloyd’s of London has insured unusual things, such as a pianist’s hands.","Lloyd’s of London began in a coffee house in the late 1600s.","Microinsurance offers small, cheap policies to people with low incomes."]},
urban:{s:[
["Why cities grow","People move to cities for jobs, schools, hospitals and opportunities. In rural areas, farming may provide little income. Cities offer services and markets, but rapid growth can bring crowding. This movement from countryside to city is called urbanization."],
["More than half of humanity","In 1950 about 30% of the world’s people lived in cities. Around 2007 the number of city dwellers passed half of all people for the first time. By 2050 the UN expects about two-thirds of people to live in urban areas, with most growth in Asia and Africa."],
["Megacities","A megacity has more than 10 million people. There were only two, New York and Tokyo, in 1950; today there are more than 30, most of them in Asia, Africa and Latin America, including Delhi, Shanghai, São Paulo, Cairo, Lagos, Dhaka and Manila. Lagos and Kinshasa are among the fastest-growing big cities in the world."],
["Getting around","Traffic is a major challenge in big cities. Metro systems, buses, bicycles and shared minibuses carry millions every day. Bogotá’s TransMilenio and Curitiba’s bus rapid transit inspired cities worldwide, and Singapore and London charge drivers to enter busy areas, a policy called congestion pricing."],
["Planning cities","Urban planners decide where homes, businesses, roads, parks and services should go. Baron Haussmann rebuilt Paris with wide boulevards in the 1850s and 1860s, and planned capitals such as Washington, D.C., Canberra and Brasília were designed from scratch. Today planners try to make cities walkable, green and resilient to floods and heat."],
["Green cities","Cities can reduce pollution and heat by planting trees, building parks, encouraging walking and cycling and improving public transport. The urban heat island effect makes cities hotter than the countryside. Singapore, a city full of gardens and green buildings, is an example studied by planners across Asia."]],
sp:["Tokyo","Tokyo, Japan, with about 37 million people in the Greater Tokyo Area, was long counted as the world’s largest metropolitan area; a 2025 United Nations report ranked it third, after Jakarta and Dhaka. Despite its size, it is known for being clean, safe and efficient. Its trains carry millions of passengers daily and are famously punctual, with average delays measured in seconds. Tokyo was rebuilt twice in the 20th century, after the 1923 Great Kanto earthquake and after bombing in the Second World War."],
g:[["Urbanization","The growth of cities"],["Megacity","A city with more than 10 million people"],["Density","The number of people per area"],["Urban heat island","A city warmer than its surroundings"]],
f:["In 1950 only two cities in the world had more than 10 million people.","Lagos, Delhi and Dhaka are among the fastest-growing megacities.","The world has about 30 or more megacities."]},
taxes:{s:[
["Why taxes exist","Taxes are payments people and businesses make to the government. They fund public services such as schools, hospitals, roads, police, the military and social programs. Without taxes, governments could not provide goods that everyone shares, called public goods."],
["Ancient taxes","Taxes are as old as civilization. Ancient Egyptians paid taxes in grain, recorded by scribes. Roman citizens paid taxes on property and sales. In medieval Europe, peasants paid in crops and labor. The famous saying that nothing is certain except death and taxes comes from Benjamin Franklin in 1789."],
["Income tax","Income tax is charged on wages, salaries and profits. Britain introduced it in 1799 to pay for wars against Napoleon, and the United States made it permanent in 1913. Rates vary widely: some Gulf countries have no personal income tax, while top rates exceed 50 percent in parts of Europe."],
["VAT","Value-added tax, VAT, is charged on the sale of goods and services at each stage of production. France introduced it in 1954, and today more than 170 countries use it. Rates vary from around 5 to 27 percent, and some goods, such as fresh food, are often exempt. VAT raises a large share of government revenue."],
["Collecting taxes","National tax agencies, such as the Internal Revenue Service in the United States and HM Revenue and Customs in Britain, collect most national taxes, while customs services collect duties on imports. Local governments collect property taxes and business fees. Most countries set a yearly deadline for filing income tax returns."],
["Fair taxes","People debate what a fair tax system is. Progressive taxes take a larger share from people with higher incomes. Regressive taxes, such as some sales taxes, take a larger share of poorer people’s incomes. Tax evasion, illegally avoiding taxes, reduces money for public services."]],
sp:["Sin taxes","Sin taxes are taxes on products considered harmful, such as tobacco, alcohol and sugary drinks. They aim to discourage use and raise money. Mexico’s tax on sugary drinks in 2014 cut purchases, and the Philippines’ Sin Tax Reform Law of 2012 raised taxes on cigarettes and alcohol and helped fund universal health care. The World Health Organization calls tobacco taxes one of the most effective ways to reduce smoking."],
g:[["Tax","A required payment to the government"],["VAT","Value-added tax on sales"],["Progressive tax","A tax taking a larger share from higher incomes"],["Revenue","Money collected by a government"]],
f:["Some countries, such as the United Arab Emirates, have long had no personal income tax.","In ancient Rome, Emperor Vespasian taxed public urine collection.","France was the first country to introduce a value-added tax, in 1954."]},
labor:{s:[
["Work through history","For most of history, people worked on farms, as hunters or as craftspeople. Many were enslaved or bound to landowners. The Industrial Revolution brought factories, where people worked for wages, often in dangerous conditions, for long hours, including young children."],
["Unions","Workers formed trade unions to bargain together for better pay, shorter hours and safer conditions. Unions organized strikes, refusing to work until demands were met. Many were suppressed at first. Poland’s Solidarity union, founded in 1980, grew into a movement that helped end communist rule."],
["The eight-hour day","Workers campaigned for eight hours of work, eight of rest and eight of leisure. In 1886, protests in Chicago, including the Haymarket affair, became the origin of International Workers’ Day on 1 May. Uruguay was among the first countries to make the eight-hour day law, in 1915, and it became the international standard in 1919."],
["Minimum wages","A minimum wage is the lowest pay employers may legally offer. New Zealand introduced the first national minimum wage in 1894, and today most countries have one, though levels vary enormously. Debates continue over raising wages to keep up with prices while protecting jobs."],
["Migrant workers","Millions of people work abroad, as nurses, domestic workers, builders, seafarers, engineers and more. Many come from India, Mexico, the Philippines, Bangladesh and Egypt, and the Gulf states depend heavily on foreign workers. Their remittances support families and economies at home, but separation is hard on families, and migrant workers can face abuse."],
["Robots and AI","Automation and artificial intelligence are changing jobs. Machines can do some tasks in factories, offices and call centers. Some jobs may disappear, while new ones appear. Experts stress training and education so workers can adapt."]],
sp:["Child labor","Child labor is work that harms children or keeps them out of school. In the 1800s, children as young as five worked in mines and factories. Laws gradually banned it in many countries. Yet the International Labour Organization estimates about 138 million children worldwide were still in child labor in 2024, most of them working on farms in Africa and Asia. 12 June is the World Day Against Child Labour."],
g:[["Wage","Payment for work"],["Union","An organization of workers"],["Strike","Refusing to work to press demands"],["Remittance","Money sent home by workers abroad"]],
f:["The International Labour Organization was founded in 1919.","New Zealand introduced the first national minimum wage, in 1894.","Some countries are experimenting with four-day work weeks."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-food1.js */
// In-depth material (Food and Agriculture, part 1): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
rice:{s:[
["Asian and African rice","There are two species of cultivated rice. Asian rice, Oryza sativa, was domesticated in China’s Yangtze valley about 9,000 years ago and is grown worldwide. African rice, Oryza glaberrima, was domesticated separately in West Africa about 3,000 years ago. Asian rice has two main types, japonica and indica."],
["Paddies and water","Most rice grows in flooded fields called paddies. The water helps control weeds and pests. Farmers transplant seedlings by hand or machine, or sow seeds directly. Rice needs a lot of water, about 2,500 liters for each kilogram of grain in some systems. Upland rice grows on dry hillsides with rain."],
["The rice plant","Rice is a grass that grows about 1 to 1.8 meters tall. Its grains form in drooping clusters called panicles. Each grain is covered by a husk. Removing the husk produces brown rice; polishing away the bran layer produces white rice, which keeps longer but loses some vitamins and fiber."],
["IRRI","The International Rice Research Institute, IRRI, was founded in 1960 in Los Baños, Laguna. In 1966 it released IR8, a short, high-yielding variety nicknamed miracle rice, which helped prevent famine in Asia during the Green Revolution. IRRI keeps the world’s largest rice gene bank, with over 130,000 types of rice."],
["Rice in culture","Rice is central to many cuisines: Chinese fried rice, Japanese sushi, Indian biryani, Persian chelow, Spanish paella, Italian risotto, West African jollof rice and Louisiana jambalaya. In many Asian languages the word for cooked rice also means a meal, and some have separate words for rice in the field, uncooked grain and cooked rice."],
["Feeding the nation","Most rice is eaten in the country where it is grown, so only about a tenth of the world’s harvest is traded. When big exporters limit sales, prices can soar, as in the 2008 rice crisis and again when India restricted exports in 2023. Farmers face typhoons, droughts, pests and rising costs."]],
sp:["Terraces of rice","In mountainous regions, farmers carved hillsides into steps to grow rice. The Honghe Hani terraces in Yunnan, China, the Jatiluwih terraces of Bali and the rice terraces of the Philippine Cordilleras are UNESCO World Heritage Sites. They are watered by ancient systems that channel rain from forests above, and they show how communities shaped whole landscapes over generations."],
g:[["Paddy","A flooded field for growing rice"],["Palay","Unhusked rice grain"],["Panicle","A loose, branching cluster of grains"],["Staple food","A food eaten regularly as a main part of the diet"]],
f:["More than half of the world’s people eat rice as a staple.","Only about a tenth of the world’s rice is traded between countries.","China and India together grow more than half of the world’s rice."]},
agri:{s:[
["The Fertile Crescent","One of the first places where farming began, around 11,000 to 10,000 years ago, was the Fertile Crescent, an arc of land from the Nile through the Levant and Mesopotamia. Early farmers grew wheat, barley, lentils, peas and flax and herded goats, sheep, pigs and cattle."],
["Seeds and selection","Early farmers kept seeds from the best plants to sow the next year. Over generations, crops changed: wheat grains stopped falling off the stalk, and corn grew larger cobs. This process, artificial selection, gradually transformed wild plants into crops that depend on people."],
["Villages and towns","Farming allowed people to settle in one place and store food. Villages grew into towns such as Jericho and Çatalhöyük, in modern Turkey, home to thousands of people by about 7000 BCE. Surplus food meant some people could become potters, builders, priests and rulers."],
["Tools and irrigation","Farmers invented tools such as sickles, grinding stones, hoes and later ploughs pulled by oxen. In Mesopotamia and Egypt, farmers dug canals to carry river water to fields, called irrigation. Managing water required cooperation and helped give rise to governments and laws."],
["The Columbian Exchange","After 1492, crops and animals crossed between the Americas and the rest of the world. Maize, potatoes, tomatoes, chili peppers, cacao and cassava spread from the Americas; wheat, rice, sugarcane, cattle, horses and pigs went the other way. This exchange transformed diets everywhere."],
["Farming today","Modern farms use machines, fertilizers, pesticides and improved seeds to produce much more food per hectare than ever. About a quarter of the world’s workers are in agriculture. Challenges include climate change, soil loss, water shortages and the need to grow more food sustainably for a growing population."]],
sp:["Taro, bananas and the first Pacific farmers","Farming began independently in the highlands of New Guinea about 7,000 to 10,000 years ago, where people grew taro, bananas and sugarcane. Austronesian peoples, whose ancestors are thought to have passed through Taiwan and the Philippines, later carried crops such as taro, yams, bananas and breadfruit by canoe across the Pacific, along with pigs, dogs and chickens. These crops fed island communities across thousands of kilometers of ocean."],
g:[["Domestication","Taming wild plants and animals for human use"],["Irrigation","Bringing water to crops"],["Surplus","More than is needed"],["Artificial selection","Breeding chosen plants or animals"]],
f:["Dogs were domesticated before any crop.","Around 40% of Earth’s land is used for agriculture.","The plough was one of the most important farming inventions."]},
coffee:{s:[
["The legend of Kaldi","According to legend, an Ethiopian goat herder named Kaldi noticed that his goats became lively after eating red berries from a certain shrub. Coffee plants do grow wild in the forests of Ethiopia. By the 1400s, coffee was being drunk in Sufi monasteries in Yemen to help people stay awake for night prayers."],
["Coffeehouses","Coffeehouses spread across the Ottoman Empire and reached Europe in the 1600s. In London they were nicknamed penny universities, because for the price of a cup, people could hear the latest news and debates. Lloyd’s of London and the London Stock Exchange both began in coffeehouses."],
["Arabica and robusta","Most coffee comes from two species. Arabica, about 60% of world production, grows at higher altitudes and has a smoother taste. Robusta is hardier, more bitter and has more caffeine. Two rarer species, liberica and excelsa, are grown in small quantities."],
["Roasting","Green coffee beans have little flavor. Roasting at about 200°C triggers chemical reactions that create hundreds of aroma compounds and turn the beans brown. Light roasts are more acidic and fruity; dark roasts taste more bitter and smoky. Ground coffee loses flavor quickly, so it is best brewed fresh."],
["Tea’s spread","Tea came from China, where it has been drunk for thousands of years. Buddhist monks carried it to Japan, where the tea ceremony developed. Dutch and British traders brought it to Europe in the 1600s. Britain later grew tea in India and Sri Lanka. Today tea is the most widely drunk beverage after water."],
["Caffeine","Caffeine is a natural stimulant found in coffee, tea, cacao and some other plants, where it helps protect them from insects. It makes people feel more alert by blocking a chemical in the brain that signals tiredness. Too much can cause anxiety, sleeplessness and a racing heart."]],
sp:["Coffee rust","Coffee leaf rust is a fungus that covers coffee leaves with orange powder and weakens the plants. It destroyed the coffee industry of Ceylon in the 1870s and 1880s, and in the early 2010s a new outbreak across Central America cut harvests and cost farmers their jobs. Breeders are developing resistant varieties, and growers plant shade trees and prune carefully to protect their crops. Climate change is expected to make rust and other diseases a bigger problem at some altitudes."],
g:[["Caffeine","A natural stimulant in coffee and tea"],["Roasting","Heating coffee beans to develop flavor"],["Arabica","The most widely grown coffee species"],["Liberica","A rarer coffee species with large, irregular beans"]],
f:["Ethiopia is the original home of the coffee plant.","Brazil produces about a third of the world’s coffee.","Kopi luwak is coffee made from beans that have passed through a civet, an expensive and controversial product."]},
wheat:{s:[
["Wild wheat","Wheat was one of the first crops domesticated, about 10,000 years ago in the Fertile Crescent. Early forms, einkorn and emmer, had grains tightly enclosed in husks. Over thousands of years, crossing with wild grasses produced bread wheat, which has larger grains that thresh freely."],
["Milling","To make flour, wheat grains are ground. Ancient people used grinding stones. Later, mills powered by water, wind and animals turned large millstones. Modern roller mills separate the bran and germ from the starchy endosperm to make white flour. Whole-wheat flour keeps all parts of the grain."],
["How bread rises","Bread rises when yeast, a tiny fungus, feeds on sugars in the dough and gives off carbon dioxide gas. Stretchy gluten, formed when flour proteins mix with water, traps the bubbles. Baking sets the structure. Flatbreads, such as chapati and tortillas, are made without much rising."],
["Pasta and noodles","Durum wheat, a hard wheat, is used to make pasta, such as spaghetti and macaroni. Noodles made from wheat flour are eaten across Asia, from Chinese lamian to Japanese udon and ramen. In Italy, pasta is made in hundreds of shapes."],
["Major producers","Wheat is grown on more land than any other crop. The biggest producers are China, India, Russia, the United States, France and Canada. Russia and Ukraine are major exporters, so the 2022 war affected world wheat prices. Many warm-climate countries, such as Egypt and Indonesia, grow little wheat and import it in large quantities."],
["Gluten problems","Some people cannot eat gluten. In celiac disease, gluten causes the immune system to damage the small intestine. About 1% of people worldwide have it. People with celiac disease must avoid wheat, barley and rye and eat gluten-free foods instead."]],
sp:["Sourdough","Sourdough is bread raised not with packaged yeast but with a starter, a mixture of flour and water in which wild yeasts and bacteria grow together. The bacteria make lactic acid, which gives the loaf its tang and helps it keep. Bakers feed a starter regularly, and some starters are kept alive for decades. Sourdough is one of the oldest forms of leavened bread and is still baked across Europe and North America."],
g:[["Gluten","A stretchy protein in wheat dough"],["Yeast","A fungus that makes dough rise"],["Endosperm","The starchy inner part of a grain"],["Durum","A hard wheat used for pasta"]],
f:["About 20% of the calories people eat worldwide come from wheat.","Whole-wheat flour keeps the bran and germ of the grain, while white flour is made from the starchy middle.","The oldest known bread crumbs, about 14,000 years old, were found in Jordan."]},
spice:{s:[
["What spices are","Spices are dried seeds, fruits, roots, bark or flower parts of plants used to flavor food. Pepper is a berry, cinnamon is bark, cloves are flower buds, ginger and turmeric are roots, and nutmeg is a seed. Herbs, by contrast, are usually fresh or dried leaves, such as basil or oregano."],
["Pepper, the king of spices","Black pepper comes from a vine native to southwestern India. It was so valuable that it was sometimes used as currency, and rent could be paid in peppercorns. In 408 CE, the Visigoth leader Alaric demanded thousands of pounds of pepper as part of a ransom from Rome."],
["Nutmeg and cloves","Nutmeg and mace come from a tree that grew naturally only on the tiny Banda Islands in eastern Indonesia, while cloves grew only on a few islands in the Moluccas. Their rarity made them enormously valuable in Europe, where a small bag could be worth a fortune."],
["Magellan’s voyage","Spain sent Ferdinand Magellan west in 1519 to find a route to the Spice Islands. He was killed in a battle in the Philippines in 1521, but his crew sailed on. The survivors reached the Moluccas, loaded cloves and returned to Spain in 1522 in a single ship, the Victoria, completing the first circumnavigation of the globe. The cloves paid for the voyage."],
["The Dutch monopoly","In the 1600s the Dutch East India Company seized control of the spice trade. In 1621 it conquered the Banda Islands, killing or enslaving most of the inhabitants, to control nutmeg. In 1667 the Dutch traded the island of Run to England in exchange for Manhattan, then called New Amsterdam."],
["Spices in everyday cooking","Every cuisine has its signature spices: ginger, garlic and turmeric in India, star anise and Sichuan pepper in China, saffron in Spain and Iran, and cumin and coriander across the Middle East and North Africa. Spice blends such as garam masala, ras el hanout and five-spice powder combine several spices into a single flavor."]],
sp:["Saffron","Saffron, the world’s most expensive spice by weight, comes from the threadlike red stigmas of the saffron crocus flower. Each flower has only three, and they must be picked by hand. It takes about 150,000 flowers to make a kilogram of saffron. It gives a golden color and distinctive flavor to dishes such as Spanish paella, Indian biryani and Persian rice. Iran grows about 90% of the world’s saffron."],
g:[["Spice","A dried plant part used for flavor"],["Monopoly","Complete control of a trade by one group"],["Circumnavigation","Sailing all the way around the world"],["Moluccas","The Spice Islands of eastern Indonesia"]],
f:["Vanilla is the second most expensive spice after saffron.","Chili’s heat comes from a chemical called capsaicin.","Turmeric is used in curries and gives them their yellow color."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-food2.js */
// In-depth material (Food and Agriculture, part 2): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
choco:{s:[
["The cacao tree","Chocolate comes from the seeds of the cacao tree, Theobroma cacao, which grows in hot, rainy places within about 20 degrees of the equator. Its flowers grow directly on the trunk and are pollinated by tiny flies called midges. The pods, the size of a small melon, each hold 20 to 60 beans in sweet white pulp."],
["Olmecs and Maya","People in Mesoamerica used cacao more than 3,000 years ago; traces have been found in Olmec pots. The Maya drank cacao as a frothy, bitter drink, often with chili, at feasts and ceremonies. Cacao beans were so valuable to the Aztecs that they were used as money; a turkey might cost 100 beans."],
["Sweet chocolate","Spanish explorers brought cacao to Spain in the 1500s, where sugar was added. Chocolate drinks became fashionable among European nobility. In 1828 Dutch chemist Coenraad van Houten invented a press that removed cocoa butter, making cocoa powder. In 1847 the British firm Fry’s made the first solid chocolate bar."],
["Milk chocolate","In 1875 Swiss chocolatier Daniel Peter, working with his neighbor Henri Nestlé’s condensed milk, created milk chocolate. In 1879 Rodolphe Lindt invented conching, long mixing that makes chocolate smooth. Switzerland became famous for chocolate, and its people eat among the most chocolate per person in the world."],
["Ways of drinking chocolate","Before it was a sweet bar, chocolate was a drink. The Maya and Aztecs drank it bitter and frothy, flavored with chili and vanilla. In Europe it was sweetened and served hot, and in Spain and Mexico people still dunk churros in thick hot chocolate. Many cultures still make a similar drink from tablets of ground cacao dissolved in hot water or milk."],
["Child labor and fair trade","About two-thirds of the world’s cacao comes from West Africa, mainly Côte d’Ivoire and Ghana, where many farmers are poor. Hundreds of thousands of children work on cacao farms, some in dangerous conditions. Fair-trade and sustainability programs aim to pay farmers better and end child labor."]],
sp:["Bean-to-bar chocolate","A newer generation of small makers buys cacao directly from farmers and turns it into chocolate themselves, a style called bean-to-bar. Because the origin appears on the wrapper, buyers can taste how soil, climate and fermentation change the flavor, as with wine or coffee. Fine-chocolate competitions such as the International Chocolate Awards have helped growers from Ecuador and Madagascar to Vietnam and the Philippines earn higher prices."],
g:[["Cacao","The tree and beans from which chocolate is made"],["Cocoa butter","The natural fat of cacao beans"],["Conching","Long mixing that smooths chocolate"],["Bean-to-bar","Chocolate made by one maker from raw cacao beans to finished bar"]],
f:["White chocolate contains cocoa butter but no cocoa solids.","Chocolate is toxic to dogs because of a chemical called theobromine.","Theobroma means food of the gods in Greek."]},
fish:{s:[
["Fish and protein","Fish and seafood provide about 15% of the animal protein eaten worldwide and much more in island and coastal nations. Fish is eaten fresh, dried, smoked, salted, fermented, as in fish sauce, or raw, as in Japanese sashimi, Peruvian ceviche and Pacific island poke."],
["The Coral Triangle","The Coral Triangle, an area of ocean shared by Indonesia, Malaysia, the Philippines, Papua New Guinea, Timor-Leste and the Solomon Islands, has the greatest variety of marine life on Earth, including more than 2,000 species of reef fish. Healthy reefs are nurseries for many fish."],
["Municipal and commercial fishers","Many countries separate small-scale fishing, by small boats close to shore, from industrial fishing, by large vessels farther out. Small-scale fishers make up about 90 percent of the world’s fishers, and many are among the poorest people in their countries. Fishing communities depend on healthy seas for food and income."],
["Tuna","Tuna are among the most valuable fish. Japan eats much of the world’s bluefin tuna as sashimi and sushi, and a single fish has sold for more than 3 million US dollars at Tokyo’s New Year auction. Ports such as Bangkok, General Santos in the Philippines and the Seychelles can tuna for export. Tuna travel vast distances across oceans, so their protection requires international cooperation."],
["Milkfish and tilapia","Aquaculture, fish farming, provides more than half the fish people eat. China has farmed carp in ponds for thousands of years. Tilapia, native to Africa, is now raised worldwide, milkfish is farmed in brackish ponds across Southeast Asia, Norway and Chile farm salmon in sea cages, and Vietnam is a leading producer of farmed catfish and shrimp."],
["Closed seasons","To let fish breed, many governments declare closed fishing seasons or set catch limits. The European Union and Norway agree quotas for North Sea fish every year, Peru pauses anchovy fishing during spawning, and seasonal bans protect sardines in parts of Southeast Asia. Fishing for some species, such as whale sharks and manta rays, is banned entirely in many countries."]],
sp:["Apo Island","Apo Island, off the island of Negros in the Philippines, became a famous example of community-run marine conservation. In 1982, with guidance from scientists led by Angel Alcala, the islanders set aside part of their reef as a no-take sanctuary. Fish populations inside grew and spilled over into nearby fishing grounds, increasing catches. Its success inspired community marine reserves in many countries."],
g:[["Aquaculture","Farming fish and other water life"],["Overfishing","Catching fish faster than they can reproduce"],["Bycatch","Animals caught by accident"],["Closed season","A period when fishing is banned"]],
f:["China produces more farmed fish than the rest of the world combined.","Small-scale fishers make up about 90 percent of the world’s fishers.","Dynamite fishing is illegal and destroys coral reefs."]},
potato:{s:[
["Wild potatoes","Wild potatoes grow from the southwestern United States to southern Chile. People in the Andes, near Lake Titicaca, domesticated the potato around 7,000 to 10,000 years ago. Early wild potatoes were bitter and sometimes toxic, and farmers selected milder plants over thousands of years."],
["Chuño","Andean peoples invented a way to preserve potatoes called chuño. They left potatoes out to freeze on frosty nights, then trampled them to squeeze out water, and dried them in the sun. Chuño can be stored for years. It helped feed the Inca Empire and its armies."],
["How potatoes grow","The part of the potato we eat is a tuber, a swollen underground stem that stores food for the plant. Farmers usually plant pieces of tubers, called seed potatoes, rather than seeds. Each plant can produce several tubers. Potatoes grow best in cool weather, which is why in the tropics they are grown mainly in the highlands."],
["Feeding Europe","Potatoes were brought to Europe by the Spanish in the 1500s. At first many people were suspicious of them. In France, Antoine-Augustin Parmentier promoted potatoes in the 1700s. By the 1800s, potatoes were a staple across Europe, helping populations grow, since they produce much food on little land."],
["Potato farming in the Andes","In the Andes, farmers grow potatoes on terraces and steep slopes at altitudes of up to about 4,000 m, planting dozens of traditional varieties side by side. The Quechua and Aymara peoples developed ways of freeze-drying potatoes into chuño, which keeps for years. These traditions protect the crop’s diversity and give Peru and Bolivia thousands of local varieties."],
["Potatoes everywhere","Potatoes are the world’s third most important food crop after rice and wheat for human consumption. China and India are the biggest producers. Potatoes are eaten as fries, chips, mashed potatoes and in stews. Around the world they appear in dishes such as the Spanish tortilla, Indian aloo gobi and Irish stew."]],
sp:["The International Potato Center","The International Potato Center, known by its Spanish initials CIP, was founded in Lima, Peru, in 1971. It keeps a gene bank with thousands of varieties of potato, sweet potato and Andean roots, protecting their diversity. Peru alone has about 3,000 potato varieties. CIP has regional offices in Africa, Asia and Latin America, where it works on sweet potatoes and improved potatoes for highland farmers."],
g:[["Tuber","A swollen underground stem that stores food"],["Seed potato","A potato piece used for planting"],["Blight","A disease that kills plants quickly"],["Chuño","Freeze-dried potato from the Andes"]],
f:["The United Nations declared 2008 the International Year of the Potato.","Potatoes were the first vegetable grown in space, in 1995.","Sweet potatoes, or kamote, are not closely related to potatoes."]},
sugar:{s:[
["Sugarcane","Sugarcane is a tall tropical grass, growing 3 to 6 meters high, with thick stalks full of sweet juice. It was first domesticated in New Guinea about 10,000 years ago and later spread to Southeast Asia and India. People chewed the stalks long before they learned to make crystals."],
["India’s invention","People in India discovered how to boil cane juice and make solid sugar crystals about 2,000 years ago or more. The word sugar comes from the Sanskrit sharkara. Persians and Arabs spread sugarcane growing to the Middle East and the Mediterranean."],
["Plantations and slavery","After Columbus took sugarcane to the Caribbean in 1493, Europeans set up huge sugar plantations in the Americas worked by enslaved Africans. Millions were forced across the Atlantic. Work was brutal, especially at harvest time. Sugar profits enriched European empires."],
["Sugar in the global economy","Sugarcane has been grown for thousands of years, first in New Guinea and then across tropical Asia. By the 1800s sugar had become one of the world’s most traded goods, shipped from the Caribbean, Brazil, Java and other colonies to Europe and North America. Today Brazil and India are the leading producers, and sugar is traded on world markets."],
["Too much sugar","Eating too much sugar increases the risk of tooth decay, obesity, type 2 diabetes and heart disease. The World Health Organization recommends that free sugars make up less than 10% of daily energy, ideally less than 5%. Sugary drinks are a major source. Many countries, including Mexico and the United Kingdom, now tax sugary drinks."],
["Sugar today","Brazil is the world’s largest sugar producer, followed by India and Thailand. About 80% of sugar comes from cane and the rest from sugar beets grown in cooler places. Sugarcane is also made into ethanol fuel and molasses, and its fibrous waste, bagasse, is burned to make electricity."]],
sp:["Sugar and the slave trade","From the 1500s European colonists in Brazil and the Caribbean set up sugar plantations that depended on millions of enslaved Africans. Sugar was the main reason for the Atlantic slave trade, and the profits helped to finance European industry and banking. Slavery was abolished in the British empire in 1833, in French colonies in 1848 and in Brazil in 1888, but sugar-growing regions long remained unequal, and these issues are still debated today."],
g:[["Sugarcane","A tall grass grown for its sweet juice"],["Hacienda","A large farm estate"],["Molasses","A thick syrup left after sugar is made"],["Bagasse","The fiber left after cane is crushed"]],
f:["Brazil is the world’s largest producer of sugar.","Muscovado is unrefined brown sugar that keeps some of its molasses.","The MassKara Festival in Bacolod began in 1980 to lift spirits during hard times in the sugar industry."]},
maize:{s:[
["Ancient Mexico","Maize was domesticated from a wild grass called teosinte in southern Mexico about 9,000 years ago. Teosinte had tiny ears with just a few hard kernels. Over thousands of years, farmers selected plants with bigger ears and softer kernels, creating maize. It is one of the greatest achievements of plant breeding."],
["The people of maize","Maize was sacred to many peoples of the Americas. In the Popol Vuh, the sacred book of the Maya K’iche’, the gods created humans from maize dough. The Aztecs worshiped maize gods. Native peoples in North America grew maize with beans and squash, the three sisters."],
["Kinds of corn","There are many kinds of maize: sweet corn eaten fresh, flint corn with hard kernels, dent corn used for animal feed and processing, flour corn and popcorn. Kernels can be yellow, white, red, blue, purple or speckled. Popcorn pops because water inside the kernel turns to steam and bursts the hard shell."],
["More than food","Most maize is not eaten directly by people. Much goes to feed cattle, pigs and chickens. It is processed into cornstarch, corn syrup, oil and ethanol fuel, and used to make plastics, glue and paper. The United States is the world’s largest producer, followed by China and Brazil."],
["Maize in the Americas","Maize was domesticated in southern Mexico and became the foundation of the Maya, Aztec and Inca civilizations. Native farmers developed hundreds of varieties in many colors. Planting maize together with beans and squash, a method known as the Three Sisters in North America, helps feed the soil and the people."],
["Corn foods","Corn is eaten in many forms around the world: tortillas and tamales in Mexico, polenta in Italy, grits in the American South, cornbread, popcorn and corn on the cob. It is also made into cornstarch, corn oil and corn syrup."]],
sp:["Pellagra and lime","When maize spread to Europe and the American South, many poor people who ate mainly corn developed a disease called pellagra, causing rashes, diarrhea and confusion. It is caused by a lack of niacin, a B vitamin. Native Americans had avoided it for thousands of years by soaking corn in limewater, nixtamalization, which releases the niacin. Scientists did not understand the cause until the 20th century."],
g:[["Teosinte","The wild grass ancestor of maize"],["Kernel","A single seed of corn"],["Ear","The part of the plant holding kernels"],["Feed","Food given to farm animals"]],
f:["An ear of corn usually has an even number of rows.","Maize is the world’s most-produced grain by weight.","Corn silk is made of strands that each lead to one kernel."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-music1.js */
// In-depth material (Music, part 1): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
beatles:{s:[
["Liverpool","The Beatles came from Liverpool, a busy port city in northwest England. American sailors brought rock and roll and rhythm and blues records into the city. In 1957 John Lennon’s band, the Quarrymen, met Paul McCartney at a church fete; George Harrison joined soon after. Ringo Starr replaced drummer Pete Best in 1962."],
["Brian Epstein and George Martin","Brian Epstein, who ran a record shop in Liverpool, became their manager in 1962, smartening their image with matching suits. Producer George Martin signed them to EMI’s Parlophone label. Their first single, Love Me Do, came out in October 1962, followed by Please Please Me, their first big hit."],
["Conquering America","On 9 February 1964, the Beatles appeared on The Ed Sullivan Show in the United States, watched by about 73 million people, a record at the time. In April 1964 they held the top five places on the American singles chart at once. This British Invasion opened the door for other British bands."],
["Changing sounds","The Beatles’ music changed rapidly. Rubber Soul and Revolver in 1965 and 1966 introduced new sounds, including the Indian sitar played by George Harrison. In 1967 Sgt. Pepper’s Lonely Hearts Club Band, a colorful concept album, was hailed as a masterpiece. Later albums included the White Album and Abbey Road."],
["Breaking up","After Epstein died in 1967, the band struggled with business problems and personal differences. Their last public performance was an unannounced concert on the rooftop of their Apple Corps offices in London in January 1969. McCartney announced his departure in April 1970. Each member then had a solo career."],
["After the Beatles","John Lennon released Imagine in 1971 and campaigned for peace. He was shot dead in New York in December 1980. George Harrison organized the Concert for Bangladesh in 1971, an early charity concert, and died in 2001. In 2023, using AI tools to separate Lennon’s voice from an old demo, the surviving members released a final song, Now and Then."]],
sp:["Candlestick Park, 1966","On 29 August 1966, the Beatles played Candlestick Park, a baseball stadium in San Francisco, before about 25,000 fans. Sensing it might be the end, Paul McCartney asked their press officer to record the show on a small tape recorder. They played 11 songs in about half an hour and never gave another paying concert, choosing instead to create music in the studio."],
g:[["British Invasion","The wave of British bands popular in the US in the 1960s"],["Concept album","An album built around a theme"],["Single","A song released on its own"],["Producer","A person who oversees a recording"]],
f:["The Beatles are the best-selling music act of all time.","Yesterday is one of the most recorded songs in history.","Abbey Road Studios is named after the street the Beatles made famous."]},
hiphop:{s:[
["The Bronx","Hip hop was born in the Bronx, New York City, in the 1970s, among African American, Caribbean and Latino young people. The area was poor, with many buildings abandoned or burned. At block parties, young people created new forms of music, dance and art that cost little to make."],
["Kool Herc","DJ Kool Herc, who moved to the Bronx from Jamaica, is often called the father of hip hop. At a party on 11 August 1973 at 1520 Sedgwick Avenue, he played the instrumental breaks of funk records, the parts people loved to dance to, extending them by switching between two copies of the same record. This date is celebrated as hip hop’s birthday."],
["MCs and rapping","Masters of ceremonies, or MCs, began speaking rhythmically over the beats, hyping up the crowd. This developed into rapping, rhyming lyrics delivered to a beat. In 1979 the Sugarhill Gang’s Rapper’s Delight became the first hip hop hit. Rappers use wordplay, storytelling and freestyle, improvised rhymes."],
["Breaking and graffiti","Breakdancing, or breaking, developed as dancers, called b-boys and b-girls, performed acrobatic moves during the breaks. Spinning on heads and backs and freezing in poses became signature moves. Graffiti artists painted colorful names and murals on subway trains and walls, spreading the culture across the city."],
["Golden age and beyond","The late 1980s and early 1990s are often called hip hop’s golden age, with artists such as Run-DMC, Public Enemy, LL Cool J and A Tribe Called Quest. Later stars included Tupac Shakur, the Notorious B.I.G., Jay-Z, Eminem, Kanye West and Kendrick Lamar, who won the Pulitzer Prize for Music in 2018."],
["Battle rap","In battle rap, two rappers face off in front of a crowd, trading verses written to insult and outwit each other. Leagues such as King of the Dot in Canada, Don’t Flop in the United Kingdom, URL in the United States and FlipTop in the Philippines film their battles, and the videos draw hundreds of millions of views online."]],
sp:["The 8 Mile effect","The 2002 film 8 Mile, starring the rapper Eminem as a young man in Detroit who finds his voice in rap battles, brought battle rap to a worldwide audience. Its song Lose Yourself became the first rap song to win the Oscar for Best Original Song. Many rappers around the world say the film inspired them to start battling."],
g:[["MC","Master of ceremonies, a rapper"],["DJ","A disc jockey who plays and mixes records"],["Breakbeat","An instrumental drum section of a song"],["Freestyle","Improvised rapping"]],
f:["Hip hop became the most consumed music genre in the United States in 2017.","The turntable became a musical instrument in hip hop.","Hip hop celebrated its 50th anniversary in 2023."]},
guitar:{s:[
["Ancestors of the guitar","Stringed instruments plucked with the fingers have existed for thousands of years, such as the oud of the Middle East, the lute of medieval Europe and the vihuela of Spain. The modern six-string guitar developed in Spain in the 1700s and 1800s. Instrument maker Antonio de Torres shaped the classical guitar in the 1850s."],
["Acoustic guitars","An acoustic guitar has a hollow wooden body that makes the strings’ vibrations louder. Classical guitars use soft nylon strings and are played with the fingers. Steel-string acoustic guitars, louder and brighter, are used in folk, country and pop. A round sound hole lets the sound out."],
["The electric guitar","Electric guitars use magnetic pickups to turn string vibrations into an electrical signal, which an amplifier makes loud. The Rickenbacker frying pan of 1932 was an early electric guitar. Leo Fender’s Telecaster, from 1950, and Stratocaster, from 1954, and Gibson’s Les Paul, from 1952, became classic designs."],
["Chords and frets","A guitar’s neck is divided by metal strips called frets. Pressing a string behind a fret shortens its vibrating length and raises the note. Playing several strings together makes a chord. Beginners often learn chords such as C, G, D, A and E minor and can play thousands of songs with just a few."],
["Guitar legends","Great guitarists include Andrés Segovia, who made the classical guitar a concert instrument; Django Reinhardt, a jazz player who used only two fingers on his fretting hand after a fire; Jimi Hendrix, who transformed rock; and B. B. King, who made his guitar Lucille sing the blues."],
["The guitar around the world","The guitar has been adapted by countless cultures. Spain gave the world flamenco, Brazil bossa nova, and West Africa palm-wine music and highlife. Hawaiian musicians invented slack-key tuning and the steel guitar, Mexican mariachi bands use the large guitarrón, and Filipino serenaders sing harana to a guitar."]],
sp:["Antonio de Torres","Antonio de Torres Jurado, a Spanish carpenter turned guitar maker, transformed the guitar in the 1850s and 1860s. He made the body larger and thinner, and placed fan-shaped wooden struts under the top so it could vibrate freely, giving a louder, richer sound. To prove that the top mattered most, he even built a guitar with a body of papier-mâché. Nearly all classical guitars today follow his design."],
g:[["Fret","A metal strip on a guitar neck"],["Chord","Several notes played together"],["Pickup","A device turning string vibrations into signals"],["Amplifier","A device that makes sound louder"]],
f:["A standard guitar has six strings tuned E, A, D, G, B, E.","The ukulele developed in Hawaii from Portuguese instruments.","Bass guitars usually have four strings."]},
mozart:{s:[
["Leopold Mozart","Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart was born on 27 January 1756 in Salzburg, now in Austria. His father, Leopold, was a violinist, composer and teacher who wrote a famous violin method. Recognizing his son’s talent, Leopold devoted himself to training Wolfgang and his gifted older sister, Maria Anna, called Nannerl."],
["Touring Europe","From the age of six, Mozart toured Europe with his family for years, performing for kings and queens in Munich, Vienna, Paris and London. He played blindfolded and improvised music on themes suggested by the audience. In London he met Johann Christian Bach, son of J. S. Bach, who influenced him."],
["Rome and the Miserere","At 14, in Rome, Mozart heard Gregorio Allegri’s Miserere, a choral work the Vatican kept secret and allowed to be sung only in the Sistine Chapel. According to the family’s letters, he wrote out the whole piece from memory after hearing it, an astonishing feat. The pope later made him a knight."],
["Freedom in Vienna","Unhappy working for the Archbishop of Salzburg, Mozart moved to Vienna in 1781 to work as a freelance musician, unusual at the time. He married Constanze Weber in 1782. He earned money from concerts, teaching and publishing but spent freely and was often in debt."],
["Operas","Mozart wrote some of the greatest operas ever composed. The Marriage of Figaro, 1786, and Don Giovanni, 1787, were written with librettist Lorenzo Da Ponte. The Magic Flute, 1791, a German singspiel with spoken dialogue, includes the famously high Queen of the Night aria."],
["Requiem","In 1791 an anonymous messenger commissioned a Requiem, a Mass for the dead. Mozart, ill, worked on it until his death on 5 December 1791, at 35. It was completed by his student Franz Xaver Süssmayr. Legends of poisoning by rival composer Antonio Salieri, popularized by the film Amadeus, are not supported by evidence."]],
sp:["The Köchel catalogue","Mozart wrote more than 600 works in his short life, including about 41 symphonies, 27 piano concertos, operas, chamber music and Masses. In 1862 Ludwig von Köchel, an Austrian botanist and music lover, published a catalogue listing them in order of composition. Each piece is still known by its K number: Eine kleine Nachtmusik is K. 525, and the Requiem is K. 626. In 2024 a newly discovered early piece was added to the catalogue."],
g:[["Prodigy","A child with exceptional talent"],["Opera","A drama set to music"],["Symphony","A long piece for orchestra"],["Libretto","The words of an opera"]],
f:["Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Star uses a French tune Mozart wrote variations on.","Mozart had a pet starling that could sing one of his tunes.","Salzburg holds a famous music festival each summer."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-music2.js */
// In-depth material (Music, part 2): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
opm:{s:[
["Kundiman","The kundiman is a Filipino art song of love, longing and devotion, usually in a slow, flowing triple time. It developed in the 1800s from folk songs. During the American period, kundiman songs such as Bayan Ko and Jocelynang Baliwag were also sung as hidden expressions of love for the country. Composers include Francisco Santiago and Nicanor Abelardo."],
["Sarswela and the American era","In the late 1800s and early 1900s, the sarswela, a musical play with songs and spoken dialogue, was hugely popular in Tagalog and other languages. During the American period, jazz, swing and Broadway songs arrived, and Filipino musicians became famous across Asia as skilled band players in hotels and clubs."],
["Filipino musicians abroad","From the 1900s onward, Filipino bands entertained audiences in Hong Kong, Shanghai, Tokyo, Singapore and later on cruise ships and in hotels worldwide. Filipino musicians were so common in Asian clubs that the phrase Filipino band became a byword for live entertainment. Many still perform abroad today."],
["The birth of the term OPM","In the 1970s, the phrase Original Pilipino Music came into use to describe popular songs written by Filipinos. The Metro Manila Popular Music Festival, launched in 1977, produced hits such as Ryan Cayabyab’s Kay Ganda ng Ating Musika and Freddie Aguilar’s Anak, which became a worldwide hit and was translated into dozens of languages."],
["The 1990s band boom","In the 1990s, bands such as the Eraserheads, Rivermaya, Parokya ni Edgar, Siakol and True Faith filled stadiums and radio playlists. The Eraserheads, often called the Philippine Beatles, wrote catchy, witty songs about everyday Filipino life, such as Ang Huling El Bimbo and Pare Ko. Their 2022 reunion concert drew tens of thousands."],
["P-pop and streaming","In the 2010s and 2020s, streaming and social media gave OPM new life. Indie-folk bands such as Ben&Ben and singers such as Moira Dela Torre and Zack Tabudlo topped charts. P-pop groups such as SB19 and BINI, inspired partly by K-pop, built huge fan bases; SB19 was the first Filipino act nominated at the Billboard Music Awards in 2021."]],
sp:["Anak","Anak, meaning child, was written and sung by Freddie Aguilar and entered in the 1978 Metro Manila Popular Music Festival. It tells of a parent’s love for a child who goes astray. Though it did not win first prize, it became the best-known Filipino song in the world, reaching the charts in many Asian and European countries. It has been recorded in more than 20 languages and has sold millions of copies."],
g:[["Kundiman","A Filipino love song in slow triple time"],["Sarswela","A musical play with songs and dialogue"],["OPM","Original Pilipino Music"],["P-pop","Filipino pop music, especially idol groups"]],
f:["The Awit Awards honor the best in Filipino recorded music.","Lea Salonga’s singing voice is heard as Jasmine in Disney’s Aladdin.","Videoke machines were popularized in the Philippines in the 1990s."]},
blues:{s:[
["Work songs and spirituals","The blues grew from the music of African Americans in the southern United States after the end of slavery in 1865. Its roots include West African music, work songs and field hollers sung while laboring, call-and-response singing and spirituals, religious songs. These traditions shaped the blues’ feeling and form."],
["The twelve-bar blues","Many blues songs use a pattern called the twelve-bar blues, with three chords repeated in a set order over 12 bars. The lyrics often follow an AAB pattern: a line is sung, repeated, and then answered by a different line. This simple structure lets musicians improvise freely."],
["W. C. Handy","W. C. Handy, a bandleader from Alabama, heard a man in a Mississippi train station around 1903 playing slide guitar with a knife and singing about a railroad crossing. Inspired, he wrote and published blues songs such as Memphis Blues and St. Louis Blues in 1912 and 1914, helping to spread the blues nationwide. He is called the father of the blues."],
["Robert Johnson","Robert Johnson, a Mississippi Delta guitarist, recorded only 29 songs in 1936 and 1937 before he died at 27 in 1938. Legend says he sold his soul to the devil at a crossroads in exchange for his skill. His songs, such as Cross Road Blues and Sweet Home Chicago, deeply influenced later rock musicians."],
["Electric blues","During the Great Migration, millions of Black southerners moved to northern cities such as Chicago. Musicians like Muddy Waters and Howlin’ Wolf plugged in electric guitars and played louder blues for noisy city clubs. Chess Records in Chicago recorded many of them, laying the groundwork for rock and roll."],
["Blues and rock","In the 1960s, British bands such as the Rolling Stones, named after a Muddy Waters song, the Animals and Led Zeppelin studied American blues records and brought the music to new audiences. Their success led many young people to discover older blues musicians, some of whom toured Europe for the first time."]],
sp:["B. B. King","Riley B. King, born in Mississippi in 1925, became known as B. B., for Blues Boy, while working as a radio DJ in Memphis. He was famous for his singing guitar style, bending notes and using vibrato. He named his guitars Lucille after a fire at a dance hall in 1949, started by two men fighting over a woman named Lucille; he had run back into the burning building to save his guitar. He played up to 300 concerts a year into his eighties."],
g:[["Blue note","A slightly flattened note giving the blues its sound"],["Twelve-bar blues","A 12-bar chord pattern used in blues"],["Slide guitar","Playing by sliding a tube or bottleneck on the strings"],["Call and response","A phrase answered by another"]],
f:["The harmonica is often called the blues harp.","Muddy Waters’ real name was McKinley Morganfield.","Memphis has a street, Beale Street, famous for blues clubs."]},
electro:{s:[
["The theremin","One of the first electronic instruments was the theremin, invented by Russian scientist Léon Theremin in about 1920. It is played without being touched: the player moves their hands near two antennas to change pitch and volume. Its eerie wobbling sound was used in science-fiction film soundtracks."],
["Musique concrète and tape","In the late 1940s, French composer Pierre Schaeffer created musique concrète, music made from recorded sounds such as trains and voices, cut and rearranged on tape. In Germany, Karlheinz Stockhausen created music from electronic tones. These experiments showed that any sound could become music."],
["The Moog synthesizer","In 1964 American engineer Robert Moog built one of the first commercial synthesizers. Wendy Carlos’s 1968 album Switched-On Bach, playing Bach’s music on a Moog, became a surprise best seller. Smaller, cheaper synthesizers soon appeared in rock and pop bands."],
["Kraftwerk and synth-pop","The German group Kraftwerk, formed in 1970, made music almost entirely with electronic instruments and drum machines, with robotic vocals and themes of technology, such as Autobahn and The Robots. They inspired synth-pop bands of the 1980s such as Depeche Mode and Pet Shop Boys, as well as hip hop and techno."],
["House and techno","In the 1980s, DJs in Chicago created house music, with a steady four-on-the-floor beat, named after the club The Warehouse. In Detroit, musicians such as Juan Atkins, Derrick May and Kevin Saunderson created techno, a more futuristic sound. Both spread to Europe and fueled the rave scene."],
["Making music on computers","Today, musicians make electronic music on laptops using software called digital audio workstations, such as Ableton Live and FL Studio. These programs include virtual instruments, samples and effects. Producers can create entire songs in a bedroom, and many hits are made this way."]],
sp:["The Roland TR-808","The Roland TR-808 drum machine, made in Japan from 1980 to 1983, was a commercial failure at first because its drums did not sound realistic. But its deep, booming bass drum, snappy snare and crisp hi-hats became the sound of hip hop, electro and later trap music. Marvin Gaye’s Sexual Healing and countless rap songs used it. Today the 808 is one of the most famous instruments in popular music, and its name is a slang term for heavy bass."],
g:[["Synthesizer","An electronic instrument that creates sounds"],["Drum machine","A device that plays programmed beats"],["Sample","A recorded sound reused in music"],["DJ","A person who plays and mixes recorded music"]],
f:["MIDI, a standard for connecting electronic instruments, was introduced in 1983.","Daft Punk, a French duo, performed in robot helmets.","Electronic dance music festivals draw hundreds of thousands of fans."]},
reggae:{s:[
["Mento","Before ska, Jamaica had mento, a folk music played on banjos, guitars, hand drums and a large thumb piano called the rumba box. Mento songs were often humorous and told stories. It was popular in the 1940s and 1950s and is sometimes confused with Trinidadian calypso."],
["Rastafari","Many reggae musicians followed Rastafari, a religious movement that began in Jamaica in the 1930s. Rastafarians honor Haile Selassie I, emperor of Ethiopia, and see Africa as a promised homeland. Many wear dreadlocks and use the colors red, gold and green. Reggae songs often express Rastafarian ideas."],
["Bob Marley and the Wailers","Bob Marley formed the Wailers with Peter Tosh and Bunny Wailer in 1963. Signing with Island Records in 1972, they reached audiences worldwide with albums such as Catch a Fire. Marley’s songs, including One Love, No Woman, No Cry, Redemption Song and Get Up, Stand Up, spoke of love, freedom and justice."],
["The One Love Peace Concert","In the 1970s, Jamaica suffered violent political conflict. In 1976 Marley was shot and wounded at his home before a concert. In April 1978, at the One Love Peace Concert in Kingston, he brought rival political leaders Michael Manley and Edward Seaga onto the stage and had them clasp hands. Marley died of cancer in 1981, aged 36."],
["Dancehall","In the late 1970s and 1980s, dancehall developed from reggae, with DJs chanting rhythmic vocals, called toasting, over digital rhythms. It became Jamaica’s most popular music, with stars such as Yellowman, Shabba Ranks, Sean Paul and Shaggy. Dancehall influenced reggaeton and modern pop."],
["Reggae in Africa","Reggae found a second home in Africa. Bob Marley played at Zimbabwe’s independence celebrations in 1980. Alpha Blondy of Côte d’Ivoire sang in French, English and Dioula, and Lucky Dube of South Africa became the continent’s best-selling reggae star, singing against apartheid. Reggae festivals are held from Ghana to Kenya."]],
sp:["UNESCO heritage","In 2018 UNESCO added reggae music of Jamaica to its list of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity. It praised reggae’s role in raising awareness of injustice, resistance, love and humanity, and its spread around the world. Jamaica celebrates Reggae Month every February, which includes Bob Marley’s birthday on 6 February. The Bob Marley Museum in Kingston is in his former home."],
g:[["Ska","Fast Jamaican music with offbeat rhythms"],["Dub","A remix style stressing bass and echo"],["Toasting","Rhythmic talking over music"],["Riddim","A Jamaican word for a rhythm track"]],
f:["Reggae’s offbeat guitar chop is called the skank.","Bob Marley’s Legend is one of the best-selling reggae albums of all time.","Jamaica’s sound system culture influenced hip hop."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-sport1.js */
// In-depth material (Sports, part 1): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
olymp:{s:[
["Pierre de Coubertin","French educator Baron Pierre de Coubertin believed sport could build character and bring nations together in peace. He founded the International Olympic Committee in 1894, and the first modern Olympic Games were held in Athens in 1896, with about 240 athletes from 14 countries, all men."],
["Women at the Games","Women first competed at the 1900 Paris Olympics, in sports such as tennis, golf and sailing. British tennis player Charlotte Cooper became the first female Olympic champion. Women’s participation grew slowly. At Paris 2024, for the first time, equal numbers of places were given to women and men."],
["The torch relay","The Olympic flame is lit at Olympia, Greece, using sunlight focused by a curved mirror, then carried by relay to the host city. The torch relay was first held for the 1936 Berlin Games. The flame burns in a cauldron throughout the Games and is put out at the closing ceremony."],
["The Paralympics","The Paralympic Games, for athletes with disabilities, grew from a competition for injured Second World War veterans organized by Dr. Ludwig Guttmann at Stoke Mandeville in England in 1948. The first Paralympics were held in Rome in 1960."],
["Boycotts and politics","The Olympics have often been affected by politics. Many countries boycotted the 1980 Moscow Games over the Soviet invasion of Afghanistan, and the Soviet Union and allies boycotted Los Angeles in 1984. At the 1972 Munich Games, terrorists killed 11 Israeli team members."],
["New sports","The Olympic program changes over time. Recent additions include surfing, skateboarding, sport climbing and 3x3 basketball at Tokyo 2020, and breaking, or breakdancing, at Paris 2024. Some old events, such as tug of war, which was an Olympic sport from 1900 to 1920, have been dropped."]],
sp:["Jesse Owens in Berlin","At the 1936 Berlin Games, which Adolf Hitler hoped would show Nazi superiority, the African American sprinter Jesse Owens won four gold medals, in the 100 m, 200 m, long jump and 4×100 m relay. His victories were a powerful rebuke to Nazi racial theories. Back in the United States he still faced segregation and discrimination. Owens’s story remains one of the best known in the history of sport."],
g:[["IOC","International Olympic Committee"],["Paralympics","Games for athletes with disabilities"],["Boycott","Refusing to take part as a protest"],["Torch relay","Carrying the Olympic flame to the host city"]],
f:["Gold medals are mostly silver, plated with about 6 grams of gold.","The first modern Olympics, in 1896, had athletes from only about 14 countries.","The Olympic motto is Faster, Higher, Stronger – Together."]},
soccer:{s:[
["The laws of the game","In 1863 representatives of English clubs met in a London tavern and formed the Football Association, writing rules that banned carrying the ball with the hands. This separated association football, or soccer, from rugby. The International Football Association Board still oversees the laws today."],
["FIFA","The Fédération Internationale de Football Association, FIFA, was founded in Paris in 1904. It has 211 member associations, more members than the United Nations. FIFA organizes the World Cup and sets international rules."],
["The first World Cup","The first FIFA World Cup was held in Uruguay in 1930, with 13 teams. Uruguay beat Argentina 4–2 in the final in Montevideo. Brazil has won the most World Cups, five. The 2022 tournament in Qatar, won by Argentina led by Lionel Messi, had one of the most exciting finals ever."],
["Positions","Each team has 11 players. The goalkeeper is the only one who may use hands, inside the penalty area. Defenders guard their goal, midfielders link defense and attack, and forwards try to score. Teams use formations, such as 4-4-2 or 4-3-3, describing how players are arranged."],
["Club football","Club competitions draw huge followings. Top leagues include England’s Premier League, Spain’s La Liga, Germany’s Bundesliga and Italy’s Serie A. The UEFA Champions League is the most prestigious club competition. Clubs such as Real Madrid, Barcelona and Manchester United are famous worldwide."],
["Women’s football","Women’s football has grown rapidly. The first FIFA Women’s World Cup was held in 1991 and won by the United States, which has won it four times. In 2023 Spain won its first title, in Australia and New Zealand, and crowds and television audiences for women’s matches have reached record levels."]],
sp:["The Miracle of Bern","The 1954 World Cup final in Bern, Switzerland, was a huge upset: West Germany, only recently readmitted to international football, beat the great Hungarian team, which had not lost in four years, by 3 goals to 2. Germans call the match the Miracle of Bern. It lifted a nation still recovering from war, and it showed that even the best team can be beaten."],
g:[["Offside","A rule against waiting behind defenders to receive the ball"],["Penalty","A free shot at goal from 11 meters"],["Formation","How players are arranged on the field"],["Hat-trick","Three goals by one player in a game"]],
f:["About 1.5 billion people watched the 2022 World Cup final.","A football match has two halves of 45 minutes each, plus stoppage time.","A football pitch is about 100 to 110 meters long."]},
bball:{s:[
["Peach baskets","In December 1891 James Naismith, a Canadian teacher at a YMCA training school in Springfield, Massachusetts, invented basketball to keep students active indoors during winter. He nailed two peach baskets to the balcony and wrote 13 rules. The first game ended 1–0. Someone had to climb a ladder to get the ball out after each basket."],
["The NBA","The National Basketball Association was formed in 1946 as the Basketball Association of America and took its current name in 1949. Stars such as Bill Russell, Wilt Chamberlain, Magic Johnson, Larry Bird, Michael Jordan, Kobe Bryant, LeBron James and Stephen Curry made it one of the most watched sports leagues in the world."],
["The Dream Team","At the 1992 Barcelona Olympics, NBA professionals were allowed to play for the first time. The US Dream Team, with Michael Jordan, Magic Johnson and Larry Bird, won every game by an average of about 44 points. It inspired young players around the world and helped make basketball a global sport."],
["Basketball spreads","YMCA teachers carried the game around the world within a few years of its invention, to China, Japan, the Philippines, France and beyond. It became an Olympic sport at the 1936 Berlin Games, where the United States beat Canada in the final, played outdoors on a muddy tennis court. FIBA, the world governing body, was founded in 1932."],
["Leagues around the world","Besides the NBA, professional leagues thrive in Europe, where the EuroLeague brings together top clubs from Spain, Greece, Türkiye and elsewhere, and in China, Australia, Japan, the Philippines and Latin America. The Basketball Africa League began in 2021 in partnership with the NBA. Women’s basketball has its own top league, the WNBA, founded in 1996."],
["Powers of the game","The United States has won most Olympic titles, but other nations have beaten it: the Soviet Union in 1972 and 1988, and Argentina in 2004. Yugoslavia and its successor Serbia, Spain, Lithuania and France are strong in Europe, and Australia, China and the Philippines in Asia and Oceania. Recent World Cups were won by Spain in 2019 and Germany in 2023."]],
sp:["The Dream Team effect","After the 1992 Olympics, when NBA stars first played for the United States, basketball boomed worldwide. Young players from Europe, Africa, Asia and Latin America began reaching the NBA, such as Dirk Nowitzki of Germany, Yao Ming of China and Manu Ginóbili of Argentina. By the 2020s more than a quarter of NBA players were born outside the United States, and several MVP awards went to international stars."],
g:[["Dribble","Bouncing the ball while moving"],["Rebound","Catching the ball after a missed shot"],["Three-pointer","A shot from beyond the three-point line"],["Free throw","An unguarded shot after a foul"]],
f:["A basketball hoop is 3.05 meters, or 10 feet, high.","The shot clock was introduced in the NBA in 1954.","Basketball has been an Olympic sport since 1936."]},
tennis:{s:[
["Lawn tennis","Modern lawn tennis was developed in England in the 1870s. Major Walter Clopton Wingfield patented a version in 1874. The first Wimbledon Championships were held in 1877 by the All England Croquet and Lawn Tennis Club. Tennis quickly spread to the United States, France and Australia."],
["Scoring","A tennis match is divided into points, games and sets. Points are counted 15, 30, 40 and game. If both players reach 40, it is called deuce, and a player must win two points in a row to win. The word love, meaning zero, may come from the French l’oeuf, egg, but its origin is uncertain."],
["The Grand Slams","The four most important tournaments are the Grand Slams: the Australian Open in January, the French Open, on red clay, in May and June, Wimbledon, on grass, in June and July, and the US Open in August and September. Winning all four in one year is called a calendar Grand Slam."],
["Great champions","Champions include Rod Laver, who won the calendar Grand Slam twice; Martina Navratilova, with nine Wimbledon singles titles; Steffi Graf, who won a Golden Slam, all four majors and Olympic gold, in 1988; Serena Williams, with 23 Grand Slam singles titles; and Novak Djokovic, Rafael Nadal and Roger Federer."],
["Billie Jean King","Billie Jean King won 39 Grand Slam titles in singles, doubles and mixed doubles. In 1973 she beat former champion Bobby Riggs in the Battle of the Sexes match, watched by about 90 million people. She led the fight for equal prize money and helped found the Women’s Tennis Association in 1973."],
["Technology and tennis","Rackets were once made of wood. In the 1970s and 1980s, metal and graphite rackets allowed players to hit faster and harder. Electronic line-calling systems such as Hawk-Eye track the ball and decide close calls. Wimbledon replaced human line judges with electronic calling in 2025."]],
sp:["Naomi Osaka","Naomi Osaka was born in Osaka, Japan, in 1997 and grew up mostly in the United States. In 2018 she beat Serena Williams to win the US Open, and she added the Australian Open in 2019 and 2021 and the US Open again in 2020. In January 2019 she became the first Asian player to be ranked number one in the world in singles. She has also spoken openly about mental health and the pressures of top-level sport."],
g:[["Deuce","A score of 40–40"],["Ace","A serve the opponent cannot touch"],["Set","A group of games in a match"],["Grand Slam","One of the four major tournaments"]],
f:["Wimbledon players must wear almost entirely white.","The fastest recorded serves exceed 260 km/h.","Strawberries and cream are a Wimbledon tradition."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-sport2.js */
// In-depth material (Sports, part 2): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
athletics:{s:[
["Ancient running","Running races were the main events of the ancient Olympic Games, which began in 776 BCE. The first and for many years only event was the stadion, a sprint of about 192 meters, the length of the stadium. Later, longer races, jumping, discus and javelin throwing and the pentathlon were added."],
["Sprints and relays","Sprints include the 100 m, 200 m and 400 m. Sprinters start from blocks and must react to the starting gun. In relays, teams of four pass a baton within a marked zone. The 4 × 100 m relay is one of the most exciting events, while the 4 × 400 m often ends a championship."],
["Hurdles and steeplechase","In hurdles races, runners leap over barriers placed along the track: 110 m hurdles for men, 100 m for women and 400 m for both. The 3,000 m steeplechase includes barriers and a water jump. Kenyan runners have dominated the Olympic men’s steeplechase for decades."],
["Jumps","The four jumping events are the long jump, triple jump, high jump and pole vault. In 1968 American Dick Fosbury won Olympic gold in the high jump by going over the bar backward, head first, a style now called the Fosbury flop and used by almost every high jumper. Swedish pole vaulter Armand Duplantis has set many world records."],
["Throws","The throwing events are the shot put, discus, hammer and javelin. Shot putters push a heavy metal ball; men’s shots weigh 7.26 kg. The hammer is a metal ball on a wire with a handle, swung around before release. Throwers compete in a circle or, for javelin, on a runway."],
["Usain Bolt","Jamaican sprinter Usain Bolt is often called the fastest man ever. At the 2009 World Championships in Berlin, he set world records of 9.58 seconds in the 100 m and 19.19 seconds in the 200 m, which still stood in 2025. He won eight Olympic gold medals and was famous for his lightning bolt pose."]],
sp:["Eliud Kipchoge","Eliud Kipchoge of Kenya is widely regarded as the greatest marathon runner in history. Born in 1984, he won Olympic marathon gold at the 2016 and 2020 Games. On 12 October 2019 in Vienna he ran 1:59:40, the first person to cover 42.195 km in under two hours, although the run did not count as an official record because of its pacing arrangements. In 2022 he set an official world record of 2:01:09 in Berlin, which Kelvin Kiptum bettered in 2023."],
g:[["Track","The oval running course"],["Field events","Jumping and throwing competitions"],["Baton","A tube passed in relay races"],["Personal best","A competitor’s best ever result"]],
f:["A standard outdoor running track is 400 meters around.","The marathon distance of 42.195 km was fixed in 1921.","Runners from Kenya and Ethiopia have long dominated the world’s distance races."]},
swimming:{s:[
["Ancient swimmers","Paintings in the Cave of Swimmers in the Egyptian desert, about 10,000 years old, appear to show people swimming. Ancient Egyptians, Greeks and Romans swam for exercise and military training. In many island cultures, swimming and diving were essential everyday skills."],
["Freestyle and the crawl","In freestyle races, swimmers may use any stroke, but almost all use the front crawl, the fastest stroke. Swimmers alternate arms overhead while kicking their legs. The crawl spread in Europe in the early 1900s after swimmers copied styles seen among Pacific Islanders and Native Americans."],
["Backstroke, breaststroke and butterfly","Backstroke is swum on the back, with alternating arms. Breaststroke uses a frog-like kick and is the slowest of the four strokes. Butterfly, with both arms swung forward together and a dolphin kick, developed from breaststroke in the 1930s. The individual medley combines all four."],
["Swimming at the Olympics","Swimming has been part of every modern Olympics since 1896. Early races were held in open water, including the River Seine in Paris in 1900. Today Olympic races take place in 50-meter pools with eight or ten lanes. Marathon swimming, 10 km in open water, was added in 2008."],
["Records and technology","Swimsuits once covered the whole body; in 2008–2009, high-tech full-body polyurethane suits helped swimmers break dozens of world records. In 2010 these suits were banned. Swimmers now wear textile suits, caps and goggles. Pools are designed to reduce waves that slow swimmers."],
["Water safety","Drowning is a leading cause of death among children in many countries. Learning to swim, wearing life jackets on boats, swimming with others and supervising children near water save lives. The World Health Organization marks World Drowning Prevention Day on 25 July."]],
sp:["Johnny Weissmuller","Johnny Weissmuller, born in 1904 in what is now Romania and raised in the United States, won five Olympic gold medals in swimming, in 1924 and 1928, and set dozens of world records. He was the first person to swim 100 m freestyle in under a minute. He later became a film star, playing Tarzan in a dozen movies, and helped make swimming popular around the world."],
g:[["Stroke","A style of swimming"],["Medley","A race using all four strokes"],["Lane","A marked section of a pool for one swimmer"],["Open water","Swimming in lakes, rivers or the sea"]],
f:["Michael Phelps won 23 Olympic gold medals.","Olympic pools are at least 2 meters deep.","Butterfly became an official separate stroke in 1952."]},
cricket:{s:[
["English beginnings","Cricket probably began in southeast England in the 1500s, played by children and later adults on village greens. By the 1700s it was popular with the aristocracy, and clubs formed. The Marylebone Cricket Club, MCC, founded in 1787 at Lord’s ground in London, became the guardian of the laws."],
["Test cricket","Test matches, the longest format, last up to five days. The first Test was played between Australia and England in Melbourne in 1877. Twelve nations now play Test cricket. Teams wear white clothing and use a red ball. Despite their length, many Tests end in a draw."],
["One-day and T20","In 1971 the first One Day International was played, with each side batting for a limited number of overs. In 2003 England introduced Twenty20, T20, in which each side faces 20 overs, about three hours in total. T20 leagues such as the Indian Premier League, started in 2008, attract huge crowds and investment."],
["Bowling","Bowlers deliver the ball with a straight arm, usually bouncing it before it reaches the batter. Fast bowlers can exceed 150 km/h. Spin bowlers make the ball turn sharply after bouncing. Bowlers try to hit the wicket or get the batter caught. Each over consists of six balls."],
["Batting","Batters try to protect their wicket and score runs. Hitting the ball over the boundary on the full scores six runs, and reaching it after it bounces scores four. Batters can also run between the wickets. A score of 100 runs is a century, a great achievement."],
["The Cricket World Cup","The first men’s Cricket World Cup was held in England in 1975, won by the West Indies. Australia has won the most titles. The Women’s World Cup began even earlier, in 1973. India’s victory in 1983 and its 2011 home win made cricket even more popular there. Cricket returns to the Olympics at Los Angeles 2028."]],
sp:["Sachin Tendulkar","Sachin Tendulkar, from Mumbai, made his Test debut for India at 16 in 1989 and played for 24 years. He scored 100 international centuries and over 34,000 international runs, both records. Known as the Little Master, he was so admired in India that some fans treated him almost like a god. In 2014 he became the first sportsperson to receive the Bharat Ratna, India’s highest civilian award."],
g:[["Over","A set of six balls bowled"],["Wicket","Three stumps topped by bails"],["Century","A score of 100 runs"],["Boundary","The edge of the field"]],
f:["Cricket has an estimated 2.5 billion fans, mostly in South Asia.","A Test match can end in a draw even after five days of play.","The Ashes series between England and Australia began in 1882."]},
martial:{s:[
["Ancient fighting arts","Fighting skills have been taught for thousands of years. Wrestling scenes appear in Egyptian tombs, and boxing and pankration, a mix of boxing and wrestling, were events at the ancient Olympics. Martial arts were practiced by soldiers, monks and ordinary people for self-defense, health and discipline."],
["Karate and judo","Karate developed in Okinawa from local fighting arts and Chinese influences and spread to mainland Japan in the 1920s. Judo, meaning gentle way, was founded by Jigoro Kano in 1882 and uses throws and holds. Judo became an Olympic sport in 1964, and karate appeared at the Tokyo 2020 Olympics."],
["Taekwondo","Taekwondo is a Korean martial art known for fast, high and spinning kicks. It was developed in the 1940s and 1950s, combining traditional Korean arts and karate. It became a full Olympic sport in 2000."],
["Boxing history","Modern boxing rules, the Marquess of Queensberry rules, were published in 1867, requiring padded gloves, three-minute rounds and a ten-second count. Great boxers include Muhammad Ali, who called himself the Greatest, Joe Louis and Sugar Ray Robinson. Boxing is an Olympic sport for men and women."],
["Famous boxers","Muhammad Ali won the world heavyweight title three times and became famous for his speed, his words and his stand against the Vietnam War draft. Sugar Ray Robinson is often called the greatest pound-for-pound boxer, and Joe Louis held the heavyweight title for more than eleven years. Manny Pacquiao of the Philippines won world titles in eight weight classes."],
["Mixed martial arts","Mixed martial arts, MMA, combines striking and grappling from many styles. It became popular through events such as the UFC, first held in 1993. Brazilian jiu-jitsu, wrestling, boxing and Muay Thai are among its core styles, and major promotions today include the UFC in the United States and ONE Championship in Asia."]],
sp:["Muhammad Ali","Muhammad Ali, born Cassius Clay in Louisville, Kentucky, in 1942, won Olympic gold in 1960 and became world heavyweight champion in 1964, when he joined the Nation of Islam and changed his name. In 1967 he refused induction into the US Army during the Vietnam War and was stripped of his title and barred from boxing for more than three years. He regained the title in 1974 in the Rumble in the Jungle in Zaire, and again in 1978. He was admired worldwide for his speed, his wit and his courage outside the ring, and he lit the cauldron at the 1996 Atlanta Olympics."],
g:[["Martial art","A system of fighting skills and discipline"],["Belt","A colored band showing a student’s rank"],["Knockout","When a boxer cannot get up within ten seconds"],["Weight division","A class grouping fighters by weight"]],
f:["Muay Thai is known as the art of eight limbs.","Bruce Lee helped make kung fu famous worldwide through films.","A black belt in many arts marks advanced skill, not the end of learning."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-lang1.js */
// In-depth material (Languages, part 1): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
langevo:{s:[
["How many languages?","About 7,000 languages are spoken in the world today. Papua New Guinea alone has over 800, Indonesia over 700, Nigeria over 500 and India over 400. About half of all people speak one of just 23 or so languages."],
["Language families","Languages that come from a common ancestor form a family. Indo-European, including English, Spanish, Hindi and Russian, has the most speakers. Sino-Tibetan includes Mandarin and Tibetan. Niger-Congo, with over 1,500 languages, has the most languages. Austronesian, which includes Malay, Malagasy and Hawaiian, spreads farthest across the globe."],
["Sir William Jones","In 1786 British judge and scholar Sir William Jones, working in India, noticed striking similarities between Sanskrit, Greek and Latin, such as words for mother and father. He suggested they had sprung from some common source, which perhaps no longer exists. This idea founded the study of the Indo-European family."],
["Grammar changes too","Languages change their grammar as well as their words. Old English had many word endings, like German today; modern English relies on word order instead. Latin had no articles, but its descendants, such as Spanish and French, developed words for the, like el and le, from Latin words for that."],
["New words","New words are created all the time. Some are blends, such as smog from smoke and fog. Some are borrowed, others shortened, such as phone from telephone. Languages also mix: Spanglish mixes Spanish and English, Hinglish Hindi and English, and Taglish Tagalog and English. Dictionaries add hundreds of words each year."],
["Saving languages","About 40% of the world’s languages are endangered, often because children grow up speaking a more dominant language. When the last speaker dies, a language becomes extinct. Some languages have been brought back: Hebrew was revived as an everyday language in the 1800s and 1900s, and Welsh and Māori are taught in schools."]],
sp:["Out of Taiwan","Linguists believe the Austronesian languages began in Taiwan about 5,000 years ago. Speakers then sailed south through the Philippines to Indonesia and Malaysia, and eventually across the Pacific and Indian Oceans, reaching Madagascar near Africa, Hawaii, Easter Island and New Zealand. With about 1,200 languages, Austronesian is one of the largest language families. Words such as mata, eye, and lima, five, are shared across this vast area."],
g:[["Language family","Languages from a common ancestor"],["Dialect","A regional or social variety of a language"],["Endangered language","A language at risk of dying out"],["Proto-language","A reconstructed ancestor language"]],
f:["Linguists reconstruct ancestor languages, such as Proto-Indo-European, by comparing descendants.","Hebrew is the best-known example of a language revived for everyday use.","Some languages have only a handful of speakers left."]},
alphabet:{s:[
["Writing begins","Writing was invented independently in several places: Mesopotamia, where Sumerians made cuneiform marks on clay about 5,200 years ago, Egypt, China and Mesoamerica. Early writing systems used hundreds or thousands of signs for words and syllables and were hard to learn."],
["The first alphabet","The first alphabet was developed about 3,800 years ago by Semitic-speaking workers in Egypt and Sinai, who adapted Egyptian hieroglyphs to represent sounds. Each sign stood for a consonant. This idea, using a small set of symbols for sounds, made writing much easier to learn."],
["The Phoenicians spread it","The Phoenicians, seafaring traders from what is now Lebanon, used a 22-letter alphabet of consonants by about 1050 BCE. As they traded across the Mediterranean, their alphabet spread. It became the ancestor of the Greek, Latin, Hebrew and Arabic alphabets."],
["Adding vowels","The Greeks adapted the Phoenician alphabet around 800 BCE and added letters for vowels, creating the first true alphabet with both consonants and vowels. Phoenician aleph, an ox, and bet, a house, became Greek alpha and beta, giving us the word alphabet."],
["The Latin alphabet","The Romans adapted the alphabet from the Etruscans, who had learned it from the Greeks. The Latin alphabet spread across Europe with the Roman Empire and Christianity. Later, colonizers and missionaries spread it worldwide. It is now the most widely used writing system, including for Vietnamese, Indonesian, Swahili and Turkish."],
["Changing scripts","Some countries have switched scripts. Türkiye replaced Arabic script with the Latin alphabet in 1928, Vietnam adopted Latin letters in the early 1900s, and Kazakhstan is moving from Cyrillic to Latin. Mongolia uses Cyrillic but is reviving its traditional vertical script, and Serbian is written in both Cyrillic and Latin letters."]],
sp:["Hangul","Hangul, the Korean alphabet, was created in 1443 by King Sejong the Great and his scholars so that ordinary people, not just the educated elite who used Chinese characters, could read and write. Its letters are designed to reflect the shapes the mouth and tongue make when pronouncing sounds. Letters are grouped into syllable blocks. Korea celebrates Hangul Day on 9 October, and linguists often praise it as one of the most logical writing systems ever created."],
g:[["Alphabet","A set of letters representing sounds"],["Consonant","A speech sound made by blocking air"],["Vowel","A speech sound made with an open mouth"],["Cuneiform","Wedge-shaped writing on clay"]],
f:["The Khmer alphabet of Cambodia has the most letters of any alphabet, 74.","The newest letter in English, J, became separate from I in the 1500s.","The Rotokas alphabet of Papua New Guinea has only 12 letters."]},
english:{s:[
["The Anglo-Saxons","English began with Germanic tribes, the Angles, Saxons and Jutes, who settled in Britain from the 400s CE. Their language, Old English, looked very different from modern English. The epic poem Beowulf was written in Old English. The word English comes from the Angles."],
["Vikings and Normans","Viking settlers from Scandinavia added Norse words, such as sky, egg, knife, they and window. In 1066 the Normans from France conquered England, and French became the language of the rulers for about 300 years. Thousands of French words entered English, such as government, court, beef and pork."],
["Chaucer and Middle English","Middle English, spoken from about 1100 to 1500, mixed Old English and French. Geoffrey Chaucer’s Canterbury Tales, written in the late 1300s, is its most famous work. In the 1400s, the Great Vowel Shift changed how long vowels were pronounced, one reason English spelling seems strange."],
["Shakespeare and the King James Bible","William Shakespeare, writing around 1600, used and invented many words and phrases still common today, such as eyeball, bedroom and break the ice. The King James Bible of 1611 also shaped English. Together they set patterns of expression that people still use."],
["English goes global","Through the British Empire and later American power, films, music and the internet, English spread worldwide. About 1.5 billion people speak it as a first or second language. It is the main language of international business, aviation, science and the internet."],
["Thomasites","English became an official or second language in many former British and American colonies, such as India, Nigeria, Kenya, Singapore and the Philippines. In India, for example, it serves as a link language among speakers of hundreds of other languages and is widely used in higher education, business and courts."]],
sp:["World Englishes","English is now spoken in many varieties, sometimes called World Englishes. Indians say prepone, the opposite of postpone. Singaporeans add lah to the end of sentences, Nigerians call a traffic jam a go-slow, and Filipinos say comfort room for toilet. The Oxford English Dictionary has added hundreds of words from these varieties, showing that English belongs to all who speak it."],
g:[["Old English","The English of the Anglo-Saxons"],["Loanword","A word borrowed from another language"],["Variety","A distinct form of a language"],["Lingua franca","A common language used between speakers of different languages"]],
f:["English has borrowed words from over 350 languages.","The Oxford English Dictionary contains over 500,000 words and phrases.","The most common letter in English is E."]},
spanish:{s:[
["Castile","Spanish developed from the Latin spoken in the Iberian Peninsula. It took its name Castilian, castellano, from the kingdom of Castile in central Spain. As Castile expanded during the Reconquista, its language spread across much of Spain. In 1492 Antonio de Nebrija published the first grammar of Spanish."],
["Arabic words","Muslim rulers controlled much of Spain from 711 to 1492, and thousands of Arabic words entered Spanish, especially words beginning with al-, such as almohada, pillow, azúcar, sugar, and aceite, oil. Many of these words passed into Portuguese and into the languages of Spain’s former colonies."],
["Spanish in the Americas","After 1492, Spanish spread across the Americas. It absorbed words from Indigenous languages, such as chocolate, tomate and aguacate from Nahuatl, the Aztec language. Mexico is now the country with the most Spanish speakers, over 120 million. Spanish varies from country to country in accent and vocabulary."],
["Sounds and spelling","Spanish spelling is mostly phonetic: words are generally pronounced as they are written. Its five vowels each have one main sound. The letter ñ, as in año, is distinctive. Questions and exclamations begin with upside-down marks, ¿ and ¡. Accents show which syllable to stress."],
["Spanish words around the world","Spanish has given words to many languages. English took words such as canyon, patio, mosquito, tornado and ranch. Philippine languages borrowed thousands, such as mesa, table, and kutsara, spoon, and many Filipino surnames come from Spanish. Guam’s Chamorro and Chavacano, a creole of the southern Philippines, also contain many Spanish words."],
["Chavacano","Spanish-based creoles developed where Spanish mixed with other languages. Chavacano, spoken by several hundred thousand people in the southern Philippines, has a largely Spanish vocabulary with grammar influenced by Philippine languages, and Palenquero in Colombia mixes Spanish with African languages. Ladino, or Judeo-Spanish, was carried by Jews expelled from Spain in 1492."]],
sp:["The Royal Spanish Academy","The Royal Spanish Academy, founded in Madrid in 1713, publishes the official dictionary and grammar of Spanish. Its motto is Limpia, fija y da esplendor, it cleans, fixes and gives splendor. Today it works with 22 other academies across the Americas, the Philippines and Equatorial Guinea, together keeping Spanish a single language understood by its hundreds of millions of speakers."],
g:[["Castilian","Another name for Spanish"],["Creole","A language formed from mixing languages that becomes a mother tongue"],["Phonetic","Spelled as it sounds"],["Reconquista","The Christian reconquest of Spain"]],
f:["Spanish is the world’s second most spoken native language, after Mandarin.","About 90 percent of Spanish speakers live in the Americas.","El Día del Idioma Español is celebrated on 23 April."]}
});

;
/* data/deep-more-lang2.js */
// In-depth material (Languages, part 2): six more sections, a Spotlight box, key words and extra facts per article.
window.DEEP2=window.DEEP2||{};Object.assign(window.DEEP2,{
mandarin:{s:[
["The most spoken language","Mandarin Chinese has more native speakers than any other language, about 940 million, and over 1.1 billion speakers in total. It is the official language of China and Taiwan and one of the official languages of Singapore. It is one of the six official languages of the United Nations."],
["Putonghua","Standard Mandarin, called Putonghua, common speech, in mainland China, is based on the dialect of Beijing. Before the 20th century, people across China spoke many very different varieties, such as Cantonese, Hokkien, Shanghainese and Hakka. These are so different that speakers often cannot understand each other, though they share a writing system."],
["Pinyin","Pinyin is the official system for writing Mandarin sounds in the Latin alphabet, adopted in China in 1958. It helps children and foreigners learn pronunciation, and it is used for typing Chinese on computers and phones. Accent marks show tones, as in mā, má, mǎ and mà."],
["Simple and traditional characters","In the 1950s and 1960s, mainland China simplified thousands of characters, reducing the number of strokes to make them easier to learn. Taiwan, Hong Kong and Macau still use traditional characters. For example, the character for horse is 马 in simplified and 馬 in traditional form."],
["Measure words","Mandarin uses measure words between a number and a noun, a little like saying a sheet of paper in English. Different measure words go with different kinds of things: 本 běn for books, 张 zhāng for flat things like tables and paper, and 个 gè as a general one. Learners must memorize which measure words go with which nouns."],
["Learning Mandarin","Mandarin is increasingly studied around the world. Universities and Confucius Institutes teach it in many countries, and the HSK exam measures a learner’s level. Chinese New Year, called Spring Festival, is celebrated by Chinese communities worldwide and is a public holiday in several countries."]],
sp:["Cantonese and Hokkien","Mandarin is only one of many Chinese languages. Cantonese is spoken in Guangdong, Hong Kong and Macau and by many overseas Chinese communities, and Hokkien, a Southern Min language, is spoken in Fujian, in Taiwan and across Southeast Asia. These languages sound very different from Mandarin, although all are written with the same characters. This is why standard Mandarin, taught in schools across China, matters so much for communication between regions."],
g:[["Tone","A pitch pattern that changes a word’s meaning"],["Pinyin","Mandarin written with Latin letters"],["Character","A written Chinese symbol"],["Measure word","A word used between a number and a noun"]],
f:["The character for person, 人, looks like a walking figure.","A Chinese dictionary may contain over 50,000 characters, but about 3,000 cover most daily reading.","Chinese has been written for more than 3,000 years."]},
latin:{s:[
["From Latium to an empire","Latin began as the language of the Latins, a people of the region of Latium around Rome, about 3,000 years ago. As Rome conquered Italy and then the Mediterranean world, Latin spread with soldiers, settlers and officials. In the western Roman Empire, it replaced many local languages."],
["Cases and word order","Latin is an inflected language: word endings show a word’s role in the sentence. In Puella canem videt, the girl sees the dog, and in Canem puella videt, the meaning stays the same. Changing endings changes meaning. Latin nouns have up to six cases."],
["Vulgar Latin","Ordinary people spoke Vulgar Latin, everyday Latin that differed from the polished language of writers like Cicero. After the western Roman Empire fell in 476, Vulgar Latin developed differently in different regions, eventually becoming the Romance languages: Italian, Spanish, French, Portuguese and Romanian."],
["The Church and scholars","Latin remained the language of the Catholic Church and of learning in Europe for over a thousand years. Scholars such as Thomas Aquinas, Copernicus, Galileo and Newton wrote major works in Latin. Until the 1960s, the Catholic Mass was celebrated in Latin worldwide."],
["Latin in law and medicine","Many legal and medical terms come from Latin: habeas corpus, ad hoc, alibi, affidavit and subpoena in law, and terms such as femur, cranium, vertebra and in vitro in medicine. Scientists use Latin names for species, such as Homo sapiens, wise human, and Panthera leo, the lion."],
["Mottoes","Many institutions use Latin mottoes. The University of Santo Tomas has Veritas in Caritate, truth in charity. The United States uses E pluribus unum, out of many, one. Harvard has Veritas, truth. The Olympic motto, Citius, Altius, Fortius, means faster, higher, stronger."]],
sp:["The Vatican’s Latin","Latin is still an official language of Vatican City. The Vatican publishes official documents in Latin and even has a Latin dictionary of modern words, the Lexicon Recentis Latinitatis, which created Latin terms for things like computers, instrumentum computatorium, and jeans, bracae linteae caeruleae. Popes have sent messages in Latin on social media. The Vatican had cash machines with instructions in Latin."],
g:[["Inflection","Changes in word endings to show grammar"],["Romance languages","Languages descended from Latin"],["Motto","A short phrase expressing an ideal"],["Vulgar Latin","Everyday spoken Latin"]],
f:["About 60% of English words come from Latin, directly or through French.","Carpe diem means seize the day.","The abbreviations a.m. and p.m. stand for ante meridiem and post meridiem."]},
arabic:{s:[
["Speakers","Arabic is spoken by over 400 million people across the Middle East and North Africa and is an official language in more than 20 countries. It is one of the six official languages of the United Nations. As the language of the Quran, it is also studied by many of the world’s nearly 2 billion Muslims."],
["Right to left","Arabic is written from right to left in a flowing, joined script with 28 letters. Most letters change shape depending on whether they come at the start, middle or end of a word. Short vowels are usually not written but can be shown by small marks above and below letters."],
["Classical and spoken Arabic","Modern Standard Arabic, based on the Classical Arabic of the Quran, is used in books, newspapers, news broadcasts and formal speeches across the Arab world. In daily life, people speak regional dialects, such as Egyptian, Levantine, Gulf and Maghrebi Arabic, which can differ greatly from one another."],
["Words from Arabic","English has borrowed many words from Arabic, often through Spanish or Latin: algebra, algorithm, alcohol, coffee, sugar, cotton, sofa, magazine, zero and giraffe. Many star names, such as Aldebaran and Betelgeuse, are Arabic too. Arabic numerals reached Europe through Arabic texts."],
["A language of learning","During the Islamic Golden Age, Arabic was the international language of science and philosophy. Greek, Persian and Indian works were translated into Arabic, and scholars wrote new works in it. Later, many of these Arabic books were translated into Latin, helping to spark learning in medieval Europe."],
["Arabic loanwords","Spanish has thousands of words of Arabic origin, including azúcar, almohada and aceite. English borrowed algebra, algorithm, zero, coffee, sugar and alcohol. Swahili takes its name from the Arabic word sawāḥil, meaning coasts, and Malay, Persian and Urdu all contain many Arabic words, often written in an Arabic-based script."]],
sp:["The Kufic and Naskh scripts","Arabic calligraphy developed several major styles. Kufic, one of the oldest, named after the city of Kufa in Iraq, is angular and was used for early Quran manuscripts and on buildings. Naskh, rounder and easier to read, became the standard for books and is the basis of most printed Arabic today. Thuluth is a grand decorative style used on mosques, and Diwani was used by the Ottoman court for official documents."],
g:[["Script","A system of written characters"],["Dialect","A regional form of a language"],["Calligraphy","The art of beautiful writing"],["Jawi","Arabic script adapted for Malay and related languages"]],
f:["The word Arabic in Arabic is al-ʿarabiyya.","UN Arabic Language Day is 18 December.","Arabic has many words for camel and love."]},
signlang:{s:[
["How sign languages work","Sign languages use handshapes, movements, locations and facial expressions to communicate. Raising the eyebrows can turn a statement into a question. Signers use the space around them to show who is doing what to whom. Sign languages have their own grammar, different from the spoken languages around them."],
["Many sign languages","There is no single universal sign language. There are more than 300 sign languages in the world. American Sign Language and British Sign Language are very different, though both countries speak English. Japanese Sign Language, Libras in Brazil and Auslan in Australia are other examples."],
["The first schools","In the 1700s, Charles-Michel de l’Épée founded a free school for deaf children in Paris, using signs. In 1817 Thomas Gallaudet and Laurent Clerc, a deaf teacher from the Paris school, founded the first American school for the deaf in Connecticut. Gallaudet University, for deaf students, was founded in 1864."],
["The Milan Congress","In 1880 a conference of educators in Milan voted to ban sign language in deaf schools in favor of oralism, teaching deaf children only to lip-read and speak. For decades, children were punished for signing. Many deaf people believe this harmed their education. In 2010 the organizers’ successors formally rejected the Milan resolutions."],
["Nicaraguan Sign Language","In the 1970s and 1980s, deaf children brought together in new schools in Nicaragua created their own sign language. Younger children made it more complex and regular. Linguists studied it as a rare chance to watch a new language being born, showing that children naturally create grammar."],
["Deaf education","The first public school for deaf children was founded in Paris by the Abbé de l’Épée in the 1760s. In 1817 Thomas Gallaudet and Laurent Clerc opened the American School for the Deaf in Hartford, and Gallaudet University in Washington, D.C., founded in 1864, is the world’s best-known university for deaf students. In many countries, schools for the deaf teach in sign language."]],
sp:["Deaf culture","Many deaf people see themselves as part of a cultural and linguistic community, often writing Deaf with a capital D. Deaf culture has its own traditions, humor, poetry, theater and history, shared through sign language. Deaf people get attention by tapping shoulders, waving or flicking lights. September is celebrated as Deaf Awareness Month, and 23 September is the International Day of Sign Languages, chosen because the World Federation of the Deaf was founded on that day in 1951."],
g:[["Sign language","A visual language using hands, face and body"],["Fingerspelling","Spelling words letter by letter with the hands"],["Interpreter","A person who translates between languages"],["Oralism","Teaching deaf people only through speech"]],
f:["Babies can learn to sign before they can speak.","Some animals, such as the gorilla Koko, were taught signs, though scientists debate whether this is language.","Helen Keller, who was deaf and blind, learned language through signs spelled into her hand."]}
});

;
